 vs. 9% for placebo), and headache (6% for 60 mg, 7% for 30 mg vs. 3% for placebo).\nNotably, there were no reported adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular edema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths [252].\nThese positive results and the favorable risk-benefit profile collectively support the ongoing clinical development of tamuzimod for treating moderately-to-severely active UC.", "date": "2025-12-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.99}, "jev_score": 0.9801, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Articles Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2468125324003868", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events were reported 3347%) 70 tamuzod  mg,34 (47%) of 73 tamuzod  mg, 24 (34%) of 70 on placebo, with most events being mild or moderate.\nThe most commonly treatment-ement adverse (in5% of any treatment group) included respiratory tract (six9% the tamimod60 mg, one [1%] the tamimod30 mg, and one [1%] in the placebo group), an (three3% four [], and [9]), andfour [%], [7], and two [3%]).\nThere were no recorded cases atrioricular block bradardia, macular ed, severe opportunistic, malign, or fatalities.\n...\nAlthough S1PR modulators showed a higher incidence of any adverse event compared to placebo, serious adverse events did not differ significantly between the groups.\n...\nTreatment with both doses of tamuzimod was well tolerated, and a dose titration over a week helped reduce-dose effects, as brycardia and conduction issues, commonly associated with this class of drugs.\nImportantly, no adverse events related to atrioventricular block, macular ed, severe opportunistic, malign, or fatalities were reported in this trial.\n...\nTamuzimod was found to be effective and well tolerated in this short-term randomized controlled trial involving patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2025-03-05", "last_updated": "2025-11-05", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9933}, "jev_score": 0.9635, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nEvent rates of serious treatment-emergent adverse events were 11·7 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 12·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nThe most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster.\nThree treatment-emergent adverse events leading to death were reported in the long-term extension-only safety population.This interim analysis supports the positive long-term risk–benefit profile for upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg among patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.AbbVie.\n...\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences.\n...\nIn the induction studies, the most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs; ≥5% of patients in any group) were abdominal pain; arthralgia; an exacerbation, flare, or worsening of Crohn's disease; headache; nasopharyngitis; nausea; and pyrexia.\nIn the maintenance study, the most frequently reported TEAEs (≥5% of patients in any filgotinib or associated placebo group) were those reported in the induction studies (except for headache) and abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain, anaemia, and flatulence.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.97}, "jev_score": 0.8827, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative colitis, active moderate - Drugs, Targets, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/disease/cf939ce88f9b42f9b400ce70eaf89a7a", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.INTERPRETATIONInduction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGVentyx Biosciences.\n...\nIn the induction study, adverse events were reported by 49% of patients in both groups (208 of 421 guselkumab-treated patients and 138 of 280 placebo-treated patients), serious adverse events were reported by 3% (12 of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 7% (20 of 280) of placebo-treated patients, and adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation were reported by 2% (seven of 421) of guselkumab-treated patients and 4% (11 of 280) of placebo-treated patients.\nIn the maintenance study, adverse event rates were similar among groups, and the most frequently reported adverse events in all groups were ulcerative colitis, COVID-19, and arthralgia.\nNo active tuberculosis, anaphylaxis, serum sickness, or clinically important hepatic disorders were reported in either study.INTERPRETATIONGuselkumab was effective and safe as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.FUNDINGJanssen Research and Development.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.98}, "jev_score": 0.882, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Sphingosine-1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators for the ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12650479/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "They also appear to have a favourable safety profile, although awareness of potential cardiovascular and ophthalmological side-effects is crucial for safe use.\n...\nBradycardia occurred more often with ozanimod in the induction cohort; however, there was no difference from placebo during the maintenance study [23].\n...\nTamuzimod is a selective S1PRM that acts on S1PR1 and has completed phase two trials in UC.\nThese trials demonstrated that tamuzimod at both 30 mg (*p* = 0.041) and 60 mg (*p* = 0.018) was associated with significantly higher rates of remission at 13 weeks compared to placebo [3].\nThere were no cardiovascular-related AEs reported and no cases of macular oedema.\nPhase 3 trials are awaited.", "date": "2025-10-29", "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.8967}, "jev_score": 0.8788, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Endpoint Outcomes", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/30/12/2245/7624797", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Serious adverse events were reported in 5.2% of patients; 2.8% discontinued treatment due to adverse events.\n...\nSevere treatment-emergent adverse events were reported in 4.5% of patients; 5.2% experienced serious adverse events, with 2.8% discontinuing treatment due to an adverse event.\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events were pyrexia, diarrhea, injection site pain, abdominal pain, and gastroenteritis.\nThere were no deaths through week 104.\n...\nCOVID-19 (12.1%) was observed as a most common adverse event (≥3%), which is not surprising, as this study was held during the COVID-19 epidemic.\n...\nNo new safety signals were identified, and the discontinuation rate due to adverse events was 2.8%.", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.9933}, "jev_score": 0.8741, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "mirikizumab 300 mg, intravenous, every 4 weeks up to week \n...\nAfter 28 weeks of maintenance therapy, \n89.6% (as observed) of baseline corticosteroid users had \ndiscontinued this therapy.\n...\nIn the 12-week dosing group, \n26 participants experienced a so-called drug event, defined \nas a change in dosing interval or re-introduction during the follow-up period.\nOf these, 22 intensified their ustekinumab therapy (mostly switching to an 8-week regimen), 3 switched therapies, and 1 discontinued therapy due to a malignancy.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\nEndoscopic remission was documented in 46.4%\n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n1. Sands BE, et al.\n...\n2. Danese S, et al.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.\n“These were, however, predominantly upper respiratory infections.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.87}, "jev_score": 0.8352, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Sphingosine 1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators as ... - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41968221/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Eight trials involving 2663 participants were included.\nAnalysis comparing S1PR modulators with placebo included etrasimod, ozanimod, tamuzimod, and KRP203.\n...\nAdverse events were slightly increased with S1PR modulators (RR 1.13; p = 0.002), while serious adverse events (p = 0.73) and discontinuations (p = 0.67) did not differ significantly from placebo.\n...\n** S1PR modulators demonstrate robust efficacy during the induction phase across all key clinical and tissue-based outcomes, with more modest or variable effects during maintenance.\nTheir safety profile remains acceptable, supporting their role as a promising therapeutic option for UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.6833}, "jev_score": 0.6423, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Mirikizumab as Induction and Maintenance Therapy for Ulcerative Colitis | NEJM", "url": "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2207940", "domain": "nejm.org", "snippet": "Opportunistic infection (herpes zoster) occurred in 1 patient who received placebo in the induction trial.During the 52-week treatment period, among the 1217 patients who received mirikizumab, cancer was reported in 8.\nAdenocarcinoma of the colon was reported in 2 patients during the induction trial, and nonmelanoma skin cancer and gastric cancer in 1 patient each during the double-blind portion of the maintenance trial (Table 2).\n...\nThere were two deaths during the follow-up period for the induction trial: one from sudden cardiac arrest, and one from disseminated intravascular coagulation.\n...\nThere were no instances of a major adverse cardiovascular event (MACE) during the induction trial.\nOne instance of MACE (ischemic stroke) occurred in the placebo group during the maintenance trial.\nIn the mirikizumab group during the induction trial, both cancers were colon adenocarcinoma.\nDuring the maintenance trial, nonmelanoma skin cancer (basal-cell carcinoma) occurred in one patient in the placebo group and gastric cancer in one patient in the mirikizumab group.\n...\nOne patient in the mirikizumab group during the maintenance trial had a suicide attempt.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.45, "evidence": 0.6633}, "jev_score": 0.2985, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1842.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q001-1", "row_id": "q001", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Tamuzimod long-term extension safety ulcerative colitis", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "search_latency_ms": 3004.0, "target": "Tamuzimod", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 543.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9912, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000416, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8504, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "The Current Sphingosine 1 Phosphate Receptor Modulators ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12111885/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "No new safety signals were identified [32,33], but the risk of rarer adverse events with longer-term use remains to be elucidated.\n...\nThe study is still ongoing, and an interim analysis demonstrated clinical response and remission rates comparable to the True North studies, with no new safety signals identified.\n...\nOverall, the risk of cardiac conduction disorders with the use of S1PR modulators is low in appropriately selected patients.\n...\nTamuzimod was investigated in a phase 2, multi-centre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study for patients with moderate to severely active UC [53].\nIt met its primary endpoint of clinical remission with superiority over placebo (27.9% vs. 11.4%, *p* = 0.0184).\nThere were no new safety signals identified.\nLong-term and open-label extension phases of the study are ongoing.\nLong term extensions and phase 4 studies of ozanimod and etrasimod will provide additional data regarding long term efficacy and safety in ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2025-05-15", "last_updated": "2026-02-15", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8767}, "jev_score": 0.8504, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "A subgroup analysis of the phase 3 LUCENT-2 study demonstrated that the corticosteroid-sparing effect of mirikizumab was clinically meaningful among\nparticipants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8733}, "jev_score": 0.8471, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ...", "url": "https://ventyxbio.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/VTX002-Phase-2-LTE-Presentation-UEG-Week-2024.pdf", "domain": "ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data\n...\ntreatment of UC\n• The efficacy and safety of tamuzimod as induction therapy was previously demonstrated in a phase 2, \nmulticentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (NCT05156125)1\n...\nKey eligibility criteria:\n•\nModerately to severely active UC\n•\nNo/insufficient response, loss of response, and/or intolerance to conventional or advanced therapies (≤ 2 biologics with different mechanisms of action or 1 biologic + a Janus kinase inhibitor)\nPrimary endpoint: clinical remission (MMS stool frequency (SF) subscore ≤ 1, rectal bleeding (RB) = 0, endoscopic subscore (ES) ≤ 1)   \nat Week 13 in patients with baseline MMS 5-9\nLong-term extension: clinical responders (≥ 2-point and ≥ 30% decrease from baseline in MMS, and ≥ 1-point decrease from baseline   \nin RB or an absolute RB ≤ 1) at Week 13 were eligible for maintenance therapy with previously assigned treatment for up to 39 weeks\n...\n• Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and   \nwell-tolerated for up to 52 weeks\n• High rates of both clinical and endoscopic remission observed during tamuzimod \ninduction and maintenance therapy potentially a result of rapid and sustained absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) reductions\n• 58.9% (tamuzimod 30 mg) and 71.5% (tamuzimod 60 mg) ALC decrease from baseline at Week 52\n• Efficacy and safety data from this LTE treatment period support the continued clinical\ndevelopment in UC and the use of tamuzimod 60 mg in phase 3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-28", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.84}, "jev_score": 0.8232, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.\n...\n## Sphingosine 1-Phosphate (S1P) Receptor Modulators as an Induction and Maintenance Therapy for Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials. (PubMed, Dig Dis Sci) - \"S1PR modulators demonstrate robust efficacy during the induction phase across all key clinical and tissue-based outcomes, with more modest or variable effects during maintenance. Their safety profile remains acceptable, supporting their role as a promising therapeutic option for UC.\"\n...\n## Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial. (PubMed, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol) - P2 | \"Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\n## Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024 (GlobeNewswire) - \"Ventyx Biosciences, Inc...announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.722, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase ...", "url": "https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-phase-2-trial-vtx002-tamuzimod", "domain": "nasdaq.com", "snippet": "### Late-breaking abstract highlights high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission among LTE completers at Week 52\n...\n(Nasdaq: VTYX) (“Ventyx”), a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on advancing novel oral therapies that address a broad range of inflammatory diseases with significant unmet medical need, today announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\n“We are excited to present the new long-term extension data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at UEG Week,” said Raju Mohan, PhD, Chief Executive Officer.\n“These 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of our S1P1R modulator tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis, with a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy.\nWe believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies for UC.”\n...\n**Title: **“Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data”", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-03-28", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.6798, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.673, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "EX-99.1 - SEC.gov", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1851194/000095017024123323/vtyx-ex99_1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "Tamuzimod (formerly VTX002, S1P1R Modulator): We recently presented new long-term extension (LTE) data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\nThese 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis (UC) including a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy.\nWe believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies.", "date": "2024-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-03-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6133}, "jev_score": 0.601, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "In the long-term extension-only safety analysis, we assessed data from 467 patients, representing 1027·9 patient-years of continuous long-term extension exposure on a consistent upadacitinib dose.\nTreatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nEvent rates of serious treatment-emergent adverse events were 11·7 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 12·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\nThe most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster.\nThree treatment-emergent adverse events leading to death were reported in the long-term extension-only safety population.This interim analysis supports the positive long-term risk–benefit profile for upadacitinib 15 mg and 30 mg among patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis.AbbVie.\n...\nWe aimed to assess the safety and efficacy of tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.This double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial was conducted at 122 centres across 15 countries in Asia, Europe, and North America.\n...\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.72, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.4824, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Interim Analysis of the True North Open-label Extension | Journal of ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/18/2/264/7257326", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "No new safety signals emerged from this analysis.\nSerious infections, malignancy, cardiovascular events, and hepatic events occurred infrequently.\n...\nRates of TEAEs, serious TEAEs, and TEAEs leading to treatment discontinuation over the ~3-year treatment period [during the True North parent study or the OLE] were 83.2%, 18.3%, and 6.1%, respectively.\n...\nImportantly, no new safety signals were observed with long-term continuous ozanimod use during the True North parent study and the subsequent OLE.\n...\nWe did not see any new safety signals during the True North parent study^8^ and subsequent OLE.\n...\nNotably, long-term ozanimod use did not increase the risk of thromboembolic events or major adverse cardiovascular events.\n...\nNo new safety signals were identified with long-term ozanimod use; AEs were manageable and infrequently led to treatment discontinuation.", "date": "2024-02-26", "last_updated": "2026-08-18", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.49, "evidence": 0.9067}, "jev_score": 0.4443, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/b4d8c6e9b19a166d/nct05156125-clinical-efficacy-safety-vtx002-ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "# A Phase 2, Multicenter, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Parallel-Group Study to Evaluate the Clinical Efficacy and Safety of VTX002 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\n...\nThis is a study to understand if taking VTX002 daily as a tablet orally is safe and effective in participants diagnosed with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis (UC).\nApproximately 189 participants will take VTX002 Dose A, VTX002 Dose B, or matching placebo, once daily.\nThe study consists of a 28-day Screening Period (to see if a participant qualifies for the study), a 13-week double-blind period (a participant receives either active Dose A, Dose B or Placebo), a Long-Term Extension (LTE) Treatment Period of up to 39 weeks, an Open-Label Extension (OLE) Treatment Period of up to 143 weeks, and a 2-week Follow-Up Period.\nThe maximal duration of treatment including the Induction Period, LTE and OLE will be 36 months.\n...\nThis is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of VTX002 in subjects with moderately to severely active UC following daily oral administration of VTX002 as a tablet.\n...\nThe study consists of a 28-day Screening Period, a 13-week double-blind Induction Treatment Period (including 7 days of titration followed by 12 weeks of treatment at the assigned dose), a Long-Term Extension (LTE) Treatment Period of up to 39 weeks, an Open-Label Extension (OLE) Treatment Period of up to 143 weeks, and a 2-week Follow-Up Period.\nThe maximal duration of treatment including the Induction Period, LTE and OLE will be 36 months.\n...\n### Safety\n**Safety**\n|Group|Serious adverse events|Deaths|\n|--|--|--|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|3 of 70|0 of 70|\n|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|2 of 73|0 of 73|\n|Placebo Once Daily (Induction Period)|0 of 70|0 of 70|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|0 of 33|0 of 33|\n|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|2 of 37|0 of 37|\n|Placebo Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|2 of 25|0 of 25|\n|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Open-Label Extension Period)|8 of 190|0 of 190|\nMost frequent serious adverse events (participants)\n...\n|Event|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Induction Period)|Placebo Once Daily (Induction Period)|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|VTX002 30 mg Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|Placebo Once Daily (Long-Term Extension Period)|VTX002 60 mg Once Daily (Open-Label Extension Period)|\n...\n|Colitis ulcerative|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|1 of 25|3 of 190|\n|Anaemia|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Cholecystitis acute|0 of 70|1 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Pulmonary oedema|1 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Rectal haemorrhage|0 of 70|1 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Anal fistula|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|1 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Diarrhoea haemorrhagic|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|1 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Peritonitis|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|1 of 37|0 of 25|0 of 190|\n|Gastroduodenal haemorrhage|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|1 of 190|\n|Angina unstable|0 of 70|0 of 73|0 of 70|0 of 33|0 of 37|0 of 25|1 of 190|", "date": "2024-11-13", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.59, "evidence": 0.56}, "jev_score": 0.3304, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3547.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q001-4", "row_id": "q001", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Tamuzimod open-label extension adverse events", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "search_latency_ms": 2791.8, "target": "Tamuzimod", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 380.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9271, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000389, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9536, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Silvio Danese - ScienceDirect.com", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004453034/silvio-danese", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent adverse events were recorded at 238·5 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 15 mg and 233·4 events per 100 patient-years for upadacitinib 30 mg.\n...\nThe most common adverse events of special interest were hepatic disorder, lymphopenia, creatine phosphokinase elevation, serious infection, neutropenia, and herpes zoster.\n...\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk−benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.Ventyx Biosciences.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9933}, "jev_score": 0.9536, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Font.pdf", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1851194/000095017025057492/vtyx_-_ars_-_2024_form_1.pdf", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "adults, tamuzimod was well tolerated with no serious adverse events.\n...\nIn our Phase 1 trial, tamuzimod was well-tolerated with no serious adverse events or notable safety findings.\n...\nTamuzimod was well tolerated in both the 30 mg and 60 mg dose cohorts, with no treatment-related serious adverse events observed.\nThere were no serious or opportunistic infections, and no instances of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, or macular edema were observed.\n...\nIn October 2024, we announced positive results from the long-term extension of the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in participants with UC.\nMaintenance treatment with tamuzimod was well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks and was associated with high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission at Week 52 (Figure 23).\nSpecifically, at week 52, 50% of patients on the tamuzimod 60 mg dose or tamuzimod\n30 mg dose achieved clinical remission compared to 11% of patients on placebo (Figure 23).\nOn the secondary endpoint of endoscopic\nimprovement (Mayo endoscopic subscore ≤1), 46% of patients on tamuzimod 60 mg and 44% of patients on tamuzimod 30 mg achieved endoscopic improvement, compared to 18% of patients on placebo.\n...\nAn “open-label” clinical trial is one where both the patient and investigator know whether the patient is receiving the investigational product candidate or either an existing approved drug or placebo.\nMost typically, open-label clinical trials test only the investigational product candidate and sometimes may do so at different dose levels.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-06", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9433}, "jev_score": 0.9244, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "10-K", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1851194/000095017025029245/vtyx-20241231.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "In our Phase 1 trial, tamuzimod was well-tolerated with no serious adverse events or notable safety findings.\n...\nTamuzimod was well tolerated in both the 30 mg and 60 mg dose cohorts, with no treatment-related serious adverse events observed.\nThere were no serious or opportunistic infections, and no instances of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, or macular edema were observed.\n...\nIn October 2024, we announced positive results from the long-term extension of the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in participants with UC.\nMaintenance treatment with tamu\nzimod was well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks and was associated with high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission at Week 52 (Figure 23).\nSpecifically, at week 52, 50% of patients on the tamuzimod 60 mg dose or tamuzimod 30 mg dose achieved clinical remission compared to 11% of patients on placebo (Figure 23).\n...\nAdditionally, some of the clinical trials we conduct in the future may be open-label in study design and may be conducted at a limited number of clinical sites on a limited number of patients.\nAn “open-label” clinical trial is one where both the patient and investigator know whether the patient is receiving the investigational product candidate or either an existing approved drug or placebo.\nMost typically, open-label clinical trials test only the investigational product candidate and sometimes may \ndo so at different dose levels.", "date": "2022-12-31", "last_updated": "2025-07-15", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.89}, "jev_score": 0.8722, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Corporate Presentation - datocms-assets.com", "url": "https://www.datocms-assets.com/92605/1772147952-ventyx-investor-deck-june-2025-final.pdf", "domain": "datocms-assets.com", "snippet": "Preliminary open-label extension data cut reported Q1 2024 (as of February 2024)\nInduction results reported Q4 2023\nOPEN-LABEL EXTENSION – VTX002 60 MG UP TO ~2 YEARS\nPatients with \nmoderate to \nsevere UC\n(N=213)\n...\nSubgroup analysis demonstrated differentiated clinical remission and endoscopic remission in patients with prior exposure to advanced therapies\nNo cases of atrioventricular block, \nbradycardia, serious or opportunistic \ninfections, or macular edema \n...\nPreliminary Open-Label Extension Data\nFurther improvement in clinical and endoscopic remission rates at OLE week 39\n...\nAt least half (NRI) of patients in 60mg treat-through group reach clinical remission or endoscopic remission at week 52\n...\n▪At least 38% (NRI) of patients in 60mg 52wk treat-through \narm were in clinical remission at both week 13 and week 52\n...\nremission", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-08", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.92}, "jev_score": 0.8464, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Interim analysis of the long-term efficacy and safety ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40053315/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The most common TEAEs were dizziness (21.8%), headache (15.3%), coronavirus infection (15.3%), somnolence (12.7%), fall (12.7%), and memory impairment (10.9%).\nSerious AEs were reported in 35 (12.7%) participants.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.9667}, "jev_score": 0.8217, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "The top-down strategy was also linked to fewer adverse \nevents (n=168 vs n=315) and serious adverse events (n=15 \nvs n=42) than the step-up approach.\n...\nIn the 12-week dosing group, \n26 participants experienced a so-called drug event, defined \nas a change in dosing interval or re-introduction during the follow-up period.\nOf these, 22 intensified their ustekinumab therapy (mostly switching to an 8-week regimen), 3 switched therapies, and 1 discontinued therapy due to a malignancy.\nOf note, comparable drug events were reported by 21 participants in the 8-week group.\n...\n“These were, however, preliminary study results, and the completed follow-up—expected in May 2025—is needed for further confirmation.”\n...\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events in the 2 mg and 3 mg arms were worsening of CD (25% and\n21.6%, respectively), headache (10.7% and 16.2%), arthralgia\n(10.7% and 13.5%), and COVID-19 (10.7% and 24.3%).\n“Severe \ninfections were seen in 3.6% and 5.4% of the participants in the \n2 and 3 mg arms,” added Prof. D’Haens.\nFinally, 2 participants\nin the 3 mg arm had a first-degree AV block, 1 participant in the 2 mg experienced bradycardia, and 1 participant in the 3 mg arm had hypertension.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAlso, participants on placebo who lost response during the long-term extension were allowed to switch to the open-label extension phase and received tamuzimod\n60 mg.\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\nEndoscopic remission was documented in 46.4%\nof participants in the 60 mg arm, 43.8% in the 30 mg arm, \n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n1. Sands BE, et al.\n...\n2. Danese S, et al.\n...\nResponders were eligible to enter the open-label extension phase, where they received tulisokibart maintenance therapy for up to\n170 weeks.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.\n“These were, however, predominantly upper respiratory infections.\nWe did not see any serious infections or infections related to systemic immunosuppression, such as herpes zoster,” clarified Dr Ma.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.81}, "jev_score": 0.7128, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.5921, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety Patterns over Time with Ozanimod During an Open-label Extension Trial in Patients with Relapsing Multiple Sclerosis (P10-6.012) | Neurology", "url": "https://www.neurology.org/doi/10.1212/WNL.0000000000204931", "domain": "neurology.org", "snippet": "The proportion of patients with RMS who experienced TEAEs in the OLE trial decreased over 6 years of ozanimod 0.92 mg treatment.", "date": "2024-04-09", "last_updated": "2025-04-04", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.51, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.1666, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "The Current Sphingosine 1 Phosphate Receptor Modulators ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12111885/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "However, its non-discriminatory effect on all S1PRs is associated with a less favourable cardiac safety profile, with bradycardia occurring in 0.6% of patients in a large real-world study [17].\n...\nIn True North, adverse events occurred in 37–49% patients in the ozanimod and placebo groups [25].\nThere was a higher rate of adverse events in the ELEVATE studies, but the rates were comparable between the etrasimod and placebo groups (71% vs. 81%, respectively) [40].\n...\nS1PR1 is expressed by cardiac tissue, and following receptor activation, an intracellular shift in potassium occurs leading to reduced excitability and transient bradycardia.\nThis occurs in a dose-dependent manner [38].\nThese effects can be mitigated with gradual dose escalation for ozanimod, but this is not required for etrasimod [25,40,44].\nBradycardia: Symptomatic bradycardia occurred in five (0.6%) patients in the treatment arm of the True North study [25].\nOf these, only one was symptomatic and resolved following drug discontinuation without the need for additional intervention.\nNo patients in the True North study developed a second or third-degree atrioventricular (AV) block.\nAcross the ELEVATE UC trials, nine occurrences of bradycardia (<1%) were observed in the treatment arms [40]; two of these were symptomatic and led to study discontinuation.\nTwo cases of first-degree AV block and one case of Mobitz type 1 AV block occurred.\nAll three cases of AV block resolved following treatment discontinuation without the need for additional intervention.\nOverall, the risk of cardiac conduction disorders with the use of S1PR modulators is low in appropriately selected patients.\n...\nS1PR modulators can disrupt this delicate balance and cause macular oedema.\nIn the trials, macular oedema occurred in three patients receiving ozanimod and two patients receiving etrasimod [25,40].\nAll bar one discontinued treatment, leading to resolution of the macular oedema (one patient receiving etrasimod continued without treatment interruption).\n...\nHerpes Zoster: In True North, the rate of herpes zoster infections was 0.4% and 2.2% in the induction and maintenance phases, respectively, with no events occurring in the placebo group [25].\nFour patients in the ELEVATE studies had herpes zoster infections (two receiving etrasimod, two receiving placebo) [40].\nNone of these adverse events led to discontinuation of the study drug.\nLymphopenia: In keeping with their mechanism of action, a reduction in the absolute lymphocyte count occurred, with a 50% reduction from baseline values by Week 2 and Week 10 in patients receiving etrasimod and ozanimod, respectively [25,40].\nThe resulting lymphopenia is reversible, with lymphocyte counts returning to baseline within 2–8 weeks of treatment cessation.\n...\nThere has only been one reported case of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) in a patient with MS who was treated with ozanimod [49].\nNo cases of PML have been reported in the UC population to date.\n...\nOne case of basal cell carcinoma was diagnosed during the induction phase of True North in a patient receiving ozanimod [25].\nDuring the maintenance period, cancer was diagnosed in four (<0.9%) patients and included two cases of basal cell carcinoma, two cases of colorectal cancer and one of breast cancer.\n...\nTamuzimod was investigated in a phase 2, multi-centre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study for patients with moderate to severely active UC [53].\nIt met its primary endpoint of clinical remission with superiority over placebo (27.9% vs. 11.4%, *p* = 0.0184).\nThere were no new safety signals identified.\nLong-term and open-label extension phases of the study are ongoing.", "date": "2025-05-15", "last_updated": "2026-02-15", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.29, "evidence": 0.3833}, "jev_score": 0.1112, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Long-term effects of tafamidis for the treatment of transthyretin familial amyloid polyneuropathy", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00415-013-7051-7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AEs were similar between groups; no patients discontinued because of an AE.\n...\nThis extension study was an open-label, multicenter, international, single-arm trial, in which all patients received oral tafamidis 20 mg once daily for 12 months.\n...\nNo new safety or tolerability issues were identified during the extension study, and the overall incidence of AEs was similar in both groups (Table 2).\nThe incidence of serious AEs (SAEs) was also similar in both groups, with five patients in the tafamidis–tafamidis group having a total of nine SAEs and four patients in the placebo–tafamidis group having a total of 14 SAEs.\nNo patient reported deterioration in renal function that required therapeutic measures.\nNo SAEs were life threatening.\nNo patients died or discontinued treatment due to an AE.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-13", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.01}, "jev_score": 0.0003, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3172.6, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q002-2", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "queries", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": "AZD5148 TcdB antibody competitors licensing", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 517.7, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 386.2, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10796, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000453, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7488, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "2026年6月17日詳解①：抗トキシンB抗体の世代交代", "url": "https://note.com/medknowledge_ai/n/nf179099775fe", "domain": "note.com", "snippet": "その構図が2つの出来事で根底から変わりました。第1に、ベズロトクスマブ（Zinplava）が2025年1月末で販売中止となり、唯一の供給元が市場から撤退して抗体の選択肢が消滅しました。第2に、次世代の抗トキシンB抗体AZD5148が、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍の中和力を示し、多様なTcdB変異株をまたぐ広域防御と、エピトープの高度な保存性を前臨床で実証し、初回エピソード予防までを射程に入れて臨床開発に入りました。「ベズロトクスマブを上乗せするか」という問いは、選択肢の消失と次世代抗体の登場により、まったく別の問いへと置き換わりつつあります。\n...\n臨床医はいま、抗体という武器を一時的に失った「空白期間」に立っています。高リスク再発予防の意思決定ツリーから抗体の分岐が消え、当面は糞便微生物移植や延長パルス型フィダキソマイシンへ重心が移ります。日本ではベズロトクスマブの販売中止が続き、規格化製剤も未承認で、空白はより深刻です。次世代抗体が来れば、対象は再発予防から初回予防へ広がりうるものの、前臨床から臨床への飛躍には不確実性が残りま\n...\n第1の出来事は、ベズロトクスマブの市場撤退です。製造販売元は2025年1月末をもって本剤の販売を中止し、唯一の供給元が市場から退いた結果、抗体は臨床現場から姿を消しました(12,13)。撤退の理由は公式には明示されておらず、有効性や安全性の否定ではなく、商\n...\n第2の出来事は、次世代抗体AZD5148の登場です(14)。これはヒト化抗トキシンBモノクローナル抗体で、毒素が宿主細胞内へ酵素活性ドメインを送り込む過程を遮断して中和します。臨床分離株で優勢な3つの主要なTcdB亜型のうち、AZD5148はTcdB1とTcdB2を、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍も低い濃度で中和しました。マウスCDIモデルでは、ベズロトクスマブよりはるかに低用量で体重減少と死亡を抑制し、抗トキシンA抗体を追加しても上乗せ効果はありませんでした。ここでもMODIFYと同じく「TcdB単独標的で十分」という結果が再現されています。さらに、試験管内では中和できなかったTcdB3産生株に対しても、マウスでは腸管浮腫と炎症細胞浸潤を有意に減らしました。\n...\nがばらつくというベズロトクスマブの弱点を、設計段階から克服しようとする試みです。AZD5148はすでにヒトでの臨床開発に入っており、再発予防を主目的とした第2b相試験が進む一方、前臨床の知見は初回エピソード予防までを将来の射程に含めています(16,17)。つまり次世代抗体は、ベズロトクスマブの「効くが亜型依存・再発予防限定」という制約を、「広域・高力価・初回予防まで」へと押し広げる構想のもとに設計されているのです。", "date": "2026-06-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-22", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.78}, "jev_score": 0.7488, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline Set - openPR.com", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4491579/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-set", "domain": "openpr.com", "snippet": "Key Clostridium Difficile Infections Companies are Acurx Pharmaceuticals, Adiso Therapeutics, Summit Therapeutics, Finch Therapeutics, Vedanta Biosciences, and others.\n...\nLeading companies such as Vedanta Biosciences, Acurx Pharmaceuticals, Summit Therapeutics, Finch Therapeutics Group, and Adiso Therapeutics are actively advancing novel drug candidates aimed at addressing recurrence, antimicrobial resistance, and poor treatment durability-key unmet needs that continue to challenge existing Clostridium Difficile Infections management strategies.\n...\n* Strategic collaborations and licensing agreements are accelerating innovation.\n...\n* In April 2026, AstraZeneca announced results of a Phase IIb, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled Study to Evaluate the Efficacy and Safety of AZD5148 for Prevention of Recurrence of Clostridioides Difficile Infection in Individuals 18 Years of Age and Above\n...\nAdditionally, bezlotoxumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting C. difficile toxin B, has been approved to prevent recurrence in high-risk patients.", "date": "2026-04-27", "last_updated": "2026-04-29", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7395, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d6acfad0-c39d-44cd-90d2-d4edff505376", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Despite these advancements—and the approval of numerous mAbs for other indications—few anti-bacterial mAbs have been approved for clinical use, which is limited to obiltoxaximab (Anthim) and raxibacumab (Abthrax) for treatment of *Bacillus anthracis* toxin exposure, and bezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) for *Clostridioides difficile* infection of the gastrointestinal tract [11,12,13].\n...\nMedarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23].\nTwo fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24].\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava^®^) was approved as a single intravenous infusion of 10 mg/kg in the USA and European Union for prevention of rCDI in individuals at high risk for CDI and receiving antibacterial treatment [23].\n...\nAstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7533}, "jev_score": 0.7307, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nAn anti-TcdB monoclonal antibody (mAb), Zinplava (bezlotoxumab), was approved in 2016 and recently withdrawn from the market by the sponsor [5].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nWe further assessed the effect of amino acid (AA) substitutions of key ribotypes (RT) on the neutralization by AZD5148 and compared that with a licensed anti-TcdB mAb.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7067}, "jev_score": 0.6643, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad Review of the Field", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13337890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Medarex was the first to report anti-toxin mAbs, which were later licensed by Merck [23].\nTwo fully human IgG1κ mAbs, anti-TcdA (CDA1/actoxumab) and TcdB (CDB1/bezlotoxumab) were selected for in vitro potency, and efficacy in a lethal intoxication hamster model [24].\n...\nAstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-18", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.6298, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The TcdB-targeting monoclonal antibody (mAb) bezlotoxumab (Zinplava) was indicated to reduce CDI recurrence in patients 18 years of age or older who are receiving antibacterial drug treatment for CDI and are at high risk for CDI recurrence.\n...\nAZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.65}, "jev_score": 0.6175, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "P-1055. Anti-Toxin B Neutralizing Monoclonal Antibody AZD5148 ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11778635/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Because toxin B is a clinically validated target for CDI, we have generated an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb) AZD5148.\nWe previously demonstrated that AZD5148 administered at 10, 1 and 0.5 mg/kg prevents severe disease development in a gnotobiotic piglet model, which recapitulates the clinical signs and gross pathology observed in human CDI, we compared the protective efficacy of AZD5148 with the clinically approved anti-toxin B mAb bezlotoxumab.\n...\nNo significant difference was observed between the c-IgG and bezlotoxumab groups.\nIn contrast, AZD5148 reduced signs of disease severity as measured by diarrhea scores, gross pathology scoring of descending and spiral colons and damage to the gut epithelium along with inflammation.\n...\nThese data suggest that AZD5148 may offer some therapeutic benefit in preventing CDI in humans.", "date": "2025-01-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-01", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.63}, "jev_score": 0.5733, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40810427/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Herein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.", "date": "2025-11-14", "last_updated": "2025-12-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5369, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "AZD5148 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/578bdd03a2f245e1babb56c500ca552e", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Drug Type\nMonoclonal antibody\n...\nTarget\ntoxB\n...\nActive Indication\nClostridium difficile infection\n...\nOriginator Organization\nAstraZeneca PLC\nActive Organization\nAstraZeneca PLC\nInactive Organization\nLicense Organization", "date": "2025-05-08", "last_updated": "2025-10-25", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.5667}, "jev_score": 0.3684, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Search for patents - Research and innovation - European Union", "url": "https://research-and-innovation.ec.europa.eu/knowledge-publications-tools-and-data/patents-and-licencing/search-patents_en", "domain": "research-and-innovation.ec.europa.eu", "snippet": "Search patents and technologies categorised by name, JRC reference number, IPR status, science area, keyword and JRC institute.", "date": "2018-08-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 903.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q001-3", "row_id": "q001", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Tamuzimod safety findings and adverse events in Moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Tamuzimod UEGW 2024 safety", "anchors": ["Tamuzimod"], "search_latency_ms": 3647.2, "target": "Tamuzimod", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 353.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9740, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000409, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9702, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39793589/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Tamuzimod (VTX002) is a selective sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 modulator in development for ulcerative colitis.\nWe aimed to assess the safety and efficacy of tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\n...\n** This double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial was conducted at 122 centres across 15 countries in Asia, Europe, and North America.\nPatients aged 18-80 years with a modified Mayo score (MMS) of 4-9 and an inadequate response or a loss of response, or intolerance to one or more approved ulcerative colitis therapies were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to once-daily oral tamuzimod (60 mg or 30 mg) or placebo for 13 weeks.\n...\nAt week 13, clinical remission was reached by 19 (28%) of 68 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 17 (24%) of 71 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and eight (11%) of 70 patients receiving placebo (risk difference 16·5% [95% CI 3·2 to 29·4], p=0·018, for tamuzimod 60 mg vs placebo and 12·5% [-0·2 to 24·9], p=0·041, for tamuzimod 30 mg vs placebo).\nTreatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 33 (47%) of 70 patients receiving tamuzimod 60 mg, 34 (47%) of 73 patients receiving tamuzimod 30 mg, and 24 (34%) of 70 patients receiving placebo.\nMost adverse events were mild or moderate.\nThe most frequently reported treatment-emergent adverse events (in ≥5% of in any treatment group) were upper respiratory tract infection (six [9%] of 70 patients in the tamuzimod 60 mg group, one [1%] of 73 in the tamuzimod 30 mg group, and one [1%] of 70 in the placebo group), anaemia (three [3%], four [5%], and six [9%]), and headache (four [6%], five [7%], and two [3%]).\nNo adverse events of atrioventricular block, bradycardia, macular oedema, severe or opportunistic infections, malignancies, or deaths occurred.\n...\n** Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nThese results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.98}, "jev_score": 0.9702, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "VTX002 UEGW 2024", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/static-files/47cbb9ba-f8c5-43e5-9235-01ca63f91f25", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data\n...\n• Tamuzimod (formerly VTX002) is a novel oral selective S1PR1 modulator in development for the \ntreatment of UC\n• The efficacy and safety of tamuzimod as induction therapy was previously demonstrated in a phase 2, \nmulticentre, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (NCT05156125)1\n...\n• Maintenance treatment with both 30 mg and 60 mg tamuzimod was efficacious and   \nwell-tolerated for up to 52 weeks\n• High rates of both clinical and endoscopic remission observed during tamuzimod \ninduction and maintenance therapy potentially a result of rapid and sustained absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) reductions\n• 58.9% (tamuzimod 30 mg) and 71.5% (tamuzimod 60 mg) ALC decrease from baseline at Week 52\n• Efficacy and safety data from this LTE treatment period support the continued clinical", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-28", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.8367}, "jev_score": 0.8283, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "tamuzimod (VTX002) / Eli Lilly", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=c9a09d69-e0fc-4b37-ab3a-5af0056bd49c", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## VTX002 Versus Placebo for the Treatment of Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=213 | Terminated | Sponsor: Oppilan Pharma Ltd | The Sponsor decided to terminate clinical conduct in the Open-Label Extension Period. The decision to terminate the study was not due to safety concerns.\n...\n## Tamuzimod in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis: a multicentre, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 2 induction trial. (PubMed, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol) - P2 | \"Induction therapy with tamuzimod was effective and well tolerated in patients with ulcerative colitis. These results and the favourable risk-benefit profile of tamuzimod collectively support the continued clinical development of tamuzimod for the treatment of moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\nOctober 15, 2024\n## Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase 2 Trial of VTX002 (Tamuzimod) in Ulcerative Colitis at UEG Week 2024 (GlobeNewswire) - \"Ventyx Biosciences, Inc...announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7667}, "jev_score": 0.7514, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/6f5a2d16ea2a572e/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6967}, "jev_score": 0.6828, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences' Tamuzimod Shows Promising Long-Term ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-from-the-phase-2-trial-of-vtx002", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n- Ventyx BiosciencesView company profile presented 52-week data from its Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease at UEG Week 2024, showcasing sustained efficacy.\n- The data reinforce tamuzimodView drug details's potential as a best-in-class S1P1R (search)Search target modulator with a favorable safety profile among oral ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease therapies.\n- High rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission suggest tamuzimodView drug details could serve as a foundational therapy in future combination treatments for ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nVentyx BiosciencesView company profile announced new 52-week data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimodView drug details (VTX002View drug details) in patients with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease.\nThe results, presented at the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria, highlight the sustained efficacy and favorable safety profile of the oral S1P1R (search)Search target modulator.\nThe long-term extension data demonstrated continued clinical and endoscopic remission rates, suggesting a durable treatment effect.\n...\nHe also noted the potential for tamuzimod to be a \"backbone of future combination therapies for UC\" due to its safety profile compared to other oral options.\n...\nThe Phase 2 trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of tamuzimodView drug details in patients with ulcerative colitis (search)Search disease over a 52-week period.\nKey highlights from the presented data include:\n- Sustained clinical remission rates\n- Continued endoscopic remission\n- A safety profile that positions tamuzimod as potentially best-in-disease among oral UC therapies", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.673, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Ventyx Biosciences Presents New 52-Week Results from the Phase ...", "url": "https://ir.ventyxbio.com/news-releases/news-release-details/ventyx-biosciences-presents-new-52-week-results-phase-2-trial/", "domain": "ir.ventyxbio.com", "snippet": "### Late-breaking abstract highlights high rates of clinical and endoscopic remission among LTE completers at Week 52\nSAN DIEGO, Oct. 15, 2024 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Ventyx Biosciences, Inc.\n(Nasdaq: VTYX) (“Ventyx”), a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company focused on advancing novel oral therapies that address a broad range of inflammatory diseases with significant unmet medical need, today announced that a late-breaking abstract including new long-term extension (LTE) data from the Phase 2 trial of tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis was presented on October 15, 2024 during the United European Gastroenterology (UEG) Week meeting in Vienna, Austria.\n“We are excited to present the new long-term extension data from the tamuzimod Phase 2 trial in patients with ulcerative colitis at UEG Week,” said Raju Mohan, PhD, Chief Executive Officer.\n“These 52-week data continue to reinforce the potential best-in-class profile of our S1P1R modulator tamuzimod in ulcerative colitis, with a potential best-in-disease safety profile amongst all the oral options for UC therapy.\nWe believe the high rates of clinical remission and endoscopic remission position tamuzimod as the backbone of future combination therapies for UC.”\n...\n**Title: **“Efficacy and safety of tamuzimod in moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis through 52 weeks: phase 2 long-term extension data”", "date": "2024-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-03-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.6633, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - CONFERENCE REPORTPEER-REVIEWED", "url": "https://conferences.medicom-publishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/E-UEGW-MCR-2024.pdf", "domain": "conferences.medicom-publishers.com", "snippet": "bowel \n...\nThe cumulative 24-week and 52-week drug survival rates were similar between the 12-week and 8-week dosing groups\n(70.2% vs 76.1%; P=0.45; see Figure).\n...\n“These were, however, preliminary study results, and the completed follow-up—expected in May 2025—is needed for further confirmation.”\n...\n“Etrasimod was well tolerated,” highlighted Prof. D’Haens.\nThe most common treatment-emergent adverse events in the 2 mg and 3 mg arms were worsening of CD (25% and\n21.6%, respectively), headache (10.7% and 16.2%), arthralgia\n(10.7% and 13.5%), and COVID-19 (10.7% and 24.3%).\n“Severe \ninfections were seen in 3.6% and 5.4% of the participants in the \n2 and 3 mg arms,” added Prof. D’Haens.\nFinally, 2 participants\nin the 3 mg arm had a first-degree AV block, 1 participant in the 2 mg experienced bradycardia, and 1 participant in the 3 mg arm had hypertension.\n...\nTamuzimod delivers promising long-term data \nin UC \nTamuzimod was associated with improved clinical, endoscopic, and histologic outcomes compared with placebo in participants with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) at week 52 of a phase 2 study.\nMoreover, the drug had a favourable safety profile.\nThe selective S1PR1 modulator tamuzimod displayed encouraging efficacy and safety data as induction therapy for patients with UC [1].\n...\nAt week 52, the clinical remission rates were 50% in both \ntamuzimod arms and 18.2% in the placebo arm (P=0.021; \nP=0.0078).\nEndoscopic remission was documented in 46.4%\nof participants in the 60 mg arm, 43.8% in the 30 mg arm, \n...\n“The drug was \nvery safe, and there were no major adverse events,” according to Prof. Danese.\n“This is probably due to the high selectivity of tamuzimod,” he argued.\n“Maintenance treatment with either 30 mg or 60 mg of\ntamuzimod was efficacious and well-tolerated for up to 52 weeks, supporting the continued development of tamuzimod as a treatment for patients with UC,” concluded\nProf. Danese.\n1. Sands BE, et al.\n...\n2. Danese S, et al.\n...\n“The severe adverse event rates were low, with 10% (n=3) \nof the participants in the 100 mg arm and 3% (n=1) of the \nparticipants in the 250 mg arm,” expressed Dr Ma.\nNo acute infusion reactions or peri-infusion reactions were reported.\nThe infection rate was 37% in the 100 mg arm and 46% in the 250 mg arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-28", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.77}, "jev_score": 0.6622, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Tamuzimod - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/6bbbf8f2c65146ceb8edfda558d17dc9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|NCT05156125 (UEGW2024) Manual|Phase 2|Ulcerative colitis, active moderate | Ulcerative colitis, active severe|85|Placebo|mftmnfbiwk(kjipcuuyyb) = rowbxvixfn glcevholsc (dcfepzicpo ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2024|\n|NCT05156125 (UEGW2024) Manual|Phase 2|Ulcerative colitis, active moderate | Ulcerative colitis, active severe|85|TamuzimodTamuzimod 30 mg|mftmnfbiwk(kjipcuuyyb) = snnzblsxso glcevholsc (dcfepzicpo ) View more|Positive|17 Oct 2024|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-25", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.58}, "jev_score": 0.5568, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "UEG Week 2024 - High5 Immunology TV", "url": "https://high5immunology.tv/ibd/ueg-week-2024", "domain": "high5immunology.tv", "snippet": "RW data of Ustekinumab and the role of biosimilars\n...\nUEG Week 2024\nTamuzimod for long term extension\n...\nTamuzimod per estensione a lungo termine\n...\nUEG Week 2024\nLong term safety of ozanimod in UC\nPeter Irving, MD", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.73, "evidence": 0.32}, "jev_score": 0.2336, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "UEGW 2024 Archives", "url": "https://www.fmcgastro.org/congres-des-breves/uegw-2024/", "domain": "fmcgastro.org", "snippet": "Claire MICHOUD, Lyon\n...\n- ## Penser à espacer l’ustekinumab de 8 à 12 semaines quand tout va bien !\n  Auteur(s) :\nMonique Devillers (Rotterdam) – LENGTHENING USTEKINUMAB DOSING INTERVALS FROM EVERY 8 WEEKS TO EVERY 12 WEEKS IN IBD PATIENTS IN REMISSION: PRELIMINARY RESULTS OF A PROSPECTIVE OBSERVATIONAL COHORT STUDY", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-05", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.0067}, "jev_score": 0.0001, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 4000.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q002-3", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "queries", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": "AZD5148 RePreve comparator anti-toxin antibody", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 1264.1, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 357.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10360, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000435, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6904, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This is supported by clinical evidence that: (1) hospital outbreaks have been caused by TcdA-negative and TcdB-positive *C. difficile* strains^7,8^; (2) higher antibody titers against TcdB (but not TcdA) correlate with a lower risk of recurrent infection^7,9^; and (3) neutralizing TcdB with bezlotoxumab reduces the risk of recurrent infections and adverse outcomes, while treatment with an anti-TcdA neutralizing mAb does not^10^.\nThis evidence makes TcdB the key therapeutic target.\n...\nHigh levels of anti-toxin neutralizing IgG antibodies correlate with decreased disease severity^9,11^; however, many people fail to develop a protective immune response after primary CDI, which is reflected in their high susceptibility to recurrence^9,12^.\n...\nAnother well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^.\n...\n- The neutralizing mAb Zinplava^™^ (bezlotoxumab) was discontinued in January 2025; however, a promising antibody candidate, AZD5148 (formerly PA41), is currently in early clinical trials (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.7267}, "jev_score": 0.6904, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "P-1055. Anti-Toxin B Neutralizing Monoclonal Antibody ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/12/Supplement_1/ofae631.1244/7988549", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Because toxin B is a clinically validated target for CDI, we have generated an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb) AZD5148.\nWe previously demonstrated that AZD5148 administered at 10, 1 and 0.5 mg/kg prevents severe disease development in a gnotobiotic piglet model, which recapitulates the clinical signs and gross pathology observed in human CDI, we compared the protective efficacy of AZD5148 with the clinically approved anti-toxin B mAb bezlotoxumab.\n...\nPiglets treated with c-IgG or bezlotoxumab exhibited signs of severe disease as measured by diarrhea color and texture.\n...\nNo significant difference was observed between the c-IgG and bezlotoxumab groups.\nIn contrast, AZD5148 reduced signs of disease severity as measured by diarrhea scores, gross pathology scoring of descending and spiral colons and damage to the gut epithelium along with inflammation.\nThese data suggest that AZD5148 may offer some therapeutic benefit in preventing CDI in humans.", "date": "2025-01-29", "last_updated": "2025-10-25", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.6433}, "jev_score": 0.5983, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1707e1c6-51fb-4727-b8d5-89de2c8be983", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\n...\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-08-29", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.5756, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nAZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab.\nIn a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab.\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), an FDA-approved mAb targeting TcdB, was effective in reducing recurrence but has recently been discontinued.\nWe previously identified AZD5148, a humanized mAb that blocks the delivery of the toxic TcdB glucosyltransferase enzyme into host cells by targeting a conserved region of the enzymatic domain.\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nPrevious studies using strain culture supernatants indicated that AZD5148 broadly neutralizes TcdB, demonstrating superior efficacy compared to bezlotoxumab, except against toxins from RT017 strains [25].\n...\nIn toxin neutralization assays conducted in Vero cells, which are highly sensitive to TcdB [27], AZD5148 neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 with EC~50~ values of 88 pM and 59 pM, respectively (Fig 1A), representing 965-fold and 14,237-fold greater potency than bezlotoxumab (Fig 1B).\nHowever, AZD5148 did not neutralize TcdB3, while bezlotoxumab retained activity with an EC~50~ of 65 pM (Fig 1C).\n...\nThese results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice.\n...\nUnlike bezlotoxumab, which binds the TcdB CROPS domain and prevents binding to the CSPG4 receptor [24,27], AZD5148 targets the TcdB GTD, blocking its translocation into host cells [25,26].\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\nBy blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.5704, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection ... - PLOS", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plospathogens/article/figures?id=10.1371/journal.ppat.1013651", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "AZD5148 potently neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB2 but not TcdB3 in vitro.\n(A-B) In vitro neutralization of 30 fM TcdB1, TcdB2, or TcdB3 by AZD5148 (A) or bezlotoxumab (B) in Vero cells.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against severe disease caused by the RT027 strain R20291.\n...\nAZD5148 provides superior protection compared to bezlotoxumab at equivalent low doses during R20291 infection.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against mortality during infection with the TcdB1-producing strain VPI 10463.\n...\nAZD5148 attenuates submucosal edema and inflammation during infection with the TcdB3-producing RT017 strain M68.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.61}, "jev_score": 0.5673, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jid/article/232/5/e741/8234296", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "*Clostridioides difficile* toxin B is a virulence factor for *C. difficile* infections and a clinically validated target for prevention of *C. difficile* infection recurrence.\nAZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nHerein, we showed that the AZD5148 epitope is highly conserved across all geographic regions and sequence types and has a higher average conservation frequency in its binding site (99.58%) when compared with bezlotoxumab (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\nOur comprehensive analysis of global sequences found the AZD5148 epitope to be highly conserved and thus unlikely to be affected by most genotypic variations in recently circulating *C. difficile* ribotypes and sequence types.\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).", "date": "2025-11-14", "last_updated": "2026-03-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5188, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "graphic", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000901832/000110465925014750/azn-20241231xex15d1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "Early science\nCompounds in early-stage clinical\ndevelopment include AZD5148, an anti-toxin\nB neutralising mAb now in Phase I trials,\nwhich may provide protection against\nClostridioides difficile (C. diff) infection,\na condition that can cause life-threatening\ndiarrhoea and intestinal inflammation.\nPreclinical data for AZD5148 were presented", "date": "2021-03-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.4826, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "AstraZeneca advances science of infectious disease ...", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/media-centre/press-releases/2024/astrazeneca-advances-science-of-infectious-disease-protection-at-idweek-2024.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Pre-clinical data will be shared showing that AZD5148, an anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody now in Phase I trials, may provide protection against *Clostridioides difficile* *(C. diff) * infection, a condition that can cause life-threatening diarrhea and intestinal inflammation.^9^\n...\n|Anti-toxin B neutralizing monoclonal antibody AZD5148 provides protection in a *clostridioides difficile* gnotobiotic piglet model|Poster PresentationSession Title: New Drug Development Date: Friday, October 18, 2024 Time: 12:15 PM - 1:30 PM PT Location: Halls J & K|", "date": "2024-10-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.5833}, "jev_score": 0.4608, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C.diff recurrence\n...\nAZD5148 C. difficile mAb - Prevention of Recurrence (US)\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\n...\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\n...\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\n...\nPhase I\n...\n•\n...\n•\n...\n•\n...\n•", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.55}, "jev_score": 0.4455, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "683. Understanding Clostridioides difficile toxinB gene conservation through surveillance of public data", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/10/Supplement_2/ofad500.745/7446541?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "CDI is mediated by the action of toxin B (TcdB) in humans.\nMonoclonal antibodies (mAbs) that neutralize TcdB function have shown protection against CDI in preclinical models and more recently in the clinic.\nSeveral regions on the TcdB gene have been targeted by therapeutic mAbs including the glucosyltransferase (GTD) and the C-terminal combined repetitive oligopeptides (CROPS) domains.\nWe have developed AZD5148, an anti-TcdB mAb targeting a region within the GTD.\nTo understand its strain coverage, we examined sequence variations of GTD including the AZD5148 epitope in contemporary *C. difficile* strains utilizing a public data repository.\n...\nWe found GTD to be highly conserved in US isolates as well as globally.\nAdditionally, among contact residues for AZD5148, any variations observed were single, and were seen for positions 323 (Y-H), 329 (G-E), 349 (V-A), and 350 (L-I).\nThese findings have important implications for drug development including for AZD5148.\nSpecifically, our findings reveal that the GTD domain of the TcdB gene has lower variance globally over the last 7 years making it a promising target for a\n*C. difficile* TcdB neutralizing mAb.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.44}, "jev_score": 0.352, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1621.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:07+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q002-1", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "queries", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": "AZD5148 competing anti-TcdB monoclonal antibodies Phase 2 2026", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 3704.2, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 509.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11501, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000483, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6944, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "2026年6月17日詳解①：抗トキシンB抗体の世代交代", "url": "https://note.com/medknowledge_ai/n/nf179099775fe", "domain": "note.com", "snippet": "その構図が2つの出来事で根底から変わりました。第1に、ベズロトクスマブ（Zinplava）が2025年1月末で販売中止となり、唯一の供給元が市場から撤退して抗体の選択肢が消滅しました。第2に、次世代の抗トキシンB抗体AZD5148が、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍の中和力を示し、多様なTcdB変異株をまたぐ広域防御と、エピトープの高度な保存性を前臨床で実証し、初回エピソード予防までを射程に入れて臨床開発に入りました。「ベズロトクスマブを上乗せするか」という問いは、選択肢の消失と次世代抗体の登場により、まったく別の問いへと置き換わりつつあります。\n...\n第2の出来事は、次世代抗体AZD5148の登場です(14)。これはヒト化抗トキシンBモノクローナル抗体で、毒素が宿主細胞内へ酵素活性ドメインを送り込む過程を遮断して中和します。臨床分離株で優勢な3つの主要なTcdB亜型のうち、AZD5148はTcdB1とTcdB2を、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍も低い濃度で中和しました。マウスCDIモデルでは、ベズロトクスマブよりはるかに低用量で体重減少と死亡を抑制し、抗トキシンA抗体を追加しても上乗せ効果はありませんでした。ここでもMODIFYと同じく「TcdB単独標的で十分」という結果が再現されています。さらに、試験管内では中和できなかったTcdB3産生株に対しても、マウスでは腸管浮腫と炎症細胞浸潤を有意に減らしました。\n...\nがばらつくというベズロトクスマブの弱点を、設計段階から克服しようとする試みです。AZD5148はすでにヒトでの臨床開発に入っており、再発予防を主目的とした第2b相試験が進む一方、前臨床の知見は初回エピソード予防までを将来の射程に含めています(16,17)。つまり次世代抗体は、ベズロトクスマブの「効くが亜型依存・再発予防限定」という制約を、「広域・高力価・初回予防まで」へと押し広げる構想のもとに設計されているのです。\n...\nこの世代交代の決着には、いくつかの条件が必要です。第1に、AZD5148のヒトでの有効性です。再発予防を主目的とした第2b相試験の結果が、前臨床の高い中和力を臨床的再発抑制へ翻訳できるかを示す最初の関門になります(16)。アクトクスマブの教訓を踏まえれば、動物モデルの輝きを臨床効果と即断はできません(14)。第2に、初回予防への適応拡大の是非です。これは有効性だけでなく、初回高リスク例を見分ける診断・予測技術の成熟と、費用対効果の評価にかかっています(22)。第3に、心不全リスクなど安全性シグナルの再評価です(4)。", "date": "2026-06-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-22", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.7233}, "jev_score": 0.6944, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad Review of the Field", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13337890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nThe two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain.\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nFurthermore, no adverse events were associated with TRL1068 treatment [243].\nFollowing this promising proof of concept, a phase II study has been initiated and is recruiting at the time of this writing [244].\n...\n- 40.AstraZeneca.\nA phase I double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the safety, tolerability and pharmacokinetics of AZD5148 in healthy adults.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Nov 2025.\n- 41.AstraZeneca.\nA phase IIb, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in individuals 18 years of age and above.\n2025. https://www.clinicaltrials.gov.\nAccessed 10 Dec 2025.\n...\n- 62.Aridis Pharmaceuticals, Inc.\n...\n- 76.Soezi M, Piri-Gavgani S, Ghanei M, et al.", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-07-18", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.582, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "A single amino acid substitution determines susceptibility of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13253237/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "2026 May 28;16:1849926.\n...\nIn this brief report, we uncover the molecular mechanism underlying a key limitation of bezlotoxumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting *Clostridioides difficile* toxin B (TcdB), specifically its reduced neutralizing activity against certain TcdB variants.\n...\nThis may also explain why AZD5148, a monoclonal antibody developed by AstraZeneca, exhibits reduced activity against TcdB3 *in vitro*, despite only a single amino acid substitution at the center of its epitope (Peritore-Galve et al., 2025).\n...\n[DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n...\n23. Peritore-Galve F.\nC., Kroh H.\nK., Shupe J.\nA., Ehni A.\nG., Cano Rodríguez R., Kordus S.\nL., et al.\n(2025).\nThe monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against TcdB-diverse Clostridioides difficile strains in mice.\nPloS Pathog.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-08-14", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.61}, "jev_score": 0.5795, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=af775e43-3e18-4b1b-b35f-7510a7360bfd", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\n...\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nFollowing this promising proof of concept, a phase II study has been initiated and is recruiting at the time of this writing [244].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.6367}, "jev_score": 0.5539, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against TcdB-diverse Clostridioides difficile strains in mice\n...\nAZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nAZD5148 potently neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB2 *in vitro*, with EC~50~ values 1,000- to 14,000-fold lower than those of bezlotoxumab.\nIn a mouse CDI model induced by TcdB1- or TcdB2-expressing strains, AZD5148 provided robust protection against weight loss and mortality at significantly lower doses than bezlotoxumab.\nFurthermore, the addition of the anti-TcdA mAb, PA50, provided no additional protective benefit.\nAlthough AZD5148 did not neutralize TcdB3 *in vitro*, it significantly reduced intestinal edema and inflammatory cell infiltration in mice infected with a TcdB3-producing strain.\nThese findings demonstrate that AZD5148 offers broad-spectrum protection against *C. difficile* strains and retains *in vivo* efficacy even in the absence of *in vitro* neutralization.\nIts distinct mechanism of action and superior potency compared to bezlotoxumab support its continued development as a promising therapeutic candidate for the prevention of a first CDI episode and prevention of recurrence.\n...\nNotably, AZD5148 was effective at lower doses than bezlotoxumab and provided protection, even against a strain that produced a TcdB variant that it could not neutralize *in vitro*.\n...\nBezlotoxumab (Zinplava), a TcdB-neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb), was FDA-approved to prevent recurrent CDI in high-risk patients, but its production was discontinued in January 2025 [16,17].\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nThese results suggest that the prophylactic administration of AZD5148 alone is effective in preventing severe CDI in mice.\n...\nThese results suggest that AZD5148 provides superior protection compared to bezlotoxumab, particularly at low doses, and significantly reduces weight loss, survival, and inflammatory responses during infection with the TcdB2-producing R20291 strain.\n...\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\n...\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated in a Phase I clinical trial for safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics in healthy adults (NCT06469151).", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.5933}, "jev_score": 0.534, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Clinical Trials Appendix - FY 2025 Results Update - AstraZeneca", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2025/Q4-FY/Full-year-Q4-2025-clinical-trial-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of C. diff recurrence\n...\n139\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\n≥ 18 years, with a qualifying C. \ndifficile infection episode at \nthe time of providing\ninformed consent\n230\n•\nRandomized, Double-blind, Placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo (1:1)\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.5633}, "jev_score": 0.4281, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Phase II\n### Phase II\n- **\n...\n** *metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis*\n#### AZD2389 - Phase II\n...\n- **Molecule size:** Small molecule\n...\n## Infectious Disease (as of 27 July 2026)\n...\n### Phase II\n...\n- **\nAZD5148\n** *reduction of C. diff recurrence*\n#### AZD5148 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb\n- **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence\n- **Molecule size:** Large molecule\n- **", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.54}, "jev_score": 0.4266, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Clostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.5133}, "jev_score": 0.4106, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline 2026: FDA Updates,", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4584063/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-2026-fda-updates", "domain": "openpr.com", "snippet": "• In April 2026, AstraZeneca announced results from a Phase IIb randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study assessing the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for preventing recurrence of Clostridioides difficile Infection in individuals aged 18 years and older.• In April 2026, Vedanta Biosciences announced results from a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III clinical trial evaluating VE303 for the prevention of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection.", "date": "2026-07-22", "last_updated": "2026-07-24", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.53}, "jev_score": 0.3922, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "H1 and Q2 2026 Results Clinical Trials Appendix", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/h1q2/H1-and-Q2-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD5148\nanti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb reduction of \nC.diff recurrence\n...\nClostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n...\n•\n...\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.4967}, "jev_score": 0.3775, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 4213.2, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q002-5", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "queries", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": "AZD5148 clinical pipeline TcdB antibody 2026", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 490.1, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 582.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11561, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000486, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7264, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "2026年6月17日詳解①：抗トキシンB抗体の世代交代", "url": "https://note.com/medknowledge_ai/n/nf179099775fe", "domain": "note.com", "snippet": "その構図が2つの出来事で根底から変わりました。第1に、ベズロトクスマブ（Zinplava）が2025年1月末で販売中止となり、唯一の供給元が市場から撤退して抗体の選択肢が消滅しました。第2に、次世代の抗トキシンB抗体AZD5148が、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍の中和力を示し、多様なTcdB変異株をまたぐ広域防御と、エピトープの高度な保存性を前臨床で実証し、初回エピソード予防までを射程に入れて臨床開発に入りました。「ベズロトクスマブを上乗せするか」という問いは、選択肢の消失と次世代抗体の登場により、まったく別の問いへと置き換わりつつあります。\n...\n第2の出来事は、次世代抗体AZD5148の登場です(14)。これはヒト化抗トキシンBモノクローナル抗体で、毒素が宿主細胞内へ酵素活性ドメインを送り込む過程を遮断して中和します。臨床分離株で優勢な3つの主要なTcdB亜型のうち、AZD5148はTcdB1とTcdB2を、ベズロトクスマブの1,000〜14,000倍も低い濃度で中和しました。マウスCDIモデルでは、ベズロトクスマブよりはるかに低用量で体重減少と死亡を抑制し、抗トキシンA抗体を追加しても上乗せ効果はありませんでした。ここでもMODIFYと同じく「TcdB単独標的で十分」という結果が再現されています。さらに、試験管内では中和できなかったTcdB3産生株に対しても、マウスでは腸管浮腫と炎症細胞浸潤を有意に減らしました。\n...\nがばらつくというベズロトクスマブの弱点を、設計段階から克服しようとする試みです。AZD5148はすでにヒトでの臨床開発に入っており、再発予防を主目的とした第2b相試験が進む一方、前臨床の知見は初回エピソード予防までを将来の射程に含めています(16,17)。つまり次世代抗体は、ベズロトクスマブの「効くが亜型依存・再発予防限定」という制約を、「広域・高力価・初回予防まで」へと押し広げる構想のもとに設計されているのです。\n対比は鮮明です。ベズロトクスマブは「唯一・限定的な中和力・再発予防・そして今や市場から消えた抗体」であり、AZD5148は「桁違いに強力・広域・初回予防まで視野に入れた・しかしまだ臨床検証の途上にある抗体」です。抗体療法は、撤退と新世代の同時進行という、めったにない移行局面を迎えています。\n...\nAZD5148への期待は正当ですが、批判的な吟味が欠かせません。最大の留保は、現時点の有効性データが前臨床（マウス・ノトバイオートブタ）にとどまる点です(14)。動物モデルでの強力な中和力が、ヒトでの再発抑制という臨床的有効性へ直結する保証はあ\n...\nこの世代交代の決着には、いくつかの条件が必要です。第1に、AZD5148のヒトでの有効性です。再発予防を主目的とした第2b相試験の結果が、前臨床の高い中和力を臨床的再発抑制へ翻訳できるかを示す最初の関門になります(16)。アクトクスマブの教訓を踏まえれば、動物モデルの輝きを臨床効果と即断はできません(14)。第2に、初回予防への適応拡大の是非です。これは有効性だけでなく、初回高リスク例を見分ける診断・予測技術の成熟と、費用対効果の評価にかかっています(22)。第3に、心不全リスクなど安全性シグナルの再評価です(4)。", "date": "2026-06-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-22", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.7567}, "jev_score": 0.7264, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\n...\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].\nAZD5148 is currently being evaluated for efficacy and safety for the prevention of recurrence of CDI in individuals 18 years of age and above in the PRISM study (NCT07285213) [40, 41].\n...\nThe binding and structural data were encourag