nts|Trial Status|\n|jRCT2071240071|P1|Not yet recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2026-01-12|\n|D8820C00002|P1|Active, not recruiting|Healthy Volunteers|2025-11-07|69%|2025-01-22|", "date": "2025-01-23", "last_updated": "2025-06-13", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.68, "evidence": 0.5733}, "jev_score": 0.3898, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "研究開発パイプライン", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.co.jp/our-science/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.co.jp", "snippet": "情報公開日2026年9月11日\n## 国内で開発中の新薬（2026年6月30日時点）\n...\n|第II相|AZD5148|C.ディフィシル感染症の再発と初発抑制|抗C.ディフィシル外毒素抗体|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.51}, "jev_score": 0.3825, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials Appendix - Q1 2026 Results Update", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/content/dam/az/PDF/2026/eq1/Q1-2026-results-clinical-trials-appendix.pdf", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "Clostridium difficile\nAZD5148 (anti-TcdB mAb)\nTrial\nPopulation\nPatients\nDesign\nEndpoints\nStatus\nPhase IIb\nPRISM\nNCT07285213\nAdults with qualifying C.\ndifficile infection episode\n230\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled\n•\nAZD5148 or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: efficacy\n•\nSecondary endpoint: safety, PK\n•\nFPCD: Q4 2025\n•\nData anticipated: >2027\nPhase I\nNCT06469151\nHealthy volunteers\n84\n•\nRandomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose escalation\n•\nCohort 1: AZD5148 (dose 1, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2a: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 2b: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.m.,  Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 3: AZD5148 (dose 2, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4a: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nCohort 4b: AZD5148 (dose 3, i.v., Chinese participants) or \nplacebo\n•\nCohort 5: AZD5148 (dose 4, i.v.) or placebo\n•\nPrimary endpoint: safety\n•\nSecondary endpoint: PK parameters\n•\nFPCD: Q2 2024\n•\nLPCD: Q4 2024\n•\nData readout: Q1 2026", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.4833}, "jev_score": 0.3721, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.astrazeneca.com/our-therapy-areas/pipeline.html", "domain": "astrazeneca.com", "snippet": "AZD4144\n** *cardiorenal disease*\n#### AZD4144 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** NLRP3\n- **Area under investigation:** cardiorenal disease\n...\nAZD5148\n** *reduction of C. diff recurrence*\n#### AZD5148 - Phase II\nClose\n- **Mechanism:** anti-clostridioides difficile TcdB mAb\n- **Area under investigation:** reduction of C. diff recurrence\n- **Molecule size:** Large molecule", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.4933}, "jev_score": 0.3404, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "*Clostridioides difficile* toxin B is a virulence factor for *C. difficile* infections and a clinically validated target for prevention of *C. difficile* infection recurrence.\nAZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against 8 recently circulating ribotypes.\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).\n...\nTaken together, this study has revealed that AZD5148 is expected to neutralize >97% of recently circulating *C. difficile* strains in 2 cell lines.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.4733}, "jev_score": 0.3124, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody (NCT07285213)", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gg/study/NCT07285213", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gg", "snippet": "RecruitingNCT07285213PRISMUpdated Aug 12, 2026\n# Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody\nA Phase 2 interventional study of AZD5148 and Placebo in Clostridioides Difficile Infection, sponsored by AstraZeneca.\nRecruiting at 116 sites in 14 countries.\nOpen to participants aged 18 Years and older.\nPer ClinicalTrials.gov, last updated 2026-08-12.\nSponsored by **AstraZeneca** · Phase 2, Interventional, and Prevention\n...\n- **Study type**\n...\n- **Enrollment**\n230\n...\nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in Individuals 18 years of age and above.\n...\nApproximately 230 participants will be enrolled and randomized 1:1 to receive a single dose of either AZD5148 or placebo (normal saline).\nRoute of administration (intramuscular or intravenous push) will be according to the Investigator's choice.\nStratification will be based on geographical region.\n...\nParticipant must be ≥ 18 years of age at the time of signing the informed consent, capable of giving signed informed consent.\nParticipants with a qualifying C. difficile infection episode at the time of providing informed consent defined by:\n- History of 3 or more unformed stools (Bristol stool scale 6 or 7) in ≤ 24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days, (Note: Diarrhea is not required to be present on the day of IMP administration) and\n- Positive local C. difficile toxin test (eg, immune assay or CCNA) on a stool sample collected during this episode, as part of routine clinical care (Note: Toxin testing, where not part of routine clinical care, may be conducted as a pre- screening activity; if this occurs, participant consent is required), and\n- Planned initiation or receipt of antibacterial drug therapy for C. difficile infection (fidaxomicin, vancomycin or metronidazole) for this episode,\n...\n## Study design\n- **Phase**\nPhase 2\n- **Primary purpose**\nPrevention\n...\nAZD5148: dose A\nParticipants will receive a single dose A of AZD5148 administered via either intramuscular injection or intravenous push.\n...\nParticipants will receive a single dose of placebo via intramuscular or intravenous push.\n...\n### Primary outcomes\n1. First occurrence of recurrence of C difficile infection\nRecurrence of C difficile infection (rCDI) occurring after initial clinical cure (ICC) of the qualifying C difficile infection (CDI).\nCDI is defined as a history of diarrhea (\\>=3 unformed stools, ie, type 6 or 7 stool on Bristol Stool Scale in \\<=24 hours for 2 consecutive calendar days) accompanied by a positive stool test for C difficile toxin.\n...\n## Key details\n- **Study ID**\nNCT07285213\n- **Lead sponsor**\nAstraZeneca\n...\n- **Start date**\nDec 10, 2025\n- **Primary completion**\nApr 20, 2027 (estimated)\n- **Completion**\nJan 18, 2028 (estimated)\n- **Last update**", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-18", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.64, "evidence": 0.4533}, "jev_score": 0.2901, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1073.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q004-1", "row_id": "q004", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "query": "MT501 mechanism of action mechanistic class", "anchors": ["MT501"], "search_latency_ms": 461.5, "target": "drugs competing with MT501: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 389.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8844, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000371, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.1566, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/477311c534e0406d8c27a73fc39e6074", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Drug Type Small molecule drug|\n...\n|Colitis, Ulcerative|Phase 2|United States|Mirador Therapeutics, Inc.|26 Jun 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.54, "evidence": 0.29}, "jev_score": 0.1566, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | MT-501 Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/33772", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "Mechanisms of Action: Unknown\nNovel Mechanism: No\nModality: Small Molecule", "date": "2025-06-19", "last_updated": "2025-07-05", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.41, "evidence": 0.14}, "jev_score": 0.0574, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "큐라클·맵틱스, MT-501 국가과제 선정", "url": "https://www.newspim.com/news/view/20260922000397", "domain": "newspim.com", "snippet": "분석 중...- MT-501은 Tie2 활성화와 C5 axis 억제로 혈관염 치료를 목표로 했다.\n...\n[서울=뉴스핌] 양태훈 기자 = 큐라클은 항체 신약개발 기업 맵틱스와 공동 개발 중인 혈관염 치료용 이중항체 'MT-501'이 중소벤처기업부 국가 연구개발 과제에 선정됐다고 22일 밝혔다.\n이번 과제는 혈관의 안정성과 항상성 유지에 관여하는 Tie2 신호를 활성화하는 동시에 염증 반응에 관여하는 보체계 C5 axis를 억제하는 MT-501을 개발하는 것이 목표다.\n양사는 이를 통해 혈관염을 비롯한 난치성 염증성 혈관질환에 대한 치료 가능성을 검증할 계획이다.\n혈관염은 과도한 면역·염증 반응과 함께 혈관 내피 손상과 혈관 기능 이상이 복합적으로 나타나는 질환이다.\n양사는 염증 반응을 조절하는 동시에 손상된 혈관을 안정화하고 기능 회복을 유도하는 방식을 치료 전략으로 연구하고 있다.\n...\nMT-501은 큐라클과 맵틱스가 공동 개발해 온 Tie2 기반 항체 파이프라인의 적용 범위를 염증성 혈관질환으로 확대한 후보물질이다.\n양사는 Tie2를 중심으로 신장·망막질환에서 혈전·허혈성 질환으로 연구 영역을 넓혀 왔으며, MT-501에는 C5 axis 억제 기전을 추가했다.\n...\n맵틱스 관계자는 \"Tie2 활성화를 통한 혈관 안정화와 C5 axis 억제를 통한 염증 조절을 동시에 추구하는 MT-501의 치료 가능성을 검증할 것\"이라며 \"EAGLES와 AI 기술을 접목해 자체 항체 개발 플랫폼도 고도화할 계획\"이라고 말했다.", "date": "2026-09-22", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.35, "evidence": 0.1267}, "jev_score": 0.0443, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Shimano disc brake MT501/MT520 | buy online - Fitstore24.com", "url": "https://www.fitstore24.com/en/shimano-disc-set-hr-blmt501-brmt5201", "domain": "fitstore24.com", "snippet": "The Shimano disc set HR BLMT501/BRMT520 impresses with SERVO WAVE performance for highest MTB demands.\nThe hydraulic disc brake with its robust 4-piston design ensures riding fun and a safe ride.\n...\nThe Servo Wave brake lever absorbs a lot of brake travel at the beginning of its movement so that the pads can be guided to the brake disc with little lever travel.\nAfter the brake pad has been applied, the power transmission factor increases rapidly.\nThe greater part of the lever travel is thus still available to increase the braking force and to dose it more precisely.\n...\n|Brake type|Hydraulic disc brake|\n...\n|Servo Wave Mechanism|✔|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-30", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "SHIMANO Hamulec hydrauliczny MT501 BL-MT501/BR-MT500 tylny ...", "url": "https://www.veloportal.pl/p-225535/shimano-hamulec-hydrauliczny-mt501-bl-mt501br-mt500-tylny-czarny-post-mount-przewod-1700-mm-okladziny-b05s", "domain": "veloportal.pl", "snippet": "Hamulec tarczowy MT501.\nSkłada się z dźwigni hamulca BLMT501 i zacisku BRMT500 bez adaptera, połączonych przewodem BH59-SS.\n...\nTyp: MTB, Całkowita waga opakowania: 549 g, Tylne, czarne, Odpowietrzanie jednokierunkowe, Długość przewodu: 1700 mm, Mocowanie: Na zacisku, Mocowanie na słupku, Materiał: Aluminium/stal\n...\n|Kategorie:|MTB|\n|--|--|\n|Umiejscowienie:|Tylne|\n|Montaż hamulca:|Post Mount|\n|Technologia hamulców:|Hydrauliczne|\n|Przeznaczenie hamulca:|MTB|\nSHIMANO Hamulec hydrauliczny MT501 BL-MT501/BR-MT500 tylny czarny Post Mount przewód 1700 mm + okładziny B05S", "date": "2026-04-01", "last_updated": "2026-04-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Maneta de freno de disco hidráulico MTB Shimano MT501", "url": "https://www.cycletyres.es/products/levier-de-frein-shimano-mt501", "domain": "cycletyres.es", "snippet": "## Maneta de freno de disco hidráulico Shimano MT501\nLa\n**maneta de freno Shimano MT501** para **frenos de disco hidráulicos** ha sido diseñada para mntarse en bicicletas urbanas, e-bikes, así como bicicletas de montaña, como pieza nueva o como repuesto en caso de rotura.\nEsta maneta de freno Shimano MT501 corresponde a un\n**sistema de frenos hidráulicos de MTB,** y forma parte de la nueva gama CUES de Shimano.", "date": "2024-01-29", "last_updated": "2025-01-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "FREIO HIDRAULICO BL-MT501/BR-MT500 - rodociclo.com.br", "url": "https://www.rodociclo.com.br/componentes/freios/freio-hidraulico-bl-mt501br-mt500", "domain": "rodociclo.com.br", "snippet": "Combinando a tecnologia da manete **BL-MT501** (que herda o mecanismo de elite *Servo Wave*) com a confiabilidade da pinça **BR-MT500**, este sistema oferece frenagens brutas com menos esforço físico, garantindo controle milimétrico e total segurança nas descidas mais técnicas.\n## 🚀 Principais Diferenciais e Tecnologias\n- **Mecanismo Servo Wave Action**: Tecnologia que acelera o curso inicial das pastilhas até o disco com um toque mínimo no manete.\nIsso garante uma resposta imediata e deixa mais curso disponível para você dosar e modular a força exata da frenagem.\n...\n|**Tipo de Freio**|Hidráulico a Disco (2 Pistões opostos)|\n...\nEmbora não leve o logotipo \"Deore\" estampado, ele utiliza o mesmo corpo e o mecanismo Servo Wave dos freios Deore de linha alta, entregando rigorosamente a mesma potência de frenagem por um valor muito mais atrativo.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-20", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Tested: Shimano BL-MT501/BR-MT520 - BikeMag", "url": "https://www.bikemag.com/mountain-bike-gear/tested-shimano-bl-mt501-br-mt520-145-per-brake", "domain": "bikemag.com", "snippet": "The BL-MT501/BR-MT520 brakes are sold as a full brake with the BR MT520 caliper and the BL MT501 lever.\nAfter some research, it was confirmed by Shimano that the MT501 lever is identical to the M6000 lever, better known as “Deore,” which we’ve seen helping bring the price down on plenty of high-end bikes.", "date": "2019-12-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Shimano MT501 + MT520", "url": "https://www.mantel.com/lu/shimano-mt501-mt520-disc-brake", "domain": "mantel.com", "snippet": "|Number of pistons in caliper|4 Pistons|\n...\nThe Shimano MT501 + MT520 MTB disc brakes are powerful brakes with great quality in this price segment.\n...\nWith a strong 4-piston design, the Shimano MT501 + MT520 hydraulic disc brakes promote the cyclist's confidence and enjoyment.\n...\nThis means that the pistons move towards the rotors faster at the beginning of the braking movement than when the pads are closer to the rotors.", "date": "2021-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-09-15", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigating the Anticancer Promise of Synthesized Methylated N-Substituted-4-Hydroxy-2-Quinolone-3-Carboxamides: Biological and Computational Characterization", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42219823/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Clipboard, Search History, and several other advanced features are temporarily unavailable.", "date": "2026-06-13", "last_updated": "2026-06-13", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 851.1, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q003-2", "row_id": "q003", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB competitors Phase 2", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "search_latency_ms": 1430.6, "target": "drugs competing with RpoB: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 431.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9799, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000412, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.3135, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative colitis - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6487890/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "There are at least 27 new drugs for ulcerative colitis with either recently completed or active trials.^170^ One example is the oral pan-janus kinase inhibitor tofacitinib, which has shown higher rates of clinical remission than placebo in phase 2 studies.\n^171^ Etrolizumab, a subcutaneous monoclonal antibody that blocks the β7 subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α4β7 and αEβ7 achieved higher clinical remission rates than placebo in a phase 2 trial.^172^ An oral anti-α4 integrin therapy (AJM300) significantly increased clinical remission and endoscopic healing in a phase 2 trial.", "date": "2016-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.57, "evidence": 0.55}, "jev_score": 0.3135, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline: 75+ Therapies & Key Companies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/insights/ulcerative-colitis-clinical-trial-analysis", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "**Key Takeaways from the Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline Report**\n- ***On March 05, 2026- Sanofi * ** conducted a Phase 2, multinational, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose ranging study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of SAR441566 in adults with moderate-to-severe UC.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess efficacy of different doses of SAR441566 on clinical remission in participants with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis.\n...\nObefazimod is an oral small-molecule drug candidate in clinical development for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and has demonstrated anti-inflammatory activity in preclinical studies and in both Phase IIa and Phase IIb clinical trials.\n...\nABBV-668 is under development for the treatment of crohn’s disease, unspecified immunological disorders and ulcerative colitis.\nThe drug candidate acts by targeting receptor interacting serine/threonine protein kinase 1 (RIPK1).\nIt is administered through oral route.\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n- **TEV-48574: Teva Pharmaceutical**\n...\nAnti-TL1A (TEV-’574) is currently in Phase 2b clinical trials for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UD) and Crohn’s disease (CD), two types of inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n- **SOR102: Sorriso Pharmaceuticals**\n...\n- Mid Stage Products (Phase II)", "date": "2026-03-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.45, "evidence": 0.49}, "jev_score": 0.2205, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Aminosalicylates [26,28,30]|5-ASA|UC|Distal colitis: rectal 5-ASA ≥ 1 g/d Extensive colitis: rectal 5-ASA ≥ 1 g/d + oral 5-ASA ≥ 2 g/d|≥1 g/d >2 g/d|- >4 g/d|\n...\nMirikizumab is a p19-driven antibody against interleukin-23.\nIts efficacy and safety in the treatment of UC were evaluated in the LUCENT-1 (induction) and LUCENT-2 (maintenance) studies.\nClinical remission after 12 and 52 weeks using Mirikizumab was 24.2% and 49.9% against 13.3% and 25.1 in the placebo group, respectively [53].\n...\nFilgotinib is a preferred inhibitor of JAK 1 (blocking potency over JAK 1 five times greater than the others) and has been evaluated in the treatment of UC through the SELECTION trials.\nUnlike most IBD therapies, there are no differences in induction or maintenance doses.", "date": "2023-09-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.43, "evidence": 0.4567}, "jev_score": 0.1964, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Reviewing treatments and outcomes in the evolving landscape of ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00325481.2017.1319730", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "The first-line therapy for patients with active, mild-to-moderate forms of distal UC is rectal 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) therapy [Citation22,Citation35].\n...\nRectal corticosteroids are also recommended for patients with mild-to-moderate distal UC [Citation22], but as second-line therapy in patients refractory to rectal 5-ASAs [Citation35].\nOral and rectal corticosteroids have demonstrated efficacy for the induction of remission of mild-to-moderate UC [Citation47] but are not recommended for long-term maintenance of remission of UC [Citation48].\n...\nSecond-generation corticosteroids (budesonide, beclomethasone dipropionate) have high first-pass hepatic metabolism and are associated with fewer corticosteroid-related adverse effects than conventional corticosteroids [Citation50–Citation57].\nBudesonide foam, a formulation approved in 2014 for the induction of remission of active, mild-to-moderate distal UC, has a maximal spread to 40 cm (mean, 25.4 cm) proximal from the rectum to the descending colon [Citation58,Citation59].\n...\nCurrent guidelines make no specific recommendations for or against the use of budesonide foam for the induction of remission of distal UC, but current opinion suggests that budesonide foam should be considered as second-line therapy for patients with UP refractory to treatment with rectal 5-ASA [Citation65].\n...\nThe three TNF-α antagonists currently approved by the US Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of patients with moderate-to-severe UC are infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab [Citation84].", "date": "2017-07-04", "last_updated": "2026-01-07", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.4, "evidence": 0.41}, "jev_score": 0.164, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/7/jjag066/8726617", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "In treating patients with mildly-to-moderately active distal UC, both per rectal 5-ASA and per rectal corticosteroids, particularly second-generation corticosteroids such as beclomethasone dipropionate and budesonide, have demonstrated efficacy in inducing clinical and endoscopic remission.\n...\nThe use of topical 5-ASA as maintenance therapy in adult patients with distal UC or proctitis was significantly superior in maintaining clinical remission compared with placebo [RR: 2.22; 95% CI: 1.26–3.90].\n...\nTo date, the main therapeutic third-line options may include one of the three following sequences: cyclosporine followed by infliximab, infliximab followed by cyclosporine, or JAKi [upadacitinib or tofacitinib] following failure of either previous agent.", "date": "2026-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.37, "evidence": 0.42}, "jev_score": 0.1554, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2025", "url": "https://www.researchandmarkets.com/reports/4989296/ulcerative-colitis-pipeline-insight-2025", "domain": "researchandmarkets.com", "snippet": "This “Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2025” report provides comprehensive insights about 70+ companies and 75+ pipeline drugs in Ulcerative Colitis pipeline landscape.\n...\nRectal application of medical therapy, via suppository or enema, is usually appropriate for isolated distal disease (proctitis); however, a rectal application is usually used in combination with systemic therapy to help target the distal colon and, therefore, decrease tenesmus.\n...\nObefazimod is an oral small-molecule drug candidate in clinical development for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis (UC) and has demonstrated anti-inflammatory activity in preclinical studies and in both Phase IIa and Phase IIb clinical trials.\n...\nABBV-668 is under development for the treatment of crohn's disease, unspecified immunological disorders and ulcerative colitis.\nThe drug candidate acts by targeting receptor interacting serine/threonine protein kinase 1 (RIPK1).\nIt is administered through oral route.\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nAnti-TL1A (TEV-’574) is currently in Phase 2b clinical trials for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UD) and Crohn's disease (CD), two types of inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nThere are approx. 70+ key companies which are developing the therapies for Ulcerative Colitis.\nThe companies which have their Ulcerative Colitis drug candidates in the most advanced stage, i.e. Phase III include Abivax.\n...\n## The report covers around 75+ products under different phases of clinical development like\n- Late stage products (Phase III)\n- Mid-stage products (Phase II)\n- Early-stage product (Phase I) along with the details of\n- Pre-clinical and Discovery stage candidates\n- Discontinued & Inactive candidates\n...\n## Key Players\n- Oppilan Pharma\n- Genentech\n- Teva Branded Pharmaceutical\n- Boehringer Ingelheim\n- Sorriso Pharmaceuticals\n- Reistone Biopharma\n- Celgene\n- AnaptysBio\n- Rise Therapeutics\n- Merck\n- AltruBio\n- AbbVie\n- Immunic AG\n- Kissei Pharmaceutical Co\n- Lmito Therapeutics\n- Morphic Therapeutic\n- Oncostellae\n- Palatin Technologies\n...\n- VTX002\n- Vixarelimab\n- TEV-48574\n- Spesolimab\n- SOR102\n- SHR0302\n- RPC1063\n- Rosnilimab\n- R-3750\n- MK-7240\n- ALTB-268\n- ABBV-668\n- IMU-838\n- KSP-0243\n- LMT503\n- MORF-057\n- OST-122\n- PL8177", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-17", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.34, "evidence": 0.44}, "jev_score": 0.1496, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/16/1/2/6390052", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "We recommend topical [rectal] 5-ASA at a dose of ≥1 g/d for the induction of remission in active distal colitis [strong recommendation, low-quality evidence]\n...\nWe recommend treatment with anti-tumour necrosis factor [TNF] agents [infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab] to induce remission in patients with moderate-to-severe UC who have inadequate response or intolerance to conventional therapy [strong recommendation, moderate-quality evidence]\n...\nWe recommend anti-TNF agents [infliximab, adalimumab, or golimumab] for the maintenance of remission in patients with UC who responded to induction therapy with the same drug [strong recommendation, high-quality evidence]\n...\nWe suggest the use of vedolizumab rather than adalimumab for the induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately-to-severely active ulcerative colitis [weak recommendation, low level of evidence]\n...\nWe recommend tofacitinib for maintaining remission in patients with UC who responded to induction therapy with tofacitinib [strong recommendation, moderate quality of evidence]", "date": "2022-01-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.36, "evidence": 0.4033}, "jev_score": 0.1452, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "What are the key players in the ulcerative colitis treatment ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/article/what-are-the-key-players-in-the-ulcerative-colitis-treatment-market", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Small molecule inhibitors, such as tofacitinib and upadacitinib, have further expanded the therapeutic horizon by acting on Janus kinases (JAKs) to modulate inflammatory signaling cascades.\n...\nstands out as a leader largely because of its blockbuster anti‐TNF agent and other biologics that are heavily utilized in inflammatory bowel disease management.\n...\nTheir portfolio includes agents used in IBD treatment, and they have built a reputation for scientific research in this area.\n• Pfizer Inc. is well known for both its conventional and advanced therapeutic agents.\nWith products such as tofacitinib, Pfizer has been influential in pioneering the use of oral JAK inhibitors, which have provided an additional treatment option for patients who have failed biologic therapies or cannot tolerate them.\n...\n• Teva Pharmaceutical Industries Ltd.\nremains a notable competitor with a wide range of generic and innovative treatment options, including biosimilars that have an impact on pricing and market share for UC therapies.\n• Additionally, companies such as Merck & Co. and Shire (now part of Takeda following its acquisition) have contributed to the richer pipeline of UC treatments, often focusing on both existing biologics and emerging novel compounds.\n...\nOther companies highlighted in later reports include Johnson & Johnson’s emphasis on advanced biologics, Bausch Health Companies Inc. with its product line for rectal therapies (e.g., UCERIS® budesonide rectal foam, which is becoming significant in the treatment of mild to moderate distal UC).\nEmerging players such as Janssen Biotech Inc.\n(notably with its FDA approval of STELARA [ustekinumab] for UC) are also key contributors, marking advances in targeting interleukin pathways to achieve better management outcomes.\nIn addition, pipeline-focused companies like Palatin Technologies are developing next-generation small-molecule candidates such as PL8177 for oral colon delivery and effective targeting of the melanocortin-1 receptor, opening up new therapeutic modalities for UC patients.\n...\n• Aminosalicylates continue to form the bedrock of treatment for mild-to-moderate disease.", "date": "2025-03-11", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.35, "evidence": 0.3833}, "jev_score": 0.1342, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis (UC) Pipeline Insight | Drugs, Therapies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/report-store/ulcerative-colitis-uc-pipeline-insight", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "DelveInsight’s, “Ulcerative Colitis - Pipeline Insight, 2026” report provides comprehensive insights about 115+ companies and 120+ pipeline drugs in Ulcerative Colitis pipeline landscape.\n...\nThis segment of the Ulcerative Colitis report encloses its detailed analysis of various drugs in different stages of clinical development, including phase II, I, preclinical and Discovery.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- ALTB-268: AltruBio\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- OD-001: Odyssey Therapeutics\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- PALI-2108: Palisade Bio\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase II stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n- R-3750: Rise Therapeutics\nR-3750, a novel oral immunotherapy, is a synthetic biology-based medicine optimized to selectively target dendritic cells, R-3750 is a first-in-class therapy with a unique mechanism of action with the potential to treat the underlying immunological basis of gastrointestinal inflammatory diseases.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is being evaluated in the Phase I stage of its development for the treatment of Ulcerative Colitis.\n...\nThe report provides insights into different therapeutic candidates in phase II, I, preclinical and discovery stage.", "date": "2025-10-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.28, "evidence": 0.4233}, "jev_score": 0.1185, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "The IBD Therapeutic Pipeline is Primed to Produce", "url": "https://practicalgastro.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/IBD-Therapeutic-Pipeline-Is-Primed-To-Produce.pdf", "domain": "practicalgastro.com", "snippet": "Two of the most promising IL23 inhibitors currently in the pipeline, are MEDI2070 (brazikumab) (Allergan) and risankizumab (AbbVie).\n...\nSeveral other IL23 specific inhibitors are rapidly entering clinical trials for CD and UC, including mirikizumab (LY3074828) (Eli Lilly), tildrakizumab (Sun Pharma), and guselkumab\n(Janssen).9 Of the three, mirikizumab is farthest \nalong in development with two phase II clinical trials recruiting, one for active CD (SERENITY) and another for moderate to severe UC (NCT02891226,\nNCT02589665 respectively).\n...\nUC.21 One such therapy is LT-02 (Nestlé Health \nScience), which demonstrated promising early results in clinical trials, including a phase II trial for UC patients refractory to mesalazine.22 This was a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled\n...\n(UC) based on phase II and phase III trials.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.21, "evidence": 0.2967}, "jev_score": 0.0623, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1862.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:08+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q003-1", "row_id": "q003", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB inhibitors pipeline", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "search_latency_ms": 2559.2, "target": "drugs competing with RpoB: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 384.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9640, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000405, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.1946, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - Gastroenterology - Merck Manuals", "url": "https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "merckmanuals.com", "snippet": "Suppositories are effective for more distal disease and are usually preferred by patients.\n...\nPatients with inflammation proximal to the sigmoid colon or left-sided disease unresponsive to topical agents should receive a biologic agent (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, guselkumab, risankizumab, mirikizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab) with or without an immunomodulator medication (azathioprine, methotrexate, 6-mercaptopurine) (1, 4, 5).\n...\nFinally, in some patients, a trial of the small-molecule Janus kinase inhibitors (tofacitinib or upadacitinib) or the sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulator ozanimod or etrasimod can be considered.\n...\n- Treat mild disease with 5-ASA by rectum and, for proximal disease, by mouth.\n- Treat extensive or severe disease with high-dose glucocorticoids, immunomodulator therapy (eg, azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine), biologics (eg, adalimumab, infliximab, vedolizumab), tofacitinib, upadacitinib, or ozanimod.", "date": "2026-03-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.42, "evidence": 0.4633}, "jev_score": 0.1946, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Oncostellae (OST-122)|Oral gut-restricted JAK3/TYK2/ARK5 inhibitor|NCT04353791|Phase 1b/2a|Completed Dec 2022; development not pursued further|\n|JAK Inhibitor|Ritlecitinib (PF-06651600)|Oral selective JAK3/TEC family kinase inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; prioritized for alopecia areata|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647)|Selective TYK2 inhibitor|NCT04209556|Phase 2b|Withdrawn with zero enrollment due to portfolio re-prioritization|\n...\n|α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027|\n...\n|IL-23R antagonist|Icotrokinra|First-in-class targeted oral peptide blocking IL-23 receptor|NCT06049017 (ANTHEM-UC)|Phase 2b|Met primary endpoint; 63.5% response (highest dose) vs 27% placebo|\n...\n|TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029|\n...\n|RIPK1 inhibitor|Eclitasertib (SAR443122)|Highly potent selective oral RIPK1 kinase inhibitor|NCT05588843 (RESOLUTE)|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\n|MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|Tilpisertib (GS-4875)|Oral MAP3K8/TPL2 inhibitor|NCT04130919, NCT06029972 (PALEKONA)|Phase 2|First study terminated Feb 2021; PALEKONA ongoing, estimated completion 2027|\n...\n|Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination.\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n|Combination|Infliximab + Ustekinumab|TNF antagonist + IL-12/23 p40 antibody|NCT06453317|Phase 2|Started Feb 2025; estimated completion June 2028|\n...\nBrepocitinib (PF-06700841) is an oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor evaluated alongside ritlecitinib in the same VIBRATO Phase 2b trial (NCT02958865) for moderate-to-severe UC with identical inclusion criteria of patients aged 18–75 years having total MS ≥6, RBS ≥1, and MES ≥2 (38).\n...\nRopsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to assess its safety and efficacy in participants with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nTralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nAn interim analysis in July 2025 showed lutikizumab achieved numerically higher endoscopic improvement rates compared to adalimumab but failed to demonstrate sufficient differentiation for statistical significance, leading the pharmaceutical company to discontinue monotherapy development in UC.\n...\nRosnilimab is a novel PD-1 agonist IgG1 monoclonal antibody designed to reduce T cell proliferation and inflammatory cytokine secretion by selectively depleting PD-1high expressing T follicular helper, T peripheral helper, and T effector cells while sparing PD-1low cells.\n...\nThe trial is currently in the recruitment Phase with first patient enrollment expected in Q3–2025 and an estimated study completion date of October 2028, therefore no efficacy or safety results are yet available for this novel dual-pathway inhibitor that aims to overcome the therapeutic ceiling observed with single-target approaches in inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.38, "evidence": 0.4633}, "jev_score": 0.1761, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Mild to Moderate Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4609145/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Biologic therapy for patients with UC includes the anti-TNF agents infliximab, adalimumab, and golimumab and the integrin inhibitor vedolizumab (Figure 4).\n...\n^31^ A second integrin inhibitor, vedolizumab, was developed that is directed against the integrin α~4~β~7~.\n...\nMoreover, vedolizumab was beneficial regardless of the patient’s treatment history, whether that included prior anti-TNF failure, prior anti-TNF loss of response, or no previous anti-TNF therapy.^32^ Vedolizumab, which is administered via intravenous infusion, received FDA approval in 2014 for patients with previously treated moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nA therapy that was designed to meet these needs is budesonide foam, which recently received FDA approval for the induction of remission in patients with mild to moderate distal UC extending up to 40 cm from the anal verge.^1^ This area includes the rectum, which spans the first 10 cm to 15 cm, as well as the sigmoid colon, which spans the next 25 cm to 30 cm.\n...\nSeveral investigational integrin inhibitors are being evaluated, including etrolizumab, which targets the integrin β7, and AMG 181, a gut-specific anti-α~4~β~7~ antibody.\nEtrolizumab demonstrated clinical efficacy in a randomized, controlled, phase 2 trial in patients with moderate to severe UC not responding to conventional therapy.\n...\nSeveral Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are being evaluated in the treatment of UC.\nThe pan-JAK inhibitor tofacitinib, which has been shown to affect multiple cytokines, demonstrated efficacy as induction therapy in patients with moderate to severe UC.^10^ Tofacitinib is currently being evaluated in phase 3 trials for induction and maintenance of remission in patients with moderately to severely active UC.^11^^,^^12^ Potential safety concerns with tofacitinib include significant immunosuppression, which is associated with a small increased risk of infection.^10^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.41, "evidence": 0.4033}, "jev_score": 0.1654, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\nTumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A pathway inhibitors demonstrate particularly robust efficacy in early trials, with clinical remission rates exceeding 25% compared to less than 2% for placebo.\n...\n|PSGL-1 agonist|Leiolizumab|Tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1|NCT06109441|Phase 2a|Started Dec 2023; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\n|TL1A antagonist|Tulisokibart (MK-7240)|Humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting TL1A|NCT04996797 (ARTEMIS-UC), NCT06052059, NCT06651281|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed; Phase 3 ongoing, estimated completion Dec 2029|\n...\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n...\nRopsacitinib (PF-06826647) is a selective TYK2 inhibitor that was evaluated in, a Phase 2B double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT with an open-label extension (NCT04209556) designed to assess its safety and efficacy in participants with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nThe same MORF-057 molecule is being evaluated in a double-blind, Phase 2b placebo-controlled RCT (EMERALD-2, NCT05611671) with 282 participants meeting similar inclusion criteria but specifically requiring inadequate response to aminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, or advanced UC therapies, measuring clinical remission using mMS at 12 weeks as primary endpoint.\n...\nTralokinumab (CAT-354) is a recombinant human monoclonal IgG4 antibody directed against IL-13 that was tested in a Phase IIa randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-arm, multicenter study (NCT01482884) evaluating its efficacy and safety as add-on therapy in patients with active, moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nRosnilimab is a novel PD-1 agonist IgG1 monoclonal antibody designed to reduce T cell proliferation and inflammatory cytokine secretion by selectively depleting PD-1high expressing T follicular helper, T peripheral helper, and T effector cells while sparing PD-1low cells.\n...\nCU104 is a new oral investigational drug that specifically inhibits IL1β, IL-6, and TNFα (69).\nIt is being studied in a double-blind, placebo-controlled, multi-center Phase 2 RCT (NCT05907330) designed to evaluate its efficacy and safety in patients with moderate to severe UC.\n...\nRO7837195 is a p40xTL1A bispecific antibody developed through a Roche-Pfizer collaboration that simultaneously targets IL-12/IL-23 (via the p40 subunit) and TL1A pathways, currently being evaluated in the Phase IIb SUNCREST trial (NCT06979336) which employs a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with a 12-week induction Phase followed by a 40-week active treatment extension in approximately 224 participants across five dosing arms.\n...\nA study examines tirzepatide, a dual GLP-1/GIP receptor agonist administered subcutaneously, combined with mirikizumab, an IL-23 p19 antibody, in adults with moderately to severely active UC who are also obese or overweight (NCT06937086).\nIt is a Phase 3b, randomized, multicenter, controlled study that began in June 2025 with an estimated completion date of April 2028.\n...\nSeveral mechanistic classes demonstrate particular promise.\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.\nNext-generation selective JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitors aim to preserve efficacy while mitigating safety concerns through enhanced selectivity.\nGut-restricted therapies including obefazimod, MORF-057, and OST-122 achieve therapeutically relevant tissue concentrations with minimal systemic exposure, potentially optimizing the benefit-risk ratio.\nCombination therapy strategies may prove essential for breaking the therapeutic ceiling, with dual biologic approaches demonstrating 83% clinical response versus 61-75% for monotherapy in controlled trials.\nAdditional promising mechanisms targeting immune checkpoints, RIPK1-mediated necroptosis, and tissue repair pathways offer complementary approaches for patients failing conventional treatments.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.38, "evidence": 0.4167}, "jev_score": 0.1583, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The IBD Therapeutic Pipeline Is Primed to Produce", "url": "https://practicalgastro.com/2019/04/10/the-ibd-therapeutic-pipeline-is-primed-to-produce/", "domain": "practicalgastro.com", "snippet": "Several other IL23 specific inhibitors are rapidly entering clinical trials for CD and UC, including mirikizumab (LY3074828) (Eli Lilly), tildrakizumab (Sun Pharma), and guselkumab (Janssen).^9^ Of the three, mirikizumab is farthest along in development with two phase II clinical trials recruiting, one for active CD (SERENITY) and another for moderate to severe UC (NCT02891226, NCT02589665 respectively).\n...\nTofacitinib (Xeljanz) (Pfizer) is a pan-JAK inhibitor with a preference for JAK1 and JAK3 that has demonstrated efficacy in the treatment of ulcerative colitis.^33^ As a result, Tofacitinib was recently approved in the United States and Europe for moderate to severe active ulcerative colitis (UC) based on phase II and phase III trials.\n...\nUpadacitinib (ABT-494) (AbbVie) is a more potent JAK1-selective inhibitor that is currently being investigated for use in Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and psoriatic arthritis.^42^ CELEST (NCT02365649) was a phase II, randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled study investigating upadacitinib for patients with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2019-04-10", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.35, "evidence": 0.4467}, "jev_score": 0.1563, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/7/jjag066/8726617", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Among the most promising alternatives to infliximab and calcineurin inhibitors in ASUC are JAKi^195^.\n...\nTo date, the main therapeutic third-line options may include one of the three following sequences: cyclosporine followed by infliximab, infliximab followed by cyclosporine, or JAKi [upadacitinib or tofacitinib] following failure of either previous agent.", "date": "2026-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.33, "evidence": 0.3667}, "jev_score": 0.121, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Exploring the Pipeline of Novel Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10045261/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "More clinical trials that study the efficacy of brazikumab for the treatment of CD and UC are in progress (Table 1).\n...\nPhase II and III trials of guselkumab, GALAXI 1, GALAXI 2, and GALAXI 3 are being conducted on patients with CD and UC [28].\n...\nAnother orally administered TNF inhibitor is OPRX-106.\nIt is a fused form of human anti-TNF receptor II and an IgG1 Fc domain expressed in plant cells, which function as a vehicle for delivery.\nA phase II randomized, two-arm, open-label clinical study included 25 patients with mild-to-moderate UC.\n...\nA phase III clinical trial and two OLE clinical trials are evaluating etrolizumab as a potential therapy for IBD [29].\nThe phase III clinical program, which has enrolled over 3000 patients, is studying etrolizumab as both an induction and maintenance therapy and includes the trials HIBISCUS I, HIBISCUS II, and GARDENIA [35,36,37].\n...\nUpadacitinib received FDA approval in March 2022 for its use in the treatment of UC [73].\nIt is an oral and highly selective JAK1 inhibitor.\n...\nPeficitinib is an orally administered pan-JAK inhibitor with moderate selectivity for JAK3.\nThis drug was approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe rheumatoid arthritis in Japan in 2019, but trials involving its use in IBD management are still underway [77].\n...\nTD-1473, also known as izencitinib, is a gut-specific, oral, pan-JAK inhibitor [79].\n...\nBrepocitinib (PF-06700841), a TYK2 and JAK1 inhibitor, has been clinically promising and well tolerated in the phase I and IIa trials for the treatment of psoriasis [84], and a phase III trial is currently in progress.\nCurrently, phase II trials are recruiting participants to study brepocitinib as a treatment for CD [85].\nVIBRATO, an umbrella study evaluating the efficacy and safety of brepocitinib and ritlecitinib, which are JAK3 and TEC inhibitors, indicated that both drugs are effective in patients with moderate-to-severe active UC compared to a placebo, as they met all the trial’s endpoints.", "date": "2023-03-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.32, "evidence": 0.35}, "jev_score": 0.112, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel Drug Developments for IBD - Highlights from ECCO 2025", "url": "https://www.charite-research.org/files/documents/250630_CRO%20WP_ECCO%202025_final.pdf", "domain": "charite-research.org", "snippet": "RO7790121 (RVT-3101, Hoffmann-La Roche) is an anti-TL1A-antibody that showed significant endoscopic improvement and favourable safety in the phase 2a TUSCANY trial [Danese et al.,\n2021].\n...\nDue to these positive phase 2b results, RO7790121 is currently being investigated in two phase \n3 studies, namely the AMETRINE-1 (NCT06589986) and AMETRINE-2 (NCT06588855) trials.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.15, "evidence": 0.3133}, "jev_score": 0.047, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline", "url": "https://www.merck.com/research/product-pipeline/", "domain": "merck.com", "snippet": "##### Ulcerative colitis\n(NCT07463183) Therapeutic Area: Immunology", "date": "2026-08-18", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.17, "evidence": 0.1433}, "jev_score": 0.0244, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Exploring the Pipeline of Novel Therapies for Inflammatory ...", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9059/11/3/747", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "https://errors.edgesuite.net/18.683c217.1742858964.400e5f04", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-24", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.12, "evidence": 0.03}, "jev_score": 0.0036, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 2943.2, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q002-4", "row_id": "q002", "mode": "queries", "objective": "C. difficile Infection Episode: Receipt of SOC Antibacterial Therapy (Fidaxomicin, Vancomycin, Metronidazole) With Planned Duration 10–25 Days at Time of IMP Administration competing agents in the same mechanistic class as AZD5148, including Monoclonal Antibody targeting Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB", "query": "AZD5148 TcdB epitope binding mechanism", "anchors": ["AZD5148"], "search_latency_ms": 3539.7, "target": "drugs competing with AZD5148: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 401.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10886, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000457, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5731, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune aspects of Clostridioides difficile infection and vaccine ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12767691/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Endosome acidification triggers structural changes in the toxins, resulting in pore formation and cytosolic delivery of an enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB consist of four functional domains: the N-terminal glucosyltransferase domain (GTD), an autoprotease domain (APD), the delivery domain (DD) with roles in receptor binding, pore formation, and GTD translocation, and the CROPS (Figure 2A)^5^.\n...\nIn binding this site, bezlotoxumab blocks the TcdB conformation that can interact with chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4), blocking cellular entry via this receptor (Figure 2B,C)^88^.\nAnother well-characterized neutralizing mAb, PA41 (now called AZD5148), binds to a single site on the GTD (Figure 2D), which allows TcdB uptake, but prevents the formation of the toxin pore and GTD translocation into the cytosol^89^.\nIncreased available *tcdB* sequences have revealed sequence variability across TcdB subtypes^90^.\nThis is concerning because there is substantial amino acid variation in the bezlotoxumab binding sites, and one key amino acid change in the AZD5148 binding site between TcdB subtypes^88–90^.\nSuch variations reduce the binding and neutralizing capabilities of mAbs *in vitro* ^89,90^, emphasizing the need to identify conserved neutralizing epitopes across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.5731, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "A single amino acid substitution determines susceptibility of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13253237/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Bezlotoxumab neutralized TcdB1 and TcdB3 mediated cell death in human colonic epithelial cells, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4.\nSequence analysis revealed that TcdB1 and TcdB3 share a conserved residue within a key antibody-neutralizing region, containing glutamic acid at position 2033 (E2033), whereas TcdB2 and TcdB4 contain an alanine at this position (A2033).\nSubstitution of A2033 with E2033 in TcdB2 restored susceptibility to antibody-mediated neutralization.\n...\nAntibodies recognize epitopes on proteins that consist of specific amino acid sequences or residues.\nStudies have shown that bezlotoxumab has limited activity against the TcdB2 variant (Chen et al., 2021), raising the possibility that naturally occurring sequence variation in TcdB may limit antibody efficacy.\n...\nImmunoblot analysis demonstrated that bezlotoxumab was able to recognize TcdB1 and TcdB3, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4 (**Figures 2A, B**).\nConsistent with these interaction findings, bezlotoxumab inhibited TcdB1 and TcdB3-mediated cell death in human colonic epithelial cells, but not TcdB2 and TcdB4 induced cell death (**Figure 2C**).\nTogether, these findings suggest that sequence variation in TcdB determines susceptibility to antibody neutralization.\n...\nGlutamate at position 2033 (E2033) of the TcdB protein has been identified in computational studies (Hernandez et al., 2015; Karnchanapandh et al., 2024), as well as our computational analysis (**Supplementary Figure 1**), as a potentially important residue in mediating interaction at a key bezlotoxumab epitope site.\nThese analyses predict a favorable electrostatic interaction between TcdB residue E2033 and bezlotoxumab residue R59 (**Supplementary Figure 1**).\nTcdB1 and TcdB3 possess E2033, whereas TcdB2 and TcdB4 contain alanine at this position (A2033) (**Figures 3A, B**).\nThis supports the hypothesis that E2033 is critical for bezlotoxumab neutralization.\n...\nUsing immunoblot analysis, caspase activation, and cell death assays, we found that substitution of A2033 with E2033 in the TcdB2 variant restored susceptibility to antibody neutralization (**Figures 3C, D**).\nThese findings demonstrate that a single amino acid substitution at position 2033 is sufficient to restore susceptibility to bezlotoxumab neutralization.\n...\nOur results demonstrate that the A2033E substitution in TcdB2 restores susceptibility to bezlotoxumab and suggests that variation at this residue may contribute to neutralization among TcdB variants.\nWe found that TcdB sequence variation influences bezlotoxumab efficacy: the antibody neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB3, which contain glutamate at position 2033, but not TcdB2 or TcdB4, and introducing E2033 into TcdB2 restores susceptibility, highlighting that a single amino acid can determine therapeutic effectiveness.\n...\nOur work provides mechanistic insight into how a single amino acid variation can drive antibody escape.\nThis may also explain why AZD5148, a monoclonal antibody developed by AstraZeneca, exhibits reduced activity against TcdB3 *in vitro*, despite only a single amino acid substitution at the center of its epitope (Peritore-Galve et al., 2025).", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-08-14", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6167}, "jev_score": 0.5674, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection against ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12594360/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a humanized anti-TcdB mAb that neutralizes toxin activity by blocking the delivery of the enzymatic glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into host cells.\n...\nWe previously identified AZD5148, a humanized mAb that blocks the delivery of the toxic TcdB glucosyltransferase enzyme into host cells by targeting a conserved region of the enzymatic domain.\nThis contrasts with bezlotoxumab, which binds to the TcdB combined repetitive oligopeptide sequence (CROPS) domain and functions by interfering with receptor binding.\n...\nThese toxins bind to host cell receptors and are internalized via endocytosis.\nEndosomal acidification triggers toxin conformational changes that facilitate pore formation and translocation of the glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) into the host cytosol.\nThe GTD then irreversibly inactivates Rho- or Ras-family GTPases, disrupting the cytoskeleton, inducing proinflammatory cytokine production, and causing cell death [7].\n...\nBezlotoxumab blocks TcdB binding to the chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4) receptor, but is less effective against different variants due to differences in receptor binding [23,24].\n...\nWe previously described PA41 (now called AZD5148), a humanized mAb that binds a highly conserved TcdB GTD epitope and prevents the delivery of the GTD enzymatic cargo into the host cytoplasm [25,26].\n...\nThese results demonstrate that the amino acid variation in the epitopes targeted by AZD5148 and bezlotoxumab plays a critical role in their ability to neutralize diverse TcdB subtypes.\n...\nUnlike bezlotoxumab, which binds the TcdB CROPS domain and prevents binding to the CSPG4 receptor [24,27], AZD5148 targets the TcdB GTD, blocking its translocation into host cells [25,26].\nThis mechanism of action downstream of surface receptor engagement could offer broader coverage across TcdB variants that differ in their receptor and substrate specificity.\n...\nThe effect of these differences is that, unlike most TcdB GTDs, which glucosylate Rho-family GTPases, the TcdB3 GTD is specific for Ras family GTPases [37,38].\n...\nThe second feature is that, despite the significant sequence differences that impact the GTD substrate specificity, the epitope where AZD5148 binds is highly conserved [21].\nThe crystal structure of the TcdB GTD:AZD5148 Fab complex revealed only one amino acid contact residue that differed between TcdB3 and other subtype sequences; TcdB1 and TcdB2 have a tyrosine at the center of the AZD5148 epitope that is replaced with a histidine in TcdB3 [21,26].\nIn conclusion, our data show that AZD5148 provides robust protection in a mouse model of CDI caused by diverse *C. difficile* strains.\nBy blocking the delivery of toxic cargo into the cell, AZD5148 efficacy is independent of the receptor tropism differences observed across TcdB subtypes.\nFurther, the AZD5148 epitope is, for the most part, strictly conserved across TcdB subtypes.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2025-11-12", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6}, "jev_score": 0.552, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Monoclonal Antibodies Targeting Bacterial Infections: A Broad ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40259-026-00787-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=2487fb5c-a174-4489-a3aa-8931585c2cf7", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "AstraZeneca is developing another anti-TcdB mAb, having licensed two anti-toxin mAbs from Progenics (both IgG1κ) [31].\nThe anti-TcdB mAb, AZD5148, was engineered with a YTE substitution, a triple-mutation M252Y/S254T/T256E in the Fc region that extends the mAb’s half-life in human sera [32, 33].\nAZD5148 (formerly PA41) binds the GTD of TcdB and prevents the delivery of TcdB enzymatic cargo into the host cell cytosol [34].\nTcdB binds at least three classes of receptors including CSPG4, Nectin-3, and PVRL3 [35,36,37].\nThe two most recently discovered receptors are known to bind TcdB outside of the CROPs domain.\nTherefore, it is possible that TcdB could escape neutralization by Zinplava^®^ [14].\nIn contrast, the strategy employed by AZD5148, focused on binding the GTD, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and may offer broader neutralizing activity.\nA recent study on 9134 global isolates showed that the AZD5148 epitope was highly conserved (99.58%) when compared to Zinplava^®^ (82.47%).\nAZD5148 also exhibited broad neutralization in vitro against eight recent circulating TcdB ribotypes [33].\nIn addition, AZD5148 conferred protection in a CDI gnotobiotic piglet model, and in a mouse CDI model against strains with different TcdB ribotypes [38, 39].", "date": "2026-06-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-04", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.4787, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "683. Understanding Clostridioides difficile toxinB gene conservation through surveillance of public data", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ofid/article/10/Supplement_2/ofad500.745/7446541?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "CDI is mediated by the action of toxin B (TcdB) in humans.\nMonoclonal antibodies (mAbs) that neutralize TcdB function have shown protection against CDI in preclinical models and more recently in the clinic.\nSeveral regions on the TcdB gene have been targeted by therapeutic mAbs including the glucosyltransferase (GTD) and the C-terminal combined repetitive oligopeptides (CROPS) domains.\nWe have developed AZD5148, an anti-TcdB mAb targeting a region within the GTD.\nTo understand its strain coverage, we examined sequence variations of GTD including the AZD5148 epitope in contemporary *C. difficile* strains utilizing a public data repository.\n...\nWe found GTD to be highly conserved in US isolates as well as globally.\nAdditionally, among contact residues for AZD5148, any variations observed were single, and were seen for positions 323 (Y-H), 329 (G-E), 349 (V-A), and 350 (L-I).\nThese findings have important implications for drug development including for AZD5148.\nSpecifically, our findings reveal that the GTD domain of the TcdB gene has lower variance globally over the last 7 years making it a promising target for a\n*C. difficile* TcdB neutralizing mAb.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.45}, "jev_score": 0.3645, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Epitope Conservation of AZD5148, a Broadly Neutralizing Anti-Toxin B Monoclonal Antibody, Among Diverse and Global Contemporary Clostridioides difficile Isolates", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12614991/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "AZD5148 is a toxin B–neutralizing human monoclonal antibody that binds to an epitope on the toxin B glucosyltransferase domain.\n...\nTcdB is a potent virulence factor and a clinically validated target [5–9], which initiates a multistep process starting with the combined repetitive oligopeptide sequences (CROPs) domain binding to a specific receptor, endocytosis, pore formation in acidified endosomes involving the delivery domain, and translocation of the glucosyltransferase domain (GTD) and autoprotease domain into the cytosol [10].\nIt was recently uncovered that the CROPs domain is often only conformationally required and its direct binding to a cell receptor is not necessary for intoxication or cell entry [11–13].\n...\nWe recently identified AZD5148: a TcdB–neutralizing human mAb that binds to an epitope on the GTD between residues 290 to 360 and prevents GTD export from the endosomes to the cytosol [16].\n...\nAZD5148 targets the GTD region of TcdB (Figure 1*A*).\n...\nThe binding epitope of AZD5148 was defined by an x-ray–determined crystal complex, which can be visualized within the TcdB structure in Figure 1*A* (PDB ID 5VQM).\nThe epitope has an average conservation frequency of 96.57%, and the binding residues have an average conservation frequency of 99.73%.\n...\nContrastingly, the strategy of targeting the GTD, as employed by AZD5148, is independent of the receptor expressed on the target cell surface and showed potent neutralization, except for RT-017, in vitro against both cell lines in our study.\nThe mechanism of blocking toxin translocation from within the endosome by targeting the GTD has proven to be an effective strategy in neutralizing TcdB activity.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-08-09", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.51}, "jev_score": 0.3621, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "The monoclonal antibody AZD5148 confers broad protection ... - PLOS", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plospathogens/article/figures?id=10.1371/journal.ppat.1013651", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "AZD5148 potently neutralizes TcdB1 and TcdB2 but not TcdB3 in vitro.\n...\nAZD5148 protects against mortality during infection with the TcdB1-producing strain VPI 10463.\n...\nAZD5148 attenuates submucosal edema and inflammation during infection with the TcdB3-producing RT017 strain M68.", "date": "2025-11-03", "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.5}, "jev_score": 0.35, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Nanobodies against C. difficile TcdA and TcdB reveal unexpected neutralizing epitopes and provide a toolkit for toxin quantitation in vivo", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10621975/", "domain": "ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In contrast, neutralizing epitopes for TcdB were found in multiple domains.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB intoxicate cells via a multistep process: receptor binding and endocytosis, pH-dependent pore formation, translocation of the GTD and APD across the endosomal membrane, autoprocessing and GTD release, and finally, GTD-mediated glucosylation of host GTPases.\n...\nTcdA and TcdB can bind to multiple receptors, but not all receptor binding sites have been defined [13].\nHistorically, receptor binding has been associated with the C-terminal CROPS domain.\n...\nTcdB has been reported to bind four protein receptor classes: chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4); Frizzled (FZD) 1, FZD2, and FZD7; Nectin3; and TFP1 [17–20].\nThe interactions can vary with TcdB subtype, but arguably all bind in a CROPS-independent interaction.\n(The interaction with CSPG4 occurs at the ‘hinge’ where the CROPS moves relative to the other toxin domains and involves some of the N-terminal residues of the CROPS [21].)\n...\nWhile the mechanism for this neutralization is currently unknown, it could suggest a role for CROPS-dependent glycan binding or as-yet unidentified protein receptors for TcdB.\nThe DD, however, was the target of all potent TcdA binding clones as well as multiple potent TcdB binding clones.", "date": "2023-10-07", "last_updated": "2024-10-07", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.58, "evidence": 0.49}, "jev_score": 0.2842, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Receptor binding mechanisms of Clostridioides difficile toxin B and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9344982/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "More specifically, TcdB exploits the Wnt receptor Frizzled (FZD) members 1, 2, and 7 and chondroitin sulfate proteoglycan 4 (CSPG4) as high-affinity receptors for binding and entering host cells [18, 21-24].\n...\nThe FZD-binding site is located in the middle of the DRBD of TcdB.\nBut besides the traditional protein-protein interactions, TcdB exploits an endogenous fatty acid as a co-receptor, which is completely buried between FZD2 and TcdB through extensive hydrophobic interactions with both proteins, to enhance binding affinity and specificity toward FZD1, 2, and 7 (Figure 1A) [25].\nThis lipid is bound in a hydrophobic groove on the CRD, which is also the docking site for a palmitoleic acid (PAM) lipid modification of Wnt [27].\nTcdB binding would lock this endogenous lipid in the PAM-binding pocket on the CRD, which is therefore unavailable to engage the PAM of Wnt.\nUsing this strategy, TcdB not only gains access to host cells via FZDs, but also inhibits Wnt signaling that may contribute to pathogenesis of CDI.\n...\nNevertheless, our structure reveals that only the first CSPG repeat of CSPG4 (termed Repeat1, residues 410–551) is mainly responsible for TcdB binding, which was also confirmed by a crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) study [18].\nIn spite of its small size, the Repeat1 of CSPG4 binds at a strategic location on TcdB where multiple TcdB domains converge, and it direct interacts with TcdB amino acids spreading across the CPD, DRBD, CROPs, and a hinge region connecting the DRBD and CROPs.\n...\nOn the context of TcdB holotoxin, the binding sites for FZDs and CSPG4 are completely separated and ~78 Å away from each other, which would permit simultaneous binding of both if they are expressed on the same cell surface (Figure 1C).\nHowever, either FZDs or CSPG4 is sufficient to mediate cell entry, as our structure-based mutagenesis studies demonstrate that FZDs and CSPG4 act as independent receptors for TcdB, which is consistent with results from Gerhard and colleagues [13].\n...\nBased on the structure of the TcdB–CRD2 complex, we found that a single mutation at F1597 of TcdB1, a key residue that simultaneously binds CRD2 and the lipid co-receptor, is sufficient to abolish FZD binding [25], which was later confirmed by other groups [12, 13, 31].\n...\nIn summary, our structure and sequence analyses suggest that ~71% of TcdB variants could bind to both FZD and CSPG4; ~17% and ~7% could only use CSPG4 or FZD as their receptor, respectively; while the receptor-binding strategies for the rest ~5% of TcdB variants remain unknown (Figure 2E).", "date": "2021-12-13", "last_updated": "2025-09-10", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.22, "evidence": 0.32}, "jev_score": 0.0704, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Receptor binding mechanisms of Clostridioides difficile toxin B and implications for therapeutics development", "url": "https://febs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/am-pdf/10.1111/febs.16310", "domain": "febs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "The FZD-binding site is located in the middle of \n95\nthe DRBD of TcdB.\n...\ngroove on the CRD, which is also the docking site for a palmitoleic acid (PAM) lipid \n...\nthis strategy, TcdB not only gains access to host cells via FZDs, but also inhibits Wnt ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-04-13", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.21, "evidence": 0.31}, "jev_score": 0.0651, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3940.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q004-3", "row_id": "q004", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "query": "MT501 inflammatory bowel disease competitors same mechanism", "anchors": ["MT501"], "search_latency_ms": 461.3, "target": "drugs competing with MT501: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 366.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10540, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000443, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6319, "results": [{"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "A Gastroenterologist's guide to drug interactions of small molecules for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ueg2.12559", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Currently approved small molecule drugs exert their effects by either of two main mechanisms– Janus kinase inhibition or Sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulation.\n...\nTofacitinib is an inhibitor of the JAK family and is approved for use in moderately to severely active UC.^21^ Tofacitinib is mainly metabolized by CYP3A4 and is also a PGP substrate.\n...\nUpadacitinib is a selective inhibitor of the JAK family, targeting primarily JAK1 and JAK2, and is approved for use in moderately to severely active UC and moderately to severely active Crohn's disease.^23^ Upadacitinib is a non-sensitive CYP3A4 substrate.^33^ As a result, it is modestly influenced by strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as grapefruit juice or Voriconazole, which increase the AUC of Upadacitinib by 75%.\n...\nFilgotinib is an adenosine triphosphate (ATP) competitive and reversible inhibitor of the JAK family^25^ that is approved for the treatment of moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nSphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulator drugs bind to the S1P receptor on lymphocytes and are then internalized by the cell.\nThis interferes with lymphocyte mobilization and exit from draining lymph nodes in the tissue.", "date": "2024-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.6319, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/inflammatory-bowel-diseases-global-competitive-landscape-report-2026/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Moreover, inflammatory Bowel Diseases (IBD) — covering ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) — anchor 1,837 active drug programs, 7,198 patent filings since January 2023, and 1,244 ongoing Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials.\nNotably, 2025-2026 has produced an exceptional wave of approvals including Janssen’s oral peptide IL-23 antagonist Icotrokinra (March 2026), Lilly’s Mirikizumab (Omvoh) for Crohn’s expansion, and multiple Chinese-developed JAK and IL-23 mAb approvals.\nSpecifically, the late-stage IBD landscape is bifurcated between injectable biologics — AbbVie’s Skyrizi (Risankizumab) and Rinvoq (Upadacitinib), Janssen’s Stelara (Ustekinumab) and Tremfya (Guselkumab), Takeda’s Entyvio (Vedolizumab), Pfizer’s Etrasimod (Velsipity), and BMS’s Zeposia (Ozanimod) — and the emerging oral biologic and JAK1-selective wave.\nMoreover, sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) modulators have established a third oral mechanism alongside JAK and TYK2 inhibitors.\nHowever, the strategic outlook is increasingly defined by oral peptide IL-23 antagonists and selective JAK1 inhibitors rather than next-generation TNF or anti-integrin biologics.\n...\nFurthermore, microbiome therapeutics, mesenchymal stem cell therapy, and IL-23 / TL1A combination biology extend the long-term modality horizon.\n...\nAcross the 200 sampled IBD programs, small molecules dominate with 114 entries (oral JAK, TYK2, S1P modulators), followed by 31 monoclonal antibodies, 6 herbal medicines, 4 mesenchymal stem cell therapies, 3 synthetic peptides, 3 cyclic peptides, 3 bispecific antibodies, 3 probiotics, and 3 polymers.\n...\n|AbbVie|US|Tier 1 — Leader|Risankizumab (Skyrizi) + Upadacitinib (Rinvoq)|Both approved across UC and CD|\n|Janssen (J&J)|US/Belgium|Tier 1 — Leader|Ustekinumab + Guselkumab + Icotrokinra|Tremfya UC/CD approvals; Icotrokinra approval 2026-03|\n|Takeda|Japan|Tier 1 — Leader|Vedolizumab (Entyvio)|Anti-α4β7 mAb; gut-selective leader|\n|Eli Lilly|US|Tier 1 — Leader|Mirikizumab (Omvoh)|UC approval 2023; Crohn’s expansion 2025|", "date": "2026-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.6433}, "jev_score": 0.5532, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "academic.oup.com · ecco-jcc · articleInflammatory bowel disease therapies and demyelinating ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/20/1/jjaf215/8361737", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "^18^ They have emerged as therapeutic targets across a range of IMIDs due to their role in regulating immune cell trafficking, particularly by controlling the egress of lymphocytes from lymph nodes into the circulation.\n...\nOzanimod, which selectively targets S1P1 and S1P5, was designed to maintain immunomodulatory efficacy while reducing cardiovascular risks.\n...\nAnti-leukocyte trafficking agents act by blocking integrins involved in lymphocyte adhesion and migration across endothelial barriers, thereby limiting immune cell infiltration into target tissues.^29^ This mechanism underlies their use in IBD and demyelinating disorders, where aberrant leukocyte trafficking contributes to chronic inflammation.\n...\nNatalizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets the α4 subunit of integrins, blocking both α4β1–VCAM-1 and α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interactions.\nBy inhibiting these adhesion pathways, it prevents lymphocyte trafficking across the endothelium into the central nervous system (CNS) and gastrointestinal tract.\nIn contrast, vedolizumab selectively inhibits the α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interaction, restricting its activity to the intestinal mucosa, while carotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral, reversible α4-integrin antagonist that transiently blocks VCAM-1 and MAdCAM-1 binding, producing a functionally gut-selective effect with minimal systemic immune suppression.\n...\nVedolizumab, with its gut-selective mechanism and favorable safety profile, may be used in combination with approved MS therapies for patients requiring treatment of both IBD and demyelinating disease, with the exception of natalizumab due to overlapping mechanisms of action.\n...\nThey exert their immunomodulatory effects primarily by inhibiting purine synthesis and lymphocyte proliferation.", "date": "2026-01-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.527, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Competitor Landscape", "url": "https://marketintelo.com/report/inflammatory-bowel-disease-drugs-market", "domain": "marketintelo.com", "snippet": "The regulatory approval of next-generation biologics targeting novel inflammatory pathways - including JAK inhibitors, integrin antagonists, and S1P receptor modulators - has substantially broadened the therapeutic arsenal.\n...\nJAK-STAT inhibitors including tofacitinib and upadacitinib demonstrate oral bioavailability combined with targeted intracellular signaling inhibition, achieving clinical remission rates of 48-56% in moderate-to-severe disease compared to 22-28% with conventional therapies.\nIntegrin antagonists such as vedolizumab provide selective gut-homing lymphocyte targeting, substantially reducing systemic immunosuppression and opportunistic infection risk.\nSphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators represent an emerging frontier, with ozanimod demonstrating 41% remission rates while preserving systemic immunity.\nFurthermore, dual-mechanism agents combining TNF inhibition with IL-23 pathway targeting are entering late-stage development, with interim data suggesting remission rates exceeding 58-62% in treatment-naive cohorts.\n...\nPatients presenting with pure colonic ulcerative colitis frequently receive integrin antagonists or JAK inhibitors as first-line biologic therapy, leveraging gut-selective mechanisms minimizing systemic immunosuppression.", "date": "2025-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.4986, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory bowel disease treatment: Mechanisms ...", "url": "https://www.spandidos-publications.com/10.3892/ijmm.2026.5961", "domain": "spandidos-publications.com", "snippet": "The mechanism of action primarily involves inhibiting natural killer cell activity, suppressing the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways of arachidonic acid metabolism, thereby decreasing prostaglandin and leukotriene levels, inhibiting th\n...\nMethotrexate (MTX) is a dihydrofolate reductase\ninhibitor that exerts therapeutic effects by inhibiting cell\n...\nMonoclonal antibodies targeting TNF-α (infliximab\nand adalimumab) are used in IBD management (82), decreasing inflammation, promoting mucosal healing and decreasing the need for surgery (83).\nTNF-α serves a key role in intestinal inflammation during IBD, promoting the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, increasing intestinal permeability, activating immune cells and \nfacilitating inflammatory cell migration (84).\nMechanistically, anti-TNF-α neutralizes soluble and transmembrane TNF-α, decreases NF-κB activation (85) and diminishes downstream cytokine cascades (IL-1β, IL-6 and IL-12/23) (86).\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody\ntargeting α4β7 integrin, exerting selective immunosuppression by\ninhibiting lymphocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa (93).\nα4β7 integrin serves a key role in the trafficking of gut-specific lymphocytes.\nIt binds the intestinal mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1), thereby mediating lymphocyte migration into intestinal tissue (94).\n...\nUstekinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the\np40 subunit common to IL-12 and IL-23, indicated for the treatment\nof moderate-to-severe CD and UC (100).\n...\nUstekinumab inhibits IL-12- and IL-23-mediated\nsignaling and cytokine cascades by blocking the binding of IL-12\nand IL-23 to the IL-12Rβ1 receptor protein on target cell surfaces\n(102).\n...\nJAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib and\nfilgotinib) block JAK-STAT signaling downstream of multiple\ncytokine receptors (including IL-2, IL-4, IL-6, IL-7 and IL-12/23),\nproducing bro\nad immunomodulation (121).\n...\nozelamod) bind S1P receptors on lymphocyte surfaces, functionally\ninhibiting lymphocyte migration out of lymphoid tissue.", "date": "2026-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.5467}, "jev_score": 0.4592, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "www.frontiersin.org › journals › pharmacologyFrontiers | Biologic and small-molecule therapies for ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2026.1948258/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Three selective interleukin (IL)-23p19 antagonists have been approved for both UC and CD; two sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor modulators and a second-generation Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitor have entered routine practice; subcutaneous formulations have altered delivery; and the first formal head-to-head trials, together with placebo-controlled trials incorporating an active reference arm, have begun to replace indirect comparison with direct evidence.\n...\nThree further strategies have generated late-phase data: anti-TL1A antibodies, which target a pathway not previously addressed pharmacologically in IBD; the miR-124 enhancer obefazimod; and the oral IL-23 receptor antagonist peptide icotrokinra.", "date": "2026-07-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.6333}, "jev_score": 0.437, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "The Expanding Therapeutic Armamentarium for Inflammatory Bowel Disease: How to Choose the Right Drug[s] for Our Patients?", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/1/105/4093864?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "For many years, the treatment of patients with moderate-to-severely active ulcerative colitis [UC] and Crohn’s disease [CD] has been dominated by the tumour necrosis factor [TNF] antagonists.\nHowever, two new classes of biologic drugs, the integrin-antagonists for both UC and CD, and the interleukin [IL]-12/23-antagonist ustekinumab for CD, are now available.\n...\nRecruitment of white blood cells from the circulation to the gut results from complex interactions between integrins on leukocyte cell surfaces and other adhesion molecules on intestinal microvascular endothelial cells.\n4 Anti-adhesion molecules antagonise this interaction by targeting either a shared epitope of the α4β7 integrin heterodimer [vedolizumab, Takeda; AMG 181, Amgen] or either of its individual subunits: α4 in the case of natalizumab [Biogen] and AJM300 [Ajinomoto Co.\nInc.]; and β7 for etrolizumab [Genentech].\nNatalizumab and AJM300 also target α4β1, the ligand of vascular cell adhesion molecule [VCAM]-1, whereas etrolizumab also targets αEβ7, preventing the latter integrin from binding E-cadherin, which is expressed on epithelial cells.\nAnother approach to interfere with leukocyte trafficking to the gut is to block mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule [MAdCAM]-1, the ligand of α4β7 that is specifically expressed on the gut endothelium [PF-00547659; Pfizer] [Figure 2].\n4\n...\nInterleukin [IL]-12 and -23 are expressed by inflammatory myeloid cells and share a common p40 subunit.\nThese cytokines play a central role in T cell-mediated responses in inflammation by influencing the development of T\n...\n16,17 Ustekinumab [Janssen] and briakinumab [Abbvie] are directed against the shared p40 subunit of IL-12 and -23.\n...\n18 MEDI2070 [Amgen], tildrakisumab [Sun Pharma], and risankisumab [Boehringer Ingelheim/Eli Lilly, recently licensed by AbbVie] target the p19 subunit which is specific for IL-23 [Figure 2].\n...\nTofacitinib [Pfizer] is an orally administered selective inhibitor of JAKs, inhibiting mainly JAK1 and JAK3, and to a lesser extent JAK2 and TYK2.\n32,33 Filgotinib [Galapagos], JNJ-54781532 [Johnson & Johnson, NCT01959282], and ABT-494 [Abbvie] are oral JAK inhibitors with improved selectivity for JAK1 [Figure 2].\n...\n42 The latter process is governed by interactions between S1P1 receptors on lymphocytes and their ligand S1P which is differentially expressed on the cell surface of lymphatic endothelial cells.\nUpon stimulation, the cell surface-associated S1P receptors become phosphorylated and are internalised and degraded, leading to sequestration of B and T lymphocytes in the peripheral lymphoid organs and reduced trafficking of these cells to inflamed tissue [Figure 2].\n...\nThe cytokine transforming growth factor [TGF]-β1 exhibits potent anti-inflammatory properties by inhibiting T cell proliferation and differentiation, macrophage activation, and dendritic cell maturation.\nThese anti-inflammatory properties are downregulated by mothers against decapentaplegic homologue 7 [SMAD7], a protein that is consistently elevated in CD patients with active inflammation.\n49 The oral antisense oligonucleotide mongersen [Celgene] binds to SMAD *7* messenger RNA and specifically causes its degradation by cellular ribonucleases.\nThe subsequent restoration of TGF-β signalling decreases the inflammatory cascade by restoring endogenous anti-inflammatory processes [Figure 2].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.5167}, "jev_score": 0.4134, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9752289/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Among the most promising molecules are anti‐IL‐23, JAK‐i\n...\nMolecules at a more preliminary stage of development (phase 1 and 2), one of which may 1 day offer an optimal benefit‐risk ratio, will also be presented as well as their respective mechanisms of action.\n...\nThe mechanisms targeted by these new therapeutic options include anti‐IL, new JAK inhibitors, therapies interfering with T‐cell trafficking such as anti‐integrins (preventing the migration of lymphocytes from the blood vessels to the gut) or sphingosine‐1‐phosphate (S1P) modulators (blocking lymphocytes in the lymph nodes) and toll like receptor (TLR) agonist, among many others.\n...\nIL‐23 is a pro‐inflammatory cytokine, composed of 2 subunits (p19, specific to IL‐23 and p40, common to IL‐12), playing a key role in IBD.\n...\ns are small molecules, having the advantage of being per os and not associated with antidrug antibodies development, acting downstream of cytokines.\n...\n^52^ , ^53^ Filgotinib, upadacitinib and ivarmacitinib are three other JAK‐inhibitors that have been developed in IBD.", "date": "2022-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.5}, "jev_score": 0.35, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacological Therapy in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10537095/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "New therapeutic perspectives emerged with biological therapy, initially with anti-TNF, followed by anti-integrins and anti-interleukins.\nDespite the different mechanisms of action, there are similarities between the general rates of effectiveness.\nThese similar results were also evidenced in JAK inhibitors and S1p modulators, the last therapeutic classes approved for the treatment of IBD.\n...\nAction of salicylates and glucocorticoids in IBD.\n5-ASA acts on pro-inflammatory factors such as molecular adhesion, Natural Killer cell activation, intestinal permeability, nuclear factor kappa B activation, IL-1β, IL-2, Interferon gamma, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes.\n...\nAnti-integrins are molecules expressed by leukocytes that bind to adhesion molecules (CAM) and allow the trafficking of these cells [85].\n...\nTofacitinib was the first small molecule released for the treatment of UC, it is a molecule that acts on all JAK, mainly on JAK 1 and 3.\n...\nS1p modulators act as functional antagonists in lymphocytic receptors, inhibiting lymphocytes dependent on this receptor from leaving secondary lymph nodes for peripheral blood, reducing the circulating number of these cells.", "date": "2023-09-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.3967}, "jev_score": 0.2817, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Understanding the therapeutic toolkit for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://s29.q4cdn.com/642306244/files/doc_downloads/2025/Publications/Danese-2025.pdf", "domain": "s29.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "Subsequent advancements led to the development of new biologic agents with distinct mechanisms of action: vedolizumab, an anti-integrin α4β7 agent29,30; ustekinumab, which inhibits IL-12 and\nIL-2331,32; and risankizumab and mirikizumab, which specifically target\n...\nand tailored lifestyle modifications to holistically address individual patient needs.\n...\naminosalicylates, corticosteroids, immunomodulators, biologic agents \n(anti-tumour necrosis factor agents, anti-integrins, anti-interleukins),\nand small molecules such as JAK inhibitors and S1P modulators.\n• Emerging therapies for IBD, including anti-IL-23, JAK inhibitors, TL1A \ninhibitors and RIPK1 inhibitors, offer targeted approaches to mitigate inflammation, expanding options for patients with IBD.\n...\nCyclosporine \nA and tacrolimus are calcineurin inhibitors that work by blocking the nuclear translocation of the transcription factor NFAT, thereby\n­preventing the initiation of T cell cytokine transcription76.\n...\nMany \nIBD treatments focus on inhibiting pro-inflammatory cytokines, which is particularly evident with anti-tumour necrosis factor (TNF) agents (such as infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab pegol and golimumab), which are approved, and investigational therapies (such as V565 and tulinercept).\nOther therapies target specific interleukins, including: lutikizumab (targets IL-1α/β);\naldesleukin and efavaleukin alfa (targets IL-2); PF-04236921 (inhibits IL-6); \nand olamkicept (targets IL-6 receptor).\nAdditionally, lusvertikimab antagonizes the IL-7 receptor and GSK1070806 inhibits IL-18.\n...\nAnti-TNF therapies bind to both soluble and transmembrane TNF, inhib- iting its interaction with receptors and therefore regulating various signalling pathways involved in IBD pathophysiology105,106.\n...\n• AMT101 (gut-selective IL-10 fusion protein)", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-08-10", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.4333}, "jev_score": 0.2686, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 827.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q004-2", "row_id": "q004", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication MT501, Mirador Therapeutics, Crohn's Disease, Moderately to Severely Active by CDAI and SES-CD", "query": "MT501 Crohn's disease target pathway", "anchors": ["MT501"], "search_latency_ms": 2865.0, "target": "drugs competing with MT501: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 344.3, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9239, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000388, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 2, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.48, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "Some of these drugs target well-known pathways, like JAK inhibitors and IL-23 blockers, while others focus on newer areas, such as specific receptors or molecules involved in inflammation.\n...\nMirikizumab, a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody targeting the p19 component of IL-23, offers both IV and SC administration options.\n...\nUpon activation, this initiates a cascade of signaling events that regulate various biological processes, including lymphocyte migration, endothelial cell permeability, angiogenesis, cell differentiation, proliferation, survival, and apoptosis.^32^ S1P receptors regulate the influx of leukocytes from primary and secondary lymphoid organs into the intestinal wall, thereby contributing to the maintenance of intestinal inflammation.\n...\n^43^ Blocking the activation of this pathway halts the activity of several chemokines involved in mediating inflammation.\nSeveral pan-JAK inhibitors and selective JAK inhibitors, including the newer TYK2 inhibitors, are in various stages of development for IBD.\n...\n### LMT503\nLMT503 is a small molecule designed to treat IBD by modulating macrophage polarization.^146^ Macrophages play a crucial role in immune response and tissue homeostasis by switching between pro-inflammatory (M1) and anti-inflammatory (M2) states.\nLMT503 reprograms macrophage metabolism, shifting them from an M1-like inflammatory state to an M2-like anti-inflammatory state.\nIn vitro studies showed that LMT503 suppresses M1 markers (e.g., iNOS) and enhances M2 markers (e.g., Arg-1).", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.6}, "jev_score": 0.48, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn's Disease Enteritis: Pathophysiological Mechanisms and ...", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/sji.70129", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "The progression of the disease is driven by complex cytokine and chemokine networks (e.g., TNF-α, IL-6, IL-17), epithelial barrier dysfunction and microbial dysbiosis, all of which contribute to chronic inflammation and mucosal damage.\n...\nUnlike monoclonal antibodies that target single cytokines, JAK inhibitors target the intracellular JAK–STAT signalling pathways—specifically JAK1—thereby modulating the downstream signalling of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines simultaneously, including IL-6, IFN-γ and IL-23 [26, 27].\nThis mechanistic ability to broadly suppress inflammatory signal transduction offers a potent alternative for patients with refractory disease [27-29].\nWhile other agents in this class, such as filgotinib, have shown variable results in clinical trials depending on patient heterogeneity and trial design, the success of JAK1 inhibition highlights the importance of intracellular signalling nodes as therapeutic targets in CD [25, 30].\n...\nCrohn's disease (CD) enteritis, a primary manifestation of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) affecting the small intestine, results from dysregulated interactions among intestinal epithelial cells (IECs), the mucosal immune system and the gut microbiota, leading to chronic, transmural inflammation (Figure 1) [31].\n...\nDuring the inflammatory flares characteristic of CD, lymphotoxin beta receptor (LTβR) signalling in IECs induces the production of chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL2, which promote neutrophil recruitment to sites of inflammation [36].\n...\nIncreased epithelial permeability permits microbial translocation into the lamina propria, initiating innate immune activation.\nMacrophages polarize towards a pro-inflammatory M1 phenotype, producing TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6 via NF-κB and MAPK signalling pathways, whereas M2 macrophages secrete IL-10 and PGE2 to promote tissue repair and resolution.\n...\nThe canonical Th1 response is driven by the cytokine IL-12, which signals through the STAT4 transcription factor to induce the master regulator T-bet, leading to the secretion of interferon-gamma (IFN-γ) [52, 53].\nIn contrast, the Th17 phenotype is promoted by cytokines such as IL-6 and TGF-β for initial differentiation and is critically maintained by IL-23, which signals via STAT3 to induce the master regulator RORγt.\n...\nCytokine and chemokine networks drive intestinal inflammation in Crohn's Disease (CD), mediating mucosal damage and immune cell recruitment [84, 85].\nPro-inflammatory mediators, including interleukin-1β (IL-1β), tumour necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), interleukin-6 (IL-6) and interleukin-17 (IL-17), amplify inflammatory cascades and disrupt homeostasis.\nIL-1β, produced by monocytes, induces fever and increases tight junction (TJ) permeability via nuclear factor-kappa B (NF-κB) activation and claudin-3 downregulation, promoting barrier dysfunction and immune cell infiltration [86, 87].\n...\nTherapies targeting cytokines, such as TNF-α blockade, IL-6 trans-signalling inhibitors (e.g., olamkicept) and JAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib), reduce inflammation and restore mucosal integrity in CD [85, 106, 107].\nThe interplay among IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-17 and JAK/STAT and NF-κB pathways underscores their role in CD and their therapeutic potential for the disease.\n...\n|TNF-α|Macrophages, monocytes, Th1 cells, ILCs|TNFR1, TNFR2; NF-κB, MAPK and apoptosis signalling|Drives a broad pro-inflammatory cascade; induces epithelial cell apoptosis and disrupts tight junctions, increasing permeability; promotes immune cell recruitment.|Neutralizing antibodies (e.g., infliximab, adalimumab) directly block TNF-α activity.|[93, 94]|\n|IL-12|Dendritic cells, macrophages|IL-12R (IL-12Rβ1/IL-12Rβ2); JAK2/TYK2, STAT4|Crucial for the differentiation of naïve T cells into Th1 cells; promotes IFN-γ production, driving cell-mediated cytotoxicity and macrophage activation.|p40 subunit inhibition (e.g., ustekinumab) blocks both IL-12 and IL-23 signalling.|[92, 95]|\n|IFN-γ|Th1 cells, NK cells, ILC1s|IFNGR1/IFNGR2; JAK1/JAK2, STAT1|Signature Th1 cytokine; activates macrophages, upregulates MHC expression and impairs intestinal epithelial barrier function by increasing apoptosis and altering tight junction proteins.|Not a direct target, but production is reduced by anti-IL-12/23 and JAK inhibitors.|[96, 97]|\n...\nConventional pharmacological therapies, comprising corticosteroids and immunosuppressants, remain foundational for inducing and maintaining remission [212].\n...\nUstekinumab targets the p40 subunit shared by both IL-12 and IL-23.\nThis dual inhibition is critical because it simultaneously disrupts two key pathogenic axes in CD: the IL-12/STAT4 pathway, which drives Th1 cell differentiation and IFN-γ production and the IL-23/STAT3 pathway, which is essential for the stabilization and expansion of pathogenic Th17 cells [233-237].\n...\nJanus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are small molecules that interfere with the intracellular JAK–STAT signalling pathway, which is utilized by numerous pro-inflammatory cytokine receptors.\nUpadacitinib, a selective JAK1 inhibitor, preferentially blocks signalling from key cytokines implicated in CD, such as IL-6, IL-23 and IFN-γ.", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.5267}, "jev_score": 0.3898, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/477311c534e0406d8c27a73fc39e6074", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn Disease|\n...\n|Crohn Disease|Phase 2|United States|Mirador Therapeutics, Inc.|26 Jun 2025|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.3133}, "jev_score": 0.1754, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancements in Targeted Therapies for the Management of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11226209/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "These medicines include biologics like anti-TNF agents, interleukin inhibitors, and small molecule inhibitors; they work by altering the modulation of immune responses and reducing inflammation.\n...\nNAT1 further binds to PPAR-gamma (peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma), a nuclear hormone receptor, brings its conformational change, and leads its migration from the cytoplasm to the nucleus where it starts the domino effect of turning down the inflammatory casket [14].\n...\nDRIP interacts with PPAR-gamma to form protein dimers.\nThe consumption of PPAR-gamma in this process downregulates the PPAR-gamma response element (PPRE) down the line that ultimately sends the signal to switch off the inflammatory casket primarily by downregulating the nuclear factor kappa B (NF-kappaB), activated protein kinase (MAPK), ceasing COX-2 activity.\nThis manifests as reduced cytokines and prostaglandin availability, which are vitals for the inflammation [11,14].\n...\nThe main mechanism of corticosteroids in the treatment and prevention of CD remission is to put a halt to the transcription of interleukin and arachidonic acid metabolism, thus preventing the indispensable steps in the casket of inflammation and triggering lymphocyte apoptosis in the gut [13].\n...\nThe goal of immunosuppressive therapy for the treatment of CD is to decrease the gut inflammatory process.\nDue to the pathophysiology of CD, a dysregulated, overactive immune response with an excess of pro-inflammatory cytokines has led to the relative importance of immunosuppressive therapy.\nThese drugs work by reducing the function of immune cells.\n...\nThe mechanism of action of methotrexate is to inhibit dihydrofolate reductase.\nDihydrofolate reductase plays a pivotal role in the metabolism of folic acid, thus ultimately inhibiting the production of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), ribonucleic acid (RNA), and protein [15,19].\n...\nThis cyclosporine is an immunomodulator that targets T-cell activation by halting cytokine production.\nIt inhibits calcineurin.\n...\nTherefore, TNF therapy provides relief in such patients by multiple mechanisms.\nNullification of TNF-α, reverse signaling, apoptosis, and cytotoxicity are the major mechanisms employed by it.\n...\nThey bind TNF-α, blocking further upregulation of pro-inflammatory molecules and blocking the production of cytokines [16,21-22].\n...\nAnti-integrin agents are similar to their working TNF-α inhibitors in a way that it is specifically designed to target the molecules that result in triggering the inflammatory casket in the intestinal mucosa.\nHowever, unlike TNF-α inhibitors, its target is integrin responsible for leukocyte trafficking [15,16].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of this drug is directly on α4β7 integrin, without disturbing α4β1 integrin [15,16].\n...\n|Natalizumab|Alpha-4 integrin|Humanized monoclonal antibody against the alpha-4 subunit of integrin molecules, thereby blocking adhesion and migration of leukocytes in the gut.|Moderate to severe|Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), skin rash, gastrointestinal symptoms including nausea and gastroenteritis [18,19,21].|\n|Risankizumab|Interleukin-23|Target inhibition of IL-23|Moderate to severe|Hypersensitivity reactions, upper respiratory tract infection, inflammation, nasopharyngitis [27].|\n...\n|Vedolizumab|α4β7 integrin|Humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the α4β7 integrin and blocks the interaction of α4β7 integrin with mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1)|Moderate to severe|Immunologic: antibody development, headaches, arthralgias, nasopharyngitis, pruritis, and gastrointestinal including nausea [30].|\n...\nRisankizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that represents the first and only targeted selective inhibitor of interleukin (IL)-23, targeting the p19 subunit, thereby modulating the inflammatory cascade implicated in CD.\nRizankizumab disrupts IL-23-driven immune response, thereby alleviating intestinal inflammation, reducing disease activity, and improving clinical outcomes in patients with CD [32].\n...\nUpadacitinib is an inhibitor of the JAK-1 enzyme, selectively blocking it, and thereby interfering with JAK-STAT signaling pathways that are linked to the immune responses, which play a role in the pathogenesis of CD.\nBy preventing the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines, inflammation diminishes while the gastrointestinal immune system recovers homeostasis, eventually resulting in an improvement of the condition and better clinical results for CD patients [39].\n...\nEfforts are underway to create novel drugs targeting pathways, including α4β7 integrin, JAK, and IL-12/IL-23.\nAs an example, the fully human monoclonal antibody ustekinumab (UST) blocks the interaction between the common receptor IL-12Rβ1 and the IL-12/IL-23 p40 subunit.", "date": "2024-06-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.44, "evidence": 0.3633}, "jev_score": 0.1599, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 for Inflammatory Bowel Disease (ASCEND-IBD Trial)", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-inflammatory-bowel-diseases-5-2025-6303d", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests a new treatment, MT-501, for individuals with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), specifically Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis.\n...\nUnlike traditional treatments, which often rely on suppressing the immune system, MT-501 targets specific inflammatory pathways, potentially reducing side effects and increasing effectiveness.\nThis precision in targeting inflammation may lead to faster symptom relief and improved long-term outcomes for patients.\n...\nStudies have shown that MT-501 may benefit people with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including Crohn's Disease and Ulcerative Colitis.\nEarly findings suggest that MT-501 can reduce gut swelling, potentially leading to fewer symptoms.\nResearch indicates that the treatment targets specific body parts responsible for this swelling, possibly offering better disease control than some current treatments.\n...\nIt aims to determine the safety and effectiveness of this investigational drug in adults with either Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis that is moderately to severely active.", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.38, "evidence": 0.3067}, "jev_score": 0.1165, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A Phase 2 Study to Evaluate Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://bridgemd.health/study/NCT07113522", "domain": "bridgemd.health", "snippet": "MT-501 is an investigational medicine, given as a pill taken by mouth, being studied as a potential treatment for inflammatory bowel disease (ibd).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-06", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.53, "evidence": 0.2133}, "jev_score": 0.113, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 - AssetMemo", "url": "https://assetmemo.bio/drug/mt-501", "domain": "assetmemo.bio", "snippet": "Phase 1 / first-in-human studies establish safety, tolerability, and dosing — they are not designed to demonstrate efficacy, so no evidence signal is assigned yet.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.63, "evidence": 0.16}, "jev_score": 0.1008, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 2 Study of MT-501 and MT-201 for Active Crohn's Disease or ...", "url": "https://aidyhq.com/clinical-trials/nct07113522", "domain": "aidyhq.com", "snippet": "This Phase 2 platform study is assessing the safety, activity, drug levels, and biological effects of MT-501 and MT-201 in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\n### What participants may receive or do\n- MT-501: Participants assigned to either the Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis MT-501 group receive MT-501 tablets.\n...\nThis is a Phase 2, multicenter platform study with three experimental groups: MT-501 tablets for Crohn’s disease, MT-501 tablets for ulcerative colitis, and intravenous MT-201 with standard of care for Crohn’s disease.\n...\nThis is a Phase 2, multicenter, platform study in adult participants with IBD (moderately to severely active Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis).\nThe primary goal of this study is to assess the safety and efficacy of multiple investigational drugs.\nThe purpose of this platform study is to assess the safety, efficacy, pharmacokinetics (PK), and pharmacodynamics (PD) to evaluate multiple oral or parenteral experimental therapies for moderately to severely active Crohn's Disease or Ulcerative Colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.51, "evidence": 0.1833}, "jev_score": 0.0935, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "MT-501 for Safety in Healthy Subjects · Info for Participants", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-1-healthy-subjects-hs-11-2024-26a90", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "The main goal of this research is to determine if MT-501 is safe and effective for people with moderate to severe forms of these digestive ...", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.35, "evidence": 0.0833}, "jev_score": 0.0292, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Treat to target in Crohn's disease: A practical guide for clinicians", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/1007-9327/full/v30/i1/50.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "Normalisation of inflammatory biomarkers such as C-reactive protein (CRP), erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and faecal calprotectin were identified as short-to-intermediate treatment targets, while endoscopic healing endures as the consensus long-term treatment target in Crohn’s disease[4].\n...\n|Biomarker normalisation|C-reactive protein < upper limit of normal; faecal calprotectin < 250 μg/g|\n|Endoscopic response|> 50% improvement in simple endoscopic score-Crohn’s disease score; or > 50% improvement in Crohn’s disease endoscopic index score|\n|Endoscopic healing|Simple endoscopic score-Crohn’s disease ≤ 2; or Crohn’s disease endoscopic index score < 3; and absence of ulcers, including aphthous ulcers|\n...\n**Key points:** (1) Endoscopic healing remains the designated long-term treatment target per STRIDE-II; (2a) Short to medium-term (0-6 mo): Endoscopic response: > 50% reduction in SES-CD or CDEIS; and (2b) Long-term (> 6 mo): Endoscopic remission, SES-CD ≤ 2 or CDEIS < 3, without ulcers.\n...\nEndoscopic healing in Crohn’s disease is generally defined as the absence of ulceration of the bowel mucosa[56].", "date": "2024-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.021, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3209.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:09+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q005-4", "row_id": "q005", "mode": "queries", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "SOR102 Grade 3 adverse events 810 mg", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "search_latency_ms": 1909.2, "target": "SOR102 / SOR102-101", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 360.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9306, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000391, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9571, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/ECCO-2025-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "6-week SOR102 treatment: 810 mg QD, 810 mg BID, or placebo BID\n...\nSafety Analysis Set\n...\nSOR102 \n810mg QD \n(N=7)\nSOR102 \n810mg BID \n(N=9)\nTotal \n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n3 (50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs leading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n1 (11)\n1 (5)\n...\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE by maximum severity\nMild\n1 (17)\n1 (14)\n2 (22)\n4 (18)\nModerate\n2 (33)\n...\nSevere\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization\n2. Abdominal pain of mild severity", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-14", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9867}, "jev_score": 0.9571, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "OP31 Phase 1b study of SOR102, a novel, orally delivered ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/19/Supplement_1/i62/7966975", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Pts with Mayo endoscopy score (ES) ≥2, rectal bleeding score (RBS) ≥1 and stool frequency score (SFS) ≥1 were randomized 1:1:1 to receive SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo for 6 weeks.\n...\nTwenty-two pts were randomized (SOR102 810mg BID=9; SOR102 810mg QD=7; placebo=6); 17 pts completed the study.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of pts receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1); most were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization occurred in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.", "date": "2025-01-22", "last_updated": "2026-09-06", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9833}, "jev_score": 0.9538, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "snippet": "TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).", "date": "2025-03-31", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9767}, "jev_score": 0.9474, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific ...", "url": "https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/173141909/Safety-and-pharmacokinetics-of-sor102-an-oral-bispecific-inhibitor-of-tnf-and-interleukin-23-in-healthy-participants-an.pdf", "domain": "pure.amsterdamumc.nl", "snippet": "In part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe\n[the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other \nTEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\n...\nIn part 1, any treatment emergent adverse event (TEAE) occurred in three (13%) of 24 healthy participants who received single doses of SOR102 compared with two (25%) of eight patients who received placebo.\n...\nSOR102 405 mg dose group.\n...\nNo treatment emergent serious adverse event occurred during parts 1 or 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of \n16 patients who received SOR102 and three (50%) of \nsix patients who received placebo (table 3).\nTwo (13%) of 16 patients treated with SOR102 (both in the twice-daily\nSOR102 group) had TEAEs of worsening ulcerative colitis that led to study discontinuation.\nAll (77%) but one TEAE in patients with at least one TEAE were mild to moderate.\nOne mild TEAE of upper abdominal pain in a patient who received twice-daily SOR102 was considered possibly related to treatment.\nThis patient also had a treatment emergent serious adverse event of worsening ulcerative colitis during the follow-up period that required hospital­ isation, and which led to study withdrawal.\nThis serious adverse event (also considered a severe TEAE) was considered unrelated to study drug.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-16", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9733}, "jev_score": 0.9441, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9767}, "jev_score": 0.9376, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In part 3, seven patients with ulcerative colitis were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 810 mg once-daily, nine to receive the same dose twice-daily, and six to receive placebo.\n...\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\n...\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nIn Sorriso’s Phase 1b study, six patients received placebo, seven received 810 mg of SOR102 once per day, and nine received 810 mg twice per day.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9567}, "jev_score": 0.9184, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR102 / Sorriso Pharma - Gastroenterology", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=f4d4f2dd-16fc-4e98-b7ec-f3a34272a168", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "| \"Safety and Tolerability: : SOR102 exhibited a favorable safety profile, with adverse events predominantly mild to moderate in severity; no new safety signals were observed....Efficacy: In the intent-to-treat (ITT) analysis, Mayo Score and modified Mayo Score clinical response were 56% and 67% in the high-dose SOR102 group compared to 17% and 33% in the placebo group, respectively....", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.91}, "jev_score": 0.8827, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "Across single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\n...\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9133}, "jev_score": 0.8768, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Pooled safety analysis and management of sotorasib-related ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/oncolo/article/30/1/oyae356/7976610", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "TRAEs were reported in 388 (70.7%) patients (Table 2).\nGrade 1 and grade 2 TRAEs were reported in 124 (22.6%) and 117 (21.3%) patients, respectively, with grade ≥3 events occurring in 147 (26.8%) patients.\n...\nOverall, grades 1, 2, and 3 treatment-related diarrhea events occurred in 78 (14.2%), 53 (9.7%), and 40 (7.3%) patients, respectively (Table 3).\n...\nGrade ≥3 events were managed with dose modification; a subset of these patients resumed sotorasib at 960 mg after treatment interruption or prior dose reduction at investigator discretion.\n...\nSotorasib-related diarrhea, including grade 3, was effectively managed with dose interruption or dose reduction with or without antidiarrheal medications in more than 90% of events; no grade 4 or fatal diarrhea event was reported.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.1, "evidence": 0.1033}, "jev_score": 0.0103, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Sotorasib in KRAS p.G12C–Mutated Advanced Pancreatic Cancer | NEJM", "url": "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa2208470", "domain": "nejm.org", "snippet": "In phase 2, patients received sotorasib at a dose of 960 mg orally once daily.\n...\nTreatment-related adverse events of any grade were reported in 16 patients (42%); 6 patients (16%) had grade 3 adverse events.\nNo treatment-related adverse events were fatal or led to treatment discontinuation.\n...\nOverall, 24 patients (63%) had adverse events of grade 3 or higher.\nAdverse events of any grade that were considered by the investigators to be related to treatment were reported in 16 patients (42%); 6 of these events (16%) were grade 3 in severity (Table 3).\nThe most common grade 3 treatment-related adverse events were diarrhea and fatigue (in 2 patients [5%] each).\n...\nAmong these events, grade 3 diarrhea that resolved in 4 days and grade 2 or 3 diarrhea that resolved in 16 days were observed in 2 patients (5%).\n...\n|Grade ≥3|9 (75)|15 (58)|24 (63)|0|6 (23)|6 (16)|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.1133}, "jev_score": 0.0057, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 2269.2, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:10+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q005-1", "row_id": "q005", "mode": "queries", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "SOR102 Crohn's disease safety adverse events", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "search_latency_ms": 2410.5, "target": "SOR102 / SOR102-101", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 423.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10294, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000432, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9506, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR-102 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/c4ffffc6839e4416b41bd1cd9d28f2d9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Colitis, UlcerativeCrohn Disease|\n...\nSOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.", "date": "2025-01-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.97}, "jev_score": 0.9506, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 is a Phase 1, 3-part, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, FIH study to determine the safety, tolerability, and PK of single, ascending oral doses (SAD) of SOR102 (Part 1) and multiple oral doses (Part 2) of SOR102 in healthy adult participants, and to assess the safety, tolerability, PK, and biological activity of multiple oral doses of SOR102 in patients with mild to severe UC (Part 3).\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nThe favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.\n...\n|SOR-102 ( IL-23 x IL-23p19 x TNF-α )|Colitis, Ulcerative More|Phase 2|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.98}, "jev_score": 0.9506, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PHASE 1B STUDY OF SOR102, A NOVEL, ORALLY ...", "url": "https://eposters.ddw.org/ddw/2025/ddw-2025/4155984/vipul.jairath.phase.1b.study.of.sor102.a.novel.orally.delivered.bispecific.html?f=menu=6*browseby=8*sortby=2*ce_id=2855*ot_id=31400*marker=5698", "domain": "eposters.ddw.org", "snippet": "TEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.\n...\nThere were no significant differences in FC and CRP between the treatment groups.\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\nEfficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to the QD and placebo arms, despite the relatively short duration of treatment.\nThese results warrant further clinical development of SOR102.\n...\nTEAEs were reported in 57%, 29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 1).\nMost were mild-to-moderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\n...\n**Conclusion**: SOR102 was safe and well tolerated.", "date": "2025-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9667}, "jev_score": 0.9474, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\nThis trial also critically demonstrated proof-of-concept for the therapeutic target and delivery mechanism: from a PK perspective, no detectable serum or urine concentrations of SOR102, SOR102-TNF, or SOR102-IL-23 were identified across study parts, suggesting minimal systemic drug exposure.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\nNotably, some ADA positivity was present at baseline, and most treatment-emergent ADA titers were low.\nDespite no apparent short-term impact, the relatively high ADA rates warrant further evaluation for potential effects on long-term efficacy, potential neutralization of drug effects, durability of response, and potential for inducing hypersensitivity reactions in later phase trials.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.\n...\nTo date, no major safety concerns have emerged; however, immunogenicity and the long-term consequences of dual-pathway immunosuppression will require careful evaluation in ongoing and future studies (11).", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.97}, "jev_score": 0.9312, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "EXPLORATORY EFFICACY RESULTS ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/DDW-2025-poster.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "Here, we present safety and efficacy results for Part 3, a\nPhase 1b randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study in patients with mild to severe UC.\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nIn most samples tested, there were no detectable levels of SOR102 or monomers in serum\n...\nADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response.\n...\nEfficacy across multiple endpoints was observed in the SOR102 BID arm compared to QD and placebo, despite the short treatment duration.\n...\nPatients with a Mayo endoscopy score (ES) of ≥2, a rectal bleeding score (RBS) of ≥1 and stool frequency score (SFS) of ≥1 were randomized 1:1:1 to receive SOR102 810mg BID,\nSOR102 810mg QD, or placebo for 6 weeks.\n...\nMost ADA titers were low (<1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response\n...\nTable 2.\nTreatment Emergent Adverse Events\nPlacebo\n(N=6)\nSOR102 \n810mg QD (N=7)\nSOR102 \n810mg BID (N=9)\nTotal\n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n(50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs \nleading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n...\n0 (0)\n1 (11)1\n...\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE \nby maximum severity\nMild\n...\nModerate\n...\nSevere\n0 (0)\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization 2.\nAbdominal pain of mild severity\n...\nTreatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were reported in 57%, \n29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 \n810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 2).\nMost were mild-to-\nmoderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne unrelated serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9567}, "jev_score": 0.928, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\nReasons for discontinuation: worsening of \nUC (n=2), study schedule non-compliance \n(n=2) and consent withdrawal (n=1).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.\n...\nHigh and sustained levels of active monomers in feces confirmed\nSOR102 is efficiently cleaved by trypsin and cytokine-binding activity is maintained after oral dosing in patients with UC.\n...\n4 QD and 2 BID patients developed ADA responses after SOR102 dosing (anti-SOR102-TNF only, n=1; anti-SOR102-IL-23 only, n=2; ADA\nagainst both monomers n=3).\nMost titers were low (≤1:240) and not associated with safety or clinical response5", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9567}, "jev_score": 0.928, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR102 / Sorriso Pharma - Gastroenterology", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=f4d4f2dd-16fc-4e98-b7ec-f3a34272a168", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "(PubMed, Transl Gastroenterol Hepatol)\n...\n- P1 | \"The favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\n| \"Safety and Tolerability: : SOR102 exhibited a favorable safety profile, with adverse events predominantly mild to moderate in severity; no new safety signals were observed....Efficacy: In the intent-to-treat (ITT) analysis, Mayo Score and modified Mayo Score clinical response were 56% and 67% in the high-dose SOR102 group compared to 17% and 33% in the placebo group, respectively....| \"The study met its primary objectives, demonstrating a favorable safety and tolerability profile.\n...\n(UEGW 2024)\n- P1 | \"SOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n...\n(DDW 2024)\n- P1 | \"Collectively these data support a low risk of systemic immunosuppression after oral SOR10 dosing in IBD patients.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8933}, "jev_score": 0.8665, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "A novel oral bispecific antibody (SOR102) targeting TNF and IL-23 shows a favourable safety profile with encouraging early efficacy in ulcerative colitis.\n...\nSOR102 is an oral bispecific antibody targeting TNF and IL-23p19 developed for ulcer ative colitis.\nThis phase 1, double blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluated its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in healthy participants and patients with active ulcerative colitis.\nAcross single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\nIn patients with ulcerative colitis treated for 42 days, clinical responses were more frequent with SOR102 than placebo, particularly with twice-daily dosing.\nSymptomatic remission was achieved in over half of patients receiving twice-daily SOR102, while no placebo-treated patients reached remission.\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.\nOverall, the favourable safety profile and early signs of clinical bene fit support further clinical development of SOR102 as a potential oral therapy for ulcerative colitis.\n...\nNo significant differences were observed in corticosteroid-free remission, clinical response, hospitalization, surgery, adverse events, or loss of response.\nOverall, the findings suggest that continuing 5-ASA alongside advanced therapies provides no clear therapeutic benefit or safety advantage, supporting consideration of 5-ASA dis continuation in patients with ulcerative colitis who have escalated to biologic or advanced treatments.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8667}, "jev_score": 0.8407, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Colite ulcerosa: nuova frontiera terapeutica con SOR102 secondo ...", "url": "https://www.hsr.it/news/2025/novembre/colite-ulcerosa-nuova-terapia-sor102-studio-internazionale", "domain": "hsr.it", "snippet": "Lo studio ha coinvolto volontari sani e pazienti con colite ulcerosa da lieve a severa.\nI risultati hanno mostrato che SOR102 è ben tollerato e non ha provocato effetti collaterali significativi.\nGli eventi avversi registrati sono stati di entità lieve o moderata e non si sono osservate alterazioni nei parametri clinici o di laboratorio.\nIl programma di sviluppo clinico di SOR102 proseguirà infatti con **studi di fase 2**, già in pianificazione, per valutare più approfonditamente efficacia e sicurezza nel trattamento della colite ulcerosa e di altre malattie infiammatorie intestinali come la malattia di Crohn.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7426, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Anti-tumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A and bispecific antibod - Apollo", "url": "https://api.repository.cam.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/f38d88f6-3633-401f-b149-325481457cca/content", "domain": "api.repository.cam.ac.uk", "snippet": "Treatment-emergent\nadverse events\noccurred in 48% of\npatients during induction\nand 59% during\nmaintenance with\noverall incidences\nsimilar to placebo; the\nmost common were\nnausea, urinary tract\ninfection, anemia, and\n...\nTulisokibart was well\ntolerated.\n46% reported\nmild/moderate\ninfections; two serious\ninfections were\nunrelated and resolved.\nAt week 50, infection\noccurred in 63%\n(100 mg) and 61%\n(250 mg) groups, none\nserious.\nNo dose-\n...\nIn addition, another molecule, SOR102, consists of V900, a humanized llama- derived domain antibody targeting the p19 subunit of\nIL-23.\nIt is also engineered for resistance to intestinal proteases, fused with V565, a previously characterized domain antibody against TNF-α.\nThe two domains are linked via a central protease-cleavable linker, forming a single molecule designed for oral administration.\nThis molecule is currently being tested in a phase I trial for both healthy volunteers and patients with UC.\nThe phase I trial is seeking to assess safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics using ascending doses.\nInitial findings suggest that SOR102 has a manageable safety profile and favorable pharmacodynamics, though more data are expected soon [12].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-20", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.7099, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2833.6, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:10+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q003-3", "row_id": "q003", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as RpoB Small Molecule", "query": "Distal Ulcerative Colitis RpoB mechanism clinical trial", "anchors": ["RpoB"], "search_latency_ms": 6469.0, "target": "drugs competing with RpoB: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 384.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8910, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000374, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.1965, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Third European Evidence-based Consensus on Diagnosis and ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/11/7/769/2962457", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "An RCT has assessed the safety and efficacy of eldelumab, a monoclonal antibody against IP-10 in 252 UC patients.\n...\nTacrolimus is a calcineurin inhibitor that acts via a mechanism similar to CsA.\n...\nInfliximab as a single dose [5 mg/kg] is an effective salvage therapy in patients with severe UC refractory to IV steroids.\n...\nPatients with endoscopically documented active distal colitis or proctitis, who fail oral corticosteroids combined with oral and rectal 5-ASA therapy, have refractory disease.\nTherapeutic options include admission for IV steroid therapy, which has been reported to induce remission in a high proportion of patients.^166^ Alternatively, there is open-label evidence, often from retrospective case reviews, supporting the use of salvage medical therapies such as oral or rectal CsA, oral or rectal tacrolimus, or IFX.^167–171^\n...\nIn ULTRA 2, the pivotal RCT of adalimumab in patients with moderate to severe UC, 494 adult patients with endoscopically confirmed moderate/severely active colitis despite treatment with corticosteroids and/or thiopurines, were randomised to receive 160 mg adalimumab at Week 0, 80 mg at Week 2, and then 40 mg every other week from Week 4.\n...\nOral or rectal 5-ASA is superior to placebo in maintaining remission in UC.\nRectal 5-ASA has equivalent or slightly superior efficacy to oral 5-ASA in distal UC.\n...\nNo head-to head, prospective trials are available.", "date": "2017-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.45, "evidence": 0.4367}, "jev_score": 0.1965, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Recent advances in the management of distal ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/2150-5349/full/v1/i2/43.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "Infliximab 5 mg/kg induction (0, 2 and 6 wk) followed by maintenance therapy (every 8 wk) offers an effective treatment option for patients with refractory distal UC.\n...\nThe efficacy of MMX mesalazine as maintenance therapy was examined in a more recent multicenter study[37].\nPatients with UC were randomized to receive MMX mesalazine 2.4 g/d once daily, or delayed-release (EUDRAGIT S-coated) mesalazine 2.4 g/d twice daily, administered in a double-dummy fashion for 12 mo.\n...\nThe data from this study indicate that MMX mesalazine 2.4 g/d once daily and delayed-release mesalazine 2.4 g/d twice daily are similarly tolerated and effective in the maintenance of remission of distal UC.\n...\nBudesonide enema is both effective and safe for the treatment of active distal UC[49].\n...\nTrials on adalimumab in UC are ongoing.", "date": "2010-04-06", "last_updated": "2026-08-17", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.38, "evidence": 0.4433}, "jev_score": 0.1685, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Colitis clinical trials", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gg/conditions/colitis", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gg", "snippet": "- A Pilot Study Testing the Safety and Feasibility of Restorative Microbiota Therapy (RMT) in Patients With Refractory Immune-checkpoint Inhibitor-related ColitisRecruitingNCT05726396Phase 21 country\n...\n- Tacrolimus Lipid Suspension for Enema in Adult Subjects with Mild to Moderately Active Left-sided/Distal Ulcerative Colitis Who Had an Inadequate Response to the TreatmentNot yet recruitingNCT06867042Phase 2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.34, "evidence": 0.3467}, "jev_score": 0.1179, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Randomised clinical trial: the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus reuteri__ ATCC 55730 rectal enema in children with active distal ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/j.1365-2036.2011.04939.x", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Randomised clinical trial: the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus reuteri__ ATCC 55730 rectal enema in children with active distal ulcerative colitis\n...\n**Background** Intestinal microbiota manipulation, one of the pathogenetic components of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), has become an attractive therapy for ulcerative colitis (UC).\n**Aim** To assess in children with active distal UC the effectiveness of __Lactobacillus (L) reuteri__ ATCC 55730 enema on inflammation and cytokine expression of rectal mucosa.\n**Methods** A total of 40 patients (median age: 7.2 years range 6–18) with mild to moderate UC were enrolled in a prospective, randomised, placebo‐controlled study.\nThey received an enema solution containing 10^10^ CFU of __L.\nreuteri__ ATCC 55730 or placebo for 8 weeks, in addition to oral mesalazine.\nClinical endoscopic and histological scores as well as rectal mucosal expression levels of IL‐10, IL‐1β, TNFα and IL‐8 were evaluated at the beginning and at the end of the trial.\n**Results** Thirty‐one patients accomplished the trial (17 males, median age 13 year, range 7–18).\nMayo score (including clinical and endoscopic features) decreased significantly in the __L.\nreuteri__ group (3.2 ± 1.3 vs. 8.6 ± 0.8, __P__<0.01) compared with placebo (7.1 ± 1.1 vs.\n8.7 ± 0.7, NS); furthermore, histological score significantly decrease only in the __L.\nreuteri__ group (0.6 ± 0.5 vs. 4.5 ± 0.6, __P__<0.01) (placebo: 2.9 ± 0.8 vs.\n4.6 ± 0.6, NS).\nAt the post‐trial evaluation of cytokine mucosal expression levels, IL‐10 significantly increased (__P__<0.01) whereas IL‐1β, TNFα and IL‐8 significantly decreased (__P__<0.01) only in the __L.\nreuteri__ group.\n**Conclusions** In children with active distal ulcerative colitis, rectal infusion of __L.\nreuteri__ is effective in improving mucosal inflammation and changing mucosal expression levels of some cytokines involved in the mechanisms of inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.29, "evidence": 0.4067}, "jev_score": 0.1179, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Medical Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2780076/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "A double-blind comparison of oral versus rectal mesalamine versus combination therapy in the treatment of distal ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.3, "evidence": 0.3633}, "jev_score": 0.109, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical outcomes of best practices for the treatment of distal ... - OUCI", "url": "https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/4YmAbXPQ/", "domain": "ouci.dntb.gov.ua", "snippet": "Distal ulcerative colitis (DUC), affecting 30–50% of ulcerative colitis (UC) patients, is a subtype characterized by inflammation limited to the rectum and sigmoid colon.\nThe pathogenesis involves a complex interplay of genetic, environmental, and immune factors, with cytokines like TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, and IL-17 playing significant roles.\n...\nThis review examined 50 randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that explored pharmacological and non-pharmacological interventions for distal ulcerative colitis, focusing on symptom remission, mucosal healing, and quality-of-life outcomes.\nKey findings revealed that pharmacologic treatments, particularly 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA), corticosteroids, and biologics, effectively achieved remission, with combined therapies beneficial in refractory cases.\n...\nImportantly, trials highlighted that individualized approaches – such as early biologic therapy for patients with moderate-to-severe disease, dose adjustments based on patient demographics, and tailored lifestyle recommendations – enhanced treatment response and patient quality of life.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-01", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.19, "evidence": 0.3433}, "jev_score": 0.0652, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A comprehensive review of usefulness of sodium butyrate ...", "url": "https://www.termedia.pl/doi_ft/10.5114/pg.2026.159719", "domain": "termedia.pl", "snippet": "These effects involve immunomodulatory mechanisms, including activation of G-protein–cou- pled receptors (e.g. GPR41) and inhibition of histone deacetylases, resulting in attenuation of NF-κB- and\n...\nA central mechanism of its immunomodulatory action involves the activation of G protein–coupled receptors (GPR41, GPR43, and\nGPR109A) expressed on T lymphocytes, macrophages, and intestinal epithelial cells [16].\nReceptor activation promotes regulatory T-cell (Treg) differentiation and activity, increases the production of anti-inflammato- ry cytokines such as interleukin-18 (IL-18), and helps maintain the Th17/Treg balance, thereby attenuating the pro-inflammatory responses characteristic of active\nIBD [17, 18].\nAnother well-established mechanism is the epigen- etic effect of butyrate mediated through the inhibition of histone deacetylase (HDAC).\n...\nEvidence indicates that butyrate upregulates the expression of tight junction–associated proteins including claudins, occludin, and zonula occludens-1, thereby strengthening the mucosal barrier and reduc- ing the excessive intestinal permeability characteristic of IBD (‘leaky gut’) [24].\nButyrate also stimulates mucin production in goblet cells (particularly mucin 2 (MUC2)), the principal component of the protective mucus layer, and enhances the synthesis of secretory immunoglobu- lin A (IgA) and antimicrobial peptides.\n...\nAnother trial demonstrated that in patients with distal UC, a combination therapy with rectally administered mesalamine and butyrate was sig- nificantly more effective than rectal mesalamine alone.\nIn this population, butyrate enemas were also shown to significantly reduce NF-κB activity in lamina propria macrophages, which was associated with decreased inflammatory activity and lower disease activity index scores [52].\nIn a subsequent multicenter trial published in 2003, topical butyrate as an adjunct to mesalamine improved clinical and endoscopic outcomes in patients with refractory distal UC, supporting its role as an effec- tive and well-tolerated add-on therapy [53].\n...\nA total of 72 patients were enrolled and randomized to receive MSB (150 mg twice daily) or placebo for 12 weeks.\n...\n(PCDAI, PUCAI), or FC levels.\n...\nIts broad biological actions – reinforcement of epithelial barrier integrity, modulation of immune and inflammatory pathways, and beneficial effects on gut microbial com- position – provide a strong mechanistic rationale for its clinical application.\n...\nCurrent evidence suggests that butyrate supplementation may improve disease-related outcomes, particularly in UC, by attenuating inflammatory activity, supporting mucosal healing, and potentially enhancing responsiveness to treatment.\n...\nHowever, the evidence base remains limited by the relatively small number of randomized controlled trials,", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-19", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.14, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.042, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis - 1,154 clinical trials", "url": "https://atlasbyauro.com/trials/condition/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "atlasbyauro.com", "snippet": "|Mirikizumab Administered at the Same Time as Tirzepatide in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis and Obesity or Overweight: Phase 3b Study (NCT06937086)|Eli Lilly and Company|2025-06-26|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-12", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.13, "evidence": 0.18}, "jev_score": 0.0234, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Gut microbiome‐targeted therapies as adjuvant treatments ...", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jgh.16795", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Thirty-eight RCTs met the inclusion criteria.\nFirstly, we compared the efficacy of MTTs in IBD patients.\nOnly FMT and probiotics were superior to placebo in all outcomes, but FMT ranked best in improving clinical response rate and clinical and endoscopic remission rate, and probiotics ranked second in reducing clinical relapse rate showed significant efficacy, while prebiotics ranked first showed nonsignificant efficacy.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.1433}, "jev_score": 0.01, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT03596645 | A Study to Assess the Efficacy and Safety ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT03596645", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "Study record managers: refer to the Data Element Definitions if submitting registration or results information.", "date": "2026-07-06", "last_updated": "2026-07-16", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.15, "evidence": 0.0133}, "jev_score": 0.002, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 6853.5, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:10+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q005-3", "row_id": "q005", "mode": "queries", "objective": "SOR102 safety findings and adverse events in Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "SOR102 ulcerative colitis individual adverse events infections deaths", "anchors": ["SOR102", "SOR102-101"], "search_latency_ms": 2383.9, "target": "SOR102 / SOR102-101", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 454.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9667, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000406, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9702, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint-Präsentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-2024-presentation.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "SOR102-101 (NCT06080048) is a Phase 1, first in human study that consists of 3 parts.\n...\nSOR102 is Safe and Well-Tolerated\nNo safety or tolerability concerns noted at single doses up to 3,645mg or multiple doses of 1,215mg BID\n-No serious adverse events\n-No clinically significant laboratory, ECG, vitals, or physical exam findings\n-No subjects discontinued \nPart 1: One TEAE considered related to study drug (Cohort #1)\nPart 2: No adverse events\nCohort / Dose\nEvent\nDuration\nSeverity\nCausality\nC1 / 135mg\nDiarrhea\n~17 hours\nMild\nProbably related\nC1 / 135mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~2.5 days\nModerate\nNot related\nC1 / 135mg\nFlatulence\n~12 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nHeadache\n~4 hours\nMild\nNot related\nC2 / Placebo\nCatheter site pain\n~5 hours\nModerate\nNot related\nC2 / 405mg\nDysmenorrhea\n~12 hours\nModerate\nNot related\nTEAE: treatment-emergent adverse event\n...\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\n...\nLow-level ADA positive signal observed in 2 subjects", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.99}, "jev_score": 0.9702, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "PowerPoint Presentation", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/DDW-2025-poster.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "Data are n (%), mean (SD), or median (min, max); FCAL=fecal calprotectin; CRP=C-reactive protein\n* One patient received adalimumab, and one patient received mirikizumab, both in clinical trials\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nADA titers were low in most patients and not associated with safety or decreased clinical response.\n...\nTable 2.\nTreatment Emergent Adverse Events\nPlacebo\n...\nSOR102 \n...\nSOR102 \n810mg BID (N=9)\nTotal\n(N=22)\nTotal Number of TEAEs\n5\n2\n6\n13\nNumber of patients with ≥1 TEAE\n(50)\n2 (29)\n5 (56)\n10 (46)\nNumber of patients with TEAEs \nleading to study discontinuation\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n2 (22)\n2 (9)\nNumber of patients with ≥1 SAE\n0 (0)\n0 (0)\n1 (11)1\n1 (5)\n...\n12 (55)\n1. Worsening of UC that led to hospitalization 2.\nAbdominal pain of mild severity\n...\nTreatment emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were reported in 57%, \n29%, and 50% of patients receiving SOR102 810mg BID, SOR102 \n810mg QD, or placebo, respectively (Table 2).\nMost were mild-to-\nmoderate in severity.\nOne AE (SOR102 810mg BID arm) was considered possibly related to SOR102 (abdominal pain of mild severity).\nOne unrelated serious AE of worsening of UC that led to hospitalization was observed in the SOR102 810mg BID arm.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-05-11", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.99}, "jev_score": 0.9603, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41242336/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** From Oct 9, 2023, to Oct 16, 2024, 157 participants were assessed for eligibility, of whom 93 were excluded and 64 were randomly assigned to either SOR102 (n=48) or placebo (n=16) across all three parts of the study.\nIn part 1, six participants were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 135 mg, six to receive SOR102 405 mg, six to receive SOR102 1215 mg, six to receive SOR102 3645 mg, and eight to receive placebo.\nIn part 2, eight participants were randomly assigned to receive SOR102 1215 mg twice-daily and two to receive placebo.\n...\nIn part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\n** The favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-19", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9633}, "jev_score": 0.9344, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Administration of SOR102 Delivers Anti-TNF/IL-23 ...", "url": "https://sorrisopharma.com/app/uploads/UEG-Week-2025-Poster-v4.pdf", "domain": "sorrisopharma.com", "snippet": "22 patients were randomized (ITT; Table \n1), 17 patients completed the study Per \nProtocol (QD n=6, BID n=5, Placebo n=6).\nReasons for discontinuation: worsening of \nUC (n=2), study schedule non-compliance \n(n=2) and consent withdrawal (n=1).\n...\nNo clinically significant changes in safety\nlaboratory \nparameters, \ndose-limiting \ntoxicities, \nor \nevents \nsuggestive \nof \npronounced immunosuppression\n•\nAll but one TEAE were mild to moderate\n•\n1 severe and serious TEAE of worsening \nUC requiring hospitalization was not \nconsidered related to treatment.\nThis\nTEAE occurred during follow-up in a \nSOR102 BID patient\n...\nSOR102 was safe and well tolerated.\n...\nSOR102 BID demonstrated strong and consistent activity across multiple clinical and tissue PD endpoints in patients with UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.96}, "jev_score": 0.9312, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Safely targeting combination TNF and IL-23 using a gut-restricted ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13263963/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overall, SOR102 was well tolerated in both healthy participants and patients with UC, with a low incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), most of which were mild to moderate in severity.\nIn part 1, TEAEs were reported in 3/24 (13%) participants receiving SOR102 compared with 2/8 (25%) receiving placebo.\nNo TEAEs were observed in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in 7/16 (44%) patients treated with SOR102 and 3/6 (50%) patients receiving placebo.\nOnly 1 severe TEAE was reported, a flare of UC requiring hospitalization, which was not considered related to the study drug.\nImportantly, no clinically meaningful effects of SOR102 were observed on electrocardiograms, vital signs, physical examination findings, or safety laboratory parameters at any dose or dosing frequency evaluated.\n...\nIn this early-phase study, anti-drug antibodies (ADAs) were detected in 2/8 (25%) subjects in part 2 and 6/16 (38%) patients in part 3.\n...\nIn summary, SOR102 demonstrated a favorable safety and tolerability profile across early-phase evaluation, with no signal of systemic toxicity or immunogenicity.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9667}, "jev_score": 0.928, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "gutflix.eu › search › dIMPROVEMENT IN INFLAMMATORY BIOMARKER LEVELS THROUGH WEEK 12 ...", "url": "https://gutflix.eu/search/d/53f9ac8e-743b-11ee-bede-0242ac140004", "domain": "gutflix.eu", "snippet": "All 42 enrolled participants completed the study.\nSeven adverse events (AE) were observed, all occurring in Part 1.\nAE severity was mild or moderate.\nThere was one treatment-related AE (loose stools) of mild intensity and 17 hours duration occurring in the lowest dose cohort.\n...\nSOR102 was well tolerated at all tested doses.\nSystemic exposure of SOR102 and its monomers was not observed, supporting its gut selectivity.\n...\nClinical remission rates at Wk48 were 77.0–83.5% among Wk12 GUS induction clinical responders and 55.6–58.3% among pts not in clinical response at Wk12, and PRO-2 remission rates were 71.3–75.8% and 46.7–49.0%, respectively (**Table**).", "date": "2023-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9467}, "jev_score": 0.9088, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Sorriso Pharmaceuticals, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/91d3bdda199f847ec2b338400a1a78ff", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In part 1, treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) occurred in three (13%) of 24 participants who received SOR102 (two in the SOR102 135 mg dose group and one in the SOR102 405 mg dose group; mild diarrhoea and moderate dysmenorrhoea both in a single participant in the SOR102 135 mg group and mild flatulence in a second participant in same group;\nmild dysmenorrhoea in one participant in the SOR102 405 mg group) compared with two (25%) of eight participants who received placebo (mild catheter site pain; mild headache).\nOnly the TEAE of mild diarrhoea was deemed treatment-related.\nNo TEAEs occurred in part 2.\nIn part 3, at least one TEAE occurred in seven (44%) of 16 patients who received SOR102 (five in the SOR102 810 mg twice-daily dose group and two in the SOR102 810 mg once-daily dose group) and three (50%) of six patients who received placebo.\nTwo patients in the SOR102 twice-daily group had TEAEs of worsening of ulcerative colitis (one of which was severe [the only serious TEAE reported] but not deemed treatment related), leading to study discontinuation.\nAll other TEAEs were mild to moderate in nature.\nNo clinically significant effect of SOR102 on electrocardiogram, vital signs, physical examination, or laboratory parameters was observed at any dose, or frequency of dosing, in any part of the study, and no deaths occurred.\n...\nThree (43%) patients in the once-daily group, five (56%) in the twice-daily group, and none in the placebo group achieved symptomatic remission by day 42.\n...\nKey Highlights:\n...\n• Safety and Tolerability: SOR102 was dosed orally and was safe and well-tolerated in both healthy participants and UC patients.\nNo unexpected adverse events were observed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.9467}, "jev_score": 0.8994, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Interleukin-23 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13212390/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The most reported adverse events were nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infections, headache, and injection-site reactions, which were generally mild to moderate and comparable to placebo rates.\nSerious adverse events and treatment discontinuations were infrequent, and no clear dose-related safety signals emerged.\nRates of serious infections remained low, with no meaningful increase in opportunistic infections or tuberculosis reactivation observed across programs.\n...\nSevere adverse events were reported in only 15 patients (5.2%), mainly related to SARS-CoV-2 infections or UC worsening.\n...\nEven more interestingly, SOR102 is an orally administered bispecific antibody that targets both TNFα and IL-23 [65].\nIn a phase I trial, the drug was administered to 48 subjects including healthy individuals and UC patients.\nThe drug showed a favorable safety profile, with mild diarrhea and dysmenorrhea being the main side effects.\nTwo of the seven patients with UC experienced a disease worsening.\n...\nSafety and pharmacokinetics of SOR102, an oral bispecific inhibitor of TNF and interleukin-23 in healthy participants and patients with ulcerative colitis: a first-in-human, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, phase 1 trial.", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.9267}, "jev_score": 0.8804, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "SOR102 / Sorriso Pharma - Gastroenterology", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=f4d4f2dd-16fc-4e98-b7ec-f3a34272a168", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "(PubMed, Transl Gastroenterol Hepatol)\n...\n- P1 | \"The favourable safety profile and preliminary evidence for efficacy support development of SOR102 for treatment of ulcerative colitis.\"\n...\n| \"Safety and Tolerability: : SOR102 exhibited a favorable safety profile, with adverse events predominantly mild to moderate in severity; no new safety signals were observed....Efficacy: In the intent-to-treat (ITT) analysis, Mayo Score and modified Mayo Score clinical response were 56% and 67% in the high-dose SOR102 group compared to 17% and 33% in the placebo group, respectively....", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.89}, "jev_score": 0.8722, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Journal Scan_Jan 2026", "url": "https://www.ibdenc.org/journalscan-jan2026", "domain": "ibdenc.org", "snippet": "SOR102 is an oral bispecific antibody targeting TNF and IL-23p19 developed for ulcer ative colitis.\nThis phase 1, double blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluated its safety, pharmacokinetics, and preliminary efficacy in healthy participants and patients with active ulcerative colitis.\nAcross single and multiple ascending doses, SOR102 was generally well tolerated, with mostly mild to moderate adverse events and no clinically significant safety signals.\nIn patients with ulcerative colitis treated for 42 days, clinical responses were more frequent with SOR102 than placebo, particularly with twice-daily dosing.\nSymptomatic remission was achieved in over half of patients receiving twice-daily SOR102, while no placebo-treated patients reached remission.\nAlthough one severe disease exacerbation occurred, it was not considered treatment related.\nOverall, the favourable safety profile and early signs of clinical bene fit support further clinical development of SOR102 as a potential oral therapy for ulcerative colitis.\n...\nNo significant differences were observed in corticosteroid-free remission, clinical response, hospitalization, surgery, adverse events, or loss of response.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-04", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8633}, "jev_score": 0.8201, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2838.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:10+00:00"}
