{"case_id": "q009-1", "row_id": "q009", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease muscarinic competitors", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "search_latency_ms": 473.6, "target": "drugs competing with Dicyclomine: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 360.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9114, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000383, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6904, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Phase II-(VI) Antispasmodic & antiulcer drugs (cyclopentolate ...", "url": "https://api.upums.ac.in/pdfs/doc-1712402525891-163237152.pdf", "domain": "api.upums.ac.in", "snippet": "Antispasmodics can be classified in three major subclasses: \n1. Anticholinergic / antimuscarinic agents: Examples are \n• Dicyclomine Hydrochloride \n• Hyoscyamine Sulfate \n• Cimetropium Bromide \n• Otilanium Bromide\n• Octylonium Bromide \n• Prifinium Bromide \n• Darifenacin \n...\nIn the United States the antispasmodics dicyclomimne and hyoscyamine are available for inflammatory bowel disease (IBS).\ncymetropium bromide is a muscarinic compound with strong antispasmodic activity.\n...\nPropantheline bromide (PRO-BANTHINE) is a widely used synthetic nonselective muscarinic receptor antagonist.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-07", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.7267}, "jev_score": 0.6904, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "An evidence-based review of treatment options for irritable bowel ...", "url": "https://www.managedhealthcareexecutive.com/view/evidence-based-review-treatment-options-irritable-bowel-syndrome", "domain": "managedhealthcareexecutive.com", "snippet": "** Antispasmodics.\n** Hyoscyamine and dicyclomine are the only 2 antispasmodic agents available in the United States.\nHyoscyamine is the levo-isomer of atropine, a belladonna alkaloid that acts by competitively and reversibly inhibiting the acetylcholine receptor on smooth muscle, inhibiting contractions and GI motility.\n...\nDicyclomine is a weak muscarinic receptor antagonist with nonspecific direct spasmolytic effects on the GI tract.", "date": "2024-05-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.6318, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pain management in patients with inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3437534/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Since one of the cardinal features of intestinal inflammation is abdominal pain, inducing remission using standard of care IBD treatments (e.g. aminosalicylates, antibiotics, corticosteroids, enteral therapy, immunomodulators, and biological response modifiers) can alleviate inflammation-related pain in most patients.\n...\nAntispasmodics have some utility in providing a bridge of pain relief as remission is induced and aiding with pain if standard medical therapies are unable to control inflammation.\nAs pain from inflammation or partial obstruction usually manifests with intestinal spasms, anticholinergic antispasmodic medications are frequently employed as analgesic agents.\n...\nOverall antispasmodics were found to be more efficacious than placebo for functional abdominal pain.\nOf the medications studied by their group, hyoscine (hyoscyamine) was found to be the most efficacious and is the only one available in US markets.\nPractically speaking, the antispasmodics used most frequently are hyoscyamine and dicyclomine.\nUnfortunately, though antispasmodics seem to be a reasonable choice for pain management associated with inflammation or partial obstruction, there is no definitive evidence supporting their usage in IBD [Makharia, 2011].\nFurthermore, one must be wary of potential side effects, including fulminant obstruction in an already partially obstructed gut or worsening dysmotility in a gut that is already prone to motility problems secondary to inflammation and its sequelae.\n...\nIn addition to standard IBD-specific treatments, medical therapy for pain arising from inflammation or strictures/adhesions could include antispasmodics [i.e. hyoscyamine (hyoscine), dicyclomine (dicycloverine)] as needed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.6231, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Antispasmodics for Chronic Abdominal Pain: Analysis of North ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8315189/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents inhibit GI smooth muscle contraction, in part, by blocking calcium transport through calcium channels (31); furthermore, these agents decrease colonic motility (32).\n...\n##### Dicyclomine.\nIn 2 randomized, placebo-controlled studies, dicyclomine improved symptoms of IBS relative to\nplacebo (Table 2) (18,43–49).\nOne study reported no difference in adverse event (AE) rates with dicyclomine vs placebo (43), whereas the other reported that AEs occurred in a greater percentage of patients (69%) receiving dicyclomine 160 mg/d continuously for 2 weeks vs patients receiving placebo (16%; Table 2) (18).\n...\nHyoscine, also known as scopolamine, is an anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agent and smooth muscle relaxant (20).\nIn 3 studies, hyoscine taken for a duration of 4 weeks to 3 months was more efficacious than placebo at improving IBS symptoms (Table 2) (44–46).\n...\nDicyclomine, hyoscine, and hyoscyamine are anticholinergic/antimuscarinic agents available in the United States.\nAlthough placebo-controlled efficacy and safety data related to the use of these antispasmodics in patients with IBS seem favorable, the studies of dicyclomine (18,43) and hyoscine (44–47) identified in this review were published in 1990 or earlier and used different doses, treatment durations, and outcome assessments.\n...\nIn summary, data supporting the use of antispasmodics for the treatment of chronic abdominal pain in patients with DGBI, including IBS and FD, are limited.\nLimited sample size, short duration of therapy, heterogeneity in outcomes, and concerns over potential bias with study design make it difficult to recommend these agents for clinical use, especially when compared with the data sets available from large, randomized, controlled trials that characterize the current US FDA-approved IBS medications.", "date": "2021-05-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6667}, "jev_score": 0.6067, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacologic Agents for Chronic Diarrhea - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4641856/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Those with anticholinergic/antimuscarinic properties include butylscopolamine, hyoscine, cimetropium bromide, pirenzepine, dicyclomine, and prifinium bromide.37,38 Anticholinergics reduce motility and secretion by blocking the binding of the neurotransmitter acetylcholine to its receptor.\nThese agents can act on other body organs through muscarinic receptors, resulting in adverse effects such as dry mouth, tachycardia, and impaired vision.\n...\nSide effects of dry mouth, nausea, and dizziness can occur due to binding to muscarinic receptors.", "date": "2015-10-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.5963, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Recommendations and Evidence - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594791/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In contrast, therapies like secretagogues for IBS-C and eluxadoline or rifaximin for IBS-D are subtype-specific.\n...\n|Dicyclomine, hyoscyamine|Antispasmodics|—|Conditional recommendation; low quality of evidence|Conditional recommendation, low certainty in evidence|—|Weak recommendation; very low quality of evidence|—|Weak recommendation; low level of evidence *|—|\n...\nPharmacologic antispasmodics available in the US include dicyclomine, hyoscyamine, and hyoscine.\n...\nThe ACG and BSG provide a strong recommendation for guanylate cyclase-C receptor agonists, such as linaclotide and plecanatide, for the treatment of IBS-C, supported by a high quality of evidence.\n...\nLoperamide, a peripheral mu-opioid receptor agonist, is commonly used for managing IBS-D.\nIt slows GI motility through decreased peristalsis and an anti-secretory effect [90].", "date": "2024-11-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.6367}, "jev_score": 0.5476, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Drugs Used in Gastrointestinal Disorders - Katzung & Trevor's ...", "url": "https://doctorlib.org/pharmacology/pharmacology-examination-board-review/59.html", "domain": "doctorlib.org", "snippet": "The anticholinergic drugs\n**dicyclomine** and **hyoscyamine** are used as antispasmodics to relieve abdominal pain; however, their efficacy has not been convincingly demonstrated.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-01", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.5433}, "jev_score": 0.4727, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Subclassification of atrial and intestinal muscarinic receptors of the rat--direct binding studies with agonists and antagonists", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1854507/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "## Abstract\n...\n3. Affinity differences of the cardioselective antagonists himbacine, AF-DX 116 and methoctramine and the M1-selective antagonist dicyclomine for right and left atrial muscarinic receptors were also minimal (less than 2 fold selective).\nWhen compared to intestinal receptors, AF-DX 116 was 3 to 4 fold, methoctramine 10 to 13 fold selective and himbacine and dicyclomine non-selective.\n4. These data provide evidence for differences between atrial and intestinal but not between right and left atrial muscarinic receptors.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-07-20", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.4867}, "jev_score": 0.3748, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacology", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "- Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor M1Antagonist\n...\n**Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS.\n...\nDicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines.\n...\nDicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine.\nDicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum.\n...\nAlcohol may increase drowsiness caused by dicyclomine.", "date": "2025-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.41}, "jev_score": 0.3485, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Summary Product Characteristics (SPC)", "url": "https://products.pharmacyboardkenya.org/uploads/de_spas_.pdf", "domain": "products.pharmacyboardkenya.org", "snippet": "DE-SPAS is indicated for the relief of pain, inflammation and smooth muscle spasm associated with intestinal colic, acute renal colic, biliary colic, ureteric colic and\ndysmenorrhoea.\n4.2 Posology and Method of Administration  \n...\nAvoid in patients with glaucoma, inflammatory bowel disease and jaundice, myasthenia \ngravis.\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\nAnimal studies\nindicate that this action is achieved via a dual mechanism:   \n(1) a specific anticholinergic effect (antimuscarinic) at the acetylcholine-receptor sites \nwith approximately 1/8 the milligram potency of atropine (in vitro, guinea pig ileum); \nand (2) a direct effect upon smooth muscle (musculotropic) as evidenced by\nDicyclomine’s antagonism of bradykinin- and histamine-induced spasms of the \nisolated guinea pig ileum.\n...\nAnimal studies indicate that this effect is mediated by the binding of acetylcholine to its muscarinic type of receptors situated in various organs of the abdominal and pelvic viscera.\nAnimal studies do indicate that \nthis action of acetylcholine can be blocked by muscarinic blocking agents like atropine.\n...\nOf these various types, muscarinic receptors of type 1 (M1) is said to be involved in the spasm of the smooth muscles of the abdomen and pelvis.\nMuscarinic agonists such as acetylcholine liberate\n...\nHence, blockade of the receptors of M1 type would be useful in the treatment of disorders associated with altered smooth muscle contractility or tone (Wallis 1995).\nAn in vitro study carried out to assess the antispasmodic effect of Dicyclomine found \nDicyclomine to produce competitive inhibition of acetylcholine induced contraction of the smooth muscles (Khanna et al 1979).\nGiachetti and co workers while studying the selectivity profile of various muscarinic receptor antagonists found Dicyclomine to have\nthe highest affinity for M1 muscarinic receptor subtype (Giachetti et al 1986).\nDicyclomine displays low affinity for cardiac muscarinic receptors as well as glandular muscarinic receptors.\nDicyclomine displayed a high affinity for M1 receptor in the smooth muscles of the abdomen and distinguishes between the various muscarinic\nreceptors (Klibinger and Stein 1988).\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm not only through blockade of muscarinic receptors but also through a direct effect upon the smooth muscle as evidenced by\nDicyclomine antagonism of histamine and bradykinin induced spasm of the guinea ileum.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-04", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.3967}, "jev_score": 0.3174, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 834.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:12+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q008-1", "row_id": "q008", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "query": "LMN-201 TcdB antitoxin competitors Phase 2 clinical", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "search_latency_ms": 7025.6, "target": "drugs competing with LMN-201: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 343.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10543, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000443, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6705, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "The Urgent Threat of Clostridioides difficile Infection - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9953237/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Many possible drugs of the future for CDI, with diverse mechanisms of action, are in development in the form of microbiota-modulating agents (e.g., ADS024, CP101, RBX2660, RBX7455, SYN-004, SER-109, VE303, DAV132, MET-2\n, and BB128), small molecules (e.g., ridinilazole, ibezapolstat, CRS3123, DNV3837, MGB-BP-3, alanyl-L-glutamine, and TNP-2198), antibodies (e.g., IM-01 and LMN-201), and non-toxic strains of CD (e.g., NTCD-M3).\n...\nThe clinical phase II trial (NCT03788434) of VE303 for the prevention of r-CDI has been completed [72,73].\n...\n#### 4.3.2. LMN-201\nLumen Bioscience is developing LMN-201, the first-in-class orally bioavailable and highly potent therapeutic monoclonal antibody cocktail against CDI.\nLMN-201 comprises three antibody-like proteins (to neutralize CD toxins such as toxin B of CDI) and one lysozyme-like enzymatic protein (which destroys the cell wall of CD) [107,108].\nThis cocktail is believed to be more potent than single antibodies and the existing antibody treatments for CDI [108].\nThe interventional phase I clinical trial (NCT04893239) was completed in February 2022.\nA phase II/III clinical trial (NCT05330182) related to the prevention of r-CDI with LMN-201 has also been started [107,108].\nThe enteric-coated capsule of LMN-201 consists of a cocktail of proteins in the edible, dried, and non-viable biomass of spirulina (*Arthrospira platensis*) [107].\n**US2021338751A1** (Lumen Bioscience) claims a non-parenterally delivered composition of recombinant spirulina with two or more proteins that bind to a Clostridium toxin (toxin B) and a lysine [109].\nThis was validated in a completed phase I clinical trial that demonstrated strong gastric protease stability, and it is currently in a phase II clinical trial (NCT05330182).\n...\n|**LMN-201**|Lumen Bioscience|Neutralizes CD toxins and destroys the cell wall of CD|Phase II/III|\n|**NTCD-M3**|Destiny Pharma|A non-toxic strain of CD that makes a “ground cover” in the colon|Phase III|\n...\nThere are some drugs (e.g., LMN-201, IM-01, and fidaxomicin) that neutralize CD toxins.\nHowever, the data are unclear about their efficacy against the binary toxin produced by CD.\nThis fact necessitates the development of drugs that can neutralize binary toxins of CD.", "date": "2023-02-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-13", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7133}, "jev_score": 0.6705, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium difficile Infections Pipeline Analysis - GlobeNewswire", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2023/05/03/2660710/0/en/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-analysis-demonstrates-18-key-companies-at-the-horizon-expected-to-transform-the-treatment-paradigm-assesses-delveinsight.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "- Promising *Clostridium difficile* infections pipeline therapies such as **SER 109, Ibezapolstat, ADS 024, Ridinilazole, RBX7455, Full Spectrum Microbiota, MBK-01, MGB-BP-3, CRS3123, VE303, DNV 3837, LMN 201, IM-01, DNV3837, VP20621, ** and others are under different phases of *Clostridium difficile* infections clinical trials.\n...\n- **In ** ** April 2022, Lumen Bioscience** announced clinical development progress for **LMN-201**, an investigational orally delivered cocktail to treat and prevent *C. difficile* infection (CDI).\nAchievements included successful completion of a Phase 1 first-in-human study, which validated drug delivery of enteric capsules into the gut.\n- **In April 2022, Vedanta Biosciences** announced the publication in the journal **Cell Host & Microbe** of the results from a Phase 1a/1b study evaluating the safety, tolerability, and colonization dynamics of **VE303 ** in healthy adults.\n...\n- **In** ** December 2021, Summit Therapeutics ** announced topline results for the Phase III Ri-CoDIFy study evaluating its investigational drug, ridinilazole, for the treatment of and Sustained Clinical Response (SCR) for patients suffering from *C. difficile * infection (*C. diff. * infection or CDI).", "date": "2023-05-03", "last_updated": "2026-05-11", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.627, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline 2026: FDA Updates,", "url": "https://www.openpr.com/news/4584063/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-2026-fda-updates", "domain": "openpr.com", "snippet": "Replidyne, and others, are developing therapies for the Clostridium Difficile Infections treatment• Emerging Clostridium Difficile Infections therapies in the different phases of clinical trials are- ADS024, DNV3837, Ibezapolstat, LMN-201, Ridinilazole, CP101, SER 109, VE303, MGB-BP-3, CRS3123, and others are expected to have a significant impact on the Clostridium Difficile Infections market in the coming years.\n...\n• In April 2026,Acurx Pharmaceuticals announced results from the Phase II IBZ-PATHFINDER study, an interventional, open-label, single-arm clinical trial evaluating oral Ibezapolstat for the treatment and reduction of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection in patients experiencing multiple recurrent infections.• In February 2026, Lumen Bioscience announced results from a Phase II randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial evaluating LMN-201 for the prevention of recurrent Clostridioides difficile Infection.\n...\nThe main goal of the study is to evaluate safety and tolerability, while secondary efficacy measures will assess mortality, symptom improvement, and recurrence rates.• In April 2025, Lumen Bioscience shared top-line findings from the sentinel cohort of its RePreve Clinical Trial assessing LMN-201 for Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI).\nLMN-201 is an oral biologic combination therapy, provided in capsule form, designed to be administered alongside and after antibiotics to enhance clinical outcomes in CDI.\n...\n22+ products under different phases of clinical development like• Late-stage products (Phase III)• Mid-stage products (Phase II)• Early-stage product (Phase I)• Pre-clinical and Discovery stage candidates• Discontinued & Inactive candidates• Route of AdministrationFurther Clostridium Difficile Infections product details are provided in the report.", "date": "2026-07-22", "last_updated": "2026-07-24", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6633}, "jev_score": 0.6036, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Using antibody synergy to engineer a high potency biologic cocktail against C. difficile", "url": "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.12.21.473715v2", "domain": "biorxiv.org", "snippet": "We applied a mathematical framework originally used to model the effects of multiple inhibitors on enzyme activity to guide the development a therapeutic antibody cocktail, LMN-201, to prevent and treat\n*C. difficile* infection (CDI).\n...\nDue to synergy the LMN-201 antibody cocktail, which targets the *C. difficile* exotoxin B (TcdB), is 300- to 3000-fold more potent at neutralizing the most clinically prevalent TcdB toxin types than bezlotoxumab, the only monoclonal antibody currently approved for treatment or prevention of CDI.\nThe efficacy of LMN-201 is further enhanced by inclusion of a phage-derived endolysin that destroys the C. difficile bacterium, and which therefore has a complementary mechanism of action to the antibody cocktail.", "date": "2022-01-14", "last_updated": "2024-11-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.6933}, "jev_score": 0.6032, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "The Urgent Threat of Clostridioides difficile Infection: A Glimpse of the Drugs of the Future, with Related Patents and Prospects", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9059/11/2/426", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "#### 4.3.2. LMN-201\n**US2021338751A1**(Lumen Bioscience) claims a non-parenterally delivered composition of recombinant spirulina with two or more proteins that bind to a Clostridium toxin (toxin B) and a lysine [109].\nThis was validated in a completed phase I clinical trial that demonstrated strong gastric protease stability, and it is currently in a phase II clinical trial (NCT05330182).\n...\n|US2018100009A1 (Vhsquared)|A polypeptide-based immunoglobulin that binds and neutralizes CD toxin A [44].|", "date": "2023-02-01", "last_updated": "2024-12-22", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.582, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/02/lmn-201-achieves-100-initial-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort-of-re-preve-trial", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial - Lumen Bioscience\n# LMN-201 Achieves 100% Initial C. difficile Clinical Cure in Preliminary Cohort of RePreve Trial\n...\nStrong potential to disrupt market for treatment of C. diff infections.\nRePreve Trial main cohort now recruiting.\n**Seattle, WA – April 3, 2025** – In a landmark achievement for its pioneering spirulina-based drug development platform, Lumen announced today top-line results from the sentinel cohort of its RePreve Clinical Trial evaluating LMN-201 for Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI).\nLMN-201 is an oral biologic cocktail, delivered in capsules, intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C. difficile infection.\n...\n**–100% Initial Clinical Cure:** 21 patients completed 7 days of LMN-201 plus standard of care (SoC) antibiotics (metronidazole, vancomycin, or fidaxomicin).\nAll patients (21/21; 95% confidence interval (CI) 85%-100%) achieved initial clinical resolution, significantly outperforming (p<<0.001) the 80% initial clinical cure rate (625/781; 95% CI 77-83%) observed in the active arm of the large MODIFYI and II studies.\nThe RePreve Trial was designed to facilitate comparisons to the MODIFY trial, which targeted the same patient population with the same SoC antibiotics combined with the IV-infused monoclonal antibody, bezlotoxumab.\n...\nSustained cure rates through Week 4 for SoC alone (MODIFY I/II), SoC plus LMN-201 (RePreve sentinel cohort) and SoC plus bezlotoxumab (MODIFY I/II) were 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56-63%), 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77-100%) and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66-72%), respectively (see enclosed figure).\nThe improvement in sustained cure through Week 4 between LMN-201 and SoC was 35.7% (p<<0.001) and between LMN-201 and bezlotoxumab was 26.3% (p<0.001).\n**-Marked CDI Recurrence Reduction:** Only 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI 0.24-23%) completing seven days of SoC plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, versus 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI 23-30%) recurrence observed after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II and 87 of 625 (14%; 95% CI 11-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II in the same time period.\n...\n**–LMN-201 Appears to be Safe and Well-Tolerated:** No dose-related severe or serious adverse events were reported.\nNotably, these sentinel cohort results were achieved with only 7 days of dosing.\nIn the RePreve Trial’s main cohort (Part B), the LMN-201 dosing window will increase from 7 days to ~70 days (until 8 weeks after initial clinical cure).\n...\nIt suggests that LMN-201’s tandem mechanism – targeting both the C. diff bacterium and its toxin – can improve outcomes, as it did in preclinical studies.”\n...\nWe can now proceed to the placebo-controlled phase with a solid foundation of safety and an encouraging preliminary efficacy signal.\nWe look forward to confirming these results in the larger trial, which is recruiting now.”\n...\nThe RePreve Trial has a two-part design.\nEnrollment criteria are the same in both cohorts, and, to facilitate comparison, they are nearly identical to those used in the MODIFY trial for bezlotoxumab.\n**Part A** (Sentinel Cohort) was designed to confirm LMN-201’s safety in high-risk individuals with CDI, and to allow Lumen to optimize the trial infrastructure and patient recruiting.\nTwenty-one CDI patients on standard antibiotic therapy were enrolled.\nAll participants in this open-label cohort received LMN-201 in addition to antibiotics, starting within 7 days of diagnosis.\nWith the sentinel cohort successfully completed, Lumen has begun Part B (main cohort).\nThis pivotal portion is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S. Participants are randomized 1:1 to receive either LMN-201 or placebo—in each case alongside standard antibiotic therapy—to rigorously evaluate the drug’s efficacy in a larger population.\nThe primary endpoint is “sustained clinical cure” (initial clinical cure plus no recurrence at 12 weeks) relative to placebo.\n...\n**LMN-201 is orally delivered (capsules; no enema or “bowel cleanse” required) and highly scalable, allowing for much broader potential use in routine CDI management.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.4902, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline 2025: Latest FDA ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/press-releases/4646010/clostridium-difficile-infections-pipeline-2025-latest-fda-approvals-clinical-trials-and-emerging-therapies", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "As per DelveInsight’s latest evaluation, the global Clostridium Difficile Infections therapeutic pipeline includes 20+ active companies involved in advancing 22+ CDI treatment candidates, covering clinical trials, therapeutic approaches, mechanisms of action, routes of administration, and key ongoing developments.\n...\nSome Key Highlights from the Clostridium Difficile Infections Pipeline Report\n...\n- Key players developing CDI therapies include Adiso Therapeutics, Deinove, Acurx Pharmaceuticals, Lumen Bioscience, Summit Therapeutics, Finch Therapeutics, Seres Therapeutics, Vedanta Biosciences, MGB Biopharma, Replidyne, and others.\n- Leading emerging CDI therapies in various trial phases include ADS024, DNV3837, Ibezapolstat, LMN-201, Ridinilazole, CP101, SER-109, VE303, MGB-BP-3, CRS3123, and more—these candidates are projected to significantly influence the CDI treatment landscape.\n- April 2025: Lumen Bioscience announced top-line results from the sentinel cohort of the RePreve Clinical Trial evaluating LMN-201, an oral spirulina-based biologic designed to be used during and after antibiotic therapy.\n...\n- LMN-201 – Lumen Bioscience\n...\nDelveInsight’s CDI Pipeline Report evaluates 22+ drug candidates across:\n- Phase III (Late-stage)\n- Phase II (Mid-stage)\n- Phase I (Early-stage)\n- Preclinical and Discovery phases\n- Discontinued/Inactive candidates", "date": "2026-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5167}, "jev_score": 0.4289, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12849448/table/Tab1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Overview of endolysin-based products that are or have been evaluated in clinical trials, together with the developing company, the indication, combination strategy with antibiotics, clinical development stage with ClinicalTrials.gov identifier (if available), as well as trial status\n...\n|SAL200|SAL-1, Tonabacase, N-Rephasin|iNtRON Biotechnology|*S. aureus* bacteraemia|Yes|Phase II (NCT03089697)|Terminated Early|\n...\n|LMN-201|LHD|Lumen Bioscience|Recurrent *C. difficile* colitis|Yes|Phase II (NCT05330182)|Ongoing|\n...\nP128 combines a virion-associated peptidoglycan hydrolase from Phage K with the lysostaphin SH3b cell-wall binding domain and is not a true endolysin, but mechanistically similar.", "date": "2026-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.67, "evidence": 0.5867}, "jev_score": 0.3931, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Thematic Highlights of Antibacterial Development 2022-23: Part 6", "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/thematic-highlights-antibacterial-development-2022-23-neha-prasad-5e", "domain": "linkedin.com", "snippet": "Lastly, LMN-201 is a clinical-stage biologic cocktail, consisting of antibodies and a lysin, that targets recurrent C. difficile infections and is orally delivered through spirulina biomass that doesn’t require refrigeration.\nOn 4/18/22, Lumen Bioscience announced FDA clearance for a phase 2/3 trial of LMN-201 in preventing C. difficile recurrence, following a successful phase 1 study.\n...\nThe FDA accepted a phase 2 IND application for CAL02 as an adjunctive treatment for severe community-acquired bacterial pneumonia on 11/14/22, with enrollment expected to begin shortly.", "date": "2023-02-10", "last_updated": "2025-04-22", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.4467}, "jev_score": 0.3306, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience Releases Data on Potent, Orally ...", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lumen-bioscience-releases-data-on-potent-orally-delivered-biologic-drug-cocktail-for-preventing-c-difficile-infection-301450274.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "-- Lumen also announces successful Cohort 1 results in Phase 1 clinical trial\n...\nSEATTLE, Dec. 23, 2021 /PRNewswire/ -- Lumen Bioscience, a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company developing biologic drugs for highly prevalent diseases, today published research describing LMN-201, an investigational orally delivered biologic cocktail to prevent *C. difficile * infection (CDI).\nLMN-201 combines four therapeutic proteins—manufactured and delivered in the edible microorganism spirulina—that work synergistically to neutralize both the *C. difficile* bacterium and the toxin that causes its virulence.\n...\nThe company also announced completion of Cohort 1 of its Phase 1 pharmacokinetic clinical trial of LMN-201.\nThe primary goal is to evaluate the dissolution kinetics of enteric capsules designed to release LMN-201's therapeutic proteins at or before the terminal ileum, where *C. difficile* becomes established in most patients.\nThe trial met its primary endpoint for Cohort 1, indicating the capsules can successfully deliver LMN-201 where needed.\nFollowing completion of the study's confirmatory Cohort 2 early next year, full analysis will be completed and published in a peer-reviewed journal.\n...\nThe first class is comprised of three antibody-like proteins that bind and neutralize the bacterial toxin that is the main cause of diarrhea and other severe symptoms of CDI.\n...\nThe newly published research demonstrates that LMN-201 is highly effective at preventing CDI in animal models.\n*In vitro* analysis elucidates how the three antibody-like proteins in the product work synergistically: in combination they vastly exceed the ability of any individual protein to bind and neutralize the key *C. difficile* exotoxin TcdB, which prior research has shown to be the primary virulence factor in CDI.\nThis combination was 300- to 3,000-fold more potent at neutralizing the most clinically prevalent TcdB toxin types than the FDA-approved monoclonal antibody therapeutic bezlotoxumab.\n...\nManufactured in recombinant spirulina, LMN-201 is delivered orally as a powder of dry spirulina biomass and is easily scalable, shelf-stable, simple to administer, and broadly neutralizing against all common *C. difficile* variants.\n...\nThe open-label, exploratory trial underway in Australia is evaluating the delivery of LMN-201 via enteric capsules in the gut in healthy volunteers with mature ileostomies.\nData from the initial cohort of 6 volunteers show that the capsules dissolved at the clinically relevant point in the GI tract, meeting the trial's primary endpoint.\nInitial analysis also confirmed the therapeutic proteins were active upon release, meeting the exploratory endpoints.\nLumen is currently planning a Phase 2 trial to study the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of LMN-201 in preventing CDI recurrence.\nPhysicians interested in participating in the Phase 2 study are encouraged to contact Lumen's clinical operations team at [email protected].", "date": "2021-12-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.43}, "jev_score": 0.3268, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 7369.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q009-3", "row_id": "q009", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHRM1 CHRM3 CHRM4 CHRM5 Small Molecule", "query": "Dicyclomine inflammatory bowel disease clinical development", "anchors": ["Dicyclomine"], "search_latency_ms": 1468.4, "target": "drugs competing with Dicyclomine: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 509.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10274, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000432, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6541, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Managing Pain in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264972/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "When pain is associated with active IBD, the primary treatment will often be escalation of IBD therapy.\n...\n|Antispasmodics|Hyoscyamine XR|0.125–0.25 mg every 4–6 hours or 0.375–0.75 mg BID|1.5 mg/day|Constipation, dizziness, dry mouth, sedation, urinary retention|\n|Antispasmodics|Dicyclomine|20 mg QID|160 mg/day|Constipation, dizziness, dry mouth, sedation, urinary retention|\n...\nControlled trials of NSAIDs in IBD have been lacking.\nA small study showed a 20–30% increased risk of flares in patients who were started on NSAIDs compared to patients who were started on acetaminophen.^46^ All NSAID-treated patients experienced a flare within 9 days of starting the medication, but most were able to re-attain remission simply by stopping the NSAID.^46^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.7033}, "jev_score": 0.6541, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Ther Adv Gastroenterol", "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt1jq8w664/qt1jq8w664_noSplash_fdbe6351b18cc7bb8acc8d1f7d921485.pdf?t=sq66vr", "domain": "escholarship.org", "snippet": "Hyoscyamine [Drugs.\n...\nDicyclomine [US \nFood and Drug \nAdministration, 2013a]\nCommonly prescribed \nfor IBS [Trinkley and \nNahata, 2014]\nOften used for \npostprandial IBS \nsymptoms [Chang et al. \n2014]\nBlock muscarinic acetylcholine \nreceptors, causing smooth \nmuscle relaxation [Trinkley and \nNahata 2014]\nHyoscyamine: up to 1.5 \nmg/day\nDicyclomine: 20–40 mg \nPO QID\n[Trinkley and Nahata 2014]\nImproves global IBS symptoms and abdominal \npain\n[Chang et al. 2014]\nA Cochrane review shows improvement in \nabdominal pain was not clinically meaningful \n[Ruepert et al. 2011]\n...\nCommon AEs: constipation, dry \n...\nWith regard to treatment, gut-acting agents, such as antispasmodics like dicyclomine, which blunt the gastrocolonic reflex responsible for the postprandial urge to defecate, are appro- priate as first-line pharmacologic treatment in this case [Chey et al. 2015b].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-02-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6833}, "jev_score": 0.6491, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "efficacy and safety of hyoscyamine, dicyclomine, and desipramine in ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/crohnscolitis360/article/8/2/otag063/8711826", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "In patients with high bowel frequency, 235 treatment courses resulted in clinical improvement in 68.5% of patients receiving hyoscyamine ER, 65.0% of those receiving dicyclomine, and 63.5% of those receiving desipramine.\n...\nApproximately 60% of treatment courses persisted beyond one year.\nLong-term persistence (≥3 years) was highest with dicyclomine (48.7%), followed by desipramine (38.7%) and hyoscyamine ER (37.8%).\n...\nIn summary, antispasmodic agents and low-dose neuromodulators appear to be effective and generally well tolerated for the management of high bowel frequency and abdominal pain in patients with irritable pouch syndrome.", "date": "2026-04-22", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.6933}, "jev_score": 0.6448, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Pain management in patients with inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3437534/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Since one of the cardinal features of intestinal inflammation is abdominal pain, inducing remission using standard of care IBD treatments (e.g. aminosalicylates, antibiotics, corticosteroids, enteral therapy, immunomodulators, and biological response modifiers) can alleviate inflammation-related pain in most patients.\n...\nAntispasmodics have some utility in providing a bridge of pain relief as remission is induced and aiding with pain if standard medical therapies are unable to control inflammation.\n...\nOverall antispasmodics were found to be more efficacious than placebo for functional abdominal pain.\nOf the medications studied by their group, hyoscine (hyoscyamine) was found to be the most efficacious and is the only one available in US markets.\nPractically speaking, the antispasmodics used most frequently are hyoscyamine and dicyclomine.\nUnfortunately, though antispasmodics seem to be a reasonable choice for pain management associated with inflammation or partial obstruction, there is no definitive evidence supporting their usage in IBD [Makharia, 2011].\nFurthermore, one must be wary of potential side effects, including fulminant obstruction in an already partially obstructed gut or worsening dysmotility in a gut that is already prone to motility problems secondary to inflammation and its sequelae.\n...\nHowever, multiple studies have demonstrated that NSAIDs may increase the risk for IBD relapse [Kefelakes *et al*.\n2009; Cryer, 2005; Felder *et al*.\n...\nUnfortunately, COX-2 inhibitors have not been shown to be beneficial in terms of safety, toxicity, or even anti-inflammatory effect.\n...\nRegardless of the criticisms of COX-2 inhibitors, it is important to note there are no trials specifically investigating the utility of these medications for abdominal pain associated with IBD.\n...\nIn addition to standard IBD-specific treatments, medical therapy for pain arising from inflammation or strictures/adhesions could include antispasmodics [i.e. hyoscyamine (hyoscine), dicyclomine (dicycloverine)] as needed.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6567}, "jev_score": 0.6042, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine: Package Insert", "url": "https://www.drugs.com/pro/dicyclomine.html", "domain": "drugs.com", "snippet": "Initial U.S. Approval: 1950\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (1) (1)\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nThe recommended initial dose is 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon [ see Adverse Reactions (6.3)].\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis [ see Contraindications (4)].\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": "2026-07-19", "last_updated": "2026-07-19", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.4767}, "jev_score": 0.4052, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "www.accessdata.fda.gov › drugsatfda_docs › labelBENTYL (dicyclomine HCl, USP) U.S. Package Insert", "url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2007/007409s040,007961s026,008370s031lbl.pdf", "domain": "accessdata.fda.gov", "snippet": "BENTYL is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent available in the following \n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.  Animal studies indicate \nthat this action is achieved via a dual mechanism: (1) a specific anticholinergic effect \n(antimuscarinic) at the acetylcholine-receptor sites with approximately 1/8 the milligram potency\nof atropine (in vitro, guinea pig ileum); and (2) a direct effect upon smooth muscle (musculotropic) as evidenced by dicyclomine’s antagonism of bradykinin- and histamine-induced spasms of the\n...\nIn man, dicyclomine is rapidly absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak values within 60-\n90 minutes.  The principal route of elimination is via the urine (79.5% of the dose).  Excretion also \noccurs in the feces, but to a lesser extent (8.4%).  Mean half-life of plasma elimination in one \nstudy was determined to be approximately 1.8 hours when plasma concentrations were\nmeasured for 9 hours after a single dose.  In subsequent studies, plasma concentrations were \n...\nsomewhat longer half-life.  Mean volume of distribution for a 20 mg oral dose is approximately \n3.65 L/kg suggesting extensive distribution in tissues.\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of\n160 mg daily (40 mg q.i.d.) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% \ntreated with placebo  (p<.05).  In these trials most of the side effects were typically anticholinergic\nin nature (see table) and were reported by 61% of the patients. \n...\nNine percent (9%) of patients were discontinued from the drug because of one or more of these\nside effects (compared with 2% in the placebo group).  In 41% of the patients with side effects, \nside effects disappeared or were tolerated at the 160 mg daily dose without reduction.  A dose \nreduction from 160 mg daily to an average daily dose of 90 mg was required in 46% of the patients with side effects who then continued to experience a favorable clinical response; their side effects either disappeared or were tolerated. (See ADVERSE REACTIONS.)\nINDICATIONS AND USAGE \nFor the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome. \n...\nWith overdosage, a curare-like action may occur (i.e., neuromuscular blockade leading to muscular weakness and possible paralysis).\n...\nTreatment should consist of gastric lavage, emetics, and activated charcoal.  Sedatives (e.g., \nshort-acting barbiturates, benzodiazepines) may be used for management of overt signs of\nexcitement.  If indicated, an appropriate parenteral cholinergic agent may be used as an antidote. \n...\nAdults-Oral.  The only oral dose clearly shown to be effective is 160 mg per day (in 4 equally\ndivided doses).  Since this dose is associated with a significant incidence of side effects, it is \nprudent to begin with 80 mg per day (in 4 equally divided doses).  Depending upon the patient’s \nresponse during the first week of therapy, the dose should be increased to 160 mg per day unless\nside effects limit dosage escalation.  If efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects \nrequire doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.  Documented safety data ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.46}, "jev_score": 0.391, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/ ...", "url": "https://nctr-crs.fda.gov/fdalabel/services/spl/set-ids/40e21575-320f-2ac6-e054-00144ff88e88/spl-doc?hl", "domain": "nctr-crs.fda.gov", "snippet": "Initial U.S. Approval: 1950\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\nDicyclomine hydrochloride is an antispasmodic and anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent indicated for the treatment of functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome (\n...\nStarting dose: 20 mg four times a day.\nAfter a week treatment with the starting dose, the dose may be escalated to 40 mg four times a day, unless side effects limit dosage escalation\n...\nDocumented safety data are not available for doses above 80 mg daily for periods longer than 2 weeks.\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride needs to be used with caution in conditions characterized by tachyarrhythmia such as thyrotoxicosis, congestive heart failure and in cardiac surgery, where they may further accelerate the heart rate.\n...\nDicyclomine hydrochloride may produce drowsiness, dizziness or blurred vision.\n...\nToxic dilatation of intestine and intestinal perforation is possible when anticholinergic agents are administered in patients with Salmonella dysentery.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon\n[see\n...\n. Dicyclomine hydrochloride is contraindicated in patients with severe ulcerative colitis\n...\nThe most serious adverse reactions reported with dicyclomine hydrochloride include cardiovascular and central nervous system symptoms\n...\nThe most common adverse reactions (> 5% of patients) are dizziness, dry mouth, vision blurred, nausea, somnolence, asthenia and nervousness (\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.4433}, "jev_score": 0.3901, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmacology", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00804", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "## Overview\n...\n- A medication used to treat disorders affecting the large intestines.\n...\n- Clinical Trials\n- Phase 0\n- 0\n- Phase 1\n- 0\n- Phase 2\n- 0\n- Phase 3\n- 0\n- Phase 4\n- 0\n...\n**Dicyclomine**is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat IBS.\n...\nDicyclomine is a muscarinic M1, M3, and M2 receptor antagonist as well as a non-competitive inhibitor of histamine and bradykinin used to treat spasms of the intestines seen in functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\nThough it is commonly prescribed, its recommendation may have been based on a small amount of evidence and so its prescription is becoming less favourable.\nDicyclomine was granted FDA approval on 11 May 1950.\n...\nDicyclomine is indicated for the treatment of functional bowel disorder and irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nDicyclomine is an anticholinergic drug used to relax the smooth muscles of the intestines.\nIt's duration of action is not especially long as it is usually taken 4 times daily with individual doses of 20-40mg orally or 10-20mg by intramuscular injection.\nDicyclomine should not be administered intravenously.\n...\nDicyclomine achieves its action partially through direct antimuscarinic activity of the M1, M3, and M2 receptors; and partially through antagonism of bradykinin and histamine.\nDicyclomine non-competitively inhibits the action of bradykinin and histamine, resulting in direct action on the smooth muscle, and decreased strength of contractions seen in spasms of the ileum.\n...\nDicyclomine is 79.5% eliminated in the urine and 8.4% in the feces.\n...\nThe mean plasma elimination half life is approximately 1.8 hours.", "date": "2025-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-29", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.3833}, "jev_score": 0.3066, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Dicyclomine 20mg", "url": "https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=a330a08e-0cb6-bc66-e053-2995a90ad9e9&type=display", "domain": "dailymed.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dicyclomine hydrochloride tablets, USP are indicated for the treatment of patients with functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome.\n...\nAfter one week treatment with the initial dose, the dose may be increased to 40 mg four times a day unless side effects limit dosage escalation.\nIf efficacy is not achieved within 2 weeks or side effects require doses below 80 mg per day, the drug should be discontinued.\nDocumented safety data are not available for doses above 80 mg daily for periods longer than 2 weeks.\n...\nCaution should be taken in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nLarge doses may suppress intestinal motility to the point of producing a paralytic ileus and the use of this drug may precipitate or aggravate the serious complication of toxic megacolon\n...\nThe data described below reflect exposure in controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times a day).\n...\nIn 41% of the patients with side effects, side effects disappeared or were tolerated at the 160 mg daily dose without reduction.\n...\nDicyclomine relieves smooth muscle spasm of the gastrointestinal tract.\n...\nIn controlled clinical trials involving over 100 patients who received drug, 82% of patients treated for functional bowel/irritable bowel syndrome with dicyclomine hydrochloride at initial doses of 160 mg daily (40 mg four times daily) demonstrated a favorable clinical response compared with 55% treated with placebo (p<0.05).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.3433}, "jev_score": 0.2746, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The Impact of Antispasmodic Use on Abdominal Pain and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12116234/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Patients with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are often prescribed antispasmodics for chronic abdominal pain.\nLarge-scale data regarding efficacy and impact on clinical outcomes are lacking.\n...\nWe included 85,859 patients (median age 50; 53.8% female) with IBD; 5,661 used antispasmodics.\nOn follow up, those with antispasmodic use had higher rates of abdominal pain and opioid use (P < 0.001) regardless of baseline abdominal pain or opioid use.\nAfter matching, 5,629 patients remained per group.\nPatients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of abdominal pain at 1 month, regardless of baseline abdominal pain.\nOpioid-naïve patients who used antispasmodics had higher rates of opioid use at follow up (1.1% *vs*.\n0.2%; P < 0.001).\nLikelihood of corticosteroid use, clinic visits, ER visits and hospitalization were higher in those with antispasmodic use.\nNo differences in IBD-related complications, surgery or mortality were observed.\n...\nAntispasmodic use in patients with IBD was associated with increased abdominal pain and opioid use in opioid-naïve patients.\nAntispasmodic use was associated with increased likelihood of corticosteroid use, clinic and ER visits and hospitalization.\n...\nIn this large, population-based study, based on propensity-matched retrospective analyses of claims-based data from the TriNetX Diamond Network, IBD patients prescribed antispasmodics demonstrated increased likelihood of abdominal pain and opioid use after one month.\n...\nAntispasmodic medications, like dicyclomine, have been used to good effect in irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)^6, 7^.\n...\nAs such, these medications are recommended by the American Gastroenterological Association for patients with IBD and IBS or functional symptoms^11^.\n...\nHere, we employed a large commercial and government insurance and electronic medical record database to assess the efficacy of antispasmodics on abdominal pain and rates of opioid use in IBD, stratified by baseline abdominal pain and opioid use.\n...\nWe identified patients receiving an antispasmodic prescription (dicyclomine or hyoscyamine) with at least 30-day supply based on RxNorm codes (Supplementary Table1).\n...\nAntispasmodic use was not associated with improvement in abdominal pain or opioid use in patients with IBD.\nIn fact, the converse was observed even after controlling for metrics of IBD severity and baseline characteristics including opioid use and abdominal pain.\n...\nConsidering the findings of this study as a whole, there was a general lack of evidence indicating that antispasmodics help control abdominal pain in the setting of IBD.\n...\nThese data represent the first large scale assessment of the efficacy of antispasmodics in patients with IBD.", "date": "2025-04-11", "last_updated": "2026-07-19", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.52, "evidence": 0.3967}, "jev_score": 0.2063, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1978.2, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q010-1", "row_id": "q010", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication LY4395089, Eli Lilly & Company, Crohn''s Disease", "query": "LY4395089 mechanism target Crohn’s disease", "anchors": ["LY4395089"], "search_latency_ms": 951.4, "target": "drugs competing with LY4395089: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 463.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9555, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000401, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5992, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Multiple Drugs for Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c639c", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests new treatments for individuals with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease, conditions that cause inflammation in the digestive tract.\nThe focus is on evaluating the effectiveness and safety of these treatments for those with moderate to severe symptoms.\n...\nFor LY4395089, less information is available because it is still under testing and not yet approved by the FDA.\nThis trial is in an early stage (Phase 2), meaning researchers are still assessing its safety and effectiveness.\nWhile some safety data exist, further testing is necessary to confirm its safety.\n...\nResearchers are excited about these treatments for ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease because they offer new ways to tackle these conditions.\nUnlike standard treatments like corticosteroids and immunosuppressants, Mirikizumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets the p19 subunit of IL-23, potentially offering a more targeted approach to inflammation.\nThe co-administration of LY4395089 with Mirikizumab introduces an oral component to the treatment, which could enhance convenience and effectiveness.\nThis combination approach could lead to more personalized and efficient treatment, potentially improving patient outcomes and quality of life.\n...\nResearch has shown that mirikizumab, one of the treatments in this trial, effectively treats both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\nStudies indicate that about 63% of patients with ulcerative colitis experienced symptom improvement.\nFor Crohn's disease, mirikizumab helped over 80% of patients maintain relief from symptoms like the urgent need to use the bathroom.\n...\nEarly results suggest that the combination might be more effective, as LY4395089 outperformed a placebo in other studies.\n...\nThe study tests new drugs (LY4395089 and Mirikizumab given by IV or injection) in people with moderate to severe Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis.\nParticipants are randomly assigned to different treatment groups.\nThe study lasts at least 62 weeks.\n...\nLY4395089 is an investigational drug, meaning it is currently being studied in clinical trials and is not yet approved by the FDA for any ...", "date": "2024-07-03", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6733}, "jev_score": 0.5992, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Ly4395089 ir mirikizumab gydymo tyrimas suaugusiems su vidutinio ir sunkios veiklos Krono liga", "url": "https://klinikiniai-tyrimai.lt/tyrimas/tyrimas-kuriame-suaugusiems-pacientams-su-vidutiniskai-iki-sunkiai-aktyvia-crohn-liga-tiriama-ly4395089-ir-mirikizumabas/", "domain": "klinikiniai-tyrimai.lt", "snippet": "Studijoje tiriami suaugusieji, sergantieji **Ulcerative Colitis** arba **Crohn’s Disease**, kurie turi vidutinio arba sunkio aktyvumo simptomus.\nViena nagrinėjama medikamentų kombinacija apima intraveninį arba po odos injekcijos pavidalu taikomą **Mirikizumab** ir tabletės pavidalu vartojamą **LY4395089**.\nŠie vaistai yra skiriami kartu arba atskirai, siekiant pagerinti žarnyno būklę.\n...\n## Kas gali dalyvauti tyrime?\n- Turite turėti patvirtintą **Ulceratyvios kolitų (UC)** arba **Krono ligos (CD)** diagnozę, kuri galioja ne trumpiau kaip 3 mėnesius, ir tai turi būti patvirtinta gydytojo, atliktos endoskopijos (virškinimo trakto vidinės apžiūros) bei audinių tyrimo (histopatologijos) rezultatais.\n...\n- CD atveju – **Krono ligos aktyvumo indeksas (CDAI)** tarp 220 ir 450 taškų, ir endoskopinis balas (**SES‑CD**) ≥6, jei liga yra žarnyno ir storosios žarnos srityje, arba ≥4, jei liga tik žarnyne; tyrimas turi būti atliktas per 21 dieną iki atsitiktinio paskyrimo.\n...\n**Mirikizumab** – tai žmogaus kūne gaminama baltyminė medžiaga (monokloninis antikūnas), kuri skiriama į veną arba po odą kaip tirpalas injekcijai.\nJi veikia slopindama uždegimo procesą žarnyne, todėl tyrime ji naudojama siekiant pagerinti simptomus ir žarnyno pažeidimo rodiklius suaugusiems pacientams, sergantiems vidutiniškai iki sunkiai aktyviu ulceraciniu kolitu arba Krono liga.\n**LY4395089** – tai eksperimentinė medžiaga, pateikiama kaip tabletė, kurią pacientas geria per burną.\nŠi priemonė tyrime nagrinėjama kartu su **Mirikizumab**, siekiant įvertinti, ar jų derinimas gali dar geriau sumažinti uždegimą ir pagerinti žarnyno būklę sergant vidutiniškai iki sunkiai aktyviu Krono liga.", "date": "2025-09-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.65}, "jev_score": 0.585, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to ...", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/trial/706080", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and mirikizumab (LY3074828)] given together can help treat Crohn’s disease (CD01 study).", "date": "2026-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6333}, "jev_score": 0.5763, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Lilly Crohn's and Ulcerative Colitis Clinical Trials | IBD Research", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/research-areas/immunology/crohns-ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "Experts believe that, in some people, normal bacteria in the digestive tract may cause the immune system to attack healthy normal cells.\nThis is called an autoimmune reaction.\n...\n### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease to test if different investigati...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nPhase\nII", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.58}, "jev_score": 0.4988, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "LY4395089 + Mirikizumab for Crohn's Disease - WithPower", "url": "https://www.withpower.com/trial/phase-2-crohn-disease-3-2026-c750c", "domain": "withpower.com", "snippet": "This trial tests a new combination treatment for individuals with moderate to severe Crohn's disease, a condition that causes inflammation in the digestive tract.\nResearchers aim to determine if combining LY4395089, an experimental drug, with mirikizumab is more effective than using mirikizumab alone.\n...\nLY4395089 is a novel oral treatment that, when combined with mirikizumab, could provide a more targeted approach to managing inflammation.\nMirikizumab, an IL-23 inhibitor, is administered intravenously and then subcutaneously, which might offer more sustained relief for patients who respond well.\nThis combination has the potential to enhance efficacy and improve patient outcomes by leveraging different mechanisms of action and routes of administration.\n...\nResearch has shown that mirikizumab effectively treats Crohn's disease.\nSpecifically, studies found that over 92% of patients who were symptom-free remained so after two years of treatment.\nAdditionally, about 60-67% of patients experienced significant symptom improvement.\n...\nLY4395089 reduces inflammation, and its use with mirikizumab may yield even better results by more effectively addressing gut inflammation.\nThis combination aims to provide strong, long-lasting control of Crohn's disease symptoms.^1^^2^^5^^6^^7^\n...\nThe study compares the safety and effectiveness of a new drug (LY4395089) given together with mirikizumab versus mirikizumab alone.\nTreatments are given by IV or under the skin over about 62 weeks.", "date": "2021-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.4862, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Eficacitatea administrării combinate a ly4395089 și mirikizumab comparativ cu mirikizumab singur la adulți cu boala Crohn moderat până la sever activă", "url": "https://studii.clinice.ro/studiu/eficacitatea-ly4395089-si-mirikizumab-in-combinatie-comparativ-cu-mirikizumab-singur-la-adultii-cu-boala-crohn-moderat-severa/", "domain": "studii.clinice.ro", "snippet": "Studiul investighează tratamentul a două afecţiuni inflamatorii ale intestinului, **Ulcerative Colitis** și **Crohn’s Disease**.\nSubstanţa activă principală testată este **mirikizumab**, administrată prin injecţie, iar în unele grupuri se adaugă şi medicamentul oral **LY4395089**.\nAmbele medicamente acţionează asupra sistemului imunitar pentru a reduce inflamaţia.\n...\n**Mirikizumab** este un anticorp monoclonal administrat prin injecție, fie intravenos, fie subcutanat.\nAcționează prin blocarea unei căi inflamatorii specifice care contribuie la inflamația cronică a intestinului în boala Crohn și colita ulcerativă.\n...\n**LY4395089** este un medicament experimental sub formă de comprimat oral.\nAcesta este conceput să modifice răspunsul imunitar și să reducă inflamația din tractul intestinal.\nÎn cadrul studiului, participanții îl iau pe cale orală pentru a evalua dacă poate îmbunătăți semnele și simptomele bolii Crohn comparativ cu tratamentul standard.\nCombinația **Mirikizumab** și **LY4395089** reprezintă terapia investigată în care pacienții primesc ambele medicamente în același timp.\nScopul este să se determine dacă administrarea simultană a celor două medicamente oferă un beneficiu mai mare în reducerea inflamației și în vindecarea mucoasei intestinale decât utilizarea doar a **Mirikizumab** singur.", "date": "2026-09-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.59}, "jev_score": 0.4838, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A Master Protocol (IIBD): A Study of Multiple Drugs in Adults With ...", "url": "https://ctv.veeva.com/study/a-master-protocol-iibd-a-study-of-multiple-drugs-in-adults-with-ulcerative-colitis-or-crohns-dis", "domain": "ctv.veeva.com", "snippet": "Drug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC)\nDrug: LY4395089\n...\nLY4395089 and Mirikizumab Co-Administration\nExperimental group\nDescription:\nStudy Period 1: Co-administration of LY4395089 administered orally and mirikizumab administered IV.\nStudy Period 2: Responders will stop LY4395089 and receive mirikizumab SC.\nTreatment:\nDrug: Mirikizumab - Intravenous (IV)\nDrug: LY4395089", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-14", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.4268, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Clinical Trial Drug Development Pipeline ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/4798717/crohns-disease-clinical-trial-drug-development-pipeline-expands-with-contributions-from-90-key-companies-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "- ***On September 22, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** initiated a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.4833}, "jev_score": 0.4108, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07483099 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07483099", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "The main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\n...\n|--|--|--|\n|Percentage of Participants with Crohn's Disease who Achieve Endoscopic Response|The percentage of participants who achieve endoscopic response at week 12 of Study Period 1.|Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants who Achieve Clinical Remission| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n|Percent Change from Baseline in Fecal Calprotectin| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-04-21", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.4533}, "jev_score": 0.3717, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "LY-4395089 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", 