{"case_id": "q010-2", "row_id": "q010", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication LY4395089, Eli Lilly & Company, Crohn''s Disease", "query": "LY4395089 competing agents Crohn’s disease same mechanism", "anchors": ["LY4395089"], "search_latency_ms": 2995.0, "target": "drugs competing with LY4395089: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 417.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9947, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000418, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8429, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LY4395089 - Crohn Disease | Phase 2 | Eli Lilly and ...", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/LLY/drug/LY4395089", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "snippet": "Highest phase Phase 2\n...\nNCT07483099A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's DiseaseCrohn Disease\n...\n|Itraconazole|DRUG|Administered orally|\n...\n## Competitive Landscape -Crohn's Disease 77 trials (matched to \"Crohn Disease\")\nTop 11 of 13 competitors\n|Company|Ticker|Trials|Lead Phase|Drugs|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970|\n|Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089|\n|AbbVie, Inc.|ABBV|14|PHASE3|Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, ABBV-8736|\n|Takeda Pharmaceutical Co.\nLtd.\nSponsored ADR|TAK|16|PHASE3|Vedolizumab, Zasocitinib, Darvadstrocel|\n|Johnson & Johnson|JNJ|13|PHASE3|Ustekinumab, Guselkumab, Golimumab, JNJ-78934804, Icotrokinra|\n|Merck & Co., Inc.|MRK|2|PHASE3|Tulisokibart|\n|AstraZeneca PLC|AZN|2|PHASE2|AZD7798|\n|Disc Medicine, Inc.|IRON|1|PHASE2|DISC-0974|\n|Spyre Therapeutics, Inc|SYRE|1|PHASE2|SPY001, SPY002, SPY003|\n|Abivax SA Sponsored ADR|ABVX|1|PHASE2|Obefazimod|\n|Palisade Bio, Inc.|PALI|1|PHASE1|PALI-2108|\n...\nLY4395089 is an investigational small molecule being studied for the treatment of Crohn's disease.\nIt is currently in Phase 2 clinical development, where it is being evaluated in combination with mirikizumab and compared against mirikizumab alone in adults with Crohn's disease.\n...\nLY4395089 is being developed by Eli Lilly and Company, a biopharmaceutical company listed on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol LLY.\n...\nLY4395089 is in Phase 2 clinical development for Crohn's disease.\nA Phase 1 study in healthy participants has been completed.\nThe drug is investigational and has not been approved by regulatory authorities.\n...\nLY4395089 is being studied in a Phase 2 trial, NCT07483099, which is recruiting adults with Crohn's disease across multiple countries including the United States, Belgium, Brazil, Canada, China, Germany, Hungary, Israel, Italy, and Poland.\n...\nYes, LY4395089 is being tested in combination with mirikizumab in a Phase 2 trial for Crohn's disease.\nThe study compares the combination of LY4395089 and mirikizumab against mirikizumab alone in adult participants with the condition.", "date": "2026-09-22", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.8967}, "jev_score": 0.8429, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ontamalimab (Ph 3) - Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Limited", "url": "https://www.biopharmawatch.com/company/TAK/drug/Ontamalimab", "domain": "biopharmawatch.com", "snippet": "Crohn's Disease | Monoclonal antibody | Gastrointestinal |Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Limited|** Last Updated: Jun 21, 2024\n## Target and mechanism\nMolecular targetMADCAM1\nTarget classInhibitor\nModalityMonoclonal antibody\n...\nIndicationAll indications (2)Crohn's DiseaseUlcerative Colitis\n...\nNCT03290781An Efficacy and Safety Study of Ontamalimab as Maintenance Therapy in Participants With Moderate to Severe Ulcerative ColitisUlcerative Colitis\n...\nNCT03283085A Safety Extension Study of Ontamalimab in Participants With Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis or Crohn's Disease (AIDA)Crohn's Disease\nCOMPLETED** 557 ** Analytics\n...\n|Crohn's disease (CD): Ontamalimab 25 mg|EXPERIMENTAL|Participants received 25 mg of ontamalimab solution for injection SC, Q4W, for up to 3 years.|\n...\nTop 11 of 13 competitors\n|Company|Ticker|Trials|Lead Phase|Drugs|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Sanofi SA Sponsored ADR|SNY|6|PHASE3|Duvakitug, SAR441566, balinatunfib, SAR442970|\n|Eli Lilly and Company|LLY|8|PHASE3|Mirikizumab, Tirzepatide, MORF-057, LY4395089|\n|AbbVie, Inc.|ABBV|14|PHASE3|Risankizumab, Upadacitinib, Lutikizumab, Trosunilimab, ABBV-8736|\n...\nOntamalimab is an investigational monoclonal antibody being developed for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\nIt is designed as a maintenance therapy for these inflammatory bowel diseases.\nIt has not been approved by the FDA and remains in clinical development.\n...\nOntamalimab targets MADCAM1, also known as mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule 1.\nIt acts as an inhibitor of this target.\nBy binding to MADCAM1, ontamalimab is thought to interfere with the migration of inflammatory cells into the gastrointestinal tract, which may help reduce inflammation in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nOntamalimab is in Phase 3 clinical development.\nIt is an investigational drug and has not been approved by the FDA for any indication.\nThe Phase 3 program includes completed trials in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nOntamalimab has been studied in two completed Phase 3 trials.\nNCT03290781 evaluated ontamalimab as maintenance therapy in 366 participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nNCT03283085, known as the AIDA study, was a safety extension study in 557 participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.8933}, "jev_score": 0.804, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn Disease Clinical Trials (171 Recruiting) | ClinicalTrialsFinder", "url": "https://www.clinicaltrialsfinder.org/conditions/crohn-disease", "domain": "clinicaltrialsfinder.org", "snippet": "Infliximab (Remicade), approved in 1998 after groundbreaking clinical trials, was the first biologic therapy for Crohn disease and revolutionized treatment by demonstrating that targeting the inflammatory protein TNF-alpha could heal the intestinal lining, not just reduce symptoms.\nSince then, trials have delivered additional biologics including adalimumab, vedolizumab, and ustekinumab, as well as the newer oral JAK inhibitor upadacitinib.\n...\nTrials are testing drugs that target new inflammatory pathways, combination strategies pairing biologics with other agents, **diet-based interventions** with scientific rigor, and even **stem cell therapies** for patients with complex fistulizing disease.\n...\n### A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's Disease\nCrohn Disease\nEli Lilly and Company60 enrolled73 locationsNCT07483099\nRecruiting\nPhase 3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-21", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7667}, "jev_score": 0.7207, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to ...", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/trial/706080", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and mirikizumab (LY3074828)] given together can help treat Crohn’s disease (CD01 study).\n...\nThe CD01 study will test if using 2 investigational medicines together can help treat Crohn’s disease.\n...\nWhat the trial is testing?\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089", "date": "2026-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.6067}, "jev_score": 0.5278, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Find Lilly Clinical Trials", "url": "https://trials.lilly.com/en-US/find", "domain": "trials.lilly.com", "snippet": "### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease to test if different investigati...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089 or another investigational medicine\nInvestigational Medicines\n...\n### A clinical research study for people with moderately to severely active Crohn’s disease to test if 2 investigational medicines [LY4395089 and ...\nENROLLING\nAGE\n18 - 80 years\nConditions\nModerately to severely active Crohn’s disease\nInvestigational Medicines\nMirikizumab (LY3074828) and LY4395089\nInvestigational Medicines", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5933}, "jev_score": 0.5043, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07483099 | ClinicalTrials.gov - ClinicalTrials.gov", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07483099", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "## A Study of LY4395089 and Mirikizumab (LY3074828) Given Together and Mirikizumab (Alone) in Adults With Crohn's Disease\n...\nThe main purpose of this study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.\nOfficial Title\nA Phase 2, Multicenter, Randomized, Open-Label, Active-Controlled Study to Investigate LY4395089/Mirikizumab Co-administration Compared With Mirikizumab in Adults With Moderately to Severely Active Crohn's Disease\n...\nIntervention / Treatment **\n- Drug: LY4395089\n- Drug: Mirikizumab - Intravenous (IV)\n- Drug: Mirikizumab - Subcutaneous (SC)\n...\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: LY4395089 and Mirikizumab Co-Administration Study Period 1: Co-administration of LY4395089 administered orally and mirikizumab administered IV.Study Period 2: Responders will stop LY4395089 and receive mirikizumab SC.|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants with Crohn's Disease who Achieve Endoscopic Response|The percentage of participants who achieve endoscopic response at week 12 of Study Period 1.|Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n...\n|Percentage of Participants who Achieve Clinical Remission| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|\n|Percent Change from Baseline in Fecal Calprotectin| |Baseline, Up to Week 12|", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-04-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.5767}, "jev_score": 0.4671, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Clinical Trial Drug Development Pipeline ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/4798717/crohns-disease-clinical-trial-drug-development-pipeline-expands-with-contributions-from-90-key-companies-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "- ***On September 22, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** initiated a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.5}, "jev_score": 0.415, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for luminal and perianal Crohn's disease management - Luisa Bertin, Martina Crepaldi, Miriana Zanconato, Greta Lorenzon, Daria Maniero, Caterina de Barba, Erica Bonazzi, Sonia Facchin, Marco Scarpa, Cesare Ruffolo, Imerio Angriman, Andrea Buda, Fabiana Zingone, Brigida Barberio, Edoardo Vincenzo Savarino, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241303651", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "We highlight promising therapies that target known mechanisms, including selective Janus kinase inhibitors, anti-adhesion molecules, tumor necrosis factor inhibitors, and IL-23 selective inhibitors.\n...\nSome of these drugs target well-known pathways, like JAK inhibitors and IL-23 blockers, while others focus on newer areas, such as specific receptors or molecules involved in inflammation.\n...\n^6,7^ To date, the therapeutic arsenal for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) has included mesalamine, immunosuppressants such as methotrexate and thiopurine, cyclosporin, anti-tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNFα) agents, anti-integrin therapies, anti-IL-12 and IL-23 monoclonal antibodies, and a few small molecules.^8–11^\n...\n|TNF inhibitors|V565|TNFα|Oral|Phase II|Completed|NCT02976129|2019-03-08|✔|✔|✘|✘|✘|✘|\n...\nIL-23, belonging to the IL-12 cytokine family, consists of p40 and p19 subunits, with p19 being exclusive to IL-23.\nIt plays a vital role in regulating and enhancing T-helper 17 cells and activating various innate immune cells, which holds significance in developing chronic inflammatory disorders like CD.\n...\nBrazikumab (MEDI2070) is a human IgG2 monoclonal antibody that binds exclusively to the p19 subunit of IL-23.\n...\nGuselkumab’s efficacy, a monoclonal human IgG1 antibody specifically targeting p19 IL-23, was analyzed in a program consisting in three separate studies: a 48-week phase II dose-ranging study (GALAXI 1) and two 48-week phase III confirmatory studies (GALAXI 2 and GALAXI 3).\n...\nBiologic agents targeting TNFα have become a well-established therapy, after authorization by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) of infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab.^27–30^ Thereafter, additional compounds have been investigated.\n...\n^33,34^ Modulating this pathway holds the potential to impede lymphocyte migration into the gut, a critical factor in chronic inflammation, thereby reducing inflammation and minimizing tissue damage.^35^ Oral small molecules that can modulate S1P receptors have been developed, offering advantages over biologic agents such as ease of administration and rapid action.\n...\n^43^ Blocking the activation of this pathway halts the activity of several chemokines involved in mediating inflammation.\nSeveral pan-JAK inhibitors and selective JAK inhibitors, including the newer TYK2 inhibitors, are in various stages of development for IBD.\n...\nNumerous clinical trials are underway to evaluate the safety and efficacy of novel therapies, including molecules like S1PR modulators, selective JAK inhibitors, anti-TL1A antibodies, and PPARγ agonists.", "date": "2024-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.5467}, "jev_score": 0.3827, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohns' Disease Pipeline Appears Robust With 90+ Key Pharma ...", "url": "https://www.barchart.com/story/news/2494549/crohns-disease-pipeline-appears-robust-with-90-key-pharma-companies-actively-working-in-the-domain-delveinsight", "domain": "barchart.com", "snippet": "Around 540 participants will be enrolled in the study at approximately 300 sites worldwide.\n- ***On June 04, 2026- Eli Lilly and Company* ** announced a phase 2 study is to see how the safety and efficacy of a farnesoid X receptor (FXR) agonist (LY4395089), given together with mirikizumab compares with mirikizumab (alone) in adults with moderately to severely active Crohn's disease (CD).\nThis study is part of the IIBD master protocol and will last approximately 62 weeks.", "date": "2026-06-16", "last_updated": "2026-07-01", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.4767}, "jev_score": 0.3814, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "LY-4395089 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/026286e1f04d4c549c14023c3c12d3ed", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Active Indication\nCrohn Disease\nCrohn's disease, active moderate\nCrohn's disease, active severe", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-16", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.47, "evidence": 0.1967}, "jev_score": 0.0924, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3412.4, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:13+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q008-4", "row_id": "q008", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "query": "LMN-201 AZD5148 clinical trial regulatory status", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "search_latency_ms": 10150.9, "target": "drugs competing with LMN-201: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 375.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9915, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000416, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6946, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Prevention of Recurrent C. Difficile Infection Study With AZD5148 Monoclonal Antibody", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07285213", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "The purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of AZD5148 for prevention of recurrence of Clostridioides difficile infection in Individuals 18 years of age and above.\n...\n## Timeline & Regulatory\n- **Start**\n2025-12-10\n- **Primary Completion**\n2027-04-20\n- **Completion**\n2028-01-18\n- **FDA Drug**\nYes\n## Countries\n- United States\n...\n- United Kingdom\n...\nNCT03824795 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE2\n...\n- LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\nNCT05330182 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2/PHASE3\n- Clostridium Difficile Vaccine 2-Dose Versus 3-Dose Study\n...\n- Study of the Clinical Effectiveness of a Human Monoclonal Antibody to C.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-06", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.6946, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 for Prevention of C. Difficile Infection Recurrence", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05330182", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "Drug: LMN-201 Drug: Placebo\n...\n- CDMRP-PR221885 ( Other Grant/Funding Number ) (OTHER_GRANT: CDMRP)\n- CDMRP-PR221885 ( Other Grant/Funding Number ) (OTHER_GRANT: CDMRP)\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n- Male or female, aged 18 or older.\n- Diagnosis of CDI defined as a new or recent history of 3 or more bowel movements per day with a loose or watery consistency (Bristol Stool Scale 5, 6, or 7); a positive stool C. difficile toxin B immunoassay (stool collected no more than 7 days before first dose of LMN-201/placebo), and no other likely explanation for diarrhea.\nNOTE: Diarrhea is not required to be present on the day of enrollment.\n- Provision of signed and dated informed consent form.\n- Scheduled to receive or planning to receive a ≤28-day course of SOC antibiotic therapy for CDI.\nParticipant must have been diagnosed with CDI for 7 or fewer days at time of initial study drug administration.\n...\nExclusion Criteria:\n- Fulminant C. difficile colitis.\n- Admitted or expect to be admitted to an intensive care unit.\n- Underlying gastrointestinal disorder characterized by diarrhea including but not limited to chronic ulcerative colitis, Crohn's disease, celiac sprue, short bowel syndrome, dumping syndrome following gastrectomy, pancreatic insufficiency, enteric parasitic infection, viral enteritis, bacterial enteritis (salmonella, shigella, ETEC, etc.).\n- Neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count of < 1000 per microliter for any reason).\n...\n- Pregnancy, anticipated pregnancy, or breastfeeding.\n- Inability or unwillingness to swallow numerous, relatively large capsules containing study drug or placebo because of a swallowing disorder or dysphagia.\n- Inability to pass swallowed capsules into the distal small intestine because of gastroparesis, repetitive vomiting, or anatomic narrowing in the esophagus, stomach, or small intestine.\n...\n**Maskin