{"case_id": "q008-5", "row_id": "q008", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Clostridioides Difficile Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as LMN-201, including Antibody Fragment Enzyme Biological Biologic Cocktail and Clostridioides Difficile Toxin B TcdB therapies", "query": "LMN-201 bezlotoxumab withdrawal current status", "anchors": ["LMN-201"], "search_latency_ms": 9150.9, "target": "drugs competing with LMN-201: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 410.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9023, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000379, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 3, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.76, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 Preliminary Cohort in Repreve Trial", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/02/lmn-201-achieves-100-initial-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort-of-re-preve-trial", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "The RePreve Trial was designed to facilitate comparisons to the MODIFY trial, which targeted the same patient population with the same SoC antibiotics combined with the IV-infused monoclonal antibody, bezlotoxumab.\n...\nSustained cure rates through Week 4 for SoC alone (MODIFY I/II), SoC plus LMN-201 (RePreve sentinel cohort) and SoC plus bezlotoxumab (MODIFY I/II) were 59.5% (460/773; 95% CI 56-63%), 95.2% (20/21; 95% CI 77-100%) and 68.9% (538/781; 95% CI 66-72%), respectively (see enclosed figure).\n...\nIf borne out in future studies, the RePreve Trial will mark a major advance in the CDI field.\nBezlotoxumab did not improve initial clinical cure rates and only modestly improved CDI recurrence, but came with a risk of heart failure on its label; it was recently withdrawn from the market.", "date": "2025-04-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8}, "jev_score": 0.76, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Navigating recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection without ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12722561/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Despite this, bezlotoxumab was withdrawn from the market by its sole supplier on January 31, 2025.\nThe withdrawal of this drug raises concerns regarding the future management and prevention of rCDI.\nThere are two other practices currently in place for prevention of rCDI—oral vancomycin prophylaxis (OVP) and fecal microbiota-based therapies (FMT), each with distinct advantages and limitations that guide their use in clinical practice.", "date": "2025-12-09", "last_updated": "2026-08-06", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.75}, "jev_score": 0.6675, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience's Oral Biologic LMN-201 Achieves 100 ...", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/news/lumen-bioscience-s-oral-biologic-lmn-201-achieves-100-clinical-cure-rate-for-c-difficile-infection-in-preliminary-trial", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "## Key Insights\n...\n- Only one patient (4.76%) experienced recurrence within 28 days after LMN-201View drug details treatment, compared to 26% with standard care alone and 14% with bezlotoxumabView drug details in previous MODIFY trials, suggesting strong potential for reducing the burden of recurrent infections.\n- The RePreve Trial has now advanced to its main cohort, a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients, with extended dosing from 7 days to approximately 70 days to provide protection throughout the period of greatest recurrence risk.\n...\nThis compares favorably to the 26% recurrence rate (161 of 621 patients; 95% CI: 23%-30%) observed after standard-of-care alone in the MODIFY I/II trials, and the 14% recurrence rate (87 of 625 patients; 95% CI: 11%-17%) after standard-of-care plus bezlotoxumabView drug details treatment.\nThe sustained clinical cure rate through Week 4—defined as initial clinical cure plus prevention of CDI (search)Search disease recurrence—was 95.2% for LMN-201View drug details plus standard-of-care, compared to 59.5% for standard-of-care alone and 68.9% for standard-of-care plus bezlotoxumabView drug details in previous trials.\n...\nWith these promising results, Lumen has now begun Part B (main cohort), a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S.\n...\n\"We are pleased that LMN-201View drug details appears to be safe and well-tolerated in patients,\" added Finrow.\n...\nLMN-201View drug details offers several potential advantages over existing treatments.\nUnlike bezlotoxumabView drug details, which was recently withdrawn from the market and carried a risk of heart failure, LMN-201 appears to be safe and well-tolerated.", "date": "2025-04-03", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.6249, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigational Oral Biologic Achieves 100% C difficile ...", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/news/2025/04/03/investigational-oral-biologic-achieves-100-c-difficile-clinical-cure-in-preliminary-cohort", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "Today, Lumen Bioscience reported that its investigational oral biologic, LMN-201, achieved a 100% clinical cure rate of C difficile infection (CDI) in the sentinel cohort (Part A) of its RePreve clinical trial.\n...\nOnly 1 of 21 patients (4.76%; 95% CI: 0.24%-23%) who completed 7 days of standard-of-care (SoC) plus LMN-201 experienced CDI recurrence within 28 days, compared to 161 of 621 patients (26%; 95% CI: 23%-30%) who experienced recurrence after SoC alone in MODIFY I/II, and 87 of 625 patients (14%; 95% CI: 11%-17%) after SoC plus bezlotoxumab treatment in MODIFY I/II within the same time period.\n...\nLMN-201 is delivered in capsules and is intended for use with and following antibiotics to improve clinical outcomes for C difficile infection.\n...\nWith the sentinel cohort now completed, Lumen has begun Part B (the main cohort).\nThis pivotal phase is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study that will enroll approximately 350 patients at research centers across the U.S. Participants will be randomized 1:1 to receive either LMN-201 or a placebo—each alongside standard antibiotic therapy—to rigorously evaluate the drug’s efficacy in a larger population.\n...\n*The RePreve Trial main cohort is now recruiting.For investigators and potential participants interested in participating, they can email trials@lumen.bio or visit the official RePreve study webpage.*", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.3767}, "jev_score": 0.2675, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "LMN-201 Clinical Mixture of 3", "url": "https://db.antibodysociety.org/db0/937/", "domain": "db.antibodysociety.org", "snippet": "|INN|None|\n|Status|Clinical|\n...\n|Most advanced stage of development (global)|Phase 2/3|\n|--|--|\n|Status|Active|\n|Start of clinical phase (IND filing or first Phase 1)|August 15, 2021|\n|Start of Phase 2| |\n|Start of Phase 3|August 29, 2024|\n...\n|US or EU approved indications|None|\n...\n|Comments about company or candidate|NCT05330182 Phase 2/3 study of Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence started in Aug 2024.\nMay 17, 2023: Lumen Bioscience announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has granted Fast Track Designation for LMN-201, an investigational, orally delivered biologic drug to treat and prevent C. difficile infection (CDI).\nThe FDA previously cleared a planned Phase 2/3 study of LMN-201, which will begin enrolling approximately 375 patients at sites across the United States later this year.|\n...\n|Anticipated events|None|\n|--|--|\n|Factor(s) contributing to discontinuation|None|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.61, "evidence": 0.4067}, "jev_score": 0.2481, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Lumen Bioscience | Home", "url": "https://www.lumen.bio/", "domain": "lumen.bio", "snippet": "### Core pipeline\n- C. difficile (LMN-201)\nOral biologic cocktail\nMOA Previously Demonstrated\nPreclinical\nPhase 1\n(safety)\nPhase 2\n(proof of concept)\nPhase 3\n(definitive efficacy)", "date": "2026-09-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.34}, "jev_score": 0.2244, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Addressing Personalized Needs in Clostridioides Difficile Infection", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT04725123", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT03829475 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE2\n- LMN-201 for Prevention of C.\nDifficile Infection Recurrence\nNCT05330182 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2/PHASE3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.58, "evidence": 0.2033}, "jev_score": 0.1179, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Pfizer, Inc., et al.; Withdrawal of Approval of 26 New Drug Applications", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/11/19/2024-26913/pfizer-inc-et-al-withdrawal-of-approval-of-26-new-drug-applications", "domain": "federalregister.gov", "snippet": "Enter a search term or FR citation e.g. 88 FR 382 30 FR 7878 2024-13208 USDA 09/05/24 RULE 0503-AA39 SORN", "date": "2024-11-19", "last_updated": "2025-05-26", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0001, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pfizer, Inc., et al.; Withdrawal of Approval of 23 New Drug Applications", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/06/03/2024-12065/pfizer-inc-et-al-withdrawal-of-approval-of-23-new-drug-applications", "domain": "federalregister.gov", "snippet": "Enter a search term or FR citation e.g. 88 FR 382 30 FR 7878 2024-13208 USDA 09/05/24 RULE 0503-AA39 SORN", "date": "2024-06-03", "last_updated": "2025-05-27", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0001, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Drug news and alerts", "url": "https://www.humana.com/pharmacy/news", "domain": "humana.com", "snippet": "The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) announced on April 6, 2023 the final decision to withdraw approval of Makena and its generic.", "date": "2024-03-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.01, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 9561.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:14+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q011-1", "row_id": "q011", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor etrolizumab mechanism IBD", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "search_latency_ms": 2176.9, "target": "drugs competing with α4β7 Inhibitor / α4β7: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 425.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10849, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000456, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7726, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-Integrins for the Treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8402953/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab (anti-β7) blocks leukocyte trafficking via α4β7 and cell adhesion via αEβ7 integrins.\n...\nThe α4β7 integrin interacts with mucosal addressin-cell adhesion molecule 1 (MAdCAM-1) expressed on high endothelial venules in GALT, which is expressed on the intestinal vascular endothelium;\n...\nVedolizumab (Takeda) is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer.\nThe antibody selectively blocks α4β7:MAdCAM-1 interactions—preventing leukocyte adhesion to intestinal endothelium—but does not affect α4β1:VCAM-1 interactions.36 Vedolizumab is FDA-approved for induction and maintenance therapy in patients with CD and CD.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks cellular adhesion by binding the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\nThe α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins function as trafficking molecules that localize leukocytes to inflammatory sites in the gut.56 Etrolizumab binds α4β7 and prevents binding to MAdCAM-1, a receptor that is highly upregulated in the intestinal venules of patients with UC and CD.\nEtrolizumab also binds αEβ7 and prevents interaction with E-cadherin found on intestinal mucosal cells.\nThe αEβ7-expressing lymphocytes residing in the gut participate in the inflammatory responses observed in IBD.57 Thus, the inhibition of αEβ7 results in decreased inflammation and increased retention of leukocytes within the intraepithelial space of the intestinal mucosa.\n...\nAntibody blockade of leukocyte trafficking to the gastrointestinal tract with anti-integrin therapy (anti-α4β7) is effective for patients with IBD and is associated with favorable safety profiles.", "date": "2021-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8133}, "jev_score": 0.7726, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Etrolizumab: Uses, Interactions, Mechanism of Action", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB12189", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "**Etrolizumab**is a humanized IgG1κ anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody under investigation for use in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1κ monoclonal antibody directed against the β7 subunit of gastrointestinal α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins that, due to its target specificity, appears as or more efficacious than vedolizumab and without the CNS effects of natalizumab.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized anti-β7 integrin subunit monoclonal antibody that impairs the targeting and retention of gut-specific lymphocytes contributing to an inflammatory microenvironment in ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\n...\nInflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, is characterized by chronic inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract.\nThis inflammation is driven, at least in part, by excessive infiltration and retention of intestinal-homing lymphocytes.\nAn adhesion cascade drives the infiltration of immune cells.\nThe binding of immune cell glycoproteins to their corresponding epithelial cell selectins results in partial retention and the \"rolling\" of immune cells along the endothelium.\nA stable arrest is mediated by stronger interactions between immune cell integrins and cell adhesion molecules (CAMs) on the endothelial cell; once arrested, immune cells will extravasate and enter the target tissue.\n...\nOf primary importance to intestinal homing is the mucosal addressin cellular adhesion molecule (MAdCAM-1), which interacts strongly with α4β7 integrins, and vascular cell adhesion molecule (VCAM-1), which binds with lower affinity.\n...\nα4β7 integrin is expressed at high levels on intestinal-homing lymphocytes but can also be found on natural killer cells, basophils, eosinophils, macrophages, activated monocytes, and mast cells.\n...\nThe infiltration and retention of immune cells as a mechanism of IBD is suggested by observations across multiple studies of an increase in the number of, and alteration in the secreted cytokine profile of, α4β7\n+and αEβ7 +immune cells in the gastrointestinal tract of IBD patients.\n...\nAlthough the mechanism has not been completely elucidated, it is clear that an increase in the number and retention of gut-homing immune cells capable of sustaining an inflammatory microenvironment is a crucial element of IBD pathophysiology.\nEtrolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody specific for the β7 integrin subunit; it has a K\ndof 116 ± 11 pmol/L against human α4β7 and 1800 ± 170 pmol/L against human αEβ7, as assessed by transfection in HEK293 cells.\nThis strong binding provides efficient inhibition of integrin/CAM interactions.\nEtrolizumab has an IC 50for the α4β7 interactions with MAdCAM-1, VCAM-1, and fibronectin of 0.075 ± 0.034, 0.089 ± 0.009, and 0.119 ± 0.056 nmol/L, respectively.\nEtrolizumab also inhibits the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, albeit less effectively (IC 50of 3.96 ± 1.78 nmol/L).\nWhile etrolizumab does not exhibit antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, it disrupts the integrin-CAM interactions involved in gut immune cell residence, in part by inducing the endocytic uptake of bound integrin receptors, and therefore reduces the inflammatory burden in IBD.\n...\nIntegrin ITGA4/ITGB7 (alpha-4/beta-7) (Peyer patches-specific homing receptor LPAM-1) is an adhesion molecule that mediates lymphocyte migration and homing to gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) (Probable).", "date": "2012-02-12", "last_updated": "2025-08-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7033}, "jev_score": 0.6611, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=0c8fefc4-9b58-46fd-9932-8178652bd368", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In IBD (CD, UC, pediatric CD), TNFR1/TNFR2 blockade dampens NF‑κB/MAPK activation in gut‑homing lymphocytes, restores epithelial barrier integrity, and limits mucosal infiltration.\n...\nTo reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23].\nPharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7.\nIt works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium.\nMouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6667}, "jev_score": 0.6134, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Cellular Mechanisms of Etrolizumab Treatment in ... - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367223/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Mechanistically, etrolizumab is known to block β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces intestinal trafficking of β7-expressing cells.\nEtrolizumab blocks β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces β7-positive lymphocyte migration and retention in the inflamed gut mucosa, but the exact mechanisms by which this inhibition occurs are not fully understood.\n...\n**Results:** As demonstrated by several different approaches, etrolizumab and etrolizumab-s treatment led to internalization of β7 integrin.\nThis resulted in impaired dynamic adhesion to MAdCAM-1.\n...\nThe anti-β7 antibody etrolizumab is currently being investigated in several phase III trials in IBD patients and blocks both α4β7-mediated gut homing as well as αEβ7-controlled retention (Vermeire et al., 2014; Zundler et al., 2017c).\n...\nWe show with complementary techniques including molecular microscopy with stimulated emission depletion (STED) imaging and ImageStream^®^ analyses that etrolizumab leads to internalization of α4β7 integrin, that this functionally impairs β7-dependent adhesion to MAdCAM-1, and that internalization of α4β7 integrin is higher with etrolizumab compared with vedolizumab.", "date": "2019-02-01", "last_updated": "2026-03-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.5885, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for inflammatory ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=1e53ce24-191f-48ed-a22e-45f1dec47600", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In IBD (CD, UC, pediatric CD), TNFR1/TNFR2 blockade dampens NF‑κB/MAPK activation in gut‑homing lymphocytes, restores epithelial barrier integrity, and limits mucosal infiltration.\n...\nTo reduce systemic toxicity, newer therapies such as vedolizumab exploit gut-selective targeting via α4β7–MAdCAM-1 blockade, limiting interference with CNS immune surveillance and respiratory tract lymphocyte trafficking [21,22,23].\nPharmacokinetic studies confirm minimal systemic immunosuppression and no observed PML cases in the pivotal GEMINI trials.Etrolizumab, by contrast, inhibits both α4β7–MAdCAM-1 and αEβ7–E-cadherin, aiming to block lymphocyte gut homing and retention in the intestinal epithelium.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody targeting β7.\nIt works by blocking the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and the interaction of αEβ7 with E-cadherin, thereby reducing the transport of lymphocytes to the intestine and their retention in the epithelium.\nMouse models and genetic analyses have shown that dual blockade of α4β7 and αEβ7 can more effectively reduce the accumulation of cytotoxic intraepithelial lymphocytes and inflammatory CD8^+^ T cells in the intestinal mucosa of patients with CD [23].", "date": "2025-12-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6533}, "jev_score": 0.588, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting T-cell integrins in autoimmune and inflammatory diseases", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/cei/article/215/1/15/7239982?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "MAdCAM-1 is expressed in the HEVs of the small intestine, Peyer’s patches, and large intestine, and interacts with l-selectin and α4β7 to mediate rolling and firm adhesion of T cells, respectively [14].\n...\nVCAM-1, which is expressed only when endothelial cells are stimulated by pro-inflammatory cytokines, also binds α4β7 (with lower affinity than MAdCAM-1 does) as well as α4β1.\n...\nThus, drugs that inhibit the action of α4β7, α4β1, and αEβ7 have been developed, trialled, and authorized for the treatment of IBD (Fig. 1) [27].\n...\nVedolizumab binding to α4β7 inhibits the interaction of α4β7 with MAdCAM-1 and fibronectin, but not VCAM-1, as VCAM-1 binds to α4β7 without clashing with vedolizumab [48].\nVedolizumab binding to α4β7 induces the internalization of α4β7, and removal of vedolizumab restores α4β7 expression and MAdCAM-1 binding to normal within 4 days, in vitro.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanised anti-β7 monoclonal antibody and, as such, binds to α4β7 and αEβ7 to inhibit their binding to MAdCAM-1 and VCAM-1, and E-cadherin, respectively.\nT cells expressing αEβ7 bind to E-cadherin that is expressed by epithelial cells on their basolateral surface, causing retention of these T cells in the lamina propria [30].\n...\nAnother mechanism of action of etrolizumab may be the inhibition of αEβ7 on colonic DCs.\n...\nIn IBD, targeting the α4β7-MAdCAM-1 interaction with vedolizumab has shown efficacy in clinical trials and possesses an advantage with regard to safety due to the selectivity of said interaction to the gut, as does targeting β7-chain on its own with etrolizumab.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.582, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "A randomised phase I study of etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7) in ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3711369/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab (rhuMAb β7, anti-β7, PRO145223) is a humanised monoclonal antibody targeting the β7 subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α4β7 and αEβ7, which are implicated in leucocyte migration and retention in ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\n- Etrolizumab (antibodies to β7) does inhibit the migration of lymphocytes to the gut and antagonises the retention of lymphocytes in the mucosa by blocking the αEβ7–E-cadherin interaction.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanised IgG1 monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7^29^ ^30^ and αEβ7^15^ integrins with high affinity, blocking binding to their ligands, MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin, respectively.\n...\nEtrolizumab provides a dual approach mechanism of action for the treatment of IBD that involves inhibition of leucocyte trafficking to the gut (through blocking of α4β7–MAdCAM-1 interactions) as well as inhibition of retention of leucocytes in the intraepithelial lining of the gut (through blocking αEβ7–E-cadherin interactions), which may provide improved efficacy over currently available therapies.\n...\nThus, etrolizumab could affect leucocyte composition within the gut mucosa through multiple mechanisms.\nFirst, as discussed above, it could block α4β7-expressing leucocytes from entering the gut by inhibiting their extravasation through MAdCAM-1-expressing mucosal endothelial venules.", "date": "2012-06-20", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.6267}, "jev_score": 0.5452, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Cellular Mechanisms of Etrolizumab Treatment in ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2019.00039/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Mechanistically, etrolizumab is known to block β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces intestinal trafficking of β7-expressing cells.\nEtrolizumab blocks β7 integrin ligand binding and reduces β7-positive lymphocyte migration and retention in the inflamed gut mucosa, but the exact mechanisms by which this inhibition occurs are not fully understood.\n...\n**Results:** As demonstrated by several different approaches, etrolizumab and etrolizumab-s treatment led to internalization of β7 integrin.\nThis resulted in impaired dynamic adhesion to MAdCAM-1.\n...\nInternalization of α4β7 integrin was increased with etrolizumab compared with vedolizumab.\n...\nIntegrin internalization may be an important mechanism of action of etrolizumab, which might explain some but not all immunological effects observed with etrolizumab.\n...\nThe anti-β7 antibody etrolizumab is currently being investigated in several phase III trials in IBD patients and blocks both α4β7-mediated gut homing as well as αEβ7-controlled retention (Vermeire et al., 2014; Zundler et al., 2017c).\n...\nWe show with complementary techniques including molecular microscopy with stimulated emission depletion (STED) imaging and ImageStream\n® analyses that etrolizumab leads to internalization of α4β7 integrin, that this functionally impairs β7-dependent adhesion to MAdCAM-1, and that internalization of α4β7 integrin is higher with etrolizumab compared with vedolizumab.\n...\nMechanistically, it is believed that targeted inhibition of the interaction of integrins with respective ligands by monoclonal antibodies leads to impaired cell trafficking and subsequent reduction of pro-inflammatory cell infiltration in the gut (Habtezion et al., 2016; Zundler and Neurath, 2017).", "date": "2019-02-01", "last_updated": "2025-04-16", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6067}, "jev_score": 0.54, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "2.4. Integrin αeβ7 (cd103)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6973243/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Through interactions between α4β7:MAdCAM‐1 or α4β1:VCAM‐1, migration of effector T lymphocytes to the inflamed gut contributes to the local production of pro‐inflammatory mediators including IFN‐γ, IL‐6, IL‐9, and IL‐17.\n...\nVedolizumab targets the α4β7 subunit and inhibits its interactions with MAdCAM‐1.\nEtrolizumab targets the β7 integrin and inhibits the interaction of α4β7 and αEβ7 with MAdCAM‐1 or E‐cadherin, respectively.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized antibody which binds to α4β7 and was shown to specifically inhibit the interaction between α4β*7* integrin and its ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and fibronectin (Table 1).106 Given that MAdCAM‐1 expression is largely restricted to the intestine, the inhibitory effects of vedolizumab are regarded as gut selective.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized immunoglobulin (Ig)G1 monoclonal antibody that selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and E‐cadherin (Table 1).", "date": "2019-06-19", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.63}, "jev_score": 0.5292, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Review article: nonclinical and clinical pharmacology ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6001693/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab exerts its effect via a unique dual MOA that inhibits both leucocyte trafficking to the intestinal mucosa and retention within the intestinal epithelial layer.\nThe gut‐selectivity of etrolizumab results from its specific targeting of the β7 subunit of α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\nEtrolizumab does not bind to α4β1 integrin, which mediates lymphocyte trafficking to tissues including the central nervous system, a characteristic underlying its favourable safety with regard to progressive multifocal leucoencephalopathy.\n...\nBinding to β7 integrin enables etrolizumab to block both leucocyte trafficking to the gut via α4β7 interactions with its endothelial ligand mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 (MAdCAM‐1),17, 18 expressed primarily on endothelium of venules of intestinal lamina\npropria and associated lymphoid tissues19, 20 as well as the retention of lymphocyte subsets within the intestinal mucosa through the interaction of αEβ7 with its ligand E‐cadherin, expressed on the gut epithelium21 (Figure 1, Table 1).22, 23, 24, 25\n...\nSince etrolizumab has a unique dual MOA associated with inhibition of both the α4β7 and αEβ7 pathways, the integrated pharmacological information summarised in this review aims to provide researchers and investigators with a clear view of its unique biology distinct from other anti‐integrin drugs in the class.\n...\nα4β7 integrin plays a crucial role in leucocyte homing to the intestinal mucosa and associated lymphoid tissues.\nα4β7 integrin is constitutively expressed at a modest level on naive T and B cells and at higher levels on intestinal‐homing effector/memory T and B cells.\n...\nIn addition, etrolizumab is highly specific for the β7 subunit of α4β7 and αEβ7 and does not bind to α4 or β1 integrin subunits.", "date": "2018-03-30", "last_updated": "2026-03-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.4843, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2602.8, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:14+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q011-4", "row_id": "q011", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor Phase 2 Phase 3 Crohn's disease competitors", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "search_latency_ms": 3708.5, "target": "drugs competing with α4β7 Inhibitor / α4β7: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 360.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10795, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000453, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9024, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9752289/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^29^ Phase 2 and phase 3 studies are currently underway in UC and CD (including a phase 3 study comparing risankizumab to UST in moderate to severe CD patients, who have failed anti‐TNF).\n...\nGuselkumab (CNTO1959) is another IL‐23 p19 inhibitor, already approved in plaque psoriasis ^31^ and psoriatic arthritis, ^32^ which has been shown to be effective in phases 2 studies in both CD and UC.\n...\n^85^ The development of ontamalimab, an anti‐ MAdCAM‐1, has recently been stopped ^86^ but 2 other anti‐integrins (etrolizumab and AJM300) are currently being evaluated in phase 3 CTs in IBD.\n...\nEtrolizumab (PRO145223) is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds the *ß* ~7~ subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α~4~β~7~ and α~E~β~7~.\n...\nAJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is a new small molecule, α4‐integrin antagonist, being evaluated in UC.\n...\n^146^ Regarding anti‐integrins, abrilumab SC (AMG181) is an anti‐integrin α~4~β~7~ with proven effectiveness in a phase 2b study in UC ^147^ , ^148^ , ^149^ and PN‐943 is a new oral gastro‐intestinal‐restricted peptide antagonist of α4β7 integrin being evaluated in a phase 2 study.", "date": "2022-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.94}, "jev_score": 0.9024, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin Inhibitor Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/integrin-inhibitor-competitive-landscape-2025-vedolizumab-oral-small-molecules-fibrosis-frontier/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In July 2024, Eli Lilly acquired Morphic Therapeutic for approximately $3.2 billion.\nThis deal validated oral α4β7 integrin inhibition as a next-generation IBD strategy.\nSpecifically, MORF-057 targets the same pathway as vedolizumab but offers oral convenience.\n...\n|Drug|Company|Target|Indication|2024 Revenue|Stage|\n|--|--|--|--|--|--|\n|Vedolizumab (Entyvio)|Takeda|α4β7|UC, Crohn’s, Pouchitis|~$5.6–5.8B|Approved|\n|Natalizumab (Tysabri)|Biogen|α4|Relapsing MS, Crohn’s|~$1.5–1.6B|Approved (biosimilar)|\n...\n|MORF-057|Eli Lilly (Morphic)|α4β7 (oral)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n|Bexotegrast (PLN-74809)|Pliant Therapeutics|αvβ6/αvβ1|IPF, PSC|N/A|Phase 2/3|\n|PTG-965|Protagonist Therapeutics|α4β7 (oral peptide)|UC, Crohn’s|N/A|Phase 2|\n...\nSpecifically, MORF-057’s Phase 2 data in UC and Lilly’s $3.2B acquisition signal that oral α4β7 inhibition is no longer a theoretical concept.\n...\nThe asset is an oral small molecule α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Phase 2 trials for ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2026-05-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9367}, "jev_score": 0.8992, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-Integrins for the Treatment of Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8402953/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Natalizumab (anti-α4) is approved for moderate to severe Crohn’s disease (CD), but its use is limited due to potential risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.\nVedolizumab (anti-α4β7) is approved for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC) and CD.\n...\nLarge phase 3 clinical trials evaluating efficacy of etrolizumab in the induction and maintenance of patients with IBD are underway.\nOther investigational anti-integrin therapies include abrilumab (anti-α4β7 IgG2), PN-943 (orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide), AJM300 (orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4), and ontamalimab (anti-MAdCAM-1 IgG).\n...\nVedolizumab (Takeda) is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer.\nThe antibody selectively blocks α4β7:MAdCAM-1 interactions—preventing leukocyte adhesion to intestinal endothelium—but does not affect α4β1:VCAM-1 interactions.36 Vedolizumab is FDA-approved for induction and maintenance therapy in patients with CD and CD.\n...\nEtrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks cellular adhesion by binding the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins.\n...\nMost patients in clinical remission came from the etrolizumab 100 mg group (*n*=39), with 21% achieving remission, followed by 10% in the 300 mg LD cohort (*n*=39), compared with 0% in the placebo cohort (*n*=41).59 Currently, there are several phase 3 clinical trials evaluating etrolizumab in IBD (UC: HIBISCUS I and II, GARDENIA, LAUREL, HICKORY;\nCD: BERGAMOT)60 and two open-label extension trials (UC: COTTONWOOD; CD: JUNIPER).\n...\nAbrilumab is a human anti-α4β7 IgG2 mAb with favorable PK and PD properties amenable to subcutaneous administration.63–65 Phase 2b trials of abrilumab were completed for both CD and UC.\nThe phase 2b placebo-controlled RCT for CD included 249 patients with inadequate or lost response to previous therapies, who were dosed with 21 mg, 70 mg, or 210 mg on day 1, week 2, week 4, and every 4 weeks for 24 weeks, with a primary endpoint of remission at week 12 and CDAI at weeks 8 and 12.66 Although the primary endpoints were not met, treatment groups had higher rates of remission at week 12, safety measures were balanced between treatment and placebo groups, and α4β7 receptor occupancy was sustained.\n...\nTo date, phase 3 trials for abrilumab have not been registered.\n...\nPN-943 is an orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide taking advantage of a topical route of administration.69 PN-943 is more effective for induction of remission in UC than PTG-100, an earlier-generation orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist cysteine knot peptide developed\n...\nCarotegrast methyl is an orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4, with demonstrated activity against α4β7 and α4β1.72 The molecule is dosed thrice daily and characterized by generally mild adverse effects.73 A phase 2a placebo-controlled RCT in 102 patients with moderately active\nUC who had inadequate or lost response to previous therapies demonstrated significantly higher rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing at 8 weeks in the treatment compared to placebo group, as well as no serious adverse events, including PML.74 A smaller trial in CD using significantly lower doses showed no significant difference in CDAI at week 4 for carotegrast methyl compared to placebo, although the results were published in abstract form only.75 A phase 3 trial in UC is ongoing (NCT03531892).\n...\nOntamalimab is a subcutaneously administered fully human IgG2κ anti-MAdCAM-1 mAb that blocks α4β7 binding to the MAdCAM-1 ligand,76 with no observed effects on cerebrospinal fluid lymphocytes.77 The OPERA phase 2 placebo-controlled RCT in patients with active moderate to severe CD enrolled 265 patients with inadequate or lost response to previous therapies.78 The primary endpoint was reduction in CDAI at week 8 or 12, which was not significantly different between treatment and placebo; however, the remission rate was higher amongst patients with a higher baseline CRP.\n...\nAntibody blockade of leukocyte trafficking to the gastrointestinal tract with anti-integrin therapy (anti-α4β7) is effective for patients with IBD and is associated with favorable safety profiles.", "date": "2021-08-24", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9167}, "jev_score": 0.8892, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Medicine Vol.65", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6495/b5ef74c5b2f46901ddb86ce97a93ccddb0b8.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Currently, antibody preparations targeting α4β7 integrin (vedolizumab) and an oral α4 integrin inhibi- tor (carotegrast methyl) are available in Japan (Figure).\n...\nEtrolizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody\n(mAb) that targets the β7 integrin subunit, inhibiting both α\n4β7-MAdCAM-1\nand\nαEβ7-with\nE-cadherin\ninterac-\ntions (7).\n...\nIn a phase II study, the subcutane- ous administration of etrolizumab demonstrated efficacy in inducing remission in UC patients (8).\nPhase III trials, com- prising multiple randomized controlled trials, are currently underway in patients with moderately to severely active UC and CD.\nThese include HIBISCUS I and II (etrolizumab versus adalimumab or placebo as induction therapy for mod- erately to severely active UC), GARDENIA (etrolizumab versus infliximab for the treatment of moderately to severely active UC), LAUREL (etrolizumab as maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active UC), HICK-\nORY (etrolizumab as induction and maintenance therapy for\nUC in patients previously treated with tumor necrosis factor inhibitors), and BERGAMOT (etrolizumab as induction and maintenance therapy in patients with moderately to severely active CD).\n...\nAbrilumab is a fully humanized IgG2 mAb that targets α\n4β7 and functions through a mechanism of action similar to\nthat of vedolizumab (10).\nA phase II study evaluated in pa- tients with moderate-to-severe UC refractory to anti-TNF-α and immunomodulatory therapies.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-15", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8967}, "jev_score": 0.8698, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel Therapies and Approaches to Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2077-0383/11/15/4374", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "For Etrolizumab (anti-β7), there are several phase 3 clinical trials evaluating its efficacy (UC: Hibiscus I and II, Gardenia, Laurel, Hickory; CD: Bergamot) and two open-label extension trials (UC: Cottonwood; CD: Juniper).\n...\nAbrilumab (anti α4β7, AMG-181/MEDI-7183) was evaluated in phase 2 studies in UC, achieving a significant clinical remission rate at week 8 compared with a placebo [9], unfortunately in CD patients, the primary endpoint (clinical remission at week 12) was not met [10].\nTo date, phase 3 trials for abrilumab have not been registered.\nCarotegrast (anti α4, AJM300) is an oral drug tested in phase 2 trials for UC, demonstrating significantly higher rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing at 8 weeks compared with the placebo group [11], and there is an ongoing phase 3 trial in UC [12].\n...\nFinally, PN-943 and PTG-100, two oral anti α4β7 drugs evaluated in UC patients [16], seem promising and there are ongoing trials evaluating both PTG-100 [17] and PN-943 (IDEAL study) [18].", "date": "2022-07-28", "last_updated": "2024-12-23", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.864, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC6537282New biologics and small molecules in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6537282/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In this context, several new drugs have recently been tested in phase II or III\ntrials in patients with IBD (Table 1).\n...\n|Anti-integrin|Abrilumab|s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting α4β7|Phase II|Phase II|\n| |AJM-300|Oral|Small molecule that blocks the α4 integrin subunit|–|Phase II|\n| |Etrolizumab|s.c.|Humanized mAb targeting the β7 subunit|–|Phase II|\n...\n| |PTG-100|Oral|Small molecule that targets the α4β7 integrin|–|Phase II|\n...\n| |Ustekinumab^ a ^|i.v. and s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting the p40 subunit of IL-12/IL-23|Phase III|Phase II|\n...\nPhase III trials in patients with Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03345836 and NCT03345849] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03653026, NCT02819635, and NCT03006068] are ongoing.\n...\nintegrin ligand, α4ß7, results in the recruitment of lymphocytes into the gut or\ngut-associated lymphoid tissue.^\n30 ^ Natalizumab, an anti-α4 integrin antibody, and vedolizumab, an anti-α4ß7 integrin antibody, are efficacious in the treatment of IBD.\n...\nPTG-100 is an oral small molecule that targets the α4β7 integrin, hereby being\ngut selective.\n...\n40 ^ An extensive phase III program is ongoing [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02118584, NCT02100696, NCT02165215, NCT02394028, NCT02403323], including head-to-head trials with adalimumab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02171429 and NCT02163759] and infliximab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02136069].\n...\nPhase III programs in Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03627091, NCT03566823, and NCT03559517] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03259308, NCT03259334, and NCT03290781] are ongoing.\nAbrilumab, formerly known as AMG-181 or MEDI 7183, is a monoclonal antibody\nagainst the α4β7 integrin.", "date": "2019-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.87}, "jev_score": 0.8352, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Role of biologics and biosimilars in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5961645/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, monoclonal antibodies against tumor necrosis factor-alpha (infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, and golimumab), integrins (vedolizumab and natalizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12 and IL-23 antagonists (ustekinumab) are approved for use in IBD.\n...\nSome of the biologics in the Phases II and III of their development along with their mechanism of action as registered in the global clinical trial registry (www.clinicaltrials. gov) for use in IBD are summarized in Table 2.\n...\n|Serial number|Agent name|Mechanism of action|Current status|\n|--|--|--|--|\n|1|Etrolizumab|α4β7 integrin antagonist|Phase III (UC and Crohn’s disease)|\n|2|Risankizumab|IL-23 inhibitor|Phase III (Crohn’s disease)|\n...\n|11|SHP 647|α4β7 integrin antagonist|Phase II (Crohn’s disease) and Phase III (UC)|\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized MAb, which was approved by the FDA in July 2014 after a priority review.\nIt is essentially an integrin α4β7 receptor antagonist, which blocks the binding of Mucosal Addressin Cell Adhesion Molecule-1 (MAdCAM-1) to the integrin receptor, thereby producing anti-inflammatory action by preventing T-cell adhesion and infiltration.\n^53^ Since integrin α4β7 receptors are found more in the gut epithelium, vedolizumab is indicated currently for moderate-to-severe CD and UC.^54^ Clinical trials in pediatric population are also ongoing for UC and CD since November 2017.^55^^,^^56^ The agent is available in the market as 300 mg of lyophilized cake in a single-dose 20 mL vial for reconstitution.", "date": "2018-05-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8567}, "jev_score": 0.8139, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "IBD therapeutics: what is in the pipeline? - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9234727/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Etrolizumab is a gut selective monoclonal antibody (mAb) that selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and α4E7 integrins.^9^ The HICKORY phase III study showed 105 mg 4 weekly etrolizumab-induced remission was significantly higher in patients with UC (18.5%) previously treated with anti-TNF agent at week 14 vs placebo (6.3%).\n...\nCarotegrast methyl (AJM300) is an oral small molecule inhibitor that targets the α4 subunit of α4β7 and α4β1.", "date": "2022-06-15", "last_updated": "2025-09-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7505, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "What Is The Best Medicine For Crohns Disease Explained", "url": "https://bookings.hants.gov.uk/personalized-medicine-3", "domain": "bookings.hants.gov.uk", "snippet": "Vedolizumab, targeting α4β7 integrin, is particularly effective in ileocolonic disease with minimal systemic immunosuppression.\nJAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib) provide an oral alternative but are reserved for refractory cases due to safety concerns.\n...\n|**Vedolizumab (Entyvio)**|Humanized monoclonal antibody against α4β7 integrin|2014 (FDA) / 2014 (EMA)|- Arthralgia, nasopharyngitis - Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) (rare, <0.1%) - Delayed efficacy (up to 14 weeks for full response) - Minimal systemic immunosuppression (lower infection risk vs. anti-TNFs)|\n...\nJAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) - Mechanism: Inhibit JAK1/3 signaling pathways, blocking cytokine-mediated inflammation (e.g., IL-6, IFN-γ, TNF-α).\nTofacitinib (Xeljanz®) and upadacitinib (Rinvoq®) are FDA/EMA-approved for moderate-to-severe CD, with upadacitinib demonstrating higher endoscopic remission rates (40–50% vs. 20–30% for placebo) in Phase 3 trials (U-ENDURE, U-EXCEED).\n...\nSuccess varies by mechanism, with immunomodulators (e.g., JAK inhibitors) showing higher Phase 3 conversion rates (~60–70%) than microbiome-based therapies (~30–40%).", "date": "2025-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.5756, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Next generation therapeutics for inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5901649/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This, along with the overall increase in clinical remission rates and treatment effect size by week 32, would suggest that treatment efficacy is time dependent, and extended treatment may be associated with improved healing.[9] Phase 3 trials in ulcerative colitis and Phase 2 trials in Crohn’s disease are currently underway and should help to address this question.\n...\nVedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that targets the α4β7 integrin, was approved for use in UC and CD, and is now widely used in routine practice.[10, 11] (Tables 1 and 2) For UC, treatment outcomes in clinical practice have mirrored those seen in the RCT with rates of clinical remission after induction ranging from 24% - 36%.[\n...\nNonetheless, these data are quite promising and phase 3 trials in UC and CD are currently underway, including a head to head comparison against adalimumab.\n...\nTofacitinib, an oral small molecule that inhibits JAK 1 and 3 (and JAK 2 at higher doses), has undergone phase 2 and 3 studies in UC.", "date": "2014-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-16", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.61}, "jev_score": 0.5002, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 4069.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:14+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q011-3", "row_id": "q011", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor abrilumab current status IBD", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "search_latency_ms": 2205.2, "target": "drugs competing with α4β7 Inhibitor / α4β7: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 497.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10670, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000448, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8601, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Medicine Vol.65", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6495/b5ef74c5b2f46901ddb86ce97a93ccddb0b8.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Currently, antibody preparations targeting α4β7 integrin (vedolizumab) and an oral α4 integrin inhibi- tor (carotegrast methyl) are available in Japan (Figure).\n...\nAbrilumab (AMG 181)\nAbrilumab is a fully humanized IgG2 mAb that targets α\n4β7 and functions through a mechanism of action similar to\nthat of vedolizumab (10).\nA phase II study evaluated in pa- tients with moderate-to-severe UC refractory to anti-TNF-α and immunomodulatory therapies.\nThe participants received subcutaneous administration of abrilumab at doses of 7, 21, or 70 mg on day 1, week 2, and week 4, followed by ran- domization every 4 weeks to receive either abrilumab (210\nmg) on day 1 or placebo.\nThe clinical remission rates at\nweek 8 were 98 (13.3%), 79 (12.7%), and 116 (4.3%) (p�\n0.05) for abrilumab 70 mg, 210 mg, and placebo, respec-\ntively.\nSerious adverse events were not observed.\nThe most commonly observed adverse events in both groups included injection site reactions, nasopharyngitis, headaches, and ar- thralgia (11).\nAnother phase II study assessed abrilumab in patients with CD, randomizing participants to receive pla- cebo or subcutaneous abrilumab at doses of 21 mg or 70 mg on day 1, week 2, and week 4, every 4 weeks for 24 weeks, or a single 210 mg dose on day 1.\nHowever, the study did not meet its primary endpoint and showed no significant dif- ference in clinical remission rates compared with the pla- cebo group (12).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-15", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8867}, "jev_score": 0.8601, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-integrin therapy for inflammatory bowel disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5937204/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Vedolizumab produces few systemic adverse effects because it acts on gut-trophic α4β7 integrin, and has been approved and is being used to treat IBD.\n...\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different integrins-related targets, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 have also been developed and are in clinical trials.\n...\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181/MEDI 7183)|Fully human IgG~2~ mAb|α4β7-integrin|*s.c*.|Phase IIb|Induction in UC|\n...\nVedolizumab was approved by the FDA and the European Medicines Agency, for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and CD adult patients which are not responding to one or more conventional treatment such as steroids, immunosuppressive agents, or TNF antagonists.\n...\nAbrilumab (AMG 181/MEDI 7183) is a fully human monoclonal IgG2 antibody against α4β7 integrin that has recently been used in several clinical trials.\nIn a phase IIb study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of abrilumab in 354 moderate-to-severe UC patients who showed an inappropriate response or LOR to anti-TNFs, immunomodulators, or corticosteroid therapy, patients were divided into a placebo group, groups receiving subcutaneous abrilumab 7, 21, or 70 mg at weeks 0, 2, and 4, followed by its administration once every four weeks, and a group receiving a single subcutaneous 210 mg dose of abrilumab[44].\nThe primary endpoint, which was remission rate at week 8, was 1.6%, 2.9%, 13.5%, and 13.4% in the abrilumab 7 mg, 21 mg, 70 mg, and 210 mg groups, respectively, and was 4.4% in the placebo group; the abrilumab 70 mg group (*P* = 0.021) and 210 mg group (*P* = 0.030) both showed a significantly higher remission rate than the placebo group.\n...\nNo PML or severe adverse events were observed in the abrilumab groups through week 24 and no patients developed neutralizing antibodies to abrilumab.\nThus, abrilumab showed advantageous pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, very low immunogenicity, and an acceptable safety profile; further results are expected in the future.\n...\nAdverse events up to week 24 were the same in the abrilumab groups and the placebo group, and there were no cases of PML or death in any of the abrilumab groups.\n...\nVDZ has been approved and is currently used in clinical practice.\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different targets associated with integrin, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 are also being developed and currently undergoing clinical trials.", "date": "2018-05-07", "last_updated": "2026-08-20", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8933}, "jev_score": 0.8486, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov · articles · PMC13453429Integrin α4β7: Pathogenic Roles in Metabolic and Inflammatory...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13453429/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^23^ Current research on integrin α4β7 primarily focuses on its association with IBD.\nIt is considered a validated therapeutic target for IBD treatment, with numerous reports on the use of α4β7 antagonists, such as vedolizumab.^24^ However, there is currently a lack of systematic summaries of its role in other inflammatory and metabolic diseases, as well as elucidations of the mechanisms by which α4β7 contributes to these diseases, including IBD.\n...\n^86^ Anti-α4β7 integrin therapy can alleviate colitis symptoms by reducing intestinal inflammation and improving the function of the intestinal epithelial barrier.\n...\nSeveral monoclonal and small-molecule antibodies targeting α4β7 have been developed.\nSome have been approved for clinical use, while others are still under investigation, primarily for the treatment of IBD, as well as for other diseases such as HIV and relapsing multiple sclerosis (RMS).\nBelow is a summary of agents that are currently approved or in clinical trials (Table 1).\n...\n|Vedolizumab|Intravenous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|UC, CD|Approved|\n...\n|Vedolizumab|Intravenous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|Chronic Pouchitis|Phase IV|\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183)|Subcutaneous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|CD|Phase IIb|\n|Abrilumab (AMG 181 or MEDI 7183)|Subcutaneous|Monoclonal Antibody|α4β7 integrin|UC|Phase IIb|\n...\nVedolizumab, a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody, primarily functions to selectively antagonize the α4β7 integrin by specifically blocking its interaction with MAdCAM-1.^167^ It was approved by the FDA in 2014 for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC and CD in patients who are refractory or intolerant to TNF-α inhibitors,^167^^,^^168^ it is used for both induction and maintenance therapy.\n...\nAbrilumab (also known as AMG 181 or MEDI7183) is a fully human IgG2κ monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks α4β7-MAdCAM-1 interactions to inhibit lymphocyte gut homing without binding to α4β1 or αEβ7 integrins, thereby preserving systemic immune surveillance.\n^176^ Administered via subcutaneous injection, Abrilumab was evaluated in a pivotal Phase IIb UC trial (NCT01694485) involving 354 patients using regimens including 7 mg, 21 mg, 70 mg every 4 weeks, and a single 210 mg dose followed by placebo.\nAt Week 8, the 70 mg and 210 mg doses demonstrated significant efficacy, achieving clinical remission rates of 13.5% and 13.4%, respectively, compared to 4.4% for placebo.^177^ A separate Japanese Phase II study similarly showed numerically higher remission rates with these doses and confirmed a favorable safety profile with no PML cases.\n^178^ However, in CD, a Phase II study (NCT01696396) missed its primary endpoint of clinical remission at Week 8, with the 21 mg and 210 mg doses failing to show statistical superiority over placebo, and inconsistent results ultimately limited its further development.^179^ Although abrilumab demonstrated a favorable safety, immunogenicity, pharmacokinetic, and efficacy profile suitable for further UC testing, its development was discontinued in 2016.\n...\n^175^ While effective for moderately to severely active CD, earning FDA approval in 2008, concerns about PML restrict its use to second- or third-line therapy in patients with inadequate response to conventional or anti-TNF agents, and it should not be combined with other immunosuppressants.^180^ A Phase II trial in active UC demonstrated modest efficacy, including significant improvements in response and remission rates at Week 6, but safety concerns and competition from gut-selective agents like vedolizumab halted further UC-specific development.\n...\nThe development of α4β7-targeted therapies has yielded clinical success, most notably with vedolizumab in IBD, and continues to evolve with novel agents such as oral small molecules and peptide antagonists.\nHowever, challenges remain, including variability in therapeutic response, the need to preserve beneficial regulatory T cell populations, and the emergence of alternative homing pathways that may limit efficacy.", "date": "2026-08-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.83}, "jev_score": 0.7636, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Our Science", "url": "https://www.aviarapharma.com/ourscience", "domain": "aviarapharma.com", "snippet": "Vedolizumab and natalizumab, the leading biological drugs (antibodies) that are used to treat IBD, act principally on the a4- integrins; the former targets the integrin α4β7, whereas the latter targets both α4β1 and α4β7 integrins.\n...\nNevertheless, both α4β1 and α4β7 integrins are validated, high-value targets and continue to be investigated clinically with the risk of PML in mind.\nA newer α4β7-specific antibody, abrilumab (AMG181) has been shown to induce remission and mucosal healing in patients with moderate to severe UC without any incidence of PML.\nTwo orally delivered, selective antagonists of α4β7 are in clinical trials: MORF-057, a small molecule from Morphic Therapeutics has completed a phase I trial; however, it does not inhibit integrin α4β1 and may have the same non-responder, and secondary loss of response issues as vedolizumab.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7584, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Janus Kinases, Stem Cells, and More", "url": "https://karger.com/dig/article/101/Suppl.%201/69/103670/Emerging-Treatment-Options-in-Inflammatory-Bowel", "domain": "karger.com", "snippet": "The α4β7 integrin inhibitor vedolizumab is an established treatment in IBD, and long-term data of pivotal studies are now available.\nAdditional molecules with therapeutic potential are α4β7-specific abrilumab, β7-specific etrolizumab, and the α4-specific small molecule AJM300.\n...\nVedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against integrin-α4β7, is an effective and approved treatment for CD and UC [67, 68].\nLong-term effectiveness and safety data of the pivotal studies with up to 9 years of follow-up are now available, demonstrating a generally favorable safety profile, with nasopharyngitis as the most frequent side effect [69].\n...\nAbrilumab is another monoclonal antibody, inhibiting integrin-α4β7, which showed some efficacy for the treatment of UC (13.3% remission with 70 mg and 12.7% remission with 210 mg vs. 4.3% with placebo) [72].", "date": "2020-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7967}, "jev_score": 0.7489, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Advances in Therapy", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12325-018-0795-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=93e08bcb-943e-4265-9cfd-c0a51a461b0d", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Other agents are currently being developed at various stages of clinical trials including anti-adhesion agents such as etrolizumab and abrilumab, JAK inhibitors such as tofacitinib, and anti-trafficking molecules.\n...\nAMG 181 (abrilumab) is an IgG2 human monoclonal antibody that targets the α4β7 heterodimer, hence blocking the interaction with the MAdCAM-1 receptor.\nThe first human study was conducted in 68 healthy subjects and in 4 subjects with active UC (3 active drug, 1 placebo), investigating its pharmacokinetic properties [11].\nThe UC patients treated with the drug achieved remission with mucosal healing at different time points.\nNo treatment-related serious adverse events were observed.\nAfter several other pharmacokinetic (PK) and pharmacodynamic (PD) studies were done [12], AMG 181 was studied in IBD patients in phase 2b clinical trials with preliminary data reported to date in the form of abstracts.\nIn CD, patients were recruited to receive subcutaneous placebo, 21 mg, 70 mg, and 210 mg every 4 weeks.\nDespite the absence of statistical significance, higher rates of remission and response were observed in the active treatment arms at week 12, particularly in patients with prior failure of TNF antagonists assigned to the 210 mg abrilumab group (24.8% and 37.4%, respectively, for the treatment group and 8.2% and 14.2% in the placebo group) [13].\nThe drug was similarly studied in 354 patients with moderate-to-severe UC [14].\nCombined remission rates (the primary endpoint for the study) were 1.6%, 2.9%, 13.5%, and 13.4% in the 7, 21, 70, and 210 mg abrilumab arm, respectively, versus 4.4% in the placebo arm.\n...\nInterestingly, efficacy did not appear to correlate with peripheral target coverage or changes in α4β7-high T cells.\nFor both studies, adverse events were balanced among groups through week 24, with no cases of PML or mortalities reported.", "date": "2018-10-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-02", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7667}, "jev_score": 0.7207, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Phase 3 studies are currently underway in UC, CD as well as in paediatrics (including LUCENT-3, VIVID-1 and 2).^44-46^\n...\nEtrolizumab (PRO145223) is a humanized monoclonal antibody that selectively binds the *ß* ~7~ subunit of the heterodimeric integrins α~4~β~7~ and α~E~β~7~.\n...\nn which IL-22 is linked with the Fc portion of IgG4 allowing improvement of pharmacokinetic characteristics).^139^ New oral JAK inhibitors are also being developed such as brepocitinib (JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitor),^140, 141^ deucravacitinib (TYK2 inhibitor),^142-144^ ritlecitinib and OST-122 (JAK-3 inhibitors)^140, 141, 145^ and peficitinib (pan-JAK inhibitor).^146^ Regarding anti-integrins, abrilumab SC (AMG181) is an anti-integrin α~4~β~7~ with proven effectiveness in a phase 2b study in UC^147-149^ and PN-9\n…", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.7567}, "jev_score": 0.7037, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC6537282New biologics and small molecules in inflammatory bowel ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6537282/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, there are three classes of biologicals available to treat IBD.\nThese are\nantagonists to tumor necrosis factor (TNF), anti-integrins and inhibitors of\ninterleukin (IL) 12/IL-23.\n...\n|Anti-integrin|Abrilumab|s.c.|Fully human mAb targeting α4β7|Phase II|Phase II|\n...\nintegrin ligand, α4ß7, results in the recruitment of lymphocytes into the gut or\ngut-associated lymphoid tissue.^\n30 ^ Natalizumab, an anti-α4 integrin antibody, and vedolizumab, an anti-α4ß7 integrin antibody, are efficacious in the treatment of IBD.\n...\n40 ^ An extensive phase III program is ongoing [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02118584, NCT02100696, NCT02165215, NCT02394028, NCT02403323], including head-to-head trials with adalimumab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT02171429 and NCT02163759] and infliximab [ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02136069].\n...\nAbrilumab, formerly known as AMG-181 or MEDI 7183, is a monoclonal antibody\nagainst the α4β7 integrin.\nThe phase IIb RCT with patients with\nmoderate-to-severe Crohn’s disease failed to meet the primary endpoint (clinical\nremission, CDAI < 150, at week 8).^\n43 ^ In contrast, in the phase IIb RCT with 354 patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis, abrilumab had a favorable safety and efficacy profile.\nPatients randomized to the active-treatment groups of abrilumab 70 mg or 210 mg had significantly higher remission rates in comparison with the placebo group (13.5%, 13.4%, and 4.4%, respectively).^\n44 ^ Phase III programs in IBD populations are yet to be announced.\n...\n54 ^ A phase III program is ongoing in Crohn’s disease [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03104413, NCT03105128, and NCT03105102] and ulcerative colitis [ClinicalTrials.gov identifiers: NCT03398148 and NCT03398135].", "date": "2019-05-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.7467}, "jev_score": 0.6795, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 4.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12649877/table/biomedicines-13-02659-t004/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Abrilumab|Human IgG2 anti-α4β7 integrin dimer mAb|CD/UC|Phase II|Amgen|CD: NCT01696396 UC: NCT01694485|", "date": "2025-02-19", "last_updated": "2026-01-16", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.6248, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|Abrilumab|Human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin|NCT01694485, NCT01959165|Phase 2b|Global study completed; 13.3% remission (70mg) vs 4.3% placebo|\n...\nAbrilumab, a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, was evaluated in two Phase 2 studies for moderate-to-severe UC.\nThe global Phase 2b trial (NCT01694485) enrolled 354 patients aged 18–65 with Mayo Score 6–12 who had inadequate response or intolerance to conventional therapies, randomizing them to subcutaneous abrilumab at 7, 21, or 70 mg (day 1, weeks 2, 4, then every 4 weeks) or a single 210 mg dose versus placebo, with the primary endpoint of clinical remission at week 8 (Mayo Score ≤2, no subscore >1).\nResults showed significantly higher remission rates with abrilumab 70 mg (13.3%, OR 3.35, p=0.021) and 210 mg (12.7%, OR 3.33, p=0.030) versus placebo (4.3%), along with superior response rates (49.0% and 46.8% vs 25.9%) and mucosal healing rates (32.7% and 29.1% vs 21.6%), with good tolerability and no cases of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy or deaths (47).", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.5967}, "jev_score": 0.4654, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2703.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:14+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q011-5", "row_id": "q011", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as Small Molecule targeting ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7", "query": "α4β7 Inhibitor AJM300 natalizumab class comparison", "anchors": ["α4β7 Inhibitor", "α4β7"], "search_latency_ms": 2649.0, "target": "drugs competing with α4β7 Inhibitor / α4β7: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 365.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11012, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000463, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8384, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Old and New Biologics and Small Molecules in Inflammatory ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12285414/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Other investigational anti-integrin therapies include abrilumab (anti-α4β7 IgG2), PN-943 (orally administered and gut-restricted α4β7 antagonist peptide), AJM300 (orally active small molecule inhibitor of α4), and ontamalimab (anti-MAdCAM-1 IgG).\n...\n|Natalizmab|α4β1, α4β7|Humanized IgG1 mAb|IV|ENAC 1/2^15^ ENCOR^16^|CD|\n...\n|Carotegrast methyl (AJM 300)|α4β1, α4β7|Small molecule|Oral|Phase III^28^|UC|\n...\nNatalizumab은 비선택적 α4 antegrin에 대한 IgG1 단클론항체로 α4β7, α4β1을 차단하는 약제이다.^29^ Gut-specific α4β7/MAdCAM-1 작용을 차단함으로써 염증이 있는 장점막에 림프구가 축적되는 것을 막게 된다.\n...\n^16^ 하지만 이 약제는 α4β1 림프구의 중추신경계로의 이동 역시 억제하여,^30^바이러스에 대한 면역력을 감소시켜 심각한 부작용인 progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)를 유발할 수 있다.\n...\nVDZ는 α4β7 integrin에 대한 IgG1 인간화 단클론 항체로 장에만 특이적으로 발현되는 α4β7과 MAdCAM-1의 작용을 차단하여 항염증 효과를 나타내는 약제이다.\n먼저 개발된 natalizumab과 차별점은 장내 림프구의 이동만 선택적으로 차단한다는 점으로 중추신경계에는 영향이 없다.^33^\n...\nCarotegrast methyl은 α4를 억제함으로써 α4β7, α4β1을 차단하는 경구 소분자 약제이다.^65^ 2상 b 임상 연구에서 102명의 중등증 궤양성 대장염 환자를 대상으로 위약 대비 8주째 높은 임상 관해 및 점막 치유율을 보여줬을 뿐 아니라 PML과 같은 심각한 부작용의 발생은 없었다.", "date": "2024-08-25", "last_updated": "2026-01-07", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.8733}, "jev_score": 0.8384, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "2.4. Integrin αeβ7 (cd103)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6973243/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Natalizumab targets the α4 integrin and thus inhibits the interaction of α4β1 or α4β7 with VCAM‐1 or MAdCAM‐1, respectively.\nVedolizumab targets the α4β7 subunit and inhibits its interactions with MAdCAM‐1.\nEtrolizumab targets the β7 integrin and inhibits the interaction of α4β7 and αEβ7 with MAdCAM‐1 or E‐cadherin, respectively.\n...\n|Natalizumab|α4|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|In the market (limited use)|Biogen|102, 103, 104|\n|Vedolizumab|α4β7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|In the market|Takeda|109, 110|\n|Etrolizumab|α4β7; αEβ7|Anti‐integrin monoclonal antibody|Phase 3|Genentech|130, 131, 132, 133|\n|AJM300|α4|Small molecule antagonist|Phase 2a|Ajinomoto Pharmaceuticals|105|\n...\nThe use of small molecule α4 antagonists has continued to be of interest for the treatment of IBD.\nThe orally active small molecule α4 antagonist AJM300 is one such agent currently in development.105 In a double‐blind, placebo‐controlled, phase 2a study in patients with moderately active UC, AJM300 improved all measured indices of IBD compared with placebo, including increased rates of clinical remission and mucosal healing, with no reported serious adverse events.105\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized antibody which binds to α4β7 and was shown to specifically inhibit the interaction between α4β*7* integrin and its ligands, MAdCAM‐1 and fibronectin (Table 1).106 Given that MAdCAM‐1 expression is largely restricted to the intestine, the inhibitory effects of vedolizumab are regarded as gut selective.", "date": "2019-06-19", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.8467}, "jev_score": 0.779, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-integrin therapy for inflammatory bowel disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5937204/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-integrin therapy blocks the action of integrin on the surface of circulating immune cells and endothelial cell adhesion molecules, thereby inhibiting the interactions between leukocytes and intestinal blood vessels.\nNatalizumab, which acts on α4-integrin, was the first such drug to be approved for Crohn’s disease, but its use is limited due to the risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.\nVedolizumab produces few systemic adverse effects because it acts on gut-trophic α4β7 integrin, and has been approved and is being used to treat IBD.\n...\nNewer anti-integrin drugs that act on different integrins-related targets, such as AJM300, abrilumab, etrolizumab, and PF-00547659 have also been developed and are in clinical trials.\n...\n|Natalizumab|Humanized IgG~4~ mAb|α4-integrin|*i.v*.|ENCORE|Induction and maintenance in CD|\n|AJM300|Small molecule|α4-integrin|Oral|Phase IIa|Induction in UC|\n...\nNatalizumab is a chimeric recombinant human IgG4 antibody that targets the α4 subunit in α4β7 and α4β1 integrins on leukocytes.\nα4β1 integrin interacts with VCAM-1.\n...\nThus, natalizumab has been approved by the United States.\nFDA only in moderate-to-severe CD patients who did not respond to or were intolerant of conventional treatment or TNF inhibitor therapy; it has not been approved for use in Europe.\n...\nDespite safety issues for natalizumab, the oral α4 integrin antagonist AJM300 was developed and evaluated for use in UC.\n...\nwhen AJM300 960 mg or placebo was administered 3 times per day, the primary end point, which was the rate of clinical response (defined as a decrease of at least 3 points, and at least 30% compared to baseline, in the complete Mayo score, as well as a decrease of at\nleast 1 point for the rectal bleeding or an absolute rectal bleeding subscore of 1 point or less) at week 8, was significantly higher in the AJM300 group, at 62.7%, than in the placebo group, at 25.5% (*P* = 0.0002)[20].\n...\nIn this clinical study, serious adverse events did not occur, and adverse events were mild and self-limiting.\nHowever, considering that AJM300 shares the mechanism of natalizumab, and the number of subjects in this trial was small, and the study period was short, there are concerns about its practicality as a therapeutic drug.\nNevertheless, the duration of effect for AJM300 is very short compared to that of natalizumab, and since it is an oral formulation, there is some expectation that it may cause fewer systemic adverse events.\n...\nThe main difference between natalizumab and VDZ is that natalizumab inhibits leukocyte trafficking in multiple organs, including the brain, whereas VDZ acts specifically only on gut-trophic α4β7 heterodimers, and therefore, inhibits lymphocyte trafficking selectively in the intestine.", "date": "2018-05-07", "last_updated": "2026-08-20", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8067}, "jev_score": 0.7664, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "The Expanding Therapeutic Armamentarium for Inflammatory Bowel Disease: How to Choose the Right Drug[s] for Our Patients?", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/1/105/4093864?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "However, two new classes of biologic drugs, the integrin-antagonists for both UC and CD, and the interleukin [IL]-12/23-antagonist ustekinumab for CD, are now available.\n...\n4 Anti-adhesion molecules antagonise this interaction by targeting either a shared epitope of the α4β7 integrin heterodimer [vedolizumab, Takeda; AMG 181, Amgen] or either of its individual subunits: α4 in the case of natalizumab [Biogen] and AJM300 [Ajinomoto Co.\nInc.]; and β7 for etrolizumab [Genentech].\nNatalizumab and AJM300 also target α4β1, the ligand of vascular cell adhesion molecule [VCAM]-1, whereas etrolizumab also targets αEβ7, preventing the latter integrin from binding E-cadherin, which is expressed on epithelial cells.\n...\nFinally, AJM300 was evaluated at a dose regimen of 960 mg three times daily for 8 weeks, in a phase 2 study including 102 patients with moderately active UC.\n10 Rates of clinical remission [defined as a total Mayo Clinic score < 3 with no subscore > 1] and mucosal healing [defined as a Mayo Clinic endoscopic subscore ≤ 1] were 23.5% vs 3.9% [ *p* = 0.0099] and 58.8% vs 29.4% [ *p* = 0.0014] in the AJM300 group vs the placebo group, respectively.\nEndoscopic improvement was accompanied by significant histological improvement as evaluated by the Riley score.\n...\nThe major safety concern of nonselective anti-integrin agents such as natalizumab and AJM300 is their interference with T cell trafficking to the central nervous system as a consequence of blocking interactions between α4β1 positive lymphocytes and VCAM-1, which is expressed on brain endothelial cells.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7584, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Treatment Options in Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Janus Kinases, Stem Cells, and More", "url": "https://karger.com/dig/article/101/Suppl.%201/69/103670/Emerging-Treatment-Options-in-Inflammatory-Bowel", "domain": "karger.com", "snippet": "The α4β7 integrin inhibitor vedolizumab is an established treatment in IBD, and long-term data of pivotal studies are now available.\nAdditional molecules with therapeutic potential are α4β7-specific abrilumab, β7-specific etrolizumab, and the α4-specific small molecule AJM300.\n...\nNatalizumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting the integrin-α4 subunit, is an effective treatment of IBD [63].\nHowever, since the integrin-α4 subunit is also critical for α4β1-dependent central nervous system homing of lymphocytes, natalizumab treatment can be complicated by progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) due to JC virus reactivation [64].\nTherefore, natalizumab is only rarely, if ever, used for IBD treatment and only in patients with JC-negative serology or with a very strict surveillance framework [65, 66].\n...\nInhibition of the integrin-α4 subunit is also therapeutically useful since AJM300, a small-molecule integrin-α4 inhibitor, induced a response in 63% of patients versus 26% with placebo at week 8 in a randomized controlled study with 102 UC patients [76].", "date": "2020-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7474, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Frontiers in Drug Research and Development for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2017.00400/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "**Natalizumab** is a recombinant humanized anti-α4 subunit monoclonal antibody belonging to the class of IgG4κ, for i.v. use, approved for the treatment of patients with highly active relapsing remitting MS who did not get any benefit from at least one disease modifying treatment, and CD, as second line therapy in the USA.\nBy binding to the integrins α 4β 1 and α 4β 7 on lymphocyte plasma membrane, natalizumab inhibits lymphocyte homing to most organs and tissues.\n...\nAnother drug in clinical development that targets a component of the integrin family is\n**AJM300** (Table 1).\nSimilar to natalizumab, it binds to α 4 subunit, inhibiting the interactions of α 4β 1 with VCAM-1 and α 4β 7 with MAdCAM-1 and VCAM-1.\nHowever, differently from natalizumab, it is not a monoclonal antibody, but a small molecule, suitable for oral administration.", "date": "2017-06-23", "last_updated": "2025-04-23", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.79}, "jev_score": 0.7426, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin antagonists as potential therapeutic options for ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4926164/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Two agents, natalizumab and vedolizumab, are approved by the United States Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of Crohn’s disease, with vedolizumab the primary anti-integrin used due to a more favorable safety profile.\n...\nAnti-integrin agents, like tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α inhibitors, are biologic agents designed to target molecules that contribute to the development of intestinal inflammation.\nUnlike TNF-α inhibitors, which are antibodies directed against the pro-inflammatory cytokine TNF-α, anti-integrin therapy modulates inflammation by binding to integrins that contribute to leukocyte trafficking, thereby preventing leukocyte migration into GI mucosa and the development of inflammation within these tissues.\n...\nAt present, natalizumab and vedolizumab are the only two anti-integrin agents approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for use in CD.\n...\nThe first anti-integrin agent approved for use in patients with CD was natalizumab, a monoclonal antibody directed against the α4-integrin chain.\nBy targeting α4, natalizumab blocks both the α4β1 and α4β7 integrins.\n...\nAlthough natalizumab is effective for the induction of clinical response and remission in patients with moderate to severely active CD, its use is associated with the development of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a rare but often fatal neurologic disease.\n...\n21] More recently, the use of natalizumab has largely been supplanted by vedolizumab, which has similar efficacy to natalizumab,[23] but has not been linked to PML.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal IgG1 antibody that blocks the α4β7 integrin heterodimer without binding to the α4β1 integrin.\nBy only binding α4β7, vedolizumab prevents leukocyte extravasation into GI mucosa as mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecular 1 (MAdCAM-1), α4β7’s ligand, is expressed on the endothelial surface of venules and lymphoid tissue within the GI tract.[\n24] Unlike natalizumab, vedolizumab does not bind α4β1.\n...\nA subsequent meta-analysis comparing the efficacy of natalizumab and vedolizumab found that both agents have similar efficacy in inducing remission and response in anti-TNF-naïve and anti-TNF-exposed patients with similar safety profiles and an absence of PML in patients treated with vedolizumab.[23,30]\n...\nAJM300 is an orally administered humanized anti-α4 integrin antagonist, preventing α4β1 from binding VCAM-1 and α4β7 from binding MAdCAM-1.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.8067}, "jev_score": 0.7341, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "AJM300 (carotegrast methyl), an oral antagonist of Î±4- ...", "url": "https://www.darmzentrum-bern.ch/fileadmin/darmzentrum/Education/Journal_Club/2022/JC-22-04-27_AJM300__carotegrast_methyl___an_oral_antagonist_of.pdf", "domain": "darmzentrum-bern.ch", "snippet": "Background AJM300 is an oral, small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist.\n...\nAJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is a small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist, developed as an induction treat­ ment for patients with moderate ulcerative colitis.10 The\nactive \nmetabolite \nof\nAJM300 \ninhibits \nleukocyte \nextravasation into inflammatory sites by blocking the interaction of α4β1 or α4β7 integrins and their receptors,\nVCAM-1 and MAd-CAM-1.10 AJM300 shares the same mechanisms of action as natalizumab,11 which has not been used in patients with ulcerative colitis, but is approved for the treatment of Crohn’s disease in the USA.\nNatalizumab is associated with a risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a rare oppor­ tunistic brain infection caused by John Cunningham virus\n(JCV).\n...\nUnlike biologicals, AJM300 is not immunogenic, and intermittent or cyclic therapy can be considered in patients who respond to induction therapy with this drug.\n...\nIn conclusion, oral administration of the small-molecule α4-integrin antagonist AJM300 induced clinical response and endoscopic remission in patients with moderately active ulcerative colitis who had an inadequate response or intolerance to mesalazine.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-24", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.7167}, "jev_score": 0.6737, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Past, Present and Future of Therapeutic Interventions Targeting Leukocyte Trafficking in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/12/suppl_2/S633/5077920?guestAccessKey=", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Subsequently, the anti-α4 monoclonal antibody natalizumab proved efficacious for induction and maintenance of remission in Crohn’s disease, but was associated with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy due to its ability to interfere with both α4β1 and α4β7 function.\nLater developments in this area took advantage of the fairly selective expression of MAdCAM-1 in the digestive organs, showing that vedolizumab, a more specific monoclonal antibody selectively blocking MAdCAM-1 binding to integrin α4β7, was efficacious for induction and maintenance of remission in ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, and it was not associated with neurological complications.\n...\n|AJM300|SM|Integrin α4|UC|Phase II|Safe [8 weeks]|^65^|", "date": "2018-08-22", "last_updated": "2026-03-07", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6933}, "jev_score": 0.624, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral treatment with a novel small molecule alpha 4 integrin antagonist, AJM300, prevents the development of experimental colitis in mice☆", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/7/11/e533/430319?login=false", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "*Results:* HCA2969 selectively inhibited the *in vitro* binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.\nOral treatment with AJM300 dose-dependently inhibited lymphocyte homing to Peyer's patches and increased the peripheral lymphocyte count in the same dose range.\nAJM300 dose-dependently prevented the development of experimental colitis in mice.\n...\nThe maximum efficacy of AJM300 (1% diet) was comparable to that achieved by the saturated α4 integrin blockade with antibody.\n...\n9 In addition, α4 integrin blockade has been validated as a therapeutic approach for IBD in clinical studies with anti-α4 integrin antibody, natalizumab 10, 11 or anti-α4β7 integrin antibody, vedolizumab.\n...\nIn addition, the efficacy of the 1% AJM300M1 was comparable with anti-α4 integrin monoclonal antibody (PS/2) treatment at a dose of 10 mg/kg administered twice weekly (Fig.\n...\nThe maximum efficacy of AJM300 was comparable to that of the saturated α4 integrin blockade achieved by antibody treatment.\nThis study demonstrated that HCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, was a specific, dual α4β1/α4β7 integrin antagonist.\n...\n37 Whereas, the blockade of α4 integrin (α4β1/α4β7) leads to the inhibition of the lymphocyte migration mediated by the binding to MAdCAM-1 as well as VCAM-1.\nIndeed, an α4 integrin blockade with antibody (natalizumab) has successfully demonstrated the clinical benefit for the patients with multiple sclerosis (MS) 38, 39 as well as IBD.\n10, 11 Therefore AJM300, which has the same pleiotropic target as natalizumab and not gut-specific, could be effective for these diseases.\n...\nIn conclusion, these results suggest that oral treatment with the small molecule α4 integrin antagonist, AJM300 prevented the development of colitis and that its efficacy was comparable to that of the antibody to α4 integrin.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.6567}, "jev_score": 0.5779, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 3014.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:15+00:00"}
