n...\n- HAIC Plus Lenvatinib and Toripalimab for Advanced HCC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.7567}, "jev_score": 0.6281, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Lenvatinib for the Treatment of Recurrent Hepatocellular Carcinoma After Liver Transplant", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT05103904", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "No results posted yet for this study\n...\nThis phase II trial evaluates lenvatinib for the treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) that has come back (recurrent) after a liver transplant.\n...\n## Timeline & Regulatory\n- **Start**\n2022-04-19\n- **Primary Completion**\n2025-05-09\n- **Completion**\n2025-05-09\n- **FDA Drug**\nYes\n...\nNCT03713593 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3\n- A Study of Nofazinlimab (CS1003) in Subjects With Advanced Hepatocellular Carcinoma\nNCT04194775 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE3", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.74, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.5254, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 5651.4, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:15+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q013-2", "row_id": "q013", "mode": "queries", "objective": "SD-133 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "SD-133 tumour regression pancreatic cancer animal model", "anchors": ["SD-133"], "search_latency_ms": 2326.1, "target": "SD-133", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 339.2, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7928, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000333, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.0704, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Gene therapy in pancreatic cancer - Baishideng Publishing Group", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/1007-9327/full/v20/i37/13343.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "In animal models, *in vivo* gene transfer of foreign MHC-class-I *H-2K* gene into tumors successfully induced a cytotoxic T-cell response and attenuated tumor growth and caused complete tumor regression in murine models[122,123].", "date": "2014-10-07", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.16, "evidence": 0.44}, "jev_score": 0.0704, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] A targeted combination therapy achieves effective pancreatic cancer ...", "url": "https://iris.unito.it/retrieve/27cb417a-7392-429f-a93a-8184cf5e9320/Liaki-Barbacid_PNAS.pdf", "domain": "iris.unito.it", "snippet": "This study describes a \ntriple combination therapy made\nof daraxonrasib along with \nafatinib, an irreversible EGFR/\nHER2 kinase inhibitor and SD36, \na selective STAT3 PROTAC that \ninduces the robust regression of \nexperimental PDACs and avoids \nthe onset of tumor resistance.\nThis triple combination is well \ntolerated in mice.\n...\nHere, we demonstrate that genetic ablation of three independent nodes involved in down- stream (RAF1), upstream (EGFR), and orthogonal (STAT3) KRAS signaling pathways leads to complete and permanent regression of orthotopic PDACs induced by KRAS/TP53 muta- tions.\nLikewise, a combination of selective inhibitors of KRAS (RMC- 6236/daraxonrasib), \nEGFR family (afatinib), and STAT3 (SD36) induced the complete regression of orthotopic\nPDAC tumors with no evidence of tumor resistance for over 200 d posttreatment.\nTis combination therapy also led to significant regression of genetically engineered mouse tum- ors as well as patient- derived tumor xenografts (PDX) in the absence of tumor relapses.\nOf importance, this combination therapy was well tolerated.\n...\nMore importantly, we have extended these obser- vations to a pharmacological setting by demonstrating that combined inhibition of KRAS, EGFR, and STAT3 induces significant and long-lasting regression of these experimental tumors without inducing significant toxicities.\n...\nMoreover, they demonstrate that con- comitant ablation of three independent targets, Raf1, Egfr, and\n Stat3, results in the complete regression of advanced PDACs \nwithout the appearance of tumor resistance, one of the main issues thwarting the efficiency of targeted therapies.\n...\nSubsequent in vitro studies revealed that the combination of daraxonrasib (1 nM), afatinib (0.5 μM), and SD36 (0.5 μM) led to the complete death of PDAC cells in standard colony formation assays.\n...\nTo extend these results to an in vivo scenario, cells derived from GEM PDACs were orthotopically implanted in C57BL/6 mice as described above.\n...\ntumor- bearing GEM mice treated with the triple combination of daraxonrasib, afatinib, and SD36 displayed efficient regressions\nof all tumors enrolled in the study (n = 12), with half of them \ndisplaying complete regressions within 30 to 60 d of treatment \n(Fig. 5 A and B and SI Appendix, Fig. S9 and Table S1).\n...\nTe presence of the KRASG12D mutation in the PDX1-3 tumor \nmodels allowed us to replace daraxonrasib with the KRASG12D \nselective MRTX-1133 inhibitor ( 11 ).\nAs illustrated in  Fig. 6C ,\nthe combination of MRTX-1133 (30 mpk, ip, qd) with the same doses of afatinib and SD36 also led to the rapid and complete regression of the PDX tumors.\n...\nFinally, our triple combination therapy was equally effective in \ninducing tumor regression in PDX tumor models.\nReplacement of daraxonrasib by the KRASG12D selective inhibitor MRTX-1133 also led to complete regressions, indicating that the triple combi- nation maintains its therapeutic activity with different RAS inhib- itors.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-06", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.12, "evidence": 0.3567}, "jev_score": 0.0428, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy and targeted therapies in animal models of ...", "url": "https://publications.rwth-aachen.de/record/1029546/files/1029546.pdf", "domain": "publications.rwth-aachen.de", "snippet": "Importantly, even after adjusting for \npotential publication bias, treatment effects persisted (adjusted reductions: 49.1% for tumor volume, 36.9% for tumor weight).\nThese findings confirmed consistent tumor regression in mouse models of\n...\nLi, Y. et al.\nThe molecular mechanisms of a novel multi-kinase inhibitor ZLJ33 in suppressing pancreatic cancer growth.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-28", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.2733}, "jev_score": 0.0246, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncolytic viral therapy as a novel potential solution for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12301545/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "A modified vaccinia Ankara virus expressing murine survivin induces significant tumor regression and increased survival in mice when combined with gemcitabine (115).\n...\nIn an immunocompromised mouse model of diffuse pancreatic cancer, MYXV in combination with gemcitabine resulted in 100% long-term survival.\n...\nAn engineered measles virus expressing the sodium iodine symporter gene (MV-NIS) has demonstrated oncolytic activity in pancreatic tumor xenografts in mice with tumor regression and increased survival.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-17", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.23}, "jev_score": 0.0138, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Challenges and advances in mouse modeling for human ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3871863/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The authors found that the inactivation of mutant *Kras^G12D^* in initiated precursor lesions or during progression to cancer through simply suspending the doxycycline administration would result in regression of the lesions, indicating that mutant *Kras^G12D^* was required for not only the initiation of pancreatic tumor but the maintenance of established neoplastic lesions.\n...\nSimilar strategies have been employed to restore p53 tumor-suppressor function, which resulted in tumor growth inhibition and regression, supporting the rationale and benefits of novel therapies aiming to restore or mimic p53 function in tumors harboring altered p53 [260, 261].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.2367}, "jev_score": 0.0071, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Experimental models of pancreas cancer: what has been the impact ...", "url": "https://www.jci.org/articles/view/191945", "domain": "jci.org", "snippet": "Among the most frequently used mouse models of pancreas cancer are subcutaneous and orthotopic transplantation models (83) and genetically engineered mouse models (84–86).", "date": "2025-08-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.2567}, "jev_score": 0.0051, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Progress in Animal Models of Pancreatic Ductal ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6995380/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The *Tgfbr2* knockout combined with *Kras(G12D)* expression developed well-differentiated PDAC with 100% penetrance and a median survival of 59 days ^29^.", "date": "2020-01-14", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.2033}, "jev_score": 0.0041, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Modeling pancreatic cancer in mice for experimental therapeutics", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8570383/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "However, the treatment with gemcitabine (100 mg/kg) as adjuvant chemotherapy following the surgery significantly reduce the local recurrence and prolonged the survival of the treated mice [223].", "date": "2021-05-01", "last_updated": "2026-07-23", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.14}, "jev_score": 0.0028, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pathogenesis of Pancreatic Cancer: Lessons from Animal ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3926968/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Confirming that activated KRAS can induce PDAC in adulthood, *iKras* mice develop PanINs when doxycycline administration is delayed until 4–6 weeks of age (Collins *et al.\n...\nMore importantly, if doxycycline is subsequently withdrawn to downregulate *Kras^G12D^*, these PanINs disappear: early-stage PanINs appear to redifferentiate into normal exocrine pancreas tissue, while more advanced PanINs undergo apoptosis.\nAnd most important, when PDAC is induced by *Tp53* deletion in *iKras* mice, doxycycline withdrawal causes regression of both local and metastatic cancer (Collins *et al.\n* 2012; Ying *et al.* 2012).", "date": "2013-10-31", "last_updated": "2025-10-25", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.1367}, "jev_score": 0.0027, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Models in Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Neoplasms: Current Perspectives and Future Directions", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/15/15/3756", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "|RIP-TAG2, pl-PDGFB KO|2022|Islet Cell Carcinoma|RIP-Tag2, PDGFB|C57BL/6|Transgenic, Selective KO|8–14 Weeks|100%|None|[97]|", "date": "2023-07-25", "last_updated": "2026-08-13", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.02, "evidence": 0.0567}, "jev_score": 0.0011, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 2665.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:15+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q014-1", "row_id": "q014", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Sirpiglenastat in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "Sirpiglenastat PDAC monotherapy TGI xenograft", "anchors": ["Sirpiglenastat", "DRP-104"], "search_latency_ms": 19865.7, "target": "Sirpiglenastat / DRP-104", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 435.0, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9254, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000389, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8745, "results": [{"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting pancreatic cancer metabolic dependencies through glutamine antagonism", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s43018-023-00647-3", "domain": "nature.com", "snippet": "To overcome these limitations, recent efforts led to the creation of sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that circumvents the toxicities of DON while retaining the covalent and irreversible properties of DON 16, 17, 18, 19.\nEarly-phase clinical trials are examining the efficacy of DRP-104 as a single agent or in combination with immune checkpoint inhibitors for non-small cell lung cancer and squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck (ClinicalTrials.gov registration: NCT04471415) 20.\nIn this study, we demonstrated that both DON and DRP-104 caused a metabolic crisis in PDAC by globally impairing Gln metabolism, resulting in a significant decrease in proliferation.\nIn addition, we observed a significant reduction in tumor growth in several in vivo PDAC models using DRP-104 as a monotherapy.\n...\nThese preclinical results suggest that broadly targeting Gln metabolism could provide an alternative therapeutic avenue for PDAC.\n...\nMost importantly, DRP-104 treatment led to an accumulation of tumor Gln levels in vivo (Fig. 2a), supporting its on-target activity as an inhibitor of Gln use.\nRemarkably, DRP-104 significantly reduced tumor growth in two independent orthotopic syngeneic PDAC models after two cycles of treatment (Fig. 2b).\n...\nDRP-104 treatment significantly reduced liver colonization in two independent models (Fig. 2c,d).\nThese data indicate that DRP-104 effectively delays tumor growth and prevents metastatic colonization in PDAC.\n...\nTo better understand the impact of Gln antagonism in other clinically relevant models, we tested Gln antagonism in a set of patient-derived organoids (PDOs) and patient-derived xenografts (PDXs) (Fig. 4a).\nDON demonstrated a reduction in proliferation in a panel of PDAC PDOs (Fig. 4b–f).\nConsistent with the PDO data and the other models tested (syngeneic, immune-deficient), we observed a significant decrease in tumor growth in multiple PDX models (Fig. 4g–j).\nTaken together, these data suggest that DON/DRP-104 is broadly effective in decreasing PDAC growth across multiple models.", "date": "2023-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-04-06", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.8833}, "jev_score": 0.8745, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) Induces Antitumor Efficacy through Direct ...", "url": "https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/7AEkDz37/", "domain": "ouci.dntb.gov.ua", "snippet": "DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) was designed as a novel prodrug of the broad-acting glutamine antagonist 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-norleucine (DON).\nDRP-104 is an inactive form that is preferentially converted to DON within tumors.\n...\nFinally, DRP-104 demonstrated significant antitumor activity as a monotherapy, which was further enhanced in combination with checkpoint blockade therapies, leading to improved survival and long-term durable cures.\nIn summary, DRP-104 broadly remodels the tumor microenvironment by inducing extensive tumor metabolism effects and enhancing the infiltration and function of multiple immune cells distinct from those obtained by checkpoint inhibitor therapy.\nThis unique mechanism of action supports the ongoing clinical development of DRP-104 alone and in combination with checkpoint inhibitors.\n...\nAbstractPancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cells use glutamine (Gln) to support proliferation and redox balance.\n...\nIn addition, we observed a profound decrease in tumor growth in several in vivo models using sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that was designed to circumvent DON-associated toxicity.\n...\nCombinatorial treatment with DRP-104 and trametinib led to a significant increase in survival in a syngeneic model of PDAC.\nThese proof-of-concept studies suggested that broadly targeting Gln metabolism could provide a therapeutic avenue for PDAC.\nThe combination with an ERK signaling pathway inhibitor could further improve the therapeutic outcome.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-15", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.7293, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Metabolic plasticity in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-026-02620-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=92febd2b-4845-46a6-b60e-41f1e6fa270d", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "However, clinical use of DON is limited by systemic toxicity and poor pharmacokinetics.\nTo overcome these limitations, a tumor-targeted prodrug, DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat), was developed.\nDRP-104 shows strong antitumor and anti-metastatic effects in syngeneic and immunocompromised PDAC mouse models by blocking multiple Gln-dependent pathways involved in central carbon, nucleotide, and lipid metabolism [375].\nTh", "date": "2026-03-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-10", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7}, "jev_score": 0.686, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] November 2022", "url": "https://www.datocms-assets.com/92605/1675224915-dh-slide-deck-november-2022.pdf", "domain": "datocms-assets.com", "snippet": "A first-in-class diazo-prodrug for the treatment of solid tumors.\nSirpiglenastat\n(DRP-104)\n(Johns\nHopkins/Dracen\nPharmaceuticals)\nis\na\ndual\nprodrug of 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-norleucine (DON), a diazoketone-based glutamine irreversible antagonist used to suppress cancer cell metabolism.\n...\nAs a dual prodrug that is selectively activated in tumor cells and deactivated in GI tissues, sirpiglenastat has a significantly better therapeutic window than DON.\nIt has impressive preclinical efficacy, and as a monotherapy leads to durable anti- tumor immunity in mouse models in part by enhancing the function of CD8+ T cells.\nSirpiglenastat is currently being evaluated in a Ph.\nI/IIa (NCT04471415) study for the treatment of advanced solid tumors as a single agent and in combination with atezolizumab in metastatic NSCLC.\nIt also received an FDA Fast Track designation in October 2020 for the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer\n(NSCLC) with KEAP1, NFEL2 and/or STK11 mutations.\n...\nSirpiglenastat is activated to release DON in tumors but forms an inert metabolite in GI tissues.\nIn tumor cells, the tryptophan amide is hydrolyzed\nfirst by serine proteases before ester hydrolysis, liberating active DON (EC50 = 4.0\nµM).\n...\nWhile\nboth DON and sirpiglenastat led to complete regressions in a tumor model, sirpiglenastat was visibly less toxic to the GI, in contrast to the widespread ulceration seen after DON dosing.\nDurable responses to monotherapy.\nMice that were initially cured with sirpiglenastat monotherapy were implanted with the same tumors after 60 days,\nand\nthe\nmice\ncompletely\nrejected\nthe\ntumors,\nsuggesting\ndevelopment\nof\nimmunologic memory, mediated by T cells.\n...\nFinally, the in vivo antitumor activity of EG-011 was demonstrated in a mouse xenograft model\n(REC1 MCL cell line) with 2.2-fold reduction in tumor size following 200\nmg/kg QD dosing for 9 days, without significant signs of toxicity.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-17", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.67}, "jev_score": 0.6566, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical development of metabolic inhibitors for oncology - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8718137/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) is a tumor-targeted prodrug of the glutamine antagonist 6-diazo-5-oxo-l-norleucine (DON), which was identified in the 1950s as a potent anticancer agent (79).\nDON is a mechanism-based, irreversible inhibitor of all glutamine-utilizing enzymes (80), and thus broadly inhibits metabolic pathways that require glutamine as a nutrient source.\n...\nBy utilizing promoieties that are preferentially cleaved by tumor-enriched enzymes (83), sirpiglenastat is able to deliver DON preferentially to the tumor, increasing its therapeutic index.\n...\nThis disentangled process has also been noted with sirpiglenastat, which significantly inhibits tumor growth with a tandem increase in TILs (including T cells, NK cells, and TAMs) and decreased MDSCs in mice with syngeneic MC38 tumors.\nSirpiglenastat was found to be superior to anti–PD-1/PD-L1 monotherapy and showed antitumor synergy as a combination, resulting in long-term durable cures (86, 87).\nSirpiglenastat also demonstrated promising effects in a glutamine-dependent KRAS-mutant lung cancer model that carries KEAP1 mutations (88, 89) and reduced KEAP1-mutant tumor growth in both murine and patient-derived lung and squamous tumor models (88).\nThese data suggest that sirpiglenastat may be a promising therapeutic agent in patients carrying such mutations.\nSirpiglenastat is currently being evaluated in phase I/IIa clinical trials as a single agent and in combination with atezolizumab for the treatment of advanced solid tumors (90).\nRecently, sirpiglenastat received a fast-track designation for the treatment of advanced NSCLC patients whose tumors express mutations in KEAP1, NFE2L2, and/or STK11.", "date": "2022-01-04", "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6633}, "jev_score": 0.6434, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104, CAS Number: 2079939-05-0)", "url": "https://www.caymanchem.com/product/41293/sirpiglenastat", "domain": "caymanchem.com", "snippet": "Sirpiglenastat is a prodrug form of the glutaminase inhibitor 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-nor-leucine (DON; Item No. 17580).\nSirpiglenastat decreases P493B lymphoma cell viability (EC~50~ = 10 µM).\n*In vivo*, sirpiglenastat is metabolized to DON in tumor tissues, but metabolized to an inactive metabolite in gastrointestinal tissues, in EL4 murine T cell lymphoma, or MC-38 murine colon, EO771 murine breast, or 3LL Lewis lung carcinoma tumor-bearing *CES1^-/-^* mice, a model designed to more closely mimic human metabolism.\nIt reduces tumor size in EL4 tumor-bearing *CES1^-/-^* mice when administered at doses of 0.1, 0.3, and 1 mg/kg.\nWARNING This product is not for human or veterinary use.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-16", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6533}, "jev_score": 0.6402, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) / Dracen Pharma", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=6a9fca95-f7ac-4f45-ad29-5c613466dd20", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "Finally, analysis of KEAP1-loss associated genes also identified the activation of glutamine metabolism, and we found the combination treatment with the glutamine antagonist DRP-104 enhanced KRAS inhibitor-mediated growth suppression in PDAC patient-derived organoids.\nIn summary, our studies have established the molecular mechanisms underlying KEAP1-NRF2 pathway-driven resistance to KRAS inhibition and validated a potential therapeutic strategy to overcome this resistance.\"\n...\nThese findings demonstrate the therapeutic potential of sirpiglenastat in HNSCC and establish a novel link between glutamine metabolism and ferroptosis, which may be uniquely translated into targeted glutamine-ferroptosis combination therapies.\"", "date": "2025-10-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-25", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6567}, "jev_score": 0.6239, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 3. Various stromal components in PDAC, agents that target them and observed effects of targeting them.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12812828/table/table3/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Glutamine metabolism.|DON, Sirpiglenastat, L-asparaginase.|Inhibition of tumour growth *in vitro* [130]|", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-20", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6367}, "jev_score": 0.6049, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "JHU083 / Johns Hopkins University, AstraZeneca - LARVOL DELTA", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=39152d96-59ba-44e4-8cc3-4899dce58abd", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Glutamine Metabolic Blockade Reprograms the Tumor Immune Microenvironment in Triple-Negative Breast Cancer (SNMMI 2026) - \"DRP-104 (Sirpiglenastat), a prodrug of 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-norleucine (DON) with markedly reduced GI toxicity, is a potent glutamine antagonist currently being tested in clinical trials...Using a newly developed immuno-PET probe – [89Zr]-deferoxamine (DFO)-H1.2F3 (anti-murine-CD69 monoclonal antibody, Edwards KJ, et al... Our data strongly suggest that glutamine metabolic blockade differentially impacts MDSC vs. T cells, changing both the abundance and functional state of immune cells. In particular, JHU083 treatment depletes MDSCs and restores effector T-cell activity, shifting immune populations from an immunosuppressive, pro-tumor state to an immune-active, anti-tumor state.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.5672, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "sirpiglenastat - Drug Hunter", "url": "https://drughunter.com/molecule/sirpiglenastat", "domain": "drughunter.com", "snippet": "IV/SC glutamine antimetabolite prodrug\nPh.\nI/IIa in advanced solid tumors\ndual prodrug of natural product\n...\n|Company Code|DRP-104|", "date": "2022-12-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.5033}, "jev_score": 0.4882, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 20300.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:15+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q014-2", "row_id": "q014", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Sirpiglenastat in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "DRP-104 pancreatic cancer single-agent tumour regression", "anchors": ["Sirpiglenastat", "DRP-104"], "search_latency_ms": 2401.4, "target": "Sirpiglenastat / DRP-104", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 344.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10030, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000421, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9042, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting pancreatic cancer metabolic dependencies through ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824664/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In addition, we observed a profound decrease in tumor growth in several in vivo models using sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that was designed to circumvent DON-associated toxicity.\n...\nIn this study, we demonstrated that both DON and DRP-104 caused a metabolic crisis in PDAC by globally impairing Gln metabolism, resulting in a significant decrease in proliferation.\nIn addition, we observed a significant reduction in tumor growth in several in vivo PDAC models using DRP-104 as a monotherapy.\n...\nRemarkably, DRP-104 significantly reduced tumor growth in two independent orthotopic syngeneic PDAC models after two cycles of treatment (Fig. 2b).\n...\nDRP-104 treatment significantly reduced liver colonization in two independent models (Fig. 2c,d).\nThese data indicate that DRP-104 effectively delays tumor growth and prevents metastatic colonization in PDAC.\n...\nDON demonstrated a reduction in proliferation in a panel of PDAC PDOs (Fig. 4b–f).\nConsistent with the PDO data and the other models tested (syngeneic, immune-deficient), we observed a significant decrease in tumor growth in multiple PDX models (Fig. 4g–j).\nTaken together, these data suggest that DON/DRP-104 is broadly effective in decreasing PDAC growth across multiple models.\n...\nWhile there was substantial single-agent activity using DRP-104, residual tumors at the experimental end point displayed higher levels of proliferation markers compared to vehicle, suggesting that these tumors developed therapeutic resistance (Fig. 5a,b).\n...\nWhile both DRP-104 and trametinib reduced tumor growth as single agents, we observed a more substantial decrease in tumor growth in the combination treatment (Fig. 6b).\n...\nOur studies demonstrated a profound decrease in tumor growth in DRP-104-treated tumors in syngeneic PDAC models.\n...\nIndeed, combination treatment with DRP-104 and the MEK inhibitor trametinib reduced tumor growth and prolonged survival in syngeneic PDAC models.\nWhile survival was significantly improved in these proof-of-concept studies, we did not see evidence of complete tumor regression, suggesting that more potent combinations will be needed to ensure durable results.", "date": "2023-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.9133}, "jev_score": 0.9042, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting pancreatic cancer metabolic dependencies through glutamine antagonism", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s43018-023-00647-3", "domain": "nature.com", "snippet": "In this study, we demonstrated that both DON and DRP-104 caused a metabolic crisis in PDAC by globally impairing Gln metabolism, resulting in a significant decrease in proliferation.\nIn addition, we observed a significant reduction in tumor growth in several in vivo PDAC models using DRP-104 as a monotherapy.\n...\nRemarkably, DRP-104 significantly reduced tumor growth in two independent orthotopic syngeneic PDAC models after two cycles of treatment (Fig. 2b).\n...\nDRP-104 treatment significantly reduced liver colonization in two independent models (Fig. 2c,d).\nThese data indicate that DRP-104 effectively delays tumor growth and prevents metastatic colonization in PDAC.\n...\nConsistent with the PDO data and the other models tested (syngeneic, immune-deficient), we observed a significant decrease in tumor growth in multiple PDX models (Fig. 4g–j).\nTaken together, these data suggest that DON/DRP-104 is broadly effective in decreasing PDAC growth across multiple models.\n...\nWhile there was substantial single-agent activity using DRP-104, residual tumors at the experimental end point displayed higher levels of proliferation markers compared to vehicle, suggesting that these tumors developed therapeutic resistance (Fig. 5a,b).\n...\nWhile both DRP-104 and trametinib reduced tumor growth as single agents, we observed a more substantial decrease in tumor growth in the combination treatment (Fig. 6b).\n...\nIndeed, combination treatment with DRP-104 and the MEK inhibitor trametinib reduced tumor growth and prolonged survival in syngeneic PDAC models.\nWhile survival was significantly improved in these proof-of-concept studies, we did not see evidence of complete tumor regression, suggesting that more potent combinations will be needed to ensure durable results.", "date": "2023-10-09", "last_updated": "2026-04-06", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.891, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Discovery of DRP-104, a tumor-targeted metabolic inhibitor prodrug - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36383674/?otool=icznmelib", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Given its clinical potential, we designed DON peptide prodrugs and found DRP-104 [isopropyl(*S*)-2-((*S*)-2-acetamido-3-(1*H*-indol-3-yl)-propanamido)-6-diazo-5-oxo-hexanoate] that was preferentially bioactivated to DON in tumor while bioinactivated to an inert metabolite in GI tissues.\nIn drug distribution studies, DRP-104 delivered a prodigious 11-fold greater exposure of DON to tumor versus GI tissues.\nDRP-104 affected multiple metabolic pathways in tumor, including decreased glutamine flux into the TCA cycle.\nIn efficacy studies, both DRP-104 and DON caused complete tumor regression; however, DRP-104 had a markedly improved tolerability profile.\nDRP-104's effect was CD8^+^ T cell dependent and resulted in robust immunologic memory.\n...\nDRP-104 is now in clinical trials under the FDA Fast Track designation.", "date": "2022-11-18", "last_updated": "2025-10-06", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.8367}, "jev_score": 0.7949, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Discovery of DRP-104, a tumor-targeted metabolic inhibitor ...", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/1c1b/f1ab55bb591877f5fa22de6b465a80457d17.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "In efficacy studies, both DRP-104 and DON caused complete tumor regression; \nhowever, DRP-104 had a markedly improved tolerability profile.\nDRP-104’s effect was CD8+ T cell dependent and resulted in robust immunologic memory.\n...\nDRP-104 is now in clinical trials under the FDA Fast Track designation.\n...\nport here that DRP-104 administration to CES1−/− mice (a model recapitulating human prodrug metabolism) resulted in prodigious\n6- and 11-fold greater exposures of DON to the tumor versus plas-\nma and GI tissue, respectively, resulting in complete tumor re- gression without GI side effects.\n...\nLast, and most remarkably, we showed that mice previously cured with DRP-104 monotherapy completely rejected tumor rechallenge, indicating that glutamine antagonism therapy triggers immunologic memory.\nOn the basis of these promising preclinical data, DRP-104 is currently in clinical trials (NCT04471415) for treatment of advanced-stage solid tumors as a single agent and in combination with immunotherapy.\n...\nBoth DRP-104 and DON caused complete re- gression of the tumor (Fig. 4A).\nHowever, DON administration resulted in a substantial 20 to 30% body weight reduction, while\nDRP-104–treated mice maintained their body weight throughout the study duration (Fig. 4B).\n...\nDRP-104 resulted in a dose-dependent inhibition of tumor growth, with significant tumor regression observed at the highest dose (Fig. 5A).\n...\nFurthermore, we showed that tumor responses during DRP-104 monotherapy were CD8+ T cell dependent, and infiltrating T cells demonstrated phenotypic changes consistent with a long-lived, stem-like phenotype characteristic of a durable antitumor response", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-09", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.76}, "jev_score": 0.7448, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Beyond the tumor: Enhancing pancreatic cancer therapy through ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12240684/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^88^ , ^89^ Although, the effect of Gln antagonists on the TME of PC has been studied, its research in reversing the sensitivity of resistance to chemotherapy drugs in PC patients has not been reported, and more thorough exploration is still needed (Figure 2).\n...\nDRP‐104 affects multiple metabolic pathways in tumors, including reduced Gln flux into the TCA cycle.\n^108^ It starved cancer cells by mimicking Gln, broadly inhibiting all the metabolic pathway using Gln.\n^109^ Previous studies have shown that DRP‐104 treatment alone reduces the growth rate of PDAC in a mouse model of PC.\nThe study found that this process did not cause changes in the immune system, and the number of infiltrating T cells was very low regardless of DRP‐104 treatment, and DRP‐104 works independently of the immune system.\n^86^ As DRP‐104 is now for clinical trials, and researchers have great expectations for its mechanism of action to precisely target therapeutic targets, an effort to expand the treatment time window and bring hope for more patients.\n...\nIn the Phase II trial NCT03965845, adding CB‐839 (telaglenastat) to nab‐paclitaxel showed some synergistic activity but was limited by gastrointestinal toxicity and only small gains in progression‐free survival.\n^148^ DRP‐104, now in a Phase I/II study (NCT04471415), has been well tolerated so far with early signs of antitumor effect.\n...\n|Targeting Gln metabolism|Use of Gln antagonists such as DON and its prodrugs (e.g., JHU‐083, DRP‐104) to inhibit Gln‐dependent metabolic pathways in tumor cells|Reduces tumor growth and reshapes immune cells; enhances chemotherapy sensitivity|Drug resistance; potential systemic toxicity; limited data in PC‐specific applications|", "date": "2025-07-09", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.722, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Discovery of DRP-104, a tumor-targeted metabolic inhibitor prodrug", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9668306/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In efficacy studies, both DRP-104 and DON caused complete tumor regression; however, DRP-104 had a markedly improved tolerability profile.\nDRP-104’s effect was CD8^+^ T cell dependent and resulted in robust immunologic memory.\n...\nDRP-104 is now in clinical trials under the FDA Fast Track designation.\nDRP-104 is bioactivated in tumor while bioinactivated in the gut, providing robust anticancer activity with minimal toxicity.\n...\nWe report here that DRP-104 administration to CES1^−/−^ mice (a model recapitulating human prodrug metabolism) resulted in prodigious 6- and 11-fold greater exposures of DON to the tumor versus plasma and GI tissue, respectively, resulting in complete tumor regression without GI side effects.\n...\nLast, and most remarkably, we showed that mice previously cured with DRP-104 monotherapy completely rejected tumor rechallenge, indicating that glutamine antagonism therapy triggers immunologic memory.\nOn the basis of these promising preclinical data, DRP-104 is currently in clinical trials (NCT04471415) for treatment of advanced-stage solid tumors as a single agent and in combination with immunotherapy.\n...\n### DRP-104 was similarly efficacious to DON in inhibiting tumor growth but with a marked reduction in toxicity\nDRP-104 and DON (1 mg/kg DON equivalent, intravenously) were administered 5 days per week for 2 weeks to C57BL/6/CES1^−/−^ mice bearing EL4 tumors.\nBoth DRP-104 and DON caused complete regression of the tumor (Fig. 4A).\n...\nDRP-104 resulted in a dose-dependent inhibition of tumor growth, with significant tumor regression observed at the highest dose (Fig.\n...\nEfficacy evaluation at equimolar doses revealed that both DRP-104 and DON were equally efficacious in causing almost complete tumor regressions; however, most (63%) of the DON-treated mice required euthanasia based on Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) weight loss guidelines; in contrast, the DRP-104 mice maintained their body weight throughout the study.", "date": "2022-11-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.6967}, "jev_score": 0.6688, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Dracen Pharmaceuticals announces oral presentation at Festival of Biologics World Immunotherapy Congress 2022", "url": "https://www.prweb.com/releases/dracen_pharmaceuticals_announces_oral_presentation_at_festival_of_biologics_world_immunotherapy_congress_2022/prweb18544215.htm", "domain": "prweb.com", "snippet": "\"Sirpiglenastat has a novel mechanism of action, and in multiple preclinical models representing various tumor types, it demonstrated profound single agent effects including cures,\" said Robert Wild, PhD, Dracen's chief scientific officer.\n...\nOur lead glutamine antagonist, DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat), is currently in early-stage clinical development.\nThe mechanisms of action for DRP-104 include a) direct inhibition of tumor cell addiction to glutamine metabolism leading to substantial single agent activity and tumor regression; b) broad metabolic remodeling of the tumor microenvironment leading to enhanced anti-tumor immune activity; and c) stimulation of T effector, NK and NKT cells and inhibition of immunosuppressive MDSC and macrophage cells, leading to greater long-term durable responses and survival.", "date": "2022-03-09", "last_updated": "2025-02-21", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6733}, "jev_score": 0.6598, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Dracen Pharmaceuticals announces publication in Molecular Cancer Therapeutics", "url": "https://www.prweb.com/releases/Dracen_Pharmaceuticals_announces_publication_in_Molecular_Cancer_Therapeutics/prweb18944062.htm", "domain": "prweb.com", "snippet": "\"By broadly targeting all 10 glutamine-metabolizing enzymes, DRP-104 leads to profound anti-tumor activity related to both direct effects on tumor metabolism as well as immune mediated activity via remodeling of the tumor microenvironment and induction of both innate and adaptive anti-tumor immunity.\nThis unique mechanism of action shows promise treating various tumors including those resistant to IO checkpoint inhibitors.\nWe are excited to continue the clinical development of this differentiated agent both as a monotherapy and in combination with immune checkpoint blockade.\"\n...\nOur lead glutamine antagonist, DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat), is currently in clinical development.\nThe mechanisms of action for DRP-104 include a) direct inhibition of tumor cell addiction to glutamine metabolism leading to substantial single agent activity and tumor regression; b) broad metabolic remodeling of the tumor microenvironment leading to enhanced anti-tumor immune activity; and c) stimulation of T effector, NK and NKT cells and inhibition of immunosuppressive MDSC and macrophage cells, leading to greater long-term durable responses and survival.", "date": "2022-10-10", "last_updated": "2025-04-10", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6633}, "jev_score": 0.65, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "The impact of broad glutamine metabolism inhibition on ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10809087/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To overcome this issue, a modified prodrug of DON, DRP-104, has recently been developed.\nDRP-104 carries two pro-moieties, an isopropyl ester, and an acetylated tryptophan, that allow it to be bioactive in tumors where proteases are abundant, and bio-inactive in gastrointestinal tissues by carboxylesterases.\nFurthermore, DRP-104 enhances the efficacy of anti-PD-1 immunotherapy through a CD8^+^ T cell-dependent mechanism, establishing immunologic memory that allows for immune clearance of tumor cells upon rechallenge.\nLastly, DRP-104 is currently being investigated in a clinical trial (NCT04471415) in combination with anti-PD-L1 therapy for treatment in solid tumors.^1^\n...\nIn contrast, for cancer cells, JHU083 suppresses glucose uptake and diminishes glucose metabolism in glycolysis and the TCA, leading to tumor regression.^3^\n...\nIn summary, the use of glutamine antimetabolites has shown significant anti-tumor efficacy both as a standalone therapy and in combination with other treatments, such as EVax.\n...\nAlthough DRP-104 has been shown to suppress glutaminase, its *in vitro* metabolic and *in vivo* immunomodulatory effects differ significantly from glutaminase inhibitors, such as CB-839, which target only a single enzymatic pathway.^5^ Pre-clinical studies have shown potent anti-tumor efficacy when using JHU083 as a single agent, possibly due to the dependence of tumor cells on glutamine.\nDRP-104 is currently evaluated in phase 1 and phase 2a clinical trials for solid tumors, and the safety profile and patients' response to this promising metabolic drug will soon be revealed.", "date": "2023-06-20", "last_updated": "2025-09-11", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6072, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "DON/DRP-104 as innovative Plasma kallikrein inhibitors ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11344713/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Efficacy studies of DRP-104 against murine lymphoblastic lymphoma (EL4), breast cancer (E0771), colon cancer (MC380), and lung cancer (3LL) cell lines demonstrated clinically accepted tumor regression capabilities, preferential tumor delivery potential, reduced off-target toxicity, and enhanced synergy with anti-PD-1 therapy, accompanied by notable immunologic memory [25].\n...\nAlthough there are no current clinical reports detailing the half-life or longevity profile of DRP-104 in vivo, pharmacokinetic analyses conducted by Rais et al.", "date": "2024-06-13", "last_updated": "2025-10-12", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.6533}, "jev_score": 0.601, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2746.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:15+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q014-3", "row_id": "q014", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Sirpiglenastat in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "Sirpiglenastat pancreatic PDX monotherapy tumour volume", "anchors": ["Sirpiglenastat", "DRP-104"], "search_latency_ms": 960.6, "target": "Sirpiglenastat / DRP-104", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 362.5, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8224, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000345, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.748, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting pancreatic cancer metabolic dependencies through ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37814010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cells use glutamine (Gln) to support proliferation and redox balance.\nEarly attempts to inhibit Gln metabolism using glutaminase inhibitors resulted in rapid metabolic reprogramming and therapeutic resistance.\nHere, we demonstrated that treating PDAC cells with a Gln antagonist, 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-norleucine (DON), led to a metabolic crisis in vitro.\nIn addition, we observed a profound decrease in tumor growth in several in vivo models using sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a pro-drug version of DON that was designed to circumvent DON-associated toxicity.\nWe found that extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK) signaling is increased as a compensatory mechanism.\nCombinatorial treatment with DRP-104 and trametinib led to a significant increase in survival in a syngeneic model of PDAC.\nThese proof-of-concept studies suggested that broadly targeting Gln metabolism could provide a therapeutic avenue for PDAC.\nThe combination with an ERK signaling pathway inhibitor could further improve the therapeutic outcome.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-03", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.748, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat – Glutamine Antagonist for Cancer Research", "url": "https://www.benchchem.com/product/b10857805", "domain": "benchchem.com", "snippet": "This compound has demonstrated significant antitumor activity as a single agent in various in vivo murine models, which are critical for evaluating the therapeutic potential of new cancer drugs.\n...\nnih.govfrontiersin.org this compound has shown anti-tumor activity in PDX models of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC),\nparticularly those with specific genetic mutations.\ndracenpharma.com For instance, in a lung adenocarcinoma PDX model with both Kras and KEAP1 mutations, this compound demonstrated notable anti-tumor effects.\ndracenpharma.com It has also shown superior anti-tumor activity compared to GLS1 inhibition in KEAP1-mutant squamous cell carcinoma PDX models.\n...\nPancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is characterized by a high demand for glutamine to support its proliferation and maintain redox balance.\nresearchgate.net Preclinical studies using this compound have demonstrated a profound decrease in tumor growth in in vivo models of PDAC.", "date": "2025-11-27", "last_updated": "2025-11-27", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.7146, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Characterization and therapeutic suppression of KEAP1-NRF2-driven resistance to KRAS inhibitors in pancreatic and lung cancer - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13131474/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Finally, we observed that KEAP1-deficient cells exhibited elevated glutamine metabolism, and combination treatment with the glutamine antagonist DRP-104 (sirpiglenastat) enhanced KRAS inhibitor suppression of pancreatic and lung tumors.\n...\nMonotherapy with either DRP-104 or daraxonrasib exhibited limited anti-tumor activity.\nIn contrast, the combination of DRP-104 and daraxonrasib significantly suppressed the tumor growth (Fig.", "date": "2026-04-21", "last_updated": "2026-07-22", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.69}, "jev_score": 0.6693, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104), a broad acting glutamine ...", "url": "https://www.dracenpharma.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Festival-of-Biologics-2022-RWild-Final.pdf", "domain": "dracenpharma.com", "snippet": "DRP-104 alone showed potent single agent efficacy with tumor stasis during treatment\n...\nDRP-104 + anti-PD-1 treatment shows synergistic tumor growth inhibition\n...\nCombination treatment leads to significant improved OS and long-term durable cures (p<0.001 vs monotherapy)\n...\nCurrently completing Phase 1 dose-escalation – (IV and SC formulations)\n...\nPhase 2 Plan\n•\nFast Track Designation for KEAP1/NFE2L2/STK11 mutations in NSCLC (monotherapy)\n•\nMonotherapy in glutamine addicted CRPC and SCCHN\n•\nCombination with PD-(L)1 checkpoint inhibitors in NSCLC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-12", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6468, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104, CAS Number: 2079939-05-0)", "url": "https://www.caymanchem.com/product/41293/sirpiglenastat", "domain": "caymanchem.com", "snippet": "Sirpiglenastat is a prodrug form of the glutaminase inhibitor 6-diazo-5-oxo-L-nor-leucine (DON; Item No. 17580).\n...\nIt reduces tumor size in EL4 tumor-bearing *CES1^-/-^* mice when administered at doses of 0.1, 0.3, and 1 mg/kg.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-16", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6402, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical development of metabolic inhibitors for oncology.", "url": "https://europepmc.org/article/pmc/pmc8718137", "domain": "europepmc.org", "snippet": "### Broad glutamine antagonism with sirpiglenastat (DRP-104).\nSirpiglenastat (DRP-104) is a tumor-targeted prodrug of the glutamine antagonist 6-diazo-5-oxo-l-norleucine (DON), which was identified in the 1950s as a potent anticancer agent (79).\nDON is a mechanism-based, irreversible inhibitor of all glutamine-utilizing enzymes (80), and thus broadly inhibits metabolic pathways that require glutamine as a nutrient source.\n...\nSirpiglenastat was found to be superior to anti–PD-1/PD-L1 monotherapy and showed antitumor synergy as a combination, resulting in long-term durable cures (86, 87).\n...\nSirpiglenastat is currently being evaluated in phase I/IIa clinical trials as a single agent and in combination with atezolizumab for the treatment of advanced solid tumors (90).\nRecently, sirpiglenastat received a fast-track designation for the treatment of advanced NSCLC patients whose tumors express mutations in KEAP1, NFE2L2, and/or STK11.\n...\nAs a single agent, devimistat has been evaluated in multiple phase I studies, including in combination with modified FOLFIRINOX (mFOLFIRINOX) for pancreatic cancer (105, 106) and in combination with cytarabine/mitoxantrone for relapsed/refractory AML.\nIn pancreatic cancer, devimistat plus mFOLFIRINOX exhibited a 61% overall response rate (ORR) (105).\n...\nCurrent phase II/III clinical trials of devimistat include combinations with mFOLFIRINOX (108) in metastatic pancreatic cancer and with cytarabine/mitoxantrone in older adults with relapsed/refractory AML (109).", "date": "2022-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-06-08", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.64}, "jev_score": 0.5952, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 2.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12571628/table/T2/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|ASNS|Sirpiglenastat|DRP-104|solid tumors|PHASE1|PHASE2|NCT04471415|TERMINATED|61|(294)|", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-11-02", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.92, "evidence": 0.4667}, "jev_score": 0.4294, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) | Glutaminase antagonist", "url": "https://www.selleckchem.com/products/sirpiglenastat.html", "domain": "selleckchem.com", "snippet": "Sirpiglenastat (DRP-104) is a broad acting **glutamine** antagonist.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.3233}, "jev_score": 0.3136, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "PDC000198 - Proteomic Data Commons - National Cancer Institute", "url": "https://proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov/pdc/study/PDC000198", "domain": "proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov", "snippet": "Warning\nThis warning banner provides privacy and security notices consistent with applicable federal laws, directives, and other federal guidance for accessing this Government system, which includes (1) this computer network, (2) all computers connected to this network, and (3) all devices and storage media attached to this network or to a computer on this network.\nThis system is provided for Government-authorized use only.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.12, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0004, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/clincancerres/article-pdf/23/3/623/2041560/623.pdf", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Cloudflare is currently unable to resolve your requested domain (aacrjournals.org).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"