up/Arm|3 mg of mocravimod orally once per day for 12 months|Intervention/Treatment||\n|Participant Group/Arm|1 mg of mocravimod orally once per day for 12 months|Intervention/Treatment||\n|Participant Group/Arm|placebo orally once per day for 12 months|Intervention/Treatment||\n...\n|--|--|--|\n|Relapse-free survival (RFS)|To compare the efficacy of mocravimod to that of placebo|12 months|\n...\n|Overall survival (OS)|To compare mocravimod's effect on overall survival (OS) to that of placebo|24 months|", "date": "2022-06-17", "last_updated": "2026-01-22", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.4, "evidence": 0.0467}, "jev_score": 0.0187, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 4337.5, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:20+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q022-1", "row_id": "q022", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication Novel UC Treatment, Cosmo Health, Distal Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Novel UC Treatment mechanism of action ulcerative colitis", "anchors": ["Novel UC Treatment"], "search_latency_ms": 1856.9, "target": "drugs competing with Novel UC Treatment: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 392.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10238, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00043, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5925, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis: Today, Tomorrow, and the Future", "url": "https://www.emjreviews.com/gastroenterology/symposium/ulcerative-colitis-today-tomorrow-and-the-future/", "domain": "emjreviews.com", "snippet": "Emerging therapies for UC currently undergoing Phase III development fall under four main mechanisms of action: leukocyte retention in lymphoid organs by S1P receptor modulators (etrasimod and ozanimod), IL-23 inhibitors (mirikizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab, and risankizumab), JAK/TYK2 inhibitors (filgotinib and upadacitinib), and leukocyte adhesion inhibitors (integrin blockers and etrolizumab).^35^\n...\nThe leukocyte adhesion inhibitor etrolizumab selectively targets the β7 subunit of both α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins and blocks interactions with their respective ligands, mucosal vascular addressin cell adhesion molecule 1 and E-cadherin, to reduce gut-specific lymphocyte trafficking to the inflamed colon.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.6733}, "jev_score": 0.5925, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This includes agents with alternative routes of administration such as oral or subcutaneous tumor necrosis factor inhibitors or novel mechanisms of action such as toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist cobitolimod and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor apremilast.\n...\nThese small molecules include Janus Kinase (JAK) inhibitors such as tofacitinib, upadacitinib and filgotinib;\n...\nEtrolizumab is an SC humanized monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity.25 It blocks binding to their ligands MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin resulting in inhibition of leukocyte trafficking to the gut and inhibition of retention of leukocytes, respectively.26 Studies on animal models indicate that the medication selectively targets lymphocyte homing on mucosal surfaces with no impact on migration to non-mucosal tissues.26\n...\nHCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, selectively inhibits binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.31 It shares the same mechanism of action as natalizumab.\n...\nMirikizumab is an IgG4-variant monoclonal antibody that binds to the p19 subunit of IL23.\n...\nCobitolimod is a topical toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist.\nIt results in the activation of TLR9 on lymphocytes and antigen presenting cells which lead to induction of regulatory T-cells, eventual production of IL-10 and suppression of TH-17 cells.", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.6333}, "jev_score": 0.551, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Pathological mechanism and targeted drugs of ulcerative colitis", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10508406/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The PI3K/Akt signaling pathway is also involved in the regulation and release of inflammatory factors, and it can indirectly activate the transcription factor NF-κB through phosphorylated IKK, thus interconnecting with the NF-κB pathway to promote enhanced expression and secretion of inflammatory factors, leading to damage of the colonic mucosa.\n...\nThe resulting targeted therapeutic agents include anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies, integrin antagonists, IL-12/IL-23 antagonists, JAK inhibitors, and SIP receptor agonists (Table 1).\n...\nVedolizumab (VDZ) is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks the binding of α4β7 integrin to MAdCAM-1 of intestinal endothelial cells, reducing leukocyte adhesion to the intestinal epithelium and has high intestinal selectivity, which can better promote intestinal mucosal repair and thus effectively induce symptom relief in UC patients.\n...\n^[113]^ Ustekinumab (UST) is a fully human IgG-type monoclonal antibody that inhibits IL-12 signaling and further activation of the Th1 subpopulation of T cells by binding to the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, and, at the same time, blocks IL-23-mediated immune responses and activation of the Th17 subpopulation of downstream T cells.\n...\n^[118]^ Animal and human organoid experiments^[119,120]^ found that Tofacitinib may exert clinical effects by reducing the activity of the JAK signaling pathway, disrupting the normalization of tight junction protein expression, correcting the function of PTPN2 in macrophages or intestinal epithelial cells, and restoring the integrity of the epithelial barrier.\n...\nSIP is a membrane-derived lysophospholipid signaling molecule that specifically binds G protein-coupled receptors (SIPR1-SIPR5), and SIP receptor agonists target lymphocyte recirculation and block lymphocyte migration from lymphoid organs to the intestine along a chemotactic gradient, thereby effectively reducing intestinal inflammation,^[127]^ leading to the therapeutic drugs Ozanimod and Etrasimod.", "date": "2023-09-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.58}, "jev_score": 0.522, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nThe introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\nIvarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC.\n...\nIntegrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium.\n...\nCXCR2 antagonists block neutrophil recruitment and activation driven by IL-8 and related chemokines, addressing the innate immune component of intestinal inflammation.\nS1PR modulators sequester lymphocytes within lymphoid organs by preventing their egress into circulation.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism involves selective inhibition of α4β7-MAdCAM-1 binding to prevent gut-homing of lymphocytes while preserving systemic immunity as demonstrated by maintained humoral responses to parenteral vaccines but blocked responses to oral vaccines, with proposed effects on naïve T and B cell trafficking to intestinal inductive sites and attenuation of lymphoid aggregates contributing to therapeutic benefit beyond simple effector cell blockade.\n...\nThese agents neutralize TNF-α through multiple mechanisms including blocking TNF-α binding to both receptors, inducing complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity of TNF-expressing cells, and triggering reverse signaling through transmembrane TNF leading to immune cell apoptosis.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.5667}, "jev_score": 0.5157, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Pharmacologic Options and Emerging Therapeutic ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402463/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "^26^ Mesalamine acts on colonic mucosa and reduces inflammation through a diverse process which includes inhibiting the mediators of lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase, interleukin-1, interleukin-2, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha.\nAlso, it activates the Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR)-gamma, which helps in the transcription of a few key target genes such as nuclear factor B, signal transducers, and activators of transcription that help in the control of intestinal inflammation.^27^\n...\nAs the name suggests, immunomodulators act by modifying the immune system activities leading to a decreased inflammatory response.\n...\nThese drugs are monoclonal antibodies that act against the TNF-alpha either by neutralizing its biological activity or by interfering with the TNF-alpha attachment to its receptor site.\n...\nThis drug acts by inhibiting the action of integrin, which is a protein involved in neutrophil function and migration by interacting with cellular adhesion molecules to allow for leukocyte adhesion to that vessel wall.\n...\nTofacitibin and Upadacitinib are JAK inhibitors, which act by inhibiting the JAK kinase, thereby inhibiting the cytokine and growth factor receptor signaling.", "date": "2024-09-09", "last_updated": "2026-08-02", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.5667}, "jev_score": 0.5044, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/75a0/e296f6c60ceffcd0af16826ef7f1d11558b9.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "These newer agents, such as mesalazine, offer a better safety profile and act locally on the colonic mucosa to exert their anti-inflammatory effects.\n...\nIts therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting\nthe epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory path- ways [21].\nMesalazine is thought to inhibit the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways, thereby reducing the synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and\n...\nOverall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response.\nIn the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues.\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls.\n...\nThe main target in the mechanism of action of golimumab is binding to human TNF and neutralizing the expression of adhesion molecules, including E-selectin, VCAM-1, and\nICAM-1, on the surface of vascular endothelium.\n...\nIts mechanism of action is based on binding to the p40 subunit of IL-12 and IL-23, which inhibits interaction with the IL-12 receptor, its signaling, and subsequent activation of Th1 cells.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-25", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.54}, "jev_score": 0.4644, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nThe introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for UC is rapidly evolving, with an unprecedented number of investigational agents currently in clinical development.\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\nMoreover, it includes drugs like obefazimod which enhances the expression of the anti-inflammatory microRNA, miR-124.\n...\nIntegrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism involves selective inhibition of α4β7-MAdCAM-1 binding to prevent gut-homing of lymphocytes while preserving systemic immunity as demonstrated by maintained humoral responses to parenteral vaccines but blocked responses to oral vaccines, with proposed effects on naïve T and B cell trafficking to intestinal inductive sites and attenuation of lymphoid aggregates contributing to therapeutic benefit beyond simple effector cell blockade.\n...\nThese agents neutralize TNF-α through multiple mechanisms including blocking TNF-α binding to both receptors, inducing complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity of TNF-expressing cells, and triggering reverse signaling through transmembrane TNF leading to immune cell apoptosis.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.5167}, "jev_score": 0.4547, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies in Inflammatory Bowel Disease - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12428888/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-TNF agents, including infliximab, adalimumab, and certolizumab pegol, represent the most extensively studied biologic class in IBD, targeting TNF-α, a key cytokine in the pathogenesis of both Crohn’s disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC).\nThese agents effectively induce and maintain remission, heal mucosa, and reduce hospitalization and surgery rates through their mechanism of neutralizing both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-α, leading to reduced inflammatory cell activation, decreased cytokine cascade amplification, and induction of regulatory immune responses [38] Table 1.\n...\nAnti-integrin therapy includes vedolizumab, a monoclonal antibody that selectively blocks α4β7 integrin, thereby preventing lymphocyte trafficking to the gut mucosa through a mechanism that specifically targets gut-homing immune cells while preserving systemic immune function.\n...\nAnti-IL-12/23 therapy is represented by ustekinumab, which targets the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, modulating Th1 and Th17 pathways implicated in both CD and UC through a mechanism that addresses key cytokine networks central to IBD pathogenesis.\n...\nBy inhibiting the JAK-STAT signaling pathway, tofacitinib blocks the transmission of signals from pro-inflammatory cytokines, including interleukins (IL-2, IL-4, IL-6) and interferons (IFN-γ) [49].\n...\n**Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators** such as ozanimod and etrasimod selectively prevent the egress of lymphocytes from lymph nodes, thereby reducing the inflammatory response in the gut.\n...\nBy selectively inhibiting IL-23, these agents reduce the inflammatory activity of Th17 cells, providing a novel mechanism of action distinct from anti-TNF agents [59].", "date": "2025-08-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5333}, "jev_score": 0.4533, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Practical guidance for managing patients with moderate-to-severe ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/jcag/article/7/4/282/7674170", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "The emergence of small molecule therapies offers novel treatment options that target mechanisms central to the immunopathology of UC.\nSphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase inhibitors are 2 classes of therapies that target unique pathways to attenuate inflammation and modulate the immune response characteristic of UC.\n...\nAdvanced small molecule therapies, such as sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase (JAK) inhibitors, are among the emerging UC treatment options designed to provide patients with alternatives to support long-term treatment goals (Figure 1).\nThese treatment options have novel mechanisms of action for patients to manage symptoms effectively and relatively safely.\n...\nEtrasimod functions by selectively activating S1P receptor subtypes 1, 4, and 5.^26^ The blockade of S1P receptors prevents lymphocytes from being recruited into the colon, allowing the mucosa to heal.^27^", "date": "2024-08-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.5367}, "jev_score": 0.4401, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in the Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis—From Conventional ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12897911/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Mesalazine is an anti-inflammatory agent with a complex, yet not fully elucidated mechanism of action in UC [20].\nIts therapeutic effect is primarily local, targeting the epithelial cells of the colonic mucosa and modulating various inflammatory pathways [21].\nMesalazine is thought to inhibit the cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways, thereby reducing the synthesis of pro-inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes [22].\nA key component of its activity involves agonism of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-), which plays a critical role in regulating intestinal inflammation.\nAdditionally, mesalazine may exert antioxidant effects and contribute to the suppression of T-cell activation and proliferation, further supporting its anti-inflammatory properties [20,22].\n...\nGlucocorticoids are steroid hormones, which gives them lipophilic properties, allowing them to easily diffuse across the cell membrane and bind to glucocorticoid receptors (GR) in the cytosol.\n...\nBy increasing the expression of anti-inflammatory genes while simultaneously reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory ones, the GR/GC complex is responsible for anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects, it also reduces the functioning of pro-inflammatory factors, including nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells (NF-κB) and activator protein 1 (AP-1) [25,26].\n...\nOverall [25,26], the mechanism of action of glucocorticoids allows for the control of the inflammatory process in the large intestine and modulation of the immune response.\nIn the context of IBD, reducing the expression of adhesion molecules plays a key role, which can limit the migration of inflammatory cells to diseased sites [27].\n...\nThe immunosuppressive effects attributed to thiopurines occur through several mechanisms, the most common of which include inhibition of the expression of proinflammatory genes, such as *ITGA4* and tumor necrosis factors [38].\n...\nThe mechanism of action of IFX in the context of IBD focuses on reducing the expression of cytokines and inflammatory mediators in intestinal tissues.\nTNF is extensively produced by immune cells during the inflammatory process and then participates in their migration from blood vessels to diseased sites.\nBy inhibiting TNF with IFX, it is possible to control the symptoms of UC [43].\n...\nAdalimumab (ADA) is another drug belonging to the TNF inhibitor class, this fully human recombinant IgG1 antibody is involved in inhibiting neutrophil activation and leukocyte migration to sites of inflammation.\nBy interacting with TNF, adalimumab induces antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, stimulates the complement system, and induces T-cell apoptosis [47].\n...\nVedolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that aims to limit lymphocyte migration toward the intestines by specifically binding to the α4β7 heterodimer found on the surface of intestinal lymphocytes.\n...\nVedolizumab’s mechanism of action is primarily based on disrupting the transport of T cells and leukocytes, which involves the adhesion, activation, and migration of these molecules across vascular walls.\n...\nThis antibody can inhibit leukocyte migration into the intestine by inhibiting α4β7 and MAdCAM-1 and retain leukocytes in the intestinal epithelium by blocking the interaction between E-cadherin and αEβ7.\n...\nIts mechanism of action is based on binding to the p40 subunit of IL-12 and IL-23, which inhibits interaction with the IL-12 receptor, its signaling, and subsequent activation of Th1 cells.", "date": "2026-02-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.5267}, "jev_score": 0.4319, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 2249.8, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:21+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q019-1", "row_id": "q019", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Innovent Biologics Inc. descriptions of IBI-3001's advantage over existing treatments for Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma in investor materials, earnings calls, investor day presentations, and press releases", "query": "IBI-3001 HNSCC investor presentation treatment advantage", "anchors": ["IBI-3001"], "search_latency_ms": 1450.3, "target": "IBI-3001", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 381.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7845, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000329, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7275, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | IBI-3001 Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/32752", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "IBI3001 is a potentially first-in-class bispecific ADC against B7-H3 and EGFR that is site-specifically glycan-conjugated using the clinically validated SYNtecanE® platform.\nIBI3001 has multiple anti-tumor mechanisms of action: (1) enhanced EGFR signaling blockade; (2) EGFR- and B7-H3-aided payload internalization and cytotoxicity; and (3) potent bystander killing effects of ADC.\nThe optimized B7-H3 arm not only enhances the EGFR signaling blockade but also reduces EGFR on-target toxicities.\nIBI3001 showed strong anti-tumor efficacy in vitro and in vivo across multiple solid tumors and is well tolerated with the therapeutic index at 40.\n...\nMechanisms of Action: B7-H3 Antagonist,EGFR Inhibitor\n...\nRoute of Administration: Intravenous\nFDA Designation: *\nApproval Status: Not Approved\n...\n|06/30/2025|News Article|Innovent Biologics Showcases \"Dual Innovations\" at Oncology R&D Day, Pioneering the Future of Cancer Treatment with Next-Generation IO and ADC Platforms|", "date": "2025-06-30", "last_updated": "2025-07-03", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.75}, "jev_score": 0.7275, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapy Detail", "url": "https://ckb.genomenon.com/therapy/show/16766", "domain": "ckb.genomenon.com", "snippet": "|Therapy Description|IBI3001 is an antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) comprising a bispecific antibody targeting CD276 (B7-H3) and EGFR linked to Exatecan, which potentially decreases tumor growth (Cancer Res (2024) 84 (7_Supplement): LB055).|\n...\n|NCT06349408|Phase Ib/II|IBI3001|IBI3001 in Participants With Unresectable, Locally Advanced or Metastatic Solid Tumors|Recruiting|AUS|1|", "date": "2024-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-06-20", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.64}, "jev_score": 0.6272, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "IBI3001 : Drug Detail - Cancer Knowledgebase (CKB) - CKB CORE", "url": "https://ckb.genomenon.com/drug/show/11774", "domain": "ckb.genomenon.com", "snippet": "|Drug Descriptions|IBI3001 is an antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) comprising a bispecific antibody targeting CD276 (B7-H3) and EGFR linked to Exatecan, which potentially decreases tumor growth (Cancer Res (2024) 84 (7_Supplement): LB055).|", "date": "2024-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-12-07", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.6014, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "IBI-3001(IBI-3001) - 药物靶点：CD276 x EGFR_在研适应症：局部晚期恶性实体瘤_专利_临床_研发", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/drug/5ee8502f51104aa1a802b2eafa6d1a3c", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "|药物类型 ADC|\n...\n|作用机制 CD276抑制剂(CD276抗原抑制剂)、EGFR拮抗剂(表皮生长因子受体erbB1拮抗剂)|\n...\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|局部晚期恶性实体瘤|临床1期|中国|2025-01-10|", "date": "2025-05-24", "last_updated": "2025-06-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.5467}, "jev_score": 0.5194, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "IBI-3001 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/5ee8502f51104aa1a802b2eafa6d1a3c", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Target CD276 x EGFR x Top I|\n|Action inhibitors, antagonists|\n|Mechanism CD276 inhibitors(CD276 antigen inhibitors), EGFR antagonists(Epidermal growth factor receptor erbB1 antagonists), TOP1 inhibitors(DNA topoisomerase I inhibitors)|\n...\n|Active Indication Locally Advanced Malignant Solid Neoplasm|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-01", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.4433}, "jev_score": 0.4211, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] 信息披露", "url": "https://paper.cnstock.com/images_new/3/2023-10/28/33/2023102833_pdf.pdf", "domain": "paper.cnstock.com", "snippet": "���\n���\n����������������\n...\n����������\n���������\n...\n�����������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.5, "evidence": 0.07}, "jev_score": 0.035, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "P123706_Inhibrx Presentation_May 2026 Public Website", "url": "https://inhibrx.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Inhibrx-Investor-Presentation_Sept-2026.pdf", "domain": "inhibrx.com", "snippet": "Set to pursue a breakthrough pathway in 1L R/M HPV+ CPS ≥1 HNSCC\n...\nHNSCC", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-10", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.2633}, "jev_score": 0.0184, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Project Details - International Cancer Research Partnership", "url": "https://www.prod.icrpartnership.org/project/173155", "domain": "prod.icrpartnership.org", "snippet": "Skip to main content\n...\nICRP Data\n...\nCommon Scientific Outline (CSO)\n...\nTestimonials", "date": "2004-02-01", "last_updated": "2025-12-10", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.0033}, "jev_score": 0.0002, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Error 403 - This web app is stopped.", "url": "https://investor.innoventbio.com/media/1062/%E6%8B%9B%E8%82%A1%E4%B9%A6%E8%8B%B1%E6%96%87.pdf", "domain": "investor.innoventbio.com", "snippet": "If you are the web app administrator, please find the common 403 error scenarios and resolution here.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-01-15", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "File Unavailable", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1420720/000142072022000022/ibio-20220512xex99d1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "# This page is temporarily unavailable.\nPlease check back again soon.", "date": "2015-01-01", "last_updated": "2024-12-11", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.24, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1831.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:21+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q022-2", "row_id": "q022", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication Novel UC Treatment, Cosmo Health, Distal Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Novel UC Treatment target mechanistic class UC", "anchors": ["Novel UC Treatment"], "search_latency_ms": 1273.3, "target": "drugs competing with Novel UC Treatment: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 384.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10565, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000444, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6688, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] New targets in inflammatory bowel disease therapy: 2021 - BINASSS", "url": "https://www.binasss.sa.cr/junio/33.pdf", "domain": "binasss.sa.cr", "snippet": "The anti a4b7 integrin antibody vedolizumab is currently approved and widely used in the treatment of both\nUC and CD [6,7].\n...\nAnother molecule targeting the a4b7 integrin is the monoclonal IgG2 antibody abrilumab (AMG-\n...\nAntiintegrins\nAJM300\nOral, novel, small\nmolecule a4 integrin\ninhibitor\n...\nSphingosine 1 phosphate receptor modulator\nLC51-0255\nS1P receptor modulator\nPhase 1\n...\nOPL-002\nS1P receptor modulator\nPhase 1\n2\nUC þ CD\n...\nAntiinterleukin antibodies\nSpesolimab\nAnti-IL36\n...\nJAK and tyrosine kinase inhibitors\nTD-1473\nOral novel, gut\nselective, pan JAK\ninhibitor\nPhase 1\n...\nThe anti-MAdCAM-1, ontamalimab (SHP647,\nPF-00547659), is a monoclonal IgG2 humanized antibody targeting the intestinal adhesion mole- cule, MAdCAM-1.\n...\nShingosine-1 phosphate (S1P) signaling on central memory T-cells facilitates their exit from lymph nodes.\nInternalization of the S1P receptor prevents lymphocytes from responding to S1P and are retained in the lymph node, thus inhibiting their recruitment to inflamed tissue [22].\n...\nOzanimod is an S1P modulator that downregulates\nS1P receptor subtypes 1 and 5 [23].\n...\nThe monoclonal antibody, usteki- numab, which is directed against the p40 subunit of\nIL-12 and IL-23 has shown success in the treatment of CD and UC [8,32,33].\n...\nMirikizumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the p19 subunit.\n...\nTofacitinib, an oral small molecule pan-JAK inhibitor, has shown success in three UC phase 3 (both induction and maintenance trials [9].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.76}, "jev_score": 0.6688, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "There are currently multiple emerging agents in the pipeline for the management of UC.\nThis includes agents with alternative routes of administration such as oral or subcutaneous tumor necrosis factor inhibitors or novel mechanisms of action such as toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist cobitolimod and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor apremilast.\n...\nThese small molecules include Janus Kinase (JAK) inhibitors such as tofacitinib, upadacitinib and filgotinib;\n...\n|Drug Class|Agent|Target|Mode of Delivery|Stage|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n|**Anti-TNF**|CT-P13|TNF|SC|Phase III completed|\n|**Anti-TNF**|OPRX-106|TNF|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Ivarmacitinib|JAK1|Oral|Phase II completed/Phase III recruiting|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|JAK1/TYK2|Oral|Phase IIb completed|\n...\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Deucravacitinib (BMS-986165)|TYK2|Oral|Phase II completed (primary endpoint not met)|\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|Etrolizumab|α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins|SC|Phase III completed|\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|Carotegrast methyl (AJM300)|α4 integrin|Oral|Phase III completed|\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|Ontamalimab (PF-00547659)|MAdCAM|SC|Phase II completed|\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|Abrilumab (AMG181)|α4β7 integrin|SC|Phase II completed|\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|PN-943|α4β7 integrin (gut restricted)|Oral|Phase II completed (primary endpoint not met)|\n...\nEtrolizumab is an SC humanized monoclonal antibody which targets the β7 subunit of the α4β7 and αEβ7 integrins with high affinity.25 It blocks binding to their ligands MAdCAM-1 and E-cadherin resulting in inhibition of leukocyte trafficking to the gut and inhibition of retention of leukocytes, respectively.26 Studies on animal models indicate that the medication selectively targets lymphocyte homing on mucosal surfaces with no impact on migration to non-mucosal tissues.26\n...\nAJM300 (carotegrast methyl) is an oral small molecule which has anti α4-integrin properties.\nHCA2969, the active metabolite of AJM300, selectively inhibits binding of α4 integrin (α4β7/α4β1) to the cell adhesion molecules.31 It shares the same mechanism of action as natalizumab.\n...\nMirikizumab is an IgG4-variant monoclonal antibody that binds to the p19 subunit of IL23.", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.7267}, "jev_score": 0.625, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for UC is rapidly evolving, with an unprecedented number of investigational agents currently in clinical development.\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\nThe JAK family comprises four intracellular tyrosine kinases: JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, and TYK2, all of which are essential for relaying cytokine-mediated signals through the STAT pathway.\n...\nIvarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC.\n...\nIntegrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium.\n...\nThese investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds;\n...\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.5733}, "jev_score": 0.5045, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Emerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for UC is rapidly evolving, with an unprecedented number of investigational agents currently in clinical development.\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\nThe JAK family comprises four intracellular tyrosine kinases: JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, and TYK2, all of which are essential for relaying cytokine-mediated signals through the STAT pathway.\n...\nIvarmacitinib (SHR0302) is a selective JAK1 inhibitor designed to block the JAK-signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, thereby controlling inflammation in UC.\n...\nIntegrin antagonists block the interaction between α4β7 and MAdCAM-1, preventing lymphocyte adhesion to and transmigration across intestinal endothelium.\nMAdCAM-1 antagonists target the endothelial ligand rather than the leukocyte receptor.\n...\nThese investigational agents represent mechanistically heterogeneous approaches that can be conceptually organized into several categories: immune checkpoint modulation strategies targeting co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathways (PD-1 agonism with rosnilimab, CD40 antagonism with ravagalimab, CD28-CD80/86 blockade with abatacept, OX40 antagonism with KHK4083), which aim to restore immune homeostasis by modulating T cell activation thresholds;\n...\nSeveral mechanistic classes demonstrate particular promise.\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.\nNext-generation selective JAK1 and TYK2 inhibitors aim to preserve efficacy while mitigating safety concerns through enhanced selectivity.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.5667}, "jev_score": 0.4987, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Small-Molecule Therapies Versus Biologic Agents ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13287821/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Evidence from eight landmark publications encompassing multiple pivotal phase 3 randomized controlled trials was synthesized to assess induction and maintenance outcomes across distinct therapeutic mechanisms, including intracellular cytokine signaling inhibition, immune trafficking modulation, and extracellular pathway blockade.\n...\nIn recent years, novel oral small-molecule therapies have emerged as an important addition to UC management, offering targeted immunomodulation with distinct pharmacologic advantages.\nJanus kinase (JAK) inhibitors are oral agents that block intracellular inflammatory signaling pathways involved in immune activation, whereas sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators reduce inflammatory cell trafficking to the gastrointestinal tract by limiting lymphocyte migration from lymphoid tissues.\nJAK inhibitors, including tofacitinib and upadacitinib, act by interrupting intracellular cytokine signaling pathways involved in lymphocyte activation and inflammatory amplification [7,8].\n...\nSimilarly, S1P receptor modulators such as ozanimod and etrasimod reduce intestinal inflammation by selectively inhibiting lymphocyte egress from lymphoid tissues, thereby limiting immune cell trafficking to the gut [9].\nTogether, these oral therapies represent a shift from extracellular cytokine blockade toward intracellular signaling and immune trafficking modulation.", "date": "2026-05-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.57}, "jev_score": 0.4731, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Raising the bar in ulcerative colitis management - Fabrizio Fanizzi, Mariangela Allocca, Gionata Fiorino, Alessandra Zilli, Federica Furfaro, Tommaso Lorenzo Parigi, Laurent Peyrin-Biroulet, Silvio Danese, Ferdinando D’Amico, 2024", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/17562848241273066", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "In cases of moderate-to-severe UC, the therapeutic approach shifts toward the utilization of advanced biological agents (including anti-tumor necrosis factor α (TNFα), anti-α4β7 integrin biologic agents, and anti-interleukin (IL)-12–23, selective IL-23 inhibitors), as well as small-molecule drugs like Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors and Sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) modulators.\n...\nIndeed, achieving deeper remission in UC may be feasible by targeting specific molecular mechanisms implicated in the inflammatory processes underlying the disease.^38^ New therapeutic molecules should target elements to restore immune dysregulation by the inhibition of proinflammatory cytokines and the implementation of anti-inflammatory cytokines effect.\nMoreover, pursuing new anti-inflammatory targets, such as regulatory T-cell therapy, Smad7 antisense, Janus-activated kinase inhibition, Toll-like receptor stimulation, leukocyte adhesion, and blockade of T-cell homing via integrins and mucosal addressin cellular adhesion molecule-1, could be turning point.^48^\n...\nTNFα inhibitors (TNFi) are a class of biologic agents that target the pro-inflammatory cytokine TNFα.\nIn the management of UC, TNFi agents such as Infliximab (IFX), Adalimumab, and Golimumab (GLM) play a crucial role.\n...\nIn this regard, a new biologic agent for UC treatment is represented by mirikizumab (MIRI), a humanized IgG4-variant monoclonal antibody that specifically binds to subunit p19 of IL-23.", "date": "2024-11-19", "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.5433}, "jev_score": 0.4672, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Pharmacologic Options and Emerging Therapeutic ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402463/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Several other novel drugs are in the final phases of clinical development or have recently received regulatory approval designed to target specific mechanisms involved in the inflammatory cascade for UC.\n...\nNow, a wide range of therapies has emerged, including alpha-4-integrin blockade agents, anti-CD3 antibodies, probiotic bacterial therapy, epidermal growth factors, etc., which offer the prospect of improved dosing and compliance with traditional molecules.\nAlso, the development of new molecules, novel formulations, and therapeutic approaches, which may deliver disease control in patients unresponsive to, or intolerant of, standard treatments, have broadened the therapeutic options of UC.\n...\n^26^ Mesalamine acts on colonic mucosa and reduces inflammation through a diverse process which includes inhibiting the mediators of lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase, interleukin-1, interleukin-2, and tumor necrosis factor-alpha.\nAlso, it activates the Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR)-gamma, which helps in the transcription of a few key target genes such as nuclear factor B, signal transducers, and activators of transcription that help in the control of intestinal inflammation.^27^\n...\n*Infliximab, Adalimumab, and Golimumab are the common TNF alpha inhibitors used for treating UC.\nThese drugs are monoclonal antibodies that act against the TNF-alpha either by neutralizing its biological activity or by interfering with the TNF-alpha attachment to its receptor site.\n...\nThis drug acts by inhibiting the action of integrin, which is a protein involved in neutrophil function and migration by interacting with cellular adhesion molecules to allow for leukocyte adhesion to that vessel wall.\n...\nTofacitibin and Upadacitinib are JAK inhibitors, which act by inhibiting the JAK kinase, thereby inhibiting the cytokine and growth factor receptor signaling.\n...\nUstekinumab is a monoclonal antibody against the p40 subunit of both IL-12 and IL-23, which is needed for Th17 and Th1 differentiation involved in the pathogenesis of UC.", "date": "2024-09-09", "last_updated": "2026-08-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.52}, "jev_score": 0.4368, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Pathological mechanism and targeted drugs of ulcerative colitis - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10508406/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Our aim is to provide more ideas for the clinical treatment and new drug development of UC, mainly by analyzing and sorting out the relevant literature on PubMed, summarizing and finding that it is related to the main genetic, environmental, immune and other factors, and explaining its pathogenesis from the NF-κB pathway, PI3K/Akt signaling pathway, and JAK/STAT signaling pathway, and obtaining anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies, integrin antagonists, IL-12/IL-23 antagonists, novel UC-targeted drugs such as JAK inhibitors and SIP receptor agonists.\n...\nThe PI3K/Akt signaling pathway is also involved in the regulation and release of inflammatory factors, and it can indirectly activate the transcription factor NF-κB through phosphorylated IKK, thus interconnecting with the NF-κB pathway to promote enhanced expression and secretion of inflammatory factors, leading to damage of the colonic mucosa.\n...\nThe resulting targeted therapeutic agents include anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies, integrin antagonists, IL-12/IL-23 antagonists, JAK inhibitors, and SIP receptor agonists (Table 1).\n...\nRisankizumab, Brazikumab, and Guselkumab, as novel biologics, selectively target the p19 subunit of IL-23 and do not interfere with the host immune response in which IL-12 is involved, thus theoretically allowing for better efficacy.", "date": "2023-09-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.5133}, "jev_score": 0.4209, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › articles › PMC12420776Ulcerative Colitis: Advances in Pathogenesis, Biomarkers, and ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420776/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Conventional medicinal strategies are reviewed alongside the emergence of biological therapies, notably those that target tumor necrosis factor (TNF), interleukins, and \nintegrins, which have significantly altered management approaches.\n...\nWe discuss novel therapies that utilize small molecule targeting, particularly those that inhibit Janus kinase (JAK) and modulate sphingosine-1-p\n...\nTherapies such as those targeting TNF, interleukins, and integrins represent biologics that have brought precision to UC treatment by selectively targeting immune pathways that drive inflammation.^10^ S1P receptor modulators and JAK inhibitors, as small molecule agents, have expanded treatment options, offeri\n...\nTargeted biologics such as mirikizumab and risankizumab have demonstrated clinical benefit by interrupting this IL-23/Th17 pathway, reducing cytokine output and promoting mucosal healing in refractory UC cases.\nTNF-α can attract immune cells, trigger cytokine activation, and cause epithelial cell apoptosis and tight junction disruption.\n...\nThis mechanism directly disrupts the IL-23/Th17 inflammatory circuit described earlier in the pathogenesis section, mitigating downstream cytokine release and epithelial injury.\n...\nSmall molecule therapies in UC operate via distinct immunomodulatory mechanisms.\nJAK inhibitors, such as tofacitinib and upadacitinib, block intracellular cytokine signal transduction by targeting Janus kinases, thereby dampening multiple cytokine pathways including IL-2, IL-6, and IL-23.\nIn contrast, S1P receptor modulators, exemplified by ozanimod, influence lymphocyte trafficking by sequestering immune cells in lymphoid tissues, reducing mucosal infiltration.", "date": "2025-09-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.4633}, "jev_score": 0.366, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Practical guidance for managing patients with moderate-to-severe ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11317630/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase inhibitors are 2 classes of therapies that target unique pathways to attenuate inflammation and modulate the immune response characteristic of UC.\n...\nAdvanced small molecule therapies, such as sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) receptor modulators and Janus-activated kinase (JAK) inhibitors, are among the emerging UC treatment options designed to provide patients with alternatives to support long-term treatment goals (Figure 1).\nThese treatment options have novel mechanisms of action for patients to manage symptoms effectively and relatively safely.\n...\nJAK inhibitors are another small molecule line of therapy for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC.\nThese therapies target the JAK family of intracellular tyrosine kinases, of which 4 different isoforms have been identified (JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, and tyk-2).", "date": "2024-05-15", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.4233}, "jev_score": 0.2794, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1657.4, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:21+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q022-3", "row_id": "q022", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication Novel UC Treatment, Cosmo Health, Distal Ulcerative Colitis", "query": "Novel UC Treatment Phase 2 competitor landscape ulcerative colitis", "anchors": ["Novel UC Treatment"], "search_latency_ms": 4167.9, "target": "drugs competing with Novel UC Treatment: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 405.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10105, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000424, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.646, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "There are currently multiple emerging agents in the pipeline for the management of UC.\nThis includes agents with alternative routes of administration such as oral or subcutaneous tumor necrosis factor inhibitors or novel mechanisms of action such as toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9) agonist cobitolimod and phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor apremilast.\n...\nSummary of Advanced Therapies in Phase II/III for the Management of Ulcerative Colitis\n|Drug Class|Agent|Target|Mode of Delivery|Stage|\n...\n|**Anti-TNF**|CT-P13|TNF|SC|Phase III completed|\n|**Anti-TNF**|OPRX-106|TNF|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Ivarmacitinib|JAK1|Oral|Phase II completed/Phase III recruiting|\n|**JAK inhibitors**|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|JAK1/TYK2|Oral|Phase IIb completed|\n...\n|**Anti-Trafficking Agents**|PTG-100|α4β7 integrin|Oral|Phase IIa completed (primary endpoint not met)|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Mirikizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Risankizumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase III completed (results pending)|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Brazikumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Development Halted|\n|**IL-23 Inhibitors**|Guselkumab|IL23/p19 subunit|IV, SC|Phase IIb/III active|\n|**S1P Receptor Modulators**|Etrasimod|S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5|Oral|Phase III completed|\n|**S1P Receptor Modulators**|CBP-307|S1PR1|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**S1P Receptor Modulators**|Amiselimod (MT-1303)|S1PR1|Oral|Phase II recruiting|\n|**PDE4 Inhibitors**|Apremilast|PDE4|Oral|Phase II completed|\n|**TLR9 Agonist**|Cobitolimod|TLR9|Topical (enema)|Phase IIb completed/Phase III recruiting|", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.76}, "jev_score": 0.646, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\n...\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\nThis pipeline encompasses novel mechanisms of action targeting diverse inflammatory pathways, including advanced TNF-α inhibition strategies, next-generation JAK and Tyrosine Kinase 2 (TYK2) inhibitors, cell trafficking modulators, IL-23 pathway antagonists, and innovative approaches targeting costimulation, checkpoint pathways, and tissue repair mechanisms.\n...\n|JAK Inhibitor|Ivarmacitinib (SHR0302)|Selective JAK1 inhibitor blocking JAK-STAT pathway|NCT03675477, NCT05181137|Phase 2, Phase 3|Phase 2 completed Feb 2021; Phase 3 status unknown|\n|JAK Inhibitor|Itacitinib (INCB039110)|JAK1 inhibitor|NCT03627052|Phase 2|Withdrawn Nov 2019 due to lack of recruitment|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|Ropsacitinib (PF-06826647)|Selective TYK2 inhibitor|NCT04209556|Phase 2b|Withdrawn with zero enrollment due to portfolio re-prioritization|\n...\n|CD40 antagonist|Ravagalimab (ABBV-323)|IgG1 monoclonal antibody antagonizing CD40|NCT03695185|Phase 2a|Completed Sept 2021; used synthetic placebo control|\n|IL-23R antagonist|Icotrokinra|First-in-class targeted oral peptide blocking IL-23 receptor|NCT06049017 (ANTHEM-UC)|Phase 2b|Met primary endpoint; 63.5% response (highest dose) vs 27% placebo|\n...\n|IL-2R agonist|Aldesleukin (ILT-101)|Low-dose IL-2 selectively expanding regulatory T cells|NCT02200445|Phase 1b/2a|Completed March 2021; 69% response at 1.0 million IU/m²/day|\n|IL-2R agonist|NKTR-358 (LY3471851)|PEG-conjugated recombinant IL-2|NCT04677179|Phase 2|Terminated Aug 2022 for enrollment futility|\n...\n|RIPK1 inhibitor|ABBV-668|Oral inhibitor of RIPK1|NCT05570006|Phase 2|Completed Dec 2024; results not yet reported|\n|RIPK1 inhibitor|Eclitasertib (SAR443122)|Highly potent selective oral RIPK1 kinase inhibitor|NCT05588843 (RESOLUTE)|Phase 2|Started Nov 2022; estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\n|Combination|Guselkumab + Golimumab|IL-23p19 antibody + TNF antagonist|NCT03662542 (VEGA), NCT05242484 (DUET-UC)|Phase 2, Phase 2b|VEGA completed Nov 2021; 83% response with combination.\nDUET-UC primary completion May 2025|\n|Combination|OD-07656 + Vedolizumab|IRAK4 inhibitor + α4β7 integrin antagonist|NCT06850727|Phase 2a|Started June 2025; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n|Combination|Infliximab + Ustekinumab|TNF antagonist + IL-12/23 p40 antibody|NCT06453317|Phase 2|Started Feb 2025; estimated completion June 2028|\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for moderate to severe UC is undergoing transformative evolution, with over 100 investigational agents targeting diverse inflammatory pathways currently in phase 2 and 3 development.\n...\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.7}, "jev_score": 0.56, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Competitive Landscape Analysis 2026 | Eureka", "url": "https://eureka.patsnap.com/blog/life-science/inflammatory-bowel-diseases-global-competitive-landscape-report-2026/", "domain": "eureka.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Moreover, inflammatory Bowel Diseases (IBD) — covering ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD) — anchor 1,837 active drug programs, 7,198 patent filings since January 2023, and 1,244 ongoing Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials.\n...\nSpecifically, the late-stage IBD landscape is bifurcated between injectable biologics — AbbVie’s Skyrizi (Risankizumab) and Rinvoq (Upadacitinib), Janssen’s Stelara (Ustekinumab) and Tremfya (Guselkumab), Takeda’s Entyvio (Vedolizumab), Pfizer’s Etrasimod (Velsipity), and BMS’s Zeposia (Ozanimod) — and the emerging oral biologic and JAK1-selective wave.\nMoreover, sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) modulators have established a third oral mechanism alongside JAK and TYK2 inhibitors.\nHowever, the strategic outlook is increasingly defined by oral peptide IL-23 antagonists and selective JAK1 inhibitors rather than next-generation TNF or anti-integrin biologics.\n...\nFurthermore, microbiome therapeutics, mesenchymal stem cell therapy, and IL-23 / TL1A combination biology extend the long-term modality horizon.\n...\nAcross the 200 sampled IBD programs, small molecules dominate with 114 entries (oral JAK, TYK2, S1P modulators), followed by 31 monoclonal antibodies, 6 herbal medicines, 4 mesenchymal stem cell therapies, 3 synthetic peptides, 3 cyclic peptides, 3 bispecific antibodies, 3 probiotics, and 3 polymers.\n...\n|Pfizer|US|Tier 2 — Active|Etrasimod (Velsipity)|S1P modulator UC approval 2023|", "date": "2026-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.6533}, "jev_score": 0.5553, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Drug Market Outlook: Advanced ...", "url": "https://www.researchandmarkets.com/reports/6235094/ulcerative-colitis-drug-market-outlook-advanced", "domain": "researchandmarkets.com", "snippet": "Today, the industry is dominated by a transition toward \"top-down\" therapeutic strategies utilizing highly advanced biologics and targeted synthetic small molecules.\n...\nThe competitive landscape of the Ulcerative Colitis market is a battleground of multinational pharmaceutical titans, characterized by aggressive R&D spending, strategic mega-mergers, and a relentless pu\nrsuit of best-in-class efficacy data.\n- **Takeda Pharmaceutical Co Ltd.:** Takeda is a dominant, entrenched leader in the gastrointestinal space.\nThe company's flagship asset, ENTYVIO (vedolizumab), an anti-integrin biologic, has fundamentally reshaped the UC treatment paradigm due to its gut-selective safety profile.\n...\nIn 2023, Merck dramatically strengthened its immunology pipeline with the blockbuster acquisition of Prometheus Biosciences, Inc.\nThis strategic buyout granted Merck access to highly coveted, novel therapeutic candidates targeting the TL1A pathway, positioning the company to compete fiercely\n...\nThe company is aggressively capturing new UC market share with its next-generation assets: Rinvoq (upadacitinib), a highly potent oral JAK inhibitor, and Skyrizi (risankizumab), a targeted IL-23 inhibitor, both demonstrating exceptional rates of mucosal healing\n...\ntargeted synthetic small molecules to the UC market with Xeljanz (tofacitinib), the first approved oral JAK inhibitor for this indication.\n...\nThey are currently advancing sophisticated, early-to-mid-stage clinical pipelines focused on novel inflammatory targets and bispecific antibodies aimed at addressing the ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.5933}, "jev_score": 0.534, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "The therapeutic pipeline for moderate to severe ulcerative colitis currently encompasses over 100 investigational agents in Phase II and III clinical development.\nEmerging mechanisms include next-generation Janus kinase and tyrosine kinase 2 inhibitors with enhanced selectivity, novel cell trafficking modulators, advanced tumor necrosis factor-alpha inhibition strategies, and selective interleukin-23 pathway antagonists.\nTumor necrosis factor-like ligand 1A pathway inhibitors demonstrate particularly robust efficacy in early trials, with clinical remission rates exceeding 25% compared to less than 2% for placebo.\n...\nThe introduction of biologic therapies targeting tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), integrin α4β7 (vedolizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12/23 pathways (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) has transformed treatment paradigms for moderate to severe UC (11, 13–21).\nMore recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\n|CXCR2 antagonist|Elubrixin (SB-656933)|Orally active selective CXCR2 antagonist and IL-8 receptor antagonist|NCT00748410|Phase 2a|Terminated early after enrolling only 3 participants|\n|PSGL-1 agonist|Leiolizumab|Tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1|NCT06109441|Phase 2a|Started Dec 2023; estimated completion Nov 2026|\n|PSGL-1 agonist|Neihulizumab (ALTB-168)|IgG4 monoclonal antibody targeting PSGL-1|NCT03298022|Phase 2|Terminated due to COVID-19; completed with 24 patients|\n|IP-10 antagonist|Eldelumab (BMS-936557)|Fully human monoclonal IgG1 antibody targeting IP-10/CXCL10|NCT00656890, NCT01294410|Phase 2, Phase 2b|Completed; failed to meet primary endpoints|\n...\n|TREM-1 antagonist|BI 3032950|TREM-1 antagonist|NCT06636656|Phase 2|Started Dec 2024; estimated completion June 2028|\n|MCR1 agonist|PL-8177|Oral selective melanocortin-1 receptor agonist|NCT05466890|Phase 2a|Completed Feb 2025; 33% remission vs 0% placebo|\n...\nAbrilumab, a human monoclonal IgG2 antibody targeting α4β7 integrin, was evaluated in two Phase 2 studies for moderate-to-severe UC.\n...\nElubrixin (SB-656933) is an orally active selective CXCR2 antagonist and IL-8 receptor antagonist that was investigated in a Phase 2, open-label, 7-day repeat dose study (NCT00748410) to evaluate its pharmacodynamics in patients with UC using 99mTc-HMPAO leukocyte SPECT scintigraphy to measure neutrophil migration to inflamed tissue.\n...\nLeiolizumab is a tetravalent agonist IgG1 antibody targeting PSGL-1 being evaluated in a Phase 2a, multicenter, single-arm, open-label study (NCT06109441) for moderately to severely active UC refractory to biologic therapy.\n...\nThe therapeutic landscape for moderate to severe UC is undergoing transformative evolution, with over 100 investigational agents targeting diverse inflammatory pathways currently in phase 2 and 3 development.\n...\nTL1A pathway inhibitors represent the most compelling novel target, with phase 2 data showing clinical remission rates exceeding 25% versus less than 2% for placebo across both treatment-naïve and treatment-experienced populations.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.5236, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis | Disease Landscape & Forecast | G7 | 2024", "url": "https://clarivate.com/life-sciences-healthcare/report/dlsfim0009-2024-biopharma-ulcerative-colitis-disease-landscape-forecast-g7-2024/", "domain": "clarivate.com", "snippet": "We expect the ulcerative colitis (UC) therapy market to grow steadily over the next decade, primarily driven by the increasing use of non-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) biologics (e.g., Takeda’s Entyvio, Johnson & Johnson Innovative Medicine’s Stelara, AbbVie’s Rinvoq) earlier in the treatment algorithm.\nAdditionally, the availability of Pfizer’s Xeljanz / Xeljanz XR, BMS’s Zeposia, and Alfasigma’s Jyseleca has expanded the treatment armamentarium and intensified market competition.\nFurthermore, the recent approvals of Pfizer’s Velsipity, Eli Lilly’s Omvoh, AbbVie’s Skyrizi, Johnson & Johnson Innovative Medicine’s Tremfya, and the expected launch of several therapies with novel mechanisms of action (e.g., ABX464, ivarmacitinib, MK-7240) will drive market growth over the next decade.\nOffsetting this growth will be the continued generic erosion of conventional agents, along with the entry of and increased physician comfort prescribing less-expensive biosimilars.", "date": "2026-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.5733}, "jev_score": 0.4586, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Market Size, Trends, Share & Growth Drivers 2031", "url": "https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/ulcerative-colitis-market", "domain": "mordorintelligence.com", "snippet": "Johnson & Johnson reported 63.5% clinical response in Phase 2b testing of oral icotrokinra, underlining the strong pipeline depth.\n...\nThe ulcerative colitis market functions as a dynamic oligopoly.\n...\n- March 2025: Johnson & Johnson announced 63.5% Phase 2b response for oral icotrokinra in ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2018-02-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.63}, "jev_score": 0.4158, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Report", "url": "https://www.evaluate.com/disease-report/ulcerative-colitis-report/", "domain": "evaluate.com", "snippet": "Despite a robust treatment landscape, 30% of patients are non-responders to first-line biologics, and biosimilar competition is reshaping market dynamics.\nThe UC market is projected to grow from $6.9B in 2024 to $11.1B by 2033, peaking in 2031 before declining due to patent expiries and biosimilar erosion.\nWhile anti-TNFs like Remicade and Humira remain foundational, their dominance is being challenged by biosimilars and novel agents such as Velsipity, Skyrizi, Tremfya, and Omvoh.\nMeanwhile, oral therapies and new mechanisms of action—including TL1A inhibitors and cap-binding complex modulators—are gaining traction.\n**In this report we cover: **\n...\n- Emerging therapies with novel mechanisms (e.g., RVT-3101, MK-7240, Obefazimod)\n...\n- Competitive positioning of over 100 marketed and pipeline drugs\n...\nGrowth is fueled by **novel oral therapies**, new biologics with **selective IL-23 inhibition**, and **rising prevalence** in key markets like the US and EU5.\n...\n**Skyrizi** and **Tremfya** show strong clinical and commercial potential.\n**Obefazimod** and **TL1A inhibitors** like **RVT-3101** are also gaining attention for their novel mechanisms.", "date": "2025-10-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.48}, "jev_score": 0.3984, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline: 75+ Therapies & Key Companies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/insights/ulcerative-colitis-clinical-trial-analysis", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "DelveInsight’s** “Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline Insight 2026”** report provides comprehensive insights about **70+ companies and 75+ pipeline drugs** in the Ulcerative Colitis Pipeline Landscape.\n...\n- ***On March 05, 2026- Sanofi * ** conducted a Phase 2, multinational, multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose ranging study to evaluate the efficacy and safety of SAR441566 in adults with moderate-to-severe UC.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess efficacy of different doses of SAR441566 on clinical remission in participants with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis.\n...\n- DelveInsight’s Ulcerative Colitis pipeline report depicts a robust space with 70+ active players working to develop 75+ pipeline therapies for Ulcerative Colitis treatment.\n...\nCurrently, the drug is in Phase II stage of its clinical trial for the treatment of ulcerative colitis.", "date": "2026-03-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.5633}, "jev_score": 0.3943, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Ulcerative Colitis Treatment Market Size, Drugs, Companies", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/report-store/ulcerative-colitis-uc-market", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "- In **July** **2025**, Abivax announced positive **Phase III results from both ABTECT 8-week induction** trials investigating Obefazimod, its first-in-class oral miR-124 enhancer, in moderate to severely active ulcerative colitis .", "date": "2026-07-26", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.6, "evidence": 0.6467}, "jev_score": 0.388, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}], "total_ms": 4573.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:21+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q023-2", "row_id": "q023", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Diseases Mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as Gut Microbiome Dysbiosis, including Live Biotherapeutic Product", "query": "MB310 Phase 2 Microbiotica licensing", "anchors": ["VE202", "MB310", "SER-287", "EXL01"], "search_latency_ms": 2503.9, "target": "drugs competing with VE202 / MB310 / SER-287 / EXL01: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 355.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8504, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000357, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.4788, "results": [{"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline - Microbiotica", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/pipeline/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "**MB310 – monotherapy**\n|Pre-clinical|Phase 1|Phase 2|Phase 3|Collaborator|\n|--|--|--|--|--|\n#### Therapeutic area\nInflammatory bowel disease\n#### Specific indication\nUlcerative colitis (UC)\n#### Live biotherapeutic product (LBP)\nMB310 – monotherapy\n#### Milestone\nPhase 1b data in 2026\n...\n**MB310**: oral capsule comprised of a consortium of eight specific live gut commensal bacterial strains.\n**Rationale**: MB310 is designed to be a best-in-class therapy that is given as a once-daily dose to deliver long-term remission to ulcerative colitis patients, without immunosuppression or unwanted side effects.\n...\nCOMPOSER-1 - UK/EU Phase 1b study in ulcerative colitis - study complete\n**Phase 1b study**: To investigate the safety, tolerability and initial signals of efficacy of a once daily oral dose of MB310 as a monotherapy for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC), an inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.4788, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Microbiotica Ltd. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/b2fefc218c72407e0b4d39be2245c2b4", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "MB310\n...\nCTIS2023-507376-50-00\n/ RecruitingPhase 1\n- MB310-01\nNCT06582264\n/ Active, not recruitingPhase 1\nA Phase 1b, Randomized, Placebo-Controlled, Double-Blind Study to Evaluate Safety, Engraftment, and Initial Signs of Clinical Activity of MB310 in Patients With Active, Mild-to-Moderate Ulcerative Colitis", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-17", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.52}, "jev_score": 0.4628, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Microbiotica Announces Impressive Results in its Phase 1b Trial of MB310 in Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/02/11/3235967/0/en/Microbiotica-Announces-Impressive-Results-in-its-Phase-1b-Trial-of-MB310-in-Ulcerative-Colitis.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "Next step is to conduct a Phase 2/3 study in UC in combination with anti-inflammatory and/or immune modulatory induction agents\n...\nWe are planning our Phase 2/3 studies where such potential is evaluated in combination with standard of care therapies.'\n...\n'During 2026, we will explore partnering and financing options to determine the best route to fund our products through later studies and towards commercialisation.'\n...\nMicrobiotica is conducting additional analysis of COMPOSER-1 data, to further explore the impact of MB310 and to inform the design of the adaptive Phase 2/3 study.\nIt is anticipated that this study will explore the disease modifying 'slower-onset longer-acting' potential of MB310 in combination with anti-inflammatory and/or immuno-modulatory induction agents.", "date": "2026-02-11", "last_updated": "2026-02-11", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5433}, "jev_score": 0.4618, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Press Release", "url": "https://www.flerie.com/en/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/10/press-release-fleries-portfolio-company-microbiotica-reports-positive-phase-1b-data-for-mb310-in-pat-260211.pdf", "domain": "flerie.com", "snippet": "Flerie\n(publ) portfolio company Microbiotica has reported positive results from its Phase \nAB’s\n1b \n study of MB310 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC).\nThe positive data \nfirst-in-human\nsupport \n progress into Phase 2/3 trials and strengthens \n favourable \nMicrobiotica’s\nFlerie’s\noutlook in the \n ongoing clinical development.\ncompany’s\nMicrobiotica’s drug candidate MB310, a precision microbiome medicine, met its primary and secondary objectives when compared with placebo in 29 UC patients.\n...\nforward as Microbiotica advances toward Phase 2/3.\n...\nownership in Microbiotica amounts to 10%.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-02-11", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.53}, "jev_score": 0.4505, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Press releases", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/press-releases/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "Microbiotica Announces Impressive Results in its Phase 1b Trial of MB310 in Ulcerative Colitis\n...\nMicrobiotica Announces Completion of Recruitment in its Phase 1b Trial (COMPOSER-1) of MB310, a Precision Microbiome Medicine, in Ulcerative Colitis Patients", "date": "2026-05-18", "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.4667}, "jev_score": 0.4014, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Microbiotica – Precision Microbiome Medicines", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "## News\n...\n- Microbiotica Announces Impressive Results in its Phase 1b Trial of MB310 in Ulcerative Colitis 11 February 2026", "date": "2026-05-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.44}, "jev_score": 0.3828, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Member profile: Microbiotica - BioIndustry Association | BIA", "url": "https://www.bioindustry.org/member/microbiotica/", "domain": "bioindustry.org", "snippet": "Microbiotica announces positive results in its Phase 1b First-in-Human trial, COMPOSER-1, of MB310 in ulcerative colitis (UC) patients.\n...\nMicrobiotica announces that patient recruitment is complete in its Phase 1b First-in-Human trial, COMPOSER-1, of MB310 in ulcerative colitis (UC) patients.\n...\nMicrobiotica announces that the first patient has been dosed in its Phase 1b First-in-Human trial, COMPOSER-1, of MB310 in ulcerative colitis (UC) patients.", "date": "2026-09-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.44}, "jev_score": 0.3784, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Partnering", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/partnering/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "Microbiotica is collaborating with the Crohn’s & Colitis Foundation for the development of MB310, its Live Biotherapeutic Product, for the treatment of ulcerative colitis (UC).\nThe collaboration has supported the cGMP manufacture of MB310, enabling an Investigational New Drug (IND) application and supporting progression into a first-in-human clinical.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.4367}, "jev_score": 0.3756, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Policy and Regulations - M2 Pharma", "url": "http://www.m2pharma.com/news/policy-and-regulations/article.php?id=107585", "domain": "m2pharma.com", "snippet": "MB310 is an investigational, once-daily oral live biotherapeutic product designed to restore gut barrier integrity without immune suppression.\nMicrobiotica plans to advance the programme into an adaptive Phase 2/3 trial in combination with anti-inflammatory and/or immune-modulatory induction agents.\nIP Group plc holds an undiluted 16.7% beneficial stake in Microbiotica, valued at GBP13.9m as at 30 June 2025.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.4367}, "jev_score": 0.3756, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "About - Microbiotica", "url": "https://microbiotica.com/about/", "domain": "microbiotica.com", "snippet": "## MB310\n**Monotherapy for ulcerative colitis (UC).**\nCOMPOSER is a UK/EU Phase 1b study to investigate the safety and initial signals of efficacy of a once daily oral dose of MB310 as a monotherapy for the treatment of ulcerative colitis, an inflammatory bowel disease.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.3733}, "jev_score": 0.3173, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 2859.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:21+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q021-2", "row_id": "q021", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Crohn's Disease Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as S1PR S1PR1 Sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor, including Small Molecule", "query": "Mocravimod Crohn's disease ulcerative colitis S1PR1 agents", "anchors": ["Mocravimod"], "search_latency_ms": 28178.8, "target": "drugs competing with Mocravimod: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 359.3, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10816, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000454, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9114, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "More recently, oral small molecule therapies including Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib, filgotinib) and sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) have expanded therapeutic options (22–26).\n...\n|S1P modulator|Mocravimod (KRP203)|Oral S1PR modulator|NCT01375179|Phase 2|Terminated early May 2012 after enrolling only 27 of 72 participants|\n...\nIn IBD, S1P has been found to be overexpressed in experimental colitis models and in patients with active UC, making S1P receptor modulation an attractive therapeutic target (42).\nS1P receptor modulators act as functional antagonists despite being receptor agonists.\nUpon binding to S1PR1 on lymphocyte surfaces, these agents cause persistent receptor internalization and proteasome-mediated degradation, resulting in functional downregulation (43).\n...\nOzanimod, a selective S1PR1/5 modulator requiring dose escalation (0.23mg days 1-4, 0.46mg days 5-7, then 0.92mg daily), received FDA and EMA approval in 2021 based on the True North program demonstrating 18.4% clinical remission at week 10 induction and 37.0% at week 52 maintenance versus 6.0% and 18.5% placebo respectively, with favorable safety profiles though requiring cardiovascular monitoring and dose titration to mitigate first-dose heart rate effects (24, 45).\nEtrasimod, a more selective S1PR1/4/5 modulator requiring no dose escalation and administered at 2mg once daily from initiation, achieved FDA approval in 2023 and EMA approval in 2024 based on ELEVATE UC 52 and UC 12 demonstrating 27% clinical\n...\nThis Phase 2 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study (NCT01375179) evaluated Mocravimod (KRP203), an oral S1PR modulator, in 27 patients with moderately active refractory UC who had inadequately responded to or were intolerant of 5-ASA therapy.\nThe primary outcome was clinical remission rate at 8 weeks, with secondary outcomes including safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetics, and inflammatory markers over 12 weeks of follow-up.\nThe study was terminated early in May 2012 after enrolling only 27 of the planned 72 participants, suggesting the trial did not demonstrate sufficient efficacy to warrant continuation.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.93}, "jev_score": 0.9114, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging therapies for ulcerative colitis - Taylor & Francis", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/1744666X.2022.2069562", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "This review focuses on three drug classes that have recently been approved or are awaiting approval for UC: antibodies against interleukin (IL)-23, sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor (S1PR) modulators, and selective inhibitors of Janus kinases (JAK).\n...\nIL-23 antagonists appear to be safest with very few (serious) adverse events, while the use of S1PR modulators or JAK inhibitors has been associated with infectious and cardiovascular/thromboembolic events, albeit in low numbers.\n...\nS1P receptor modulators bind to one of the S1PR on activated lymphocytes, which leads to internalization of the receptor and subsequent loss of responsiveness to the S1P gradient [Citation32].\n...\nOzanimod (RPC-1063) is a modulator of S1PR1 and S1PR5, with a 27-fold selectivity for S1PR1 over S1PR5 and 10,000-fold greater selectivity for S1PR1 in comparison with S1PR2-4 [Citation35].\nThe drug has been approved for relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis and UC (Zeposia ®, Bristol Myers Squibb).\n...\nEtrasimod (Arena Pharmaceuticals) is a S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5 modulator, which demonstrated superiority over placebo in a Phase 2 study in UC at the 2 mg dose (clinical remission: 33% vs. 8.1%; endoscopic improvement: 41.8% vs.\n...\nEtrasimod has not yet been approved for any indication.\n...\nOf the three classes, IL-23 inhibitors have the most favorable safety profile, while both S1PR modulators and selective JAK inhibitors carry a somewhat higher risk of cardiovascular and infectious adverse events.", "date": "2022-05-04", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.8633}, "jev_score": 0.8115, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Signaling in Immune ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8116828/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|S1PR agonists|AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, AUY954, cenerimod.\nceralifimod, CS-077, CS-2100, CYM544, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK1842799, GSK2018682, mocravimod, ozanimod, Ponesimod, RP-001, SEW2871, siponimod, ST-968, ST-1071, Syl948, TC-G 1006, TC-SP14, amgen 8,14| |Fingolimod, ST-968, ST-1071|Amiselimod, etrasimod, fingolimod|A-971432, AMG 369, amiselimod, ASP0028, ASP4058, cerafimod, etrasimod, fingolimod, GSK2018682, ozanimod, siponimod|\n...\nOver the past few years, the S1P/S1PRs axis has been identified as a potential therapeutic target for immune-mediated diseases, and the safety and efficacy of several S1PRs modulators have been explored in MS, RA, SLE, psoriasis, atopic dermatitis (AD), ulcerative colitis (UC), and Crohn’s disease (CD) [23, 36].\nS1PR1 agonists render lymphocytes incapable of sensing the S1P gradient and exiting lymph nodes, preventing their access to circulation and sites of inflammation.\n...\nMocravimod/KRP-203 is a prodrug agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4, and S1PR5 and partial agonist of S1PR3.\nDespite attenuating colitis in mouse models [2, 54], it was minimally effective in patients with UC (NCT01375179) [55].\nA multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group study evaluated the efficacy, safety, and tolerability of KRP203 in moderately active UC refractory to 5-aminosalicylates over 8 weeks [55].\n...\nClinical remission was achieved in 2/14 patients (14%) receiving KRP203 compared with none in the placebo group.\nThis study did not meet the minimum clinically relevant threshold for efficacy [55] and was terminated (NCT01375179); however, its small sample size was a major limitation.\nOverall, KRP203 was well tolerated and safe, and no adverse cardiac events were reported [55].\n...\nOzanimod/RPC1063/Zeposia is an oral S1PR modulator that selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5 [58, 59], reducing concerns around S1PR3 engagement.", "date": "2021-05-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-08", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8167}, "jev_score": 0.7922, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Sphingosine-1-Phosphate Pathway - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12057646/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In this review, we revisit the role of the S1P/S1PR axis in the pathogenesis of IBD and discuss efficacy and safety data from clinical trials and real-world reports on the two S1PR modulators, ozanimod and etrasimod, that are currently approved for IBD treatment, and comment on their potential positioning in the IBD day-to-day management.\n...\nTo date, two such drugs, specifically ozanimod and etrasimod have been approved for use in moderate to severe UC treatment.\n...\nMost importantly, S1P-S1PR1 binding is a key regulator of inflammatory responses and lymphocyte extravasation to sites of inflammation [16].\n...\nSphingosine-1-phosphate modulators function mainly as agonists.\nAfter their binding, they prevent lymphocytes from egressing to the circulation, and from reaching the inflammation sites [6].\n...\nOzanimod (RPC1063) is an orally administered prodrug that is phosphorylated by SphK, and its active metabolite selectively binds to S1PR1 and S1PR5.\nWhile ongoing clinical trials (NCT03915769, NCT05644665, NCT05076175) continue to explore the efficacy of ozanimod in treating UC, it has already garnered approval for the management of moderate to severe cases, largely due to the conclusive findings from the TOUCHSTONE and True North studies [54, 55].\n...\nVTX002 (OPL-002) is an orally administered directly acting highly selective S1PR1 agonist, that shows promising results in the treatment of moderately to severely active UC [74].\n...\nMocravimod (KRP-203) is an orally administered prodrug, the active metabolite of which, mocravimod-P (KRP-203-P) is an agonist of S1PR1, S1PR4 and S1PR5 and only partially antagonizes S1PR3, leading to reduced risk of bradycardia development, in contrast to the first generation of S1P modulators.\n...\nIn a multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, randomized (1:1) trial assessing the safety and efficacy of mocravimod in 27 patients with moderately active 5-ASA-resistant UC, mocravimod did not meet the minimum clinical efficacy criteria, i.e., clinical remission defined as partial Mayo Score of 0 or 1 and a modified Baron Score of 0 or 1 with no macroscopic bleeding (partial Mayo sub-score of 0 for rectal bleeding) at a statistically significant threshold compared to placebo and thus study was terminated [83].\n...\nThe S1P modulators ozanimod and etrasimod act by blocking a previously untapped pathophysiological pathway and induce significantly better outcomes in terms of clinical, endoscopic and histological endpoints, as compared to placebo.\nThus, they are approved as either first- or second-line therapies for UC.", "date": "2024-09-26", "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7867}, "jev_score": 0.7631, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Unveiling the biological role of sphingosine-1-phosphate ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850947/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Amiselimod (MT-1303), etrasimod (APD-334), ozanimod (RPC-1063), and KRP-203 (2-amino-2-1,3-propanediol hydrochloride) belong to the class of S1P modulators currently in clinical development for IBD, and they help prevent lymphocyte migration into the gut[3].\n...\nSmall molecules acting as S1PR modulators can prevent lymphocyte invasion of inflamed tissues by the process described above.\nS1PR1 agonists can induce persistent lymphopenia by leading to internalization and subsequent ubiquitination and proteasome degradation of the receptor.\n...\nS1PR modulators tested in preclinical and clinical studies (*e.g.*, JTE013, mocravimod, amiselimod, ozanimod, etrasimod, FTY720) have shown a promising role as immunomodulators in the prevention of chronic inflammation and the treatment of inflammatory gastrointestinal cancers[8].\n...\nAn important mechanism of action of S1P receptor modulators is that they antagonize S1P1 receptors on lymphocytes, thereby inhibiting their migration from secondary lymphoid organs to the periphery, resulting in a decrease in the number of circulating lymphocytes, including autoreactive T cells, thus causing immunomodulation[73].\n...\n|Etrasimod (APD-334)|Agonist of S1P1, partial agonist of S1P4/S1P5; Lymphopenia, ↓mucosal thickness, immune cell infiltration, expression of T-cell and monocyte markers; ↓Pro-inflammatory cytokines TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, and IL-17A, ↑anti-inflammatory IL-10|Phase II/III|NCT03996369, NCT03945188, NCT03950232, NCT04173273[84-86]|\n|Ozanimod (RPC-1063)|Selective S1P1 and S1P5 receptor agonist; ↓T cell migration→↓peripheral lymphocytes↑S1P1 receptor internalization and degradation; ↓Circulating B and CCR7+ T lymphocytes→↓inflammation; ↓Mononuclear cell infiltrate and mucosal thickness|Phase II/III|NCT03440385, NCT03464097, NCT03467958, NCT03440372[87-92]|\n...\nOzanimod (RPC-1063) is a small molecule, selective S1P1 and S1P5 receptor agonist.\nRPC-1063 can bind to S1P1 and S1P5 receptors, limiting the migration of T cells from peripheral lymphoid organs and reducing the number of peripheral lymphocytes[87].", "date": "2023-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-03-03", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.77}, "jev_score": 0.7469, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Efficacy and safety of sphingosine-1-phosphate receptor ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12421927/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "S1P receptor modulators significantly improved clinical remission (OR = 1.49, 95% CI: 1.21–1.84, p < 0.001), histologic remission (OR = 1.74, 95% CI: 1.31–2.31,\n...\nThis meta-analysis supports S1P receptor modulators, particularly ozanimod and etrasimod, as effective and safe treatments for UC.\n...\nS1PrMs has shown promising therapeutic potential in UC and Crohn’s disease, describing it as an innovative approach in IBD treatment.\n...\nSeveral S1PrMs, including Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have demonstrated efficacy in managing moderate to severe IBD, significantly improving patient health and quality of life.\nNotably, Ozanimod has been approved in both the United States and the European Union for the treatment of UC, underscoring its therapeutic potential.\nIts high-affinity binding to S1P receptor subtypes, particularly S1P1 and S1P5, further strengthens its role as a viable treatment option for UC and Crohn’s disease (14).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-20", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.76}, "jev_score": 0.7372, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety and efficacy of S1P receptor modulators for the ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11383267/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "A total of 7 RCTs involving 2471 patients were included.\n...\nOutcomes assessed in maintenance phase significantly favored the intervention group over placebo as well, histologic remission (RR = 2.39; 95% CI [1.83, 3.11]; *P* < .00001), endoscopic improvement (RR = 2.20; 95% CI [1.28, 3.77];\n...\nS1PrMs show promising potential for establishing histologic remission, endoscopic improvement, clinical remission, and corticosteroid-free clinical remission.\n...\nSphingosine 1-phosphate receptor modulators (S1PrMs) are emerging as a effective class of drugs for IBD.\nThese compounds target the S1P receptor, a key regulator of inflammation and immune responses, offering a new approach to cope with the disease mechanism.\nThe S1PrMs such as Ozanimod, Etrasimod, Mocravimod, and Amiselimod, have been effective in treating IBD, from moderate to severe patients and improving their health and lifestyle.\n...\nIt shows high affinity binding to the subtypes of S1P receptors like S1P and S5P, proving it a potential therapeutic option for the various forms of IBD, including UC and Crohn’s disease.^[7]^\n...\nThere was more incidence of clinical remission in the S1PrM group as compared to the placebo group, and the results are statistically significant (RR = 2.23; 95% CI [1.43, 3.46]; *P* < .0004; *I* ^2^ = 55%).\n...\nOzanimod and Etrasimod are considered more effective S1P modulators for Ulcerative Colitis than Mocravimod and Amiselimod due to their efficacy, safety profiles, and mechanisms of action.", "date": "2024-09-06", "last_updated": "2026-09-21", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.7333}, "jev_score": 0.7113, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "NCATS Inxight Drugs — Mocravimod Hydrochloride", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/substance/3YH0N35CE3", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation.\nThis drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated.\nIn addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed.\n...\n|Target ID: P21453 Gene ID: 1901.0 Gene Symbol: S1PR1 Target Organism: Homo sapiens (Human) Sources: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|\n...\n|Primary|Unknown ## Approved Use Unknown|", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-08-18", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.62}, "jev_score": 0.4836, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod Hydrochloride - Inxight Drugs", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/drug/3YH0N35CE3", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "KYORIN Pharmaceutical has developed a new sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) receptor type 1 agonist, 2-amino-2-propanediol hydrochloride (KRP-203), for immunomodulation in autoimmune diseases and organ transplantation.\nThis drug was in phase II clinical trial for the treatment Ulcerative Colitis, but this study was terminated.\nIn addition, it has been investigated for the treatment of Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus and Crohn's Disease, these phases II clinical trial studies were successfully completed.\n...\n|Sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 7 Target ID:&nbspP21453 Gene ID:&nbsp1901.0 Gene Symbol:&nbspS1PR1 Target Organism:&nbspHomo sapiens (Human) Sources:&nbsphttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16674913|Agonist 2752| | |\n...\n|Ulcerative colitis 125 Sources:&nbsphttps://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT01375179|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|\n...\n|Crohn's disease 64 Sources:&nbsphttp://www.clinicaltrials.jp/user/showCteDetailE.jsp?japicId=JapicCTI-132108|Primary| |Phase II 1147|Unknown\n#### Approved Use Unknown|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.5833}, "jev_score": 0.4491, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Mocravimod", "url": "https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB18151", "domain": "go.drugbank.com", "snippet": "# Mocravimod\n...\n## Identification\n- Generic Name\n- Mocravimod\n...\n- Indication\nNot Available\n...\nTarget Actions Organism USphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1agonist Humans\n...\n**1.\nSphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1**\n- Kind\n- Protein\n- Organism\n- Humans\n- Pharmacological action\n- Unknown\n- Actions\n- Agonist\n- General Function\n...\nG-protein coupled receptor for the bioactive lysosphingolipid sphingosine 1-phosphate (S1P) that seems to be coupled to the G(i) subclass of heteromeric G proteins.\nSignaling leads to the activation of RAC1, SRC, PTK2/FAK1 and MAP kinases.", "date": "2023-09-12", "last_updated": "2025-08-16", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.4033}, "jev_score": 0.3146, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 28538.1, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:22+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q023-4", "row_id": "q023", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Diseases Mild-to-moderate ulcerative colitis Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanistic class as Gut Microbiome Dysbiosis, including Live Biotherapeutic Product", "query": "EXL01 ReMind Phase 2 licensing Crohn's disease", "anchors": ["VE202", "MB310", "SER-287", "EXL01"], "search_latency_ms": 2373.8, "target": "drugs competing with VE202 / MB310 / SER-287 / EXL01: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 350.2, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9699, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000407, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 2, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.3905, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences: a new generation of microbiota-derived ...", "url": "https://www.universite-paris-saclay.fr/en/news/exeliom-biosciences-new-generation-microbiota-derived-biotherapies", "domain": "universite-paris-saclay.fr", "snippet": "By comparing the microbiota of people suffering from Crohn's disease, a chronic inflammatory condition of the digestive tract, they observed that a specific bacterium, *Faecalibacterium prausnitzii* (Fprau), was consistently absent in patients experiencing a relapse but present in those in remission.\n...\nExeliom Biosciences' core technology is based on EXL01, a non-genetically modified strain of the intestinal bacterium Faecalibacterium prausnitzii (F. prausnitzii).\n...\nAt the same time, the start-up is running two clinical trials outside the field of oncology.\nThe first focuses on infectious diseases in partnership with the Lyon University Hospital (CHU), the Hospices Civils de Lyon (HCL).\nThe other targets Crohn's disease with the REMIND academic group, which specialises in translational research in inflammatory diseases of the digestive tract.\n...\nThe next few years will see major clinical milestones, with initial Phase 2 results expected between 2026 and 2028.\n\"Our ambition is to be in a position to move forward with a Phase 3 submission by 2029, for a first commercial launch around 2033,\" says Benjamin Hadida.", "date": "2026-02-27", "last_updated": "2026-07-18", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.55}, "jev_score": 0.3905, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "MAINTAIN POP submitted to ANSM - Exeliom Biosciences", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/maintain-pop-submitted-to-ansm", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "🔈 We are thrilled to share that the MAINTAIN POP trial was submitted, in collaboration with REMIND, to the ANSM Agence nationale de sécurité du médicament et des produits de santé.\nMAINTAIN POP is a phase II randomized placebo-controlled trial which will assess the safety and efficacy of EXL01, a single-strain live biotherapeutic, against endoscopic endpoints in patients with Crohn’s disease that have been successfully brought into remission by surgery.\nThe trial will take place across 13 sites in the REMIND group, a network of specialist gastroenterologists in IBD across France.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.4767}, "jev_score": 0.3814, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "La Gazette du LABORATOIRE AFRIQUE", "url": "https://www.gazettelabo.fr/afrique/breves/15577Exeliom-Biosciences-REMIND.html", "domain": "gazettelabo.fr", "snippet": "**• Cette étude de phase 2 vise à évaluer l'efficacité de EXL01 en traitement préventif des récidives post-opératoires chez les patients atteints de la maladie de Crohn\n• Elle s'appuie sur les données de la partie A de l'étude MAINTAIN, qui montre que EXL01 dispose d’un bon profil de sécurité en traitement d'entretien après une rémission induite par les stéroïdes chez des patients atteints d'une maladie de Crohn légère à modérée\n• La partie A de l'étude MAINTAIN a été arrêtée en raison d’un recrutement trop lent\n• L'étude MAINTAIN POP commencera début 2025 ; les premiers résultats sont attendus au premier trimestre 2027**\nParis, France, le 25 juin 2024 - Exeliom Biosciences, société de biotechnologie au stade clinique développant de nouvelles thérapies en immuno-inflammation et immuno-oncologie, annonce aujourd’hui une collaboration avec REMIND, consortium national de 14 services de gastro-entérologie, autour de MAINTAIN POP, un essai clinique interventionnel post-opératoire de phase 2 dans la maladie de Crohn.\n...\nMAINTAIN POP est un essai de phase 2 randomisé contre placebo auquel participent 13 sites du groupe REMIND.\nIl vise à recruter 80 patients atteints de la maladie de Crohn et étant entrés en rémission suite à une intervention chirurgicale, afin d'évaluer EXL01 comme traitement d'entretien, seul ou en association avec des anti-TNF.\nL'étude évaluera la sécurité et l'efficacité de EXL01 par rapport à des critères d'évaluation endoscopiques (par exemple, la récurrence endoscopique six mois après l’intervention).\nCette étude interventionnelle post-opératoire est coordonnée par le groupe REMIND.\nMAINTAIN POP est la continuation du travail entrepris avec MAINTAIN (NCT05542355), un essai de phase 1 chez l'homme évaluant EXL01 pour le maintien de la rémission induite par les stéroïdes chez des patients atteints d’une maladie de Crohn légère à modérée.\n...\nLes résultats sont attendus pour le premier semestre 2025, y compris les données préliminaires translationnelles concernant l'engagement des cibles.\n...\nL'autorisation pour l'essai clinique MAINTAIN POP sera soumise en septembre 2024, avec pour objectif de commencer le recrutement début 2025 et de publier les principaux résultats au premier trimestre 2027.\n...\nEXL01 est un produit biothérapeutique vivant (LBP - live biotherapeutic product) contenant une souche unique non modifiée de F. prausnitzii, un acteur important du microbiome intestinal humain, qui est développé en tant que nouveau médicament modulant le système immunitaire.", "date": "2024-06-28", "last_updated": "2025-01-22", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.5}, "jev_score": 0.345, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "News - Exeliom Biosciences", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/newsroom/news", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "Jun 25, 2024\nExeliom Biosciences collaborates with REMIND in MAINTAIN POP study\n...\nExeliom Biosciences Announces that the Independent Data Monitoring Committee (IDMC) Recommends Continuation of Phase 1 Clinical Trial of EXL01 in Crohn’s Disease\n...\nFeb 24, 2022\nExeliom Biosciences announces approval of Clinical Trial Application from the Belgian Agency for Single Strain Live Biotherapeutic EXL01 (Faecalibacterium prausnitzii) for the treatment of Crohn’s Disease\nExplore", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.4433}, "jev_score": 0.3325, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Delving into the Latest Updates on EXL-01 with Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/9500af2e0b0346d8b3f9d8d939800b1b", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Originator Organization\nExeliom Biosciences\nActive Organization\nExeliom Biosciences\nInactive Organization\nLicense Organization\nDrug Highest Phase\nPhase 2\n...\nCTIS2024-511357-22-00\nRecruiting\nPhase 2\nIIT\nA Phase 2 multicentre, randomised, parallel-arm, placebo-controlled, double-blind study to evaluate the safety and target engagement of EXL01 in the prevention of post-operative recurrence of Crohn’s Disease - REMIND-2024-001\nStart Date\n31 Mar 2025\n...\nCrohn Disease\nPhase 2\nFrance\nExeliom Biosciences", "date": "2025-06-18", "last_updated": "2025-08-14", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.4767}, "jev_score": 0.3099, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences' Post - LinkedIn", "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/exeliombio_microbiome-immunooncology-cancertreatment-activity-7275801142511472640-yp5E", "domain": "linkedin.com", "snippet": "💰 In March, we announced a $500K grant from the Crohn's & Colitis Foundation to advance our EXL01 program, focused on maintaining remission in Crohn’s Disease.\n...\n💥 2024 was a huge year for clinical milestones, let’s recap: 🏥 We completed our Phase I study in Crohn’s Disease, demonstrating the excellent safety profile of EXL01.\nEXL01, our lead candidate, is a next-generation immune modulator harnessing the immune-reprogramming properties of Faecalibacterium prausnitzii on macrophages.\n...\n🥼 In June, we introduced the MAINTAIN POP study, a Phase II trial evaluating EXL01 for Crohn’s disease, in collaboration with REMIND and 14 French gastroenterology departments.\nIn October, this trial was submitted to the French National Agency for Medicines and Health Products (ANSM Agence nationale de sécurité du médicament et des produits de santé).", "date": "2024-12-20", "last_updated": "2026-08-10", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.62, "evidence": 0.4933}, "jev_score": 0.3058, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences collaborates with REMIND in ...", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/exeliom-biosciences-collaborates-with-remind-in-maintain-pop-study", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "·      **Phase IItrial will assess efficacy of EXL01 as treatment to prevent post-operative recurrencein patients with Crohn’s disease**\n...\n**Paris, France, June 25, 2024 –** ExeliomBiosciences, a clinical-stage biotechnology company developing new therapies inimmuno-inflammation and immuno-oncology,today announces its collaboration with REMIND, a nationwide consortium of 14 Frenchgastroenterology departments, in the MAINTAIN POP study, a phase IIpost-operative interventional trial in Crohn’s disease.\n...\nMAINTAIN POP is a phase IIrandomized placebo-controlled trial that will involve 13 sites in the REMINDgroup.\nIt aims to enroll 80 patients with Crohn's disease who have successfullybeen brought to remission by surgery, to evaluate EXL01 as a stand-alonemaintenance treatment or in combination with anti-TNFs.\nThe study will assessboth the safety and efficacy of EXL01 against endoscopic endpoints (e.g.endoscopic recurrence at six months post-surgery).\nThis is a post-operativeinterventional study - coordinated by the REMIND group.\nMAINTAIN POP is a continuation of thework undertaken in MAINTAIN (NCT05542355), a first-in-human phase I trialevaluating EXL01 for the maintenance of steroid-induced remission in patientswith mild to moderate Crohn’s disease.\nThe preliminary results support agood safety profile for EXL01 in combination with corticosteroids and as amonotherapy in patients with Crohn’s disease, as recently confirmed by theIDMC.\n...\nThe clinical trial authorization for MAINTAIN POP willbe submitted in September 2024, with the aim of starting enrollment in early2025 and publishing topline results in Q1, 2027.\n...\nEXL01 is a live biotherapeutic product (LBP) containingan unmodified single-strain of *F. prausnitzii*, a key player in the humangut microbiome, which is being developed as a new immune modulating medicine.EXL01 acts on key innate immune system targets to trigger non-suppressiveinflammation resolving mechanisms.", "date": "2024-06-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.4133}, "jev_score": 0.2852, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Exeliom Biosciences collabore avec REMIND sur l’essai", "url": "https://ala.associates/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/240625-Exeliom-MAINTAIN-POP-FR-1.pdf", "domain": "ala.associates", "snippet": " Cette étude de phase 2 vise à évaluer l'efficacité de EXL01 en \ntraitement préventif des récidives post-opératoires chez les \npatients atteints de la maladie de Crohn \n...\n La partie A de l'étude MAINTAIN a été arrêtée en raison d’un\nrecrutement trop lent \n L'étude MAINTAIN POP commencera début 2025 ; les \npremiers résultats sont attendus au premier trimestre 2027 \nParis, France, le 25 juin 2024 - Exeliom Biosciences, société de biotechnologie au stade clinique développant de nouvelles thérapies en immuno-inflammation et immuno- oncologie, annonce aujourd’hui une collaboration avec REMIND, consortium national de 14 services de gastro-entérologie, autour de MAINTAIN POP, un essai clinique interventionnel post-opératoire de phase 2 dans la maladie de Crohn.\n...\nMAINTAIN POP est un essai de phase 2 randomisé contre placebo auquel participent 13 sites du groupe REMIND.\nIl vise à recruter 80 patients atteints de la maladie de Crohn et étant entrés en rémission suite à une intervention chirurgicale, afin d'évaluer EXL01 comme traitement d'entretien, seul ou en association avec des anti-TNF.\nL'étude évaluera la sécurité et l'efficacité de EXL01 par rapport à des critères d'évaluation endoscopiques\n(par exemple, la récurrence endoscopique six mois après l’intervention).\nCette étude \ninterventionnelle post-opératoire est coordonnée par le groupe REMIND.\nMAINTAIN POP est la continuation du travail entrepris avec MAINTAIN (NCT05542355), un essai de phase 1 chez l'homme évaluant EXL01 pour le maintien de la rémission induite par les stéroïdes chez des patients atteints d’une maladie de Crohn légère à modérée.\n...\nL'autorisation pour l'essai clinique MAINTAIN POP sera soumise en septembre 2024, avec pour objectif de commencer le recrutement début 2025 et de publier les principaux résultats au premier trimestre 2027.\n...\nEXL01 est un produit biothérapeutique vivant (LBP - live biotherapeutic product) contenant une souche unique non modifiée de F. prausnitzii, un acteur important du microbiome intestinal humain, qui est développé en tant que nouveau médicament modulant le système immunitaire.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-19", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.4233}, "jev_score": 0.2751, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "News - exeliom", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/several-exl01-studies-hit-recruitment-milestones", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "Inflammatory Disease - Crohn’s Disease\n- MAINTAIN POP (NCT06925061): Recruitment is ongoing as planned since the launch in April.\nThis is a Phase 2 randomized clinical trial evaluating EXL01 in postoperative Crohn’s disease patients, in collaboration with hashtag#REMIND and 14 French gastroenterology departments.\n...\n- LIVEDIFF (NCT063060): Recruitment has resumed and the first patients have been enrolled in part two of the study.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.37}, "jev_score": 0.2405, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "The BIG Phase 2 study (Gastric Cancer is moving forward!", "url": "https://www.exeliombio.com/news/the-big-phase-2-study-gastric-cancer-is-moving-forward", "domain": "exeliombio.com", "snippet": "Exeliom Biosciences collaborates with REMIND in MAINTAIN POP study\n...\nOct 19, 2023\nExeliom Biosciences Announces that the Independent Data Monitoring Committee (IDMC) Recommends Continuation of Phase 1 Clinical Trial of EXL01 in Crohn’s Disease", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.5, "evidence": 0.2233}, "jev_score": 0.1116, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 2724.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:22+00:00"}
