hase 2\n...\nNCT05247216\nCompleted\nPhase 2\n...\nNCT03977480\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nA Multicenter, Randomized, Double-blind, Placebo Parallel Controlled Clinical Study on the Effecacy and Safety of Different Dosing Regimens of Hemay007 in Patients With Active Ulcerative Colitis\nThis study adopts a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, low-medium-high dose group and placebo parallel controlled clinical study design.\nAfter screening, patients with active ulcerative colitis who meet the inclusion criteria and do not meet the exclusion criteria will be randomized by 1:1:1:1 to Hemay007 400 mg BID group, 800 mg QD group, 600 mg BID group or placebo group, with proposed 72 cases in each group.\nAfter 12 weeks of double-blind inductive treatment period, the patients will enter the Hemay007 open treatment period of 12 weeks when Hemay007 600 mg BID will be used as the medication regimen.\nAll randomized subjects who have received the investigational drug should be subjected to a 4-week observation after the end of treatment.\nStart Date\n13 Aug 2019\nSponsor / Collaborator\nTianjin Hemei Biological Technology Co.,Ltd.\nCTR20182150\nTerminated\nPhase 2\nHemay007不同剂量给药方案用于活动性溃疡性结肠炎患者有效性和安全性多中心、随机、双盲、安慰剂平行对照临床研究\n[Translation]\nA multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical study on the efficacy and safety of different dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with active ulcerative colitis\n...\nPrimary objective: To explore the efficacy and dose-effect relationship of Hemay007 at different dosing regimens for active ulcerative colitis compared with placebo.\nSecondary objective: To evaluate the safety of Hemay007 at different dosing regimens for active ulcerative colitis compared with placebo.\nTo observe the population pharmacokinetic characteristics of Hemay007 in patients with ulcerative colitis.\nStart Date\n13 Aug 2019\nSponsor / Collaborator\nXiajiang Hemei Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.\n...\n10\n...\nDiscovery\nChina\nTianjin Hemay Pharmaceutical Technology Co., Ltd.\n13 Aug 2019", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.61, "evidence": 0.26}, "jev_score": 0.1586, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 2580.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:23+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q024-4", "row_id": "q024", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as ITGA4 ITGB7 Integrin Alpha-4 Beta-7 Integrin α4β7 Small Molecule therapies", "query": "emvistegrast integrin alpha-4 beta-7 ulcerative colitis clinical trial", "anchors": ["emvistegrast"], "search_latency_ms": 8630.8, "target": "drugs competing with emvistegrast: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 371.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9957, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000418, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.873, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|PN-943|Oral gastrointestinal-restricted cyclic peptide targeting α4β7|NCT04504383|Phase 2|Completed Feb 2023; primary endpoint not met for 450mg dose|\n...\n|α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027|\n...\n#### 2.2.5 Emvistegrast\nEmvistegrast is an oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor designed to block immune cell migration to the gastrointestinal tract, reducing intestinal inflammation in UC patients.\nA Phase 2 double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter RCT evaluates three different doses of GS-1427 against placebo over 76 weeks, with the primary endpoint measuring clinical response at week 12 defined as a decrease in mMS of at least 2 points with 30% reduction from baseline and improved RBS (NCT06290934).\nParticipants must be adults aged 18–75 with moderately to severely active UC confirmed by endoscopy and histology, having at least 15 cm disease extent from anal verge, modified MS of 5–9 points with MES of at least 2, and inadequate response or intolerance to prior treatments including corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapies, while excluding those with CD, toxic megacolon, prior vedolizumab exposure, or contraindicated medications, with the study completing primary outcomes in October 2025 and final completion estimated for February 2027.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.873, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "drug hunter on X: \"Gilead's Recently Disclosed, Selective α4β7 ...", "url": "https://x.com/drughunter_com/status/1970510609163723171", "domain": "x.com", "snippet": "Gilead’s Recently Disclosed, Selective α4β7 Inhibitor Emvistegrast Advances to Phase 2 for UC | https://drughunters.com/42ESG2P Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is a selective oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor in Ph.\n2 for UC, first disclosed by Gilead’s Leena Patel at the ACS Fall 2025 meeting in Washington, DC.\nIt targets the same clinically validated pathway as vedolizumab (Entyvio®), aiming to deliver gut-selective efficacy in an orally administered drug.\n...\nGS-1427 is now in Ph.\n2 clinical trials for UC, a welcome advance for the estimated 2 million patients living with recurrent UC.", "date": "2025-09-23", "last_updated": "2025-10-21", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.6433}, "jev_score": 0.5983, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Integrin alpha 4 beta 7 분자 정보 - ACROBiosystems", "url": "https://kr.acrobiosystems.com/category/target-protein/integrin-alpha-4-beta-7", "domain": "kr.acrobiosystems.com", "snippet": "|Drugs in Clinical Trials|14|Target Synonym|LPAM-1，Lymphocyte Peyer's patch Adhesion Molecule 1|\n...\n|Emvistegrast|GS-1427|Phase 2 Clinical|Gilead Sciences Sl|Colitis, Ulcerative; Inflammatory Bowel Diseases|Details|", "date": "2024-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.6167}, "jev_score": 0.5304, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|α4β7 antagonist|Emvistegrast (GS-1427)|Oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor|NCT06290934|Phase 2|Primary completion Oct 2025, final completion Feb 2027|\n...\n#### 2.2.5. Emvistegrast\nEmvistegrast is an oral α4β7 integrin inhibitor designed to block immune cell migration to the gastrointestinal tract, reducing intestinal inflammation in UC patients.\nA Phase 2 double-blind, placebo-controlled, multicenter RCT evaluates three different doses of GS-1427 against placebo over 76 weeks, with the primary endpoint measuring clinical response at week 12 defined as a decrease in mMS of at least 2 points with 30% reduction from baseline and improved RBS (NCT06290934).\nParticipants must be adults aged 18–75 with moderately to severely active UC confirmed by endoscopy and histology, having at least 15 cm disease extent from anal verge, modified MS of 5–9 points with MES of at least 2, and inadequate response or intolerance to prior treatments including corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapies, while excluding those with CD, toxic megacolon, prior vedolizumab exposure, or contraindicated medications, with the study completing primary outcomes in October 2025 and final completion estimated for February 2027.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.6033}, "jev_score": 0.4766, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "UCSF Inflammatory Bowel Disease Clinical Trials for 2026 ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/inflammatory-bowel-disease", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "snippet": "The purpose of this study is to determine the safety, efficacy, and pharmacokinetics (PK) of etrasimod for the treatment of moderately to severely active ulcerative colitis in adolescents (≥ 12 years up to < 18 years of age).\n...\n- ## GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\n** Sorry, in progress, not accepting new patients\nThe goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nThe study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12.", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.4732, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emvistegrast - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents - Patsnap Synapse", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/af89e3ebb252478e983f2263a86ec636", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Small molecule drug\nSynonyms\nGS 1427,\nGS-1427\nTarget\nα4β7\nAction\nantagonists\nMechanism\nα4β7 antagonists(Integrin alpha-4/beta-7 antagonists)\n...\nActive Indication\nColitis, Ulcerative\n...\n2\nClinical Trials associated with Emvistegrast\nNCT06290934\nActive, not recruiting\nPhase 2\nA Phase 2, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Multicenter, Dose-Ranging Study Evaluating the Efficacy and Safety of GS-1427 in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (UC)\nThe goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nThe study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12.\nStart Date\n27 Mar 2024\nSponsor / Collaborator\nGilead Sciences, Inc.\nACTRN12621000612819\nCompleted\nPhase 1\nA Phase 1, Dose-Escalation Study in Healthy Volunteers to Evaluate the Pharmacokinetics, Safety, and Tolerability of GS-1427 Following Single and Multiple Dosing\nStart Date\n...\nOne upcoming catalyst, for instance, is a readout for the integrin inhibitor emvistegrast, which Gilead is studying in the Phase 2 SWIFT trial for ulcerative colitis.\nThe study’s primary completion date was in March, but data are expected later this year.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-11", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.57}, "jev_score": 0.4446, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "emvistegrast | Ligand page", "url": "https://www.guidetopharmacology.org/GRAC/LigandDisplayForward?tab=clinical&ligandId=14189", "domain": "guidetopharmacology.org", "snippet": "|**Synonyms:** example 356 [WO2020092375] | GS-1427 | GS1427 **Compound class:** Synthetic organic **Comment:** Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is an oral α4/β7 integrin inhibitor.\nIt was designed to reduce α4β7 integrin-mediated recruitment of α4β7-expressing lymphocytes to the gut, as disease-modifying mechanism for inflammatory bowel disease.\nGS-1427 is one of the inhibitors claimed in patent WO2020092375A1 [1].\n...\n|Emvistegrast (GS-1427) is a clinical lead candidate.\nIt is being developed (by Gilead Sciences) for immunomodulatory potential in ulcerative colitis.|\n...\n|Clinical Trial ID|Title|Type|Source|Comment|References|\n|NCT06290934|Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis|Phase 2 Interventional|Gilead Sciences| | |", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-17", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.55}, "jev_score": 0.4345, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "emvistegrast (GS-1427) / Gilead - LARVOL DELTA", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=43b8710c-7d26-400d-99a6-57e1ce79a7ba", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF EMVISTEGRAST (FORMERLY GS-1427), AN ORAL Α4Β7 INTEGRIN INHIBITOR, FOR MODERATE TO SEVERE ULCERATIVE COLITIS: RESULTS OF THE INDUCTION PHASE OF THE PHASE 2 SWIFT TRIAL (UEGW 2026) - \"Full abstract will be published on the day of presentation\"\n...\n## Pharmacokinetics, Safety, and Tolerability of GS-1427, an Oral Prodrug of a Potent and Selective α4β7 Integrin Inhibitor, in Healthy Participants. (PubMed, J Clin Pharmacol) - \"The bioavailability of tablet versus capsule formulations and the effect of food and an acid-reducing agent (omeprazole) on exposure were also assessed. All AEs were grade 1 in severity and no serious AEs or deaths were reported. The pharmacokinetics, safety, and tolerability results from this study support further evaluation of GS-1427 in a phase 2 trial in patients with UC.\"\n...\n## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Active, not recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Trial completion date: Jul 2027 ➔ Apr 2028\n...\n## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Active, not recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Recruiting ➔ Active, not recruiting\n...\n## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Trial completion date: Feb 2027 ➔ Jul 2027 | Trial primary completion date: Oct 2025 ➔ Mar 2026\n...\n## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=228 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | N=423 ➔ 228 | Trial completion date: Mar 2028 ➔ Feb 2027 | Trial primary completion date: May 2027 ➔ Oct 2025\n...\n## SWIFT: Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (clinicaltrials.gov) - P2 | N=423 | Recruiting | Sponsor: Gilead Sciences | Not yet recruiting ➔ Recruiting\n### Enrollment open • Gastroenterology • Gastrointestinal Disorder • Immunology • Inflammatory Bowel Disease • Ulcerative Colitis", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.79, "evidence": 0.53}, "jev_score": 0.4187, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "UCSF Ulcerative Colitis Clinical Trials for 2026", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/ulcerative-colitis", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "snippet": "** San Francisco, California** and other locations\n- ## GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\n** Sorry, in progress, not accepting new patients\nThe goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nThe study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12.", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.3367}, "jev_score": 0.2391, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu/trial/NCT06290934", "domain": "clinicaltrials.ucsf.edu", "snippet": "UCSF Inflammatory Bowel Disease Trial → GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\n1. Inflammatory Bowel Disease Studies\n# Study of GS-1427 in Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\na study on Inflammatory Bowel Disease Ulcerative Colitis\n## Summary\n- **** Eligibility**\n**for people ages 18-75** (full criteria)\n- **** Location**\nat Oakland, California and other locations\n- **** Dates**\nstudy started March 2024 study ends around April 2028\n...\nThe goal of this study is to learn if emvistegrast (formerly GS-1427) is effective in treating participants with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis.\nThe study will compare participants in different treatment groups treated with emvistegrast with participants treated with placebo.\nThe primary objective of this study is to assess the efficacy of emvistegrast, compared with placebo control, in achieving clinical response at Week 12.\n### Official Title\nA Phase 2, Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Multicenter, Dose-Ranging Study Evaluating the Efficacy and Safety of GS-1427 in Adult Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis (UC)\n...\n### You can join if…\n**Open to people ages 18-75**\n- Individuals have Ulcerative Colitis (UC) with symptoms of at least 90 days duration before randomization, with the diagnosis confirmed by endoscopy and histology at any time prior to randomization.\nDocumentation of endoscopy and histology consistent with the diagnosis of UC must be available in the source documents.\n- Individuals have UC with minimum disease extent of 15 cm from the anal verge.\n- Individuals have moderately to severely active UC as determined by endoscopy occurring during screening with a total modified Mayo Clinic Score (mMCS) of 5 to 9 points, including a centrally read endoscopic subscore of at least 2.\n- Individuals have an inadequate response or loss of response or are intolerant to at least 1 of the following UC treatments: corticosteroids, immunomodulators, or advanced therapy.\n- Individuals have an inadequate response or loss of response or are intolerant to < 3 AT mechanisms of action for UC (use of 2 or more AT with the same mechanism of action, eg, 2 TNF-α inhibitors, counts as 1 mechanism of action)\n### You CAN'T join if...\n- Have a current diagnosis of Crohn's Disease (CD) or clinical findings suggestive of CD, diagnosis of indeterminate colitis due to etiologies such as an enteric pathogen, or lymphocytic or collagenous colitis.\n- Have a current diagnosis of toxic megacolon, symptomatic colonic stricture, acute severe colitis, fulminant colitis, or abdominal abscess at screening or randomization.\n- Have any history of exposure to vedolizumab or other integrin antagonists\n- Requirement for ongoing therapy with or use of any prohibited medication as specified in the protocol\n...\n## Locations\n- UCSF Benioff Children's Hospital Oakland\nOakland California 94609 United States\n- University Of California, Davis\nSacramento California 95817 United States\n## Details\n- **Status**\nin progress, not accepting new patients\n- **Start Date**\nMarch 2024\n- **Completion Date**\nApril 2028 (estimated)\n- **Sponsor**\nGilead Sciences\n- **Links**\n** Gilead Clinical Trials Website\n- **ID**\nNCT06290934\n- **Phase**\nPhase 2 research study\n- **Study Type**\nInterventional\n- **Participants**\nExpecting 228 study participants\n- **Last Updated**", "date": "2024-03-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.69, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.207, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 9002.6, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:23+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q025-4", "row_id": "q025", "mode": "queries", "objective": "Ulcerative Colitis competing agents with the same mechanism of action as HEMAY007, targeting TNF-α Tumor Necrosis Factor Alpha", "query": "HEMAY007 ulcerative colitis anti-TNF biosimilar landscape", "anchors": ["HEMAY007"], "search_latency_ms": 2117.6, "target": "drugs competing with HEMAY007: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 340.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10365, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000435, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8122, "results": [{"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Anti-TNFα in inflammatory bowel disease: from originators to ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11303209/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The recent expiration of patents of some anti-TNFα biologics (such as infliximab and adalimumab) facilitated the development of biosimilars.\nComparable pharmacokinetic, efficacy, safety, and immunogenicity profiles between anti-TNFα originators and biosimilars were demonstrated in different studies.\n...\nIn order to prevent its pro-inflammatory process, monoclonal antibodies to TNFα including infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, and certolizumab have been developed and approved for CD and/or UC treatment (Leone et al., 2023).\n...\nSeven years later, biosimilars of infliximab, Remsima and Inflectra (CT-P13) got approval for CD or UC in September 2013.\n...\nCT-P13 is the first approved biosimilar of infliximab used in immune-mediated diseases including RA, ankylosing spondylitis (AS), psoriasis, CD, and UC (Parigi et al., 2021).\n...\nPF-06438179/GP1111 is another biosimilar of infliximab, which was approved for immune-mediated diseases by the FDA in 2017 and by the EMA in 2018 (Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023a; Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023b).\n...\nABP 710, a biosimilar of infliximab, was approved for CD, UC, RA, AS, psoriasis, and psoriasis arthritis by the FDA in 2019 (Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023b).\n...\nABP 501, the first biosimilar of adalimumab, was approved for various diseases including RA, CD, UC, AS, and others by the FDA in 2016 and by the EMA in 2017(Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023a; Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023b).\n...\nBiosimilars of adalimumab including FKB327, MSB11022, AVT02, PF-06410293, CHS-1420, CT-P17, and others were also approved for treatment of CD and UC (Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023b; Generics and Biosimilars Initiative, 2023a).\n...\nBiosimilars do play a key role in the treatment of IBD.\nWith the expiration of patents of some anti-TNFα biologics, an increasing number of anti-TNFα biosimilars entered the market.", "date": "2024-07-24", "last_updated": "2026-08-26", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.93, "evidence": 0.8733}, "jev_score": 0.8122, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Role of biologics and biosimilars in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5961645/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Currently, monoclonal antibodies against tumor necrosis factor-alpha (infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab, and golimumab), integrins (vedolizumab and natalizumab), and interleukin (IL)-12 and IL-23 antagonists (ustekinumab) are approved for use in IBD.\n...\n^10^ With the advent of advances in medical science and technology, a new group of drugs emerged for various chronic disease conditions called the biologics that are derived partly or completely from living biological sources such as animals and humans.^12^ The most widely used biologics are the TNF-α inhibitors such as adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab, and infliximab, which are highly effective in the treatment of both UC and CD.\n...\n|Adalimumab (innovator)|Humira^®^ (AbbVie)|UC and CD|SC|160 mg on day 1 and|40 mg every 4 weeks|\n|Adalimumab-atto (biosimilar)|Amjevita™ (Amgen)|80 mg on day 15|from day 29| | |\n...\n|Company|Name of the agent|Status|\n|--|--|--|\n|Biosimilars of adalimumab| | |\n|Coherous Biosciences|CHS1420|Phase III (plaque psoriasis)|\n|Fresenius Kabi|MSB11022|Preregistration (plaque psoriasis) and Phase III (UC, CD, and others)|\n|Fujifilm Kyowa Kirin Biologics|FKB327|Preregistration (RA).\nSubmitted to EMA in May 2017|\n|LG Chem|LBAL|Phase III (RA)|\n|Momenta Pharmaceuticals|M923|Phase III (RA and plaque psoriasis)|\n...\nAdalimumab is indicated for use in moderate-to-severe RA, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, moderate-to-severe CD and UC with inadequate response to conventional therapy, moderate-to-severe chronic plaque psoriasis, hidradenitis suppurativa, uveitis, and Behcet’s disease.\n...\n^74^ These drugs are called as biosimilars, which may be defined as a biologic product that is highly like the reference biologic with no clinically meaningful differences in clinical safety or efficacy.^75^ Biologics are a highly complex protein or other substances derived from a biological source, and hence, complete reproducibility of reference biologics is not possible.\nThus, minor differences in clinically inactive components are permissible.^76^ The current biosimilars approved for use in IBD belong to TNF-α inhibitors namely adalimumab and infliximab.^77^ Biosimilars of adalimumab include adalimumab-atto (Amjevita™) by Amgen Pharmaceuticals (Thousand Oaks, CA, USA) and adalimumab-abdm (Cyltezo^®^) by Boehringer Ingelheim.\n...\nWith regard to infliximab, there are again three biosimilars, which are approved by FDA namely infliximab-dyyb (Inflectra^®^) by Pfizer (New York City, NY, USA), infliximab-abda (Renflexis™) by Samsung Bioepis (Seoul, South Korea), and infliximab-qbtx (Ixifi™) by Pfizer in 2016, 2017, and 2017, respectively.", "date": "2018-05-16", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.91, "evidence": 0.85}, "jev_score": 0.7735, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|\n...\nNext-generation TNF pathway inhibitors employ several innovative strategies to address these limitations: selective TNFR1 antagonists preserving TNFR2 signaling (balinatunfib/SAR441566), bispecific antibodies targeting TNF-α plus additional mediators for synergistic blockade (SAR442970 targeting TNF-α and OX40L\n), small molecule TNF modulators avoiding immunogenicity (hemay007), CD40 antagonists indirectly modulating TNF-related pathways (ravagalimab), enhanced imaging techniques using fluorescently labeled agents to optimize dosing (adalimumab-680LT), and biosimilar formulations providing increased access (CT-P13).\nThese approaches aim to improve upon the 30-60% clinical response and 15-35% remission rates of current anti-TNF agents while reducing immunogenicity, optimizing pharmacokinetics, and enhancing safety profiles.\n...\nA Phase II study (NCT03977480) evaluated Hemay007, a small molecule that acts as a TNF-α regulator, using a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design with four treatment arms comparing different dosing regimens (400 mg BID, 800 mg QD, 600 mg BID, and placebo) over 12 weeks followed by an open-label extension.\n...\nThe study was terminated early due to recruitment difficulties, completing in August 2022 with only 70 of the planned 288 patients enrolled, and no efficacy or safety results have been posted on ClinicalTrials.gov.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.8067}, "jev_score": 0.7583, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Accelerating Earlier Access to Anti-TNF- Agents with Biosimilar Medicines in the Management of Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/b3ee/47fc77d4c6e6c4ace86137d14d514f59a81f.pdf", "domain": "pdfs.semanticscholar.org", "snippet": "Biosimilar anti-TNF-α medicines may represent a valuable opportunity for cost savings and optimized patient outcomes by improving access to advanced therapies and allowing earlier anti-TNF-α treatment initiation.\n...\nThe anti-tumor necrosis factor alpha (anti-TNF-α) agents infliximab and adalimumab are effective biologic treatments used to treat people with moderate-to-severe IBD [4,5], as well as populations defined as at ‘high-risk’ for disease complications [6].\n...\nBiosimilar biologics, which are biologics that are designed to have equivalent efficacy and comparable safety to another approved biologic ‘reference’ medicine (Table 1), pro- vide access to effective therapies without inflating costs.\n...\nAs of July 2024, there are 17 biosimilar medicines for IBD approved in the United States (US) and the European\nUnion (EU), and 15 in Canada [18–20].\nAnti-TNF-α biosimilars currently dominate the IBD biosimilar landscape, but as other high-efficacy treatments begin to reach patent expiry, the\nIBD biosimilar landscape is expanding with the approval of ustekinumab biosimilars and\n...\ncommonly used to treat IBD include anti-TNF-α agents (e.g., infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab, and certolizumab), adhesion molecule antagonists (e.g., vedolizumab), interleukin antagonists (e.g., ustekinumab), and JAK inhibitors (e.g., tofacitinib) [27].\n...\nA biosimilar is a biologic medicine that is designed to match an approved biologic medicine (the ‘reference’ medicine) in structure and function.\n...\nBiosimilar medicines used to treat IBD include biosimilars of infliximab, adalimumab, and ustekinumab biologics [18–20].\n...\nWith regard\nto UC, the evidence supporting the early use of biologic anti-TNF-α agents is less clear, potentially due to a lack of a defined ‘window of opportunity’ for early UC [30,31].\n...\nRecommends the use of anti-TNF-α\ntherapies over no treatment for\nmoderate-to-severe UC;\nconditionally recommends early\nanti-TNF-α use after failure of\n5-aminosalicylate therapy", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-07-16", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.639, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Guide to Navigating the Landscape of Biosimilars for ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11348546/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Biosimilars, which are biologic medications that are highly similar to the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved reference product with no clinically meaningful differences in safety, purity, or potency, offer the same therapeutic benefits at a reduced cost.\n...\nBiosimilars are developed from the same type of living source and administered by the same route, frequency, and dosage.\n...\n^7^ Interchangeable biosimilars can be substituted and dispensed for the reference product at the pharmacy level without the approval of the prescribing provider, depending on state pharmacy laws.\n...\nA list of all currently available infliximab and adalimumab biosimilars (some of which are interchangeable, as well as some products that are unbranded formulations of infliximab and adalimumab) indicated for IBD is provided in the Table.\nThe FDA approved 2 biosimilars for ustekinumab: ustekinumab-auub (Wezlana, Amgen), which is interchangeable, in 2023; and ustekinumab-aekn (Selarsdi, Teva Pharmaceuticals) in 2024.\n...\n^15^ Biosimilars for vedolizumab are not anticipated to enter the US marketplace until 2032.^16^\n...\n|Avsola (infliximab-axxq)|Amgen|2019|CD (adult/pediatric), UC (adult/pediatric), RA, AS, PsA, Ps|\n|Inflectra (infliximab-dyyb)|Pfizer|2016|CD (adult/pediatric), UC (adult/pediatric), RA, AS, PsA, Ps|\n...\n|Abrilada (adalimumab-afzb)^b^|Pfizer|2019|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Amjevita (adalimumab-atto)^c^^-^^d^|Amgen|2016|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Cyltezo (adalimumab-adbm)^b^^-^^d^|Boehringer Ingelheim|2017|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Hadlima (adalimumab-bwwd)^d^|Organon/ Samsung Bioepis|2019|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n...\n|Simlandi (adalimumab-ryvk)^b^|Alvotech/Teva|2024|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n|Yuflyma (adalimumab-aaty)|Celltrion|2023|RA, JIA, PsA, AS, CD, UC, Ps, HS, UV|\n...\nAlthough most infliximab and adalimumab biosimilars received FDA approval for use in IBD through the process of extrapolation, many published studies and realworld evidence from Europe demonstrate that biosimilars result in similar patient outcomes as expected with the reference product, such as sustaining remission without increased adverse effects or immunogenicity risks.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.75}, "jev_score": 0.615, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Biosimilars in inflammatory bowel disease: Beyond evidence ...", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/1007-9327/full/v32/i28/119462.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "Biosimilars offer an important opportunity to expand access to advanced treatment, yet their uptake in IBD remains inconsistent despite substantial evidence supporting comparable efficacy, safety, and immunogenicity.\n...\nBiosimilars offer an important response to this access crisis.\nThey are biological products that are highly similar to an already approved reference product, with no clinically meaningful differences in safety, efficacy, or immunogenicity[6,7].\n...\nThe Italian PROSIT-BIO cohort, a prospective multicenter study of 547 IBD patients treated with CT-P13, reported clinical response rates of 73.7% in biologic-naive patients and 78.9% in switched patients at 24 weeks, with no significant differences across treatment groups[16].\n...\nMore recently, comparable remission maintenance with adalimumab biosimilars (ABP501 and SB5) was demonstrated in a propensity score-weighted IBD cohort[18], while no significant differences in clinical remission rates, hospitalizations, or emergency department visits between originator biologics and biosimilars were reported in a Canadian real-world registry[19].\n...\nAvailable data indicate that persistence with adalimumab biosimilars in IBD reaches approximately 68.6% at 6 months and 60.4% at 12 months in patients switched from originators[22].\n...\nCollectively, both the American Gastroenterological Association 2024 guidelines for UC and the 2025 guidelines for CD now explicitly recognize biosimilars as appropriate alternatives to originator biologics, endorsing their use across clinical scenarios[24,25].\n...\nTaken together, the totality of evidence supports the clinical use of biosimilars in IBD across the spectrum of treatment scenarios—from biologic-naive initiation to single and multiple switching.\n...\nA comprehensive narrative review demonstrated that immunogenicity is a class-related phenomenon inherent to all biologic therapies, including originators, and that ADA profiles observed with biosimilars are quantitatively and qualitatively comparable to those of their reference products[29].\nThis finding has been further corroborated in the IBD-specific context by a 2025 review that confirmed that neither switching nor initiating biosimilar infliximab or adalimumab confers an incremental immunogenicity risk beyond that expected with the originator molecule[30].", "date": "2026-07-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.5333}, "jev_score": 0.3466, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "TNF-Alpha Proteins, Antibodies & ELISA Kits", "url": "https://www.acrobiosystems.com/category/target-protein/tnf-alpha", "domain": "acrobiosystems.com", "snippet": "|Recombinant anti-TNFα monoclonal antibody (Wuhan Institute of Biological Products)|Phase 3 Clinical|Wuhan Institute Of Biological Products Co Ltd|Plaque psoriasis|Details| |\n...\n|Hemay007|Hemay-007|Phase 2 Clinical|Tianjin Hemay Bio-Tech Co Ltd|Arthritis, Rheu\n…", "date": "2024-09-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-24", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.4867}, "jev_score": 0.3164, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TNF modulator|Hemay007|Small molecule TNF-α regulator|NCT03977480|Phase 2|Terminated early Aug 2022 due to recruitment difficulties|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-14", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.41}, "jev_score": 0.3075, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "BUSINESS", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107411/a123461/sehk25052902266.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Hemay007.\nOur key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 significantly reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys, and pathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue almost returned to a normal state.\nAs of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed the phase II clinical trial for monotherapy for UC.\n...\nHemay007\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model,\nHemay007 significantly reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys.\nPathological examinations of the intestines after treatment revealed that the colon tissue had almost returned to a normal state.\nAs of the Latest Practicable Date, we have completed phase\nII clinical trials for monotherapy for UC in China.\nWe plan to prioritize the clinical trial of our\nCore Products and currently do not have an expected timetable for the next steps of Hemay007.\nMechanism of Action\nHemay007 inhibited the TLR4 signaling pathway, a key driver of inflammation.\n...\nBy inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 effectively blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly\nTNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation.\n...\nWe intend to pursue marketing", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-13", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.72, "evidence": 0.3933}, "jev_score": 0.2832, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "OVERVIEW", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2025/107914/a127807/sehk25120100842.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Our key product, Hemay007 is screened from a compound library directed by multiple pharmacological functional substructures and is a TNF-�small molecule regulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model, Hemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys, and pathological\n...\nHemay007 is screened from a compound library using multiple pharmacological approaches and is a TNF-�small molecule modulator.\nIn the spontaneous UC monkey model,\nHemay007 reduced the number of ulcers in the colons of the diseased monkeys.\n...\nHemay007 inhibited the TLR4 signaling pathway, a key driver of inflammation.\n...\nBy inhibiting this pathway, Hemay007 blocks the release of pro-inflammatory factors, particularly TNF-�, thereby reducing the inflammatory response triggered by immune stimulation.\n...\nThe clinical trial was a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo- controlled, parallel-group study designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and dose-response relationship of various dosing regimens of Hemay007 in patients with UC.\nA total of 70 patients with UC were enrolled and randomized into four parallel groups: 16 patients received\nHemay007 400 mg twice daily (BID), 18 patients received 800 mg once daily (QD), 17 patients received 600 mg BID, and 19 patients received a placebo.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-04", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.41}, "jev_score": 0.2706, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 2458.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:23+00:00"}
