 announced that the FDA granted Priority Review for its supplemental New Drug Application (sNDA) for Revuforj (revumenib), targeting relapsed or refractory mutant NPM1 acute myeloid leukemia (AML).", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.29, "evidence": 0.6967}, "jev_score": 0.202, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukaemia - Drug Discovery World (DDW)", "url": "https://www.ddw-online.com/t/acute-myeloid-leukaemia/", "domain": "ddw-online.com", "snippet": "The US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved Daiichi Sankyo’s quizartinib (Vanflyta) for the treatment of adult patients with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) that is FLT3 internal tandem duplication (ITD)-positive.", "date": "2026-08-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.23, "evidence": 0.6433}, "jev_score": 0.148, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "venetoclax for newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia|On May 13, 2026, the Food and Drug Administration approved an oral combination of decitabine and cedazuridine tablets (Inqovi, Taiho Oncology, Inc.) with venetoclax for the treatment of newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults 75 years or older, or who have comorbidities that preclude the use of intensive induction chemotherapy.|5/13/2026|", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.24, "evidence": 0.5567}, "jev_score": 0.1336, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmaceutical research and development pipeline", "url": "https://www.bms.com/research-and-development/pipeline.html", "domain": "bms.com", "snippet": "phase/phase-1\",\"researcharea\":\"<p>Myeloid<\\/p>\",\"category\":\"bms:therapeutiic-area/hematology\",\"subcategory\":\"bms:tumor/acute-myeloid-leukemia\",\"registrationstatus\":\"\"},{\"compoundname\":\"<p><b>Dual Targeting", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.28, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.084, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "acute myeloid leukaemia | pharmaphorum", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/tag/acute-myeloid-leukaemia", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "snippet": "Kura has claimed FDA approval for oral menin inhibitor Komzifti for acute myeloid leukaemia, setting up a battle with Syndax's rival Revuforj.", "date": "2026-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.15, "evidence": 0.49}, "jev_score": 0.0735, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | From monoclonals to bispecific T cell engagers: the evolving antibody-based therapy landscape in acute myeloid leukemia", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2026.1803185/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "As the most studied target antigen for antibody-based therapies in AML and following the success of GO, several agents targeting CD33 have been developed and evaluated in clinical trials, including up to phase-III, but none have achieved regulatory approval (49–56) (**Table 1**).", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.14, "evidence": 0.43}, "jev_score": 0.0602, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Leukemia", "url": "https://ascopost.com/topics/hematologic-malignancies/leukemia/", "domain": "ascopost.com", "snippet": "On May 13, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved an oral combination of decitabine and cedazuridine tablets (Inqovi) plus venetoclax for the treatment of newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults aged 75 years or older or who have comorbidities that preclude them from...", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.3233}, "jev_score": 0.0226, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Pharmaceutical press releases & news", "url": "https://www.bms.com/news-and-media/press-releases.html", "domain": "bms.com", "snippet": "Explore Bristol Myers Squibb press releases covering research milestones, corporate developments and patient focused innovation.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.13, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 3552.2, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:24+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q027-1", "row_id": "q027", "mode": "queries", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "HQL-79 pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "search_latency_ms": 3361.6, "target": "HQL-79", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 410.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 44094, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.001852, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 2, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.684, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "www.creative-biolabs.com › blog › adcHyaluronidase-Conjugated Liposomes May Effectively Target ...", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "snippet": "The research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes.\nThis novel liposome was named HQL.\n...\nIn vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls.\n...\nIn experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups.\nIn in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model.\nResults showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%.\nFluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver.\nThe drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours.\nFurthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours.\nSafety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects.\nMechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.76, "evidence": 0.9}, "jev_score": 0.684, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Hence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.72}, "jev_score": 0.5976, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Structural and functional characterization of HQL-79, an orally ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16547010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Orally administered HQL-79 (30 mg/kg body weight) inhibited antigen-induced production of PGD2, without affecting the production of PGE2 and PGF2alpha, and ameliorated airway inflammation in wild-type and human H-PGDS-overexpressing mice.", "date": "2006-06-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-09", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.34}, "jev_score": 0.3026, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "f�pz�Xq���lܾ�rC���y�]���t�d��y��[w ��۟�^�)�-|���^|��`v�Ko}�Sw�Z�6��~����B� w=q�#/��Ƨ� ���/X����v۽��~5{i�<�����$~LͲ�*��iF���J o��) �X���,���gd͚+�ub�K;��N~�t����)�v��Y�;c{֪W��1�����kP��_�I8W�ǯ��9���7�y�ak$�f釕��試mmZ�g�ɬz��Ɨ?r�T�/ǪCN��� �����mmTB�J��>Y��Lbg����P��u3�-h3���N~�4��w��o��EEo�s 8��Q�㵷��3[�ط��6]�:���������; Z1J��={Y2��%VP�qf��u�~��\"e3rΏ)�в#���o~/O1e����'��0��/��er��z�_`��$*���g�������S�d0�{�l��Gi�Y�~W��G��/�),B�W�!Z]1�*˞��:Y���I�QC����Bi�턺}�