{"case_id": "q027-4", "row_id": "q027", "mode": "queries", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "HQL-79 tumour growth inhibition pancreatic cancer", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "search_latency_ms": 1530.0, "target": "HQL-79", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 416.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8804, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00037, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6264, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Huaier suppresses pancreatic cancer progression via activating cell autophagy induced ferroptosis", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2022.960858/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "**Results:** The growth inhibitory effect of Huaier on PCs was concentration- and time-dependent, but this effect was significantly attenuated by ferroptosis inhibitors.\n...\nThe results revealed that Huaier inhibited cell growth in a concentration-dependent manner in Panc-1 and MiaPaCa-2 cells (Figures 1B, C).\n...\nTo sun up, these results indicated that Huaier can suppress the proliferation of pancreatic cancer cell *in vitro* and pancreatic tumour growth *in vivo*.\n...\nWe found that Huaier markedly suppressed tumour growth, and a robust decrease in tumour weight was observed in the Huaier group compared with the control group, but this phenomenon was reversed by Fer-1 (Figure 3A).\n...\nIn brief, this evidence confirmed that Huaier inhibited tumour growth *in vivo* by inducing ferroptosis.", "date": "2022-09-30", "last_updated": "2025-05-20", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.7733}, "jev_score": 0.6264, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40127723/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# Quercetin liposomes conjugated with hyaluronidase: An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer\n...\nPancreatic cancer characterized with intense hydraulic tissue in tumor extracellular matrix (ECM) resists most of chemotherapeutic drugs.\nIncreased levels of hyaluronic acid (HA) represent the primary component of the hydraulic tissue, rendering tumors protective from drug targeting.\nQuercetin (Que), a natural flavonoid, has the ability to inhibit tumor cell growth in a number of cancers; however, its poor water solubility and low bioavailability largely limit its application in cancer therapy.\nHence, we developed an efficient drug delivery system by encapsulation of Que. into liposomes and conjugation with hyaluronidase (HAase) at liposome surface, termed as HQL.\nIn the presence of HAase, HQL were predominantly accumulated at tumor with enhanced permeability and retention effect.\nTreatment of xenografted tumor mice with HQL gave rise to suppressed tumor growth, while no toxic effects were observed in mice.\nHQL demonstrated the strong ability to inhibit cell proliferation, promote cell apoptosis, and induce arrest at G2/M cell cycle in pancreatic cancer lines, three-dimensional cultured cell spheroids and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)-derived organoids.\nMechanistically, HQL downregulated expression of cell cycle-associated protein (CCNB1, CDK1 and PLK1) and cell apoptosis-associated factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, HQL degraded HA in the tumor microenvironment to enhance nano-particle penetration and inhibited tumor cell growth, eliciting efficacy of anti-tumor therapy.\nThereof, HQL may provide a novel efficient drug delivery approach for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-06-10", "last_updated": "2025-09-17", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.78, "evidence": 0.73}, "jev_score": 0.5694, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Molecular Mechanism of Autophagy and Its Regulation by Cannabinoids in Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/13/6/1211", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Evidence has revealed the efficacy of CQ and HCQ in hindering cancer cell proliferation through autophagy inhibition in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and bladder cancers [109].\nHQL is not effective as a monotherapy but shows improvement when used in combination with gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel.\nParallel inhibition of ERK synergistically enhances HQL inhibition.\nInhibiting ERK impairs metabolic processes such as glycolysis and mitochondrial function, which leads to a dependence on autophagy for the tumour to progress.\nPairing that with HQL inhibition of autophagy leads to an enhanced efficacy of treatment [110].\n...\nIn addition, inhibition of GPR55 receptor by CBD decreased cancer cell proliferation and augmented gemcitabine efficacy in pancreatic cancer through MAPK signalling downregulation.", "date": "2021-03-10", "last_updated": "2026-08-13", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.8, "evidence": 0.6867}, "jev_score": 0.5494, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Buy 1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone", "url": "https://www.smolecule.com/products/s7484455", "domain": "smolecule.com", "snippet": "In Stock\n* This item is exclusively intended for research purposes and is not designed for human therapeutic applications or veterinary use.\n...\n1-(8-hydroxy-4-methyl-3,4-dihydro-2H-quinolin-1-yl)-2-methylsulfonylethanone, also known as HQL-79, is a small molecule that belongs to the family of quinoline-based inhibitors of the enzyme poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase-1 (PARP-1).\nPARP-1 is an important enzyme involved in the DNA-repair mechanisms of cells, and its inhibition has become a promising strategy for the treatment of cancer, inflammatory diseases, and other disorders involving DNA damage and oxidative stress.\nHQL-79 is a potent and selective PARP-1 inhibitor that has been extensively studied in vitro and in vivo for its pharmacological properties.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to exhibit potent PARP-1 inhibitory activity, with an IC50 value of 9 nM in a biochemical assay.\nIt also has selectivity over other PARP isoforms, such as PARP-2 and tankyrase-1, as well as other DNA-repair enzymes, such as DNA-dependent protein kinase (DNA-PK) and ataxia telangiectasia mutated (ATM).\nHQL-79 has been tested in various cellular and animal models of DNA damage and oxidative stress, and has been shown to enhance the cytotoxicity and sensitization of cancer cells to chemotherapy and radiation therapy, as well as to reduce the inflammation and tissue damage in models of arthritis, stroke, and neurodegeneration.\n...\nHQL-79 has been shown to have low toxicity and good safety profiles in in vitro and in vivo studies.\nIt does not induce mutagenicity or genotoxicity in bacterial or mammalian cells, and does not cause significant changes in the vital organs or hematological parameters of rodents or primates.\nHowever, some studies have reported potential side effects of PARP inhibitors, including myelosuppression, nausea, vomiting, asthenia, and cardiovascular events.\nTherefore, the safety and tolerability of HQL-79 and other PARP inhibitors need to be carefully monitored and evaluated in clinical trials.\n...\nThe research on PARP inhibitors, including HQL-79, is a rapidly evolving field.\nAs of 2021, there are several PARP inhibitors approved by regulatory agencies for the treatment of various cancers, including ovarian, breast, prostate, and pancreatic cancers.\nThese inhibitors include olaparib, rucaparib, niraparib, and talazoparib.\nMany ongoing clinical trials are investigating the use of PARP inhibitors in other cancer types, such as lung, bladder, and gastric cancers, as well as in combination with other therapies, such as immune checkpoint inhibitors, chemotherapy, and radiotherapy.\n...\nThe potential implications of HQL-79 and other PARP inhibitors in various fields of research and industry include:\n- Cancer therapy: PARP inhibitors have been shown to enhance the sensitivity of cancer cells to DNA damage and induce synthetic lethality in cancer cells with genetic defects in DNA repair pathways.\nTherefore, they have the potential to be used as monotherapy or combination therapy in cancer patients with different molecular and histological backgrounds.\n...\nIn conclusion, HQL-79 is a potent and selective PARP-1 inhibitor that has been extensively studied for its pharmacological properties and therapeutic potential in cancer, inflammation, and other diseases involving DNA damage and oxidative stress.", "date": "2024-01-05", "last_updated": "2025-06-11", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.56}, "jev_score": 0.5488, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine as anti-cancer agents", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5718030/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "* A similar observation can be made when comparing CQ efficacy in pancreatic cancer mouse models and a lung cancer mouse model: CQ significantly slowed down tumour growth and increased survival in the first, but not in the latter [27].\n...\nNext, CQ and HCQ were tested via *in vitro* assays, in which they were found to suppress pancreatic cancer cell proliferation [82, 83].\n...\nFurthermore, a study in a pancreatic cancer patient-derived xenograft model showed that CQ specifically targets highly aggressive cancer stem cells through inhibition of their self-renewal process.", "date": "2017-11-23", "last_updated": "2026-08-15", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.64}, "jev_score": 0.4928, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "www.creative-biolabs.com › blog › adcHyaluronidase-Conjugated Liposomes May Effectively Target ...", "url": "https://www.creative-biolabs.com/blog/adc/hyaluronidase-conjugated-liposomes-may-effectively-target-pancreatic-cancer/", "domain": "creative-biolabs.com", "snippet": "Recently, a study published in *J Control Release* entitled “Quercetin liposomes conjugated with hyaluronidase: An efficient drug delivery system to block pancreatic cancer” has brought new hope for pancreatic cancer treatment.\nThe research team developed an efficient drug delivery system: quercetin was encapsulated into liposomes, and hyaluronidase (HAase) was conjugated to the surface of the liposomes.\nThis novel liposome was named HQL.\n...\nIn vitro experiments showed that HQL could stably exist in 10% fetal bovine serum and plasma for 15 days.\nIts release rate was approximately 30% in an acidic environment (pH = 5.5) and about 20% in a neutral environment (pH = 7.4) within 4 hours, with sustained release for over 50 hours.\n...\nAdditionally, HAase in HQL remained highly active, laying the foundation for degrading HA in the tumor microenvironment.\nIn vitro cell experiments demonstrated that HQL had a significant proliferation-inhibiting effect on pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1 and Miapaca-2), with an IC50 value lower than that of quercetin and other controls.\nIt had no obvious inhibitory effect on the proliferation of normal pancreatic ductal epithelial cells (HPNE), showing good tumor cell specificity.\nHQL had stronger penetration ability in HA-rich agarose gels.\nIn 2D and 3D cell culture models, it was more effectively taken up by pancreatic cancer cells, promoted cancer cell apoptosis, and induced cell cycle arrest at the G2/M phase.\nIn experiments with 3D-cultured PANC-1 cells and PDAC organoids, HQL also showed superior ability to inhibit cell proliferation and promote apoptosis compared to other control groups.\nIn in vivo experiments, the researchers established a pancreatic cancer mouse model.\nResults showed that the tumor volume and weight in the HQL treatment group were significantly lower than those in the control group and other treatment groups, reducing tumor volume by 73% and decreasing HA levels in tumor tissues by 95%.\nFluorescent labeling experiments revealed that HQL mainly accumulated in tumor tissues, with less distribution in organs such as the liver.\nThe drug concentration of HQL in the blood could still be maintained at an effective level (50 μg/mL) within 48 hours, while the concentration of orally administered quercetin dropped to below 0.2 μg/mL after 48 hours.\nFurthermore, the in vivo enzymatic activity of HQL peaked at 60 U/mL at 2 hours, then gradually decreased and remained at approximately 20 U/mL within 48 hours.\nSafety evaluation indicated that HQL had no obvious impact on mouse body weight or the morphology and function of major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney), showing no potential toxic side effects.\nMechanistic studies found that HQL may exert its effects of inhibiting tumor cell growth and promoting apoptosis by downregulating the expression of cell cycle-related proteins (CCNB1, CDK1, and PLK1) as well as apoptosis-related factors PI3K/AKT and Bcl-2.\nIn summary, the HQL liposomal drug delivery system developed in this study can effectively degrade HA in the pancreatic cancer tumor microenvironment, enhance drug penetration and delivery efficiency, significantly inhibit pancreatic cancer growth, and has good safety.\nIt provides a new and promising drug delivery method for the adjuvant treatment of pancreatic cancer, which is expected to bring new hope to pancreatic cancer patients in the future and promote further development in the field of pancreatic cancer treatment.", "date": "2025-08-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.6367}, "jev_score": 0.4457, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Phytochemicals as Multitarget Therapeutics in Pancreatic Cancer: Mechanisms, Clinical Evidence, and Translational Challenges", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ptr.70430", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Curcumin, a Jab1 inhibitor, reduces tumor growth.\n...\nQuercetin has shown potential to inhibit tumor cell growth, but its effectiveness is limited by low bioavailability and poor solubility.\nHQL's ability to degrade HA in the tumor microenvironment facilitated NP penetration and contributed to its antitumor efficacy (Sun et al. 2025).\nQuercetin-3-*O*-glucoside may enhance gemcitabine's chemotherapeutic effects and inhibit metastasis, determined by various growth factors in PC (Lee et al. 2016).\n...\nGenistein inhibits tumor growth in PC.", "date": "2026-08-16", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.53, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.3303, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Structural and functional characterization of HQL-79, an orally ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16547010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HQL-79 selectively inhibited PGD2 production by H-PGDS-expressing human megakaryocytes and rat mastocytoma cells with an IC50 value of about 100 microm but only marginally affected the production of other prostanoids, suggesting the tight functional engagement between H-PGDS and cyclooxygenase.", "date": "2006-06-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-09", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.89, "evidence": 0.36}, "jev_score": 0.3204, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Stromal Pro-Tumoral Hyaluronan-CD44 Pathway in Pancreatic Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1422-0067/22/8/3953", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "The independent inhibition of hyaluronan-producing cells, hyaluronan synthesis, and/or CD44 expression, has been found to decrease the tumor cell’s proliferation, motility, invasion, and metastatic abilities.", "date": "2021-04-12", "last_updated": "2026-08-11", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.2, "evidence": 0.38}, "jev_score": 0.076, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "4-(Benzhydryloxy)-1-[3-(1H-tetraazol-5-YL)propyl]piperidine", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/hql-79", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "- DTXSID50424898\n- DTXCID70375732\n...\n377.5 g/mol", "date": "2025-07-12", "last_updated": "2025-07-14", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.0233}, "jev_score": 0.0219, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 1946.8, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:25+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q027-3", "row_id": "q027", "mode": "queries", "objective": "HQL-79 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "HQL-79 tumour regression animal model", "anchors": ["HQL-79"], "search_latency_ms": 2982.9, "target": "HQL-79", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 345.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8234, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000346, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 3, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.7566, "results": [{"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 7.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig7/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HPGDS inhibition overcomes immunotherapy resistance and is effective in human settings.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and representative images () of ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ genetically engineered mice (GEM) treated with vehicle, HQL-79 15 mg/kg twice daily, αPD1, or the combination of HQL-79 and αPD1 ( = 6).\n...\nHistologic quantification of CD8^+^ T cells at the tumor margin vs. core in ^*V600E*^^*−/−*^ GEM treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 6\n). and Tumor weight () and representative pictures () of KPC FC1245 PDAC-bearing mice treated with vehicle/IgG, HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 in combination with αPD1 ( = 11–16).\n...\nBlocking HPGDS induces vascular normalization and immune surveillance, overcoming ICB resistance ().", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.78}, "jev_score": 0.7566, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Activated T Cells Break Tumor Immunosuppression by Macrophage ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "When monitoring the growth of orthotopic YUMM1.7 melanomas, tumor volume, and weight were significantly lower (80% and 60%, respectively) in *Hpgds*^*ΔMø*^ mice compared with Ctrl littermates, with four of nine *Hpgds*^*ΔMø*^ mice displaying tumor rejection (Fig.\n...\nTo translate our genetic results in a clinically relevant setting, we used the HPGDS inhibitor HQL-79 (38) in YUMM 1.7 melanoma-bearing mice.\nSystemic administration of HQL-79 (15 mg/kg twice daily) recapitulated the antitumor phenotype observed in *Hpgds*^ΔMø^ mice (Fig.\n6A–C; Supplementary Fig. S9A and S9B), achieving 78% of tumor growth inhibition with one of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Supplementary Fig. S9B).\nThe impairment in tumor growth was accompanied by a shift in TAMs toward antitumoral markers (Fig.\n6D–F) and robust infiltration of CD8^+^ T cells (Supplementary Fig. S9C–S9E).\nLike macrophage-specific *Hpgds* deletion, HQL-79 strongly affected the tumor vasculature by decreasing vessel density and size and by improving pericyte coverage and perfusion (Supplementary Fig.\nS9F–S9L), together with reduced hypoxia (Supplementary Fig. S9M and S9N), cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream (Fig. 6G), and lung colonization (Fig. 6H).\n...\nHQL-79 was also tested in orthotopic *NRAS*^*Q61 K*^; *Ink4a*^*-/-*^ melanomas, resulting in 80% tumor growth inhibition with two of six mice undergoing total tumor rejection (Fig. 6K–M; Supplementary Fig. S10A and S10B).\n...\nTogether, these data prove that pharmacologic inhibition of HPGDS could provide a promising therapeutic option against melanoma.\n...\nFinally, we took advantage of a spontaneous *Braf*^*V600E;*^*Pten*^*-/-*^ melanoma model (41), induced by the administration of 4-hydroxytamoxifen (4-HT) on the skin of these genetically engineered mice, and treated the mice with HQL-79, αPD1, or HQL-79 plus αPD1.\nHere, neither HPGDS inhibition by HQL-79 nor αPD1 alone affected tumor progression; however, their combination was greatly effective (Fig. 7A–C; Supplementary Fig. S11A–S11D).", "date": "2025-04-07", "last_updated": "2025-09-23", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.94, "evidence": 0.66}, "jev_score": 0.6204, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Figure 6.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223510/figure/fig6/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Pharmacologic HPGDS inhibition phenocopies the effect of its genetic deletion in TAMs.\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79, 15 mg/kg BID or with vehicle control ( = 6).\n...\nFACS analysis of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ cancer cell intravasation into the bloodstream in melanoma-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with HQL-79 or with vehicle control ( = 6).\n...\nmelanoma-bearing mice treated with vehicle or HQL-79 ( = 5–6). and Tumor growth () and tumor weight () of YUMM 1.7 CD90.1^+^ melanoma-bearing Ctrl or ^*ΔMø-ERT2*^ mice treated with vehicle or HQL-79 ( = 4).\n...\nTumor growth (), tumor weight (), and tumor representative pictures () of ^*Q61 K*^; ^*−/−*^ melanoma tumor-bearing mice treated by oral gavage with vehicle or HQL-79 15 mg/kg BID ( = 6).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-08", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.6167}, "jev_score": 0.5859, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Structural and functional characterization of HQL-79, an orally ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16547010/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "HQL-79 selectively inhibited PGD2 production by H-PGDS-expressing human megakaryocytes and rat mastocytoma cells with an IC50 value of about 100 microm but only marginally affected the production of other prostanoids, suggesting the tight functional engagement between H-PGDS and cyclooxygenase.\nOrally administered HQL-79 (30 mg/kg body weight) inhibited antigen-induced production of PGD2, without affecting the production of PGE2 and PGF2alpha, and ameliorated airway inflammation in wild-type and human H-PGDS-overexpressing mice.", "date": "2006-06-02", "last_updated": "2025-09-09", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.4133}, "jev_score": 0.372, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "FIGURE 4.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12259406/figure/jcpe14180-fig-0004/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Effect oral administration of haematopoietic prostaglandin D synthase (HPGDS) inhibitor HQL‐79 on KK‐A^y^ mice with or without ligatures.\n...\n(D) Quantified alveolar bone loss of each mouse administered HQL‐79 (30 mg/kg) or vehicle ( = 6).\n...\n(K) Prostaglandin D2 (PGD2) levels in the glomeruli of C57BL/6 and KK‐A^y^ mice administered vehicle or HQL‐79 in the presence or absence of ligature ( = 6).", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-18", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.82, "evidence": 0.16}, "jev_score": 0.1312, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Safety, pharmacokinetics, and antitumor activity of the anti ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0923753422000035", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-26", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.57, "evidence": 0.07}, "jev_score": 0.0399, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Investigating the Anticancer Promise of Synthesized Methylated N-Substituted-4-Hydroxy-2-Quinolone-3-Carboxamides: Biological and Computational Characterization", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42219823/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih