, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "1784-Neuroendocrine advanced capecitabine and temozolomide", "url": "https://www.eviq.org.au/medical-oncology/upper-gastrointestinal/neuroendocrine/1784-neuroendocrine-advanced-capecitabine-and-temo", "domain": "eviq.org.au", "snippet": "### Cycle 1 and further cycles\n|Drug|Dose|Route|Day|\n|--|--|--|--|\n|Capecitabine|750 mg/m^2^ TWICE a day * (Cap dose at 2500 mg/day)|PO|1 to 14|\n|Temozolomide|100 mg/m^2^ TWICE a day * #|PO|10 to 14|\n* Consider lower doses (capecitabine 600 mg/m^2^ BD and temozolomide 75 mg/m^2^ BD) if extensive prior chemotherapy/radiation therapy.\nDosing may require rounding to the nearest tablet/capsule strength to enable delivery of a measurable dose.\n^# ^Alternative dosing schedule 200 mg/m^2^ ONCE a day.\n- **Frequency:**\n28 days\n- **Cycles:**\nContinuous until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity\n- **Drug status:**\nCapecitabine and temozolomide are on the PBS general schedule Capecitabine is available as **150 mg (non-PBS)** and **500 mg (PBS listed)** tablets.\nTemozolomide is available as **5 mg**, **20 mg**, **100 mg**, **140 mg, 180 mg ** and **250 mg ** capsules.\n...\n|Capecitabine|750 mg/m^2^ (PO) (Cap dose at 2500 mg/day)|TWICE a day within 30 minutes after the end of a meal*|\n|Capecitabine|750 mg/m^2^ (PO) (Cap dose at 2500 mg/day)|TWICE a day within 30 minutes after the end of a meal*|\n...\n|Temozolomide|100 mg/m^2^ (PO)|TWICE a day on days 10 to 14 on an empty stomach, at least one hour before or two hours after food* #|\n|Temozolomide|100 mg/m^2^ (PO)|TWICE a day on days 10 to 14 on an empty stomach, at least one hour before or two hours after food* #|\n...\n- Neuroendocrine tumours (pancreatic or other)\n...\n|less than 50|Delay treatment until recovery and consider reducing capecitabine and temozolomide by 25% for subsequent cycles|\n...\n|Moderate|Reduce capecitabine by 25%|\n|Severe|Reduce capecitabine by 50%|\n...\n#### Capecitabine\n##### Administer capecitabine:\n- orally TWICE a day on **days 1 to 14**\n- to be swallowed whole with a glass of water; do not break, crush or chew\n- to be taken morning and night (approximately 12 hours apart) within thirty minutes after the end of a meal\n...\n**Note:** missed doses should not be replaced; if a dose is forgotten or vomited, normal dosing should be resumed at the next scheduled dose.\n...\n##### Administer temozolomide:\n- administer orally TWICE a day on **days 10 to 14**\n- to be swallowed whole with a glass of water; do not break, crush or chew\n- to be taken on an empty stomach, one hour before or two hours after food", "date": "2016-01-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.04}, "jev_score": 0.0016, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Pembrolizumab, carboplatin and paclitaxel 1 of 5", "url": "https://www.kmcc.nhs.uk/s3/assets/lun-043-pembrolizumab,-carboplatin-and-paclitaxel-v4.pdf", "domain": "kmcc.nhs.uk", "snippet": "Repeat every 21 days for 4 cycles then repeat every 21 or 42 days depending on cycle \nchoice.\nMaximum of 4 cycles of pembrolizumab, carboplatin & paclitaxel followed by, in the absence of disease progression, continued treatment with pembrolizumab monotherapy* to continue for a total treatment duration of 2 years (maximum of 35 cycles including the initial 4 induction cycles of treatment or its equivalent if 6-weekly pembrolizumab monotherapy dosing is used from cycle 5) or until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity or withdrawal of patient consent, whichever occurs first.\n*There are 2 alternative dosing schedules for pembrolizumab monotherapy, 200mg IV \nevery 3 weeks or 400mg IV every 6 weeks.\n...\nDosing delay or discontinuation may be required based on individual safety and tolerability.\n...\nCycle 5 onwards: Repeat every 21 days\n...\nRepeat every 42 days (alternative dosing schedule): \nDay \n...\nAdministration \n1 \nMetoclopramide\n...\nPO \n...\nPEMBROLIZUMAB \n400mg \nIV \n30min ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.02}, "jev_score": 0.0008, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1126.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:25+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q029-1", "row_id": "q029", "mode": "queries", "objective": "TP-317 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "anchors": ["TP-317 KPC", "TP-317"], "search_latency_ms": 2183.1, "target": "TP-317 KPC / TP-317", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 363.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 18114, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000761, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 4, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.874, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract 1135: TP317, a first-in-class resolvin E1 small molecule, potentiates the efficacy of immune checkpoint (ICI) inhibitors in ICI-resistant and ICI-sensitive tumors", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article/83/7_Supplement/1135/722183/Abstract-1135-TP317-a-first-in-class-resolvin-E1", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Methods: Subcutaneous murine models of lung (LLC), melanoma (B16F10), and pancreatic (Panc02; KPC) tumors were used to investigate TP-317 monotherapy and combinations with ICI.\n...\nIn KRAS-mutant KPC tumors (N=10/group), TP317 (7.5 µg Q6D) demonstrated significant anti-tumor activity compared to placebo (500 ± 110 vs 1314 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.001) and was comparable to anti-PD1, while TP-317 + anti-PD1 was superior to anti-PD1 alone (353 ± 82 vs 710 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.01).\nIn Panc02 and KPC models, TP317’s anti-tumor efficacy was attenuated by CD8+ T cell or NK cell depletion.\n...\nConclusions: TP317 monotherapy and ICI combinations significantly inhibited tumor growth in various murine models of cancer.\n...\nIn summary, TP317, an RvE1 drug with a high therapeutic index, has potent single agent efficacy and offers a novel approach in combination with ICI to treat ICI-resistant and ICI-sensitive tumors.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-17", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.92}, "jev_score": 0.874, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Award | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/203006", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak\nPanigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal\nmodels as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is\nto complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317\ninto clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\n...\nPhase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP-\n317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft\nmodel.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or\ncombination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with\nresponse/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.7267}, "jev_score": 0.6904, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Firm | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/portfolio/406944", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak Panigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal models as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is to complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317 into clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\nThis goal will be met through the following aims: Phase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP- 317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft model.\nIn addition, we will evaluate the effect of TP-317 on multiple biomarkers (e.g., inflammatory cytokines, immune cell phenotyping via flow cytometry, and RNA sequencing).\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or combination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with response/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.6816, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract 6578: N17465, a systemically deliverable elastase, attenuates tumorigenesis and stimulates anti-tumor immunity", "url": "https://bohrium.dp.tech/paper/arxiv/bcc520a6c74c4c9c805a6e08e177fe906b62392442ac87ef8724d296df50daf7", "domain": "bohrium.dp.tech", "snippet": "Abstract A172: TP-317, a first-in-class resolvin E1 small molecule, drives adjuvant efficacy in solid tumors by engaging innate and adaptive anti-tumor immunity in the tumor microenvironment (TME) and has neoadjuvant potential\n...\nWe recently reported TP-317 anti-tumor efficacy in murine solid tumors as monotherapy and in combination with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) and chemotherapy (Kipper, 2023, Can.\nRes, 83 (7_Supp: 1135).\nHere we elaborate on the mechanism of action (MOA) of TP-317 in the adjuvant setting and report TP-317 efficacy in a neoadjuvant regimen Methods: 125-240 mm3 established subcutaneous murine melanoma (B16F10) and pancreatic (Panc02-H7; KPC) tumors were treated with placebo, TP-317 or ICI and tumors excised at 12-14 days for bulk RNAseq or flow cytometry.\n...\nResults: RNAseq analysis showed that adjuvant TP-317 broadly engages innate (macrophage, dendritic, NK cells) and adaptive (CD8, CD4, B cells) immune pathways in the TME of PANC2-H7, KPC and B16F10 models.\n...\nIn the LLC resection model, TP-317 monotherapy reduced lung (p<0.05) and lymph node metastases (p<0.01) 19 days post-resection, but cisplatin was not effective.\n...\nTP-317 monotherapy shows metastasis control when initated prior to surgical resection and long term survival when combined with neoadjuvant anti-PD1 in the LLC model of NSCLC.", "date": "2024-04-07", "last_updated": "2025-05-13", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.6767}, "jev_score": 0.5955, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Am J Transl Res 2020;12(3):1031-1043", "url": "https://e-century.us/files/ajtr/12/3/ajtr0104826.pdf", "domain": "e-century.us", "snippet": "Abstract: Dinaciclib is a small molecule cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor with the potential to treat multiple can­ cers.\nTo better understand its cytotoxic action in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), we evaluated dinaciclib therapeutic effects in the transgenic mouse model (LSL-KrasG12D/+; LSL-Trp53R172H/+; Pdx-1-Cre mice; KPC mice).\n...\nDinaciclib \ntherapy significantly delayed tumor progression (P < 0.001) and prolonged survival (P = 0.007) in KPC mice.\nIn vitro \n...\nDinaciclib treatment inhibited proliferation and induced apoptosis in KPC tumor as as­ sessed by Ki67 and cleaved caspase 3, respectively.\n...\nThese \nfinding suggest that dinaciclib as a single agent can inhibit tumor growth and improve the overall survival in KPC \nmice.\n...\nOverall, our results revealed that dinaciclib therapy significantly improved the OS of treated KPC mice and supported clinical practice in this context.\n...\nInterestingly, dinaciclib treatment significantly delayed tumor growth compared with control group (Figure\n2B, 2C).\nIn addition, we evaluated the OS of \nKPC mice in different groups.\nTreatment with dinaciclib prolonged the OS of KPC mice from\n31 to 57 days (P < 0.01 (Median)) (Figure 2D).\n...\nSecond, in this study, no complete tumor elimination was observed in\nKPC mice bearing established highly invasive tumors, underlining that dinaciclib combination therapies may be important for achieving dura­ ble tumor regression in PDAC.\n...\nIn conclusion, our study demonstrated that dinaciclib as a single agent can inhibit tumor growth and improve the OS in KPC mice.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-06-08", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.5, "evidence": 0.65}, "jev_score": 0.325, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "߄w����I*�|b�r˕٘�}>�g��_��\":^)Љ��P�}�꼖���(e��㡱Y;�~�]���E�����/�IbU�̓����-Ʊ�{�{t{LA=�Tm)P,�*u����Q:ʾ�,����F�Ѕ��g�cA]�L����A�����LA��´�~�!C;��������X���֟K�|��Xz*�Rp�h�Z-�6�n�*�������.��G9A+��ƴϵXLMfs�^���˝ِ�j�����g?~ܳn�����v.U�-,��ڸ�ٯ_�cw���eo���S�v��k?Ys��'v*�0J�{ �������}����] �Iz�OKU5�������q�\\xy�Z]z#�;Gӧ�-/x�C�)y�&.w��f#�� Z�{�d!� ,�1��Ł�Nt��zm͜+q+����,��Հ�uO��Ž�h�����5��TPGY|���&ws(��\"�����߬���_l`��������S�ߕ�����`wmP����u�ݮ����̣����Ss��&������`�7\\�%�u��1�)U�c�� �V�7J%R����mۦ�S����h>]_(-{����s�yܛ�r$Ŭ��������>v�v�G���.F��@G'�t��1)�����r��3�+�ߝ�˼����K$�ŏ�h3��+)����\\]g��%������j̦Q28>���K��#%��j�Sm|�i:��l1,+�>�1��T���k�&ZV�Oe*��<��6�3���] ��������ǎ���}h�^�N�P_f ?@X�jG܃��ɺ�.�|�7K�K��)���