ignations and Approvals", "url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/", "domain": "accessdata.fda.gov", "snippet": "Searches may be run by entering the product name, orphan designation, and dates.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1030.3, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:28+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q033-3", "row_id": "q033", "mode": "queries", "objective": "DXP-007 sponsor development stage indication public source", "query": "DXP-007 inflammatory bowel disease", "anchors": ["DXP-007"], "search_latency_ms": 4063.4, "target": "DXP-007", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 374.4, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7995, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000336, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.2851, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Introduction", "url": "https://www.spandidos-publications.com/2726/viewXml", "domain": "spandidos-publications.com", "snippet": "The aim of the present study was to elucidate the therapeutic potential of dexpanthenol (Dxp) on the amelioration of colitis in rats.\n...\nAA treatment led to an increase in oxidative parameters and a decrease in antioxidant systems.\nHistopathological examination showed that AA treatment caused tissue injury and increased caspase-3 activity in the distal colon and triggered apoptosis.\nDxp treatment caused biochemical and histopathological improvements, indicating that Dxp may have an anti-oxidant effect in colitis; therefore, Dxp may be a potential therapeutic agent for the amelioration of IBD.\n...\nDexpanthenol (Dxp) is a biologically active alcohol-analog of pantothenic acid (PA) that is converted into PA inside the cell (\n8\n...\nDxp was administered as a single dose of 500 mg/kg for three days, under mild anesthesia (as described above).\n...\nThe present study indicated that when Dxp was IP administered to rats with AA-induced colitis can reduce the extent of colonic mucosal damage, abate the increase in MDA, TOS and OSI levels, and restore diminished antioxidant enzymes and substances such as SOD, CAT, GPX, GSH and TAC.\n...\nIn conclusion, the present study employed biochemical and histopathological analysis to demonstrate that oxidative stress and apoptosis are elevated in IBD.\nThis, in turn, showed that oxidative stress and apoptosis may have a major role in IBD pathogenesis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-23", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.47, "evidence": 0.6067}, "jev_score": 0.2851, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Molecular fingerprints of neutrophil-dependent oxidative ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17940831/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# Molecular fingerprints of neutrophil-dependent oxidative stress in inflammatory bowel disease\nJ Gastroenterol.\n2007 Oct;42(10):787-98.\ndoi: 10.1007/s00535-007-2096-y.\nEpub 2007 Oct 15.\n...\nNeutrophil accumulation within epithelial crypts and in the intestinal mucosa directly correlates with clinical disease activity and epithelial injury in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\n...\nEspecially, activated neutrophils produce reactive oxygen and nitrogen species and myeloperoxidase within intestinal mucosa, which induce oxidative stress.\nPosttranslational modification of proteins generated by these reactive species serves as a \"molecular fingerprint\" of protein modification by lipid peroxidation-, nitric oxide-, and myeloperoxidase-derived oxidants.\nMeasurement of these modified proteins may serve both as a quantitative index of oxidative stress and an important new biological marker of clinical relevance to IBD.\nWe have succeeded in the clinical development of a novel granulocyte adsorptive apheresis therapy for IBD.\nIn this review, we discuss current advances in defining the role of neutrophil-dependent oxidative stress in IBD.", "date": "2007-10-03", "last_updated": "2026-03-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.18, "evidence": 0.4467}, "jev_score": 0.0804, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "This review aims to provide physicians an update of emerging therapies in IBD by focusing on drugs currently in phase 3 clinical trials.", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.17, "evidence": 0.3133}, "jev_score": 0.0533, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Diseases", "url": "https://www.clinpgx.org/disease/PA446116/literature", "domain": "clinpgx.org", "snippet": "Implications of Tioguanine Dosing in IBD Patients with a TPMT Deficiency.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.34}, "jev_score": 0.0306, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Selective Nanoparticulate Systems for Drug Delivery in Inflammatory ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12844886/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Inflammatory bowel disease is a result of inappropriate continuous non-specific inflammation in the intestinal tract, which in turn is aggravated by defects in the activation of the mucosal immune system and in the barrier function of the intestinal epithelium.\nThe most prominent manifestations of IBD are ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD).\n...\nThe pathogenesis of IBD is poorly understood, but genetic and environmental factors seem to contribute to the loss of intestinal immune homeostasis, leading to chronic inflammation [5].\nCurrently, there is no cure for IBD, and the treatment relies mainly on anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive agents with local or systemic action.", "date": "2025-12-31", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.0294, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Small Molecule Drugs in Inflammatory Bowel Diseases - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8308576/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Another more advanced candidate, PF-00547659 (ontamalimab), also prevents the link between MAdCAM1 and the α4β7 integrin [106].\n...\nFinally, BT-11 is an orally administered, gut-restricted, and first-in-class small molecule that targets the lanthionine synthetase C-like 2 (LANCL2) pathway and immunometabolic mechanisms.", "date": "2021-06-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.2367}, "jev_score": 0.0213, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://www.malacards.org/card/inflammatory_bowel_disease", "domain": "malacards.org", "snippet": "Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) encompasses a group of inflammatory conditions affecting the colon and small intestine.\nThe main types are Crohn's disease, which impacts various parts of the digestive tract, and ulcerative colitis, which primarily affects the colon and rectum.\nBoth conditions involve chronic inflammation and an abnormal immune response within the gastrointestinal tract.\n- Inflammatory Bowel Diseases\n- Other Specified Inflammatory Bowel Diseases\n...\nInflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) is a broad term that describes conditions characterized by chronic inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract.\nThe two most common inflammatory bowel diseases are ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.", "date": "2025-02-24", "last_updated": "2025-07-01", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.3567}, "jev_score": 0.0178, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "new drugs for inflammatory bowel disease treatment ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38980426/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), encompassing Crohn's disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC), continues to challenge treatment paradigms.\nAdvancements in therapeutic options have been have been driven by Phase 2 and 3 clinical trials of new drug classes, particularly sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) modulators and interleukin-23 (IL-23) inhibitors.\n...\n** This review synthesizes findings from Phase 2 and 3 clinical trials conducted up to early 2024, focusing on the impact of S1P modulators and IL-23 inhibitors on IBD management.\nDrugs such as ozanimod, etrasimod, risankizumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, and brasikumab were evaluated for their efficacy and safety profiles.\n...\n** S1P modulators, such as ozanimod and etrasimod, effectively regulate immune cell trafficking to reduce inflammation and several trials highlight their clinical effectiveness in both inducing and maintaining remission in IBD, highlighting its long-term safety and sustained therapeutic effects.\nAdditionally, IL-23 inhibitors including risankizumab, mirikizumab, and guselkumab, which disrupt key inflammatory cytokine pathways, have already shown significant effectiveness in inducing and maintaining remission in both CD and UC, with favorable safety profiles across multiple studies, suggesting their potential as critical components in managing IBD.\n...\n** The clinical trials indicate that both S1P modulators and IL-23 inhibitors offer promising therapeutic benefits and maintain strong safety profiles, positioning them as potential cornerstone treatments for IBD.\nDespite these advancements, further exploration into long-term safety and the development of personalized treatment strategies is essential for maximizing clinical outcomes.", "date": "2024-07-09", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.3}, "jev_score": 0.015, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel Therapies for Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11938-022-00371-4", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "Emerging therapeutic agents currently in phase III trials including IL-23 inhibitors (risankizumab, guselkumab, and mirikizumab), JAK inhibitors (filgotinib and upadacitinib), and S1P1 modulators (ozanimod and etrasimod) are effective in the treatment of patients with moderate to severe CD and UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-09-19", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.2867}, "jev_score": 0.0143, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Review article: novel oral‐targeted therapies in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/apt.14669", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "In randomised controlled trials primary efficacy endpoints were met for tofacitinib (jak 1/3 inhibitor-phase iii), upadacitinib (jak 1 inhibitor-phase ii) and ajm300 (α4-integrin antagonist-phase ii) in ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-22", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.05, "evidence": 0.1567}, "jev_score": 0.0078, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 4437.8, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:28+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q035-2", "row_id": "q035", "mode": "queries", "objective": "BS-HH-002 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "query": "BS-HH-002 market launch AML", "anchors": ["BS-HH-002"], "search_latency_ms": 1217.1, "target": "BS-HH-002", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 341.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7868, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00033, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9408, "results": [{"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Ozmosi | BS HH 002.SA Drug Profile", "url": "https://pryzm.ozmosi.com/product/29037", "domain": "pryzm.ozmosi.com", "snippet": "BS-HH-002 is a newly developed drug with excellent antitumor activity, which resulted from the modification and optimization of the side structure of the homoharringtonine (HHT).\nIt is particularly efficient in treatment for acute myeloid leukemia and myelodysplastic syndromes.\n...\nRoute of Administration: Subcutaneous\nFDA Designation: *\nApproval Status: Not Approved\nApproved Countries: None\nApproved Indications: None\n...\n|BS HH 002.SA|P1|Unknown status|Myelodysplastic Syndrome|Preleukemia|Acute Myeloid Leukemia|2025-02-01|8%|2025-02-04|Primary Endpoints|", "date": "2025-02-04", "last_updated": "2025-07-30", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.96}, "jev_score": 0.9408, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapeutic effects of an innovative BS-HH-002 drug on ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8633684/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "BS-HH-002 is a newly developed drug with excellent antitumor activity, which resulted from the modification and optimization of the side structure of the homoharringtonine (HHT).\nIt is particularly efficient in treatment for acute myeloid leukemia and myelodysplastic syndromes.\n...\nRecent clinical studies demonstrated its efficacy for r