 Hong Kong Ltd.\n11 Jun 2024", "date": "2025-05-08", "last_updated": "2026-02-07", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.87, "evidence": 0.7767}, "jev_score": 0.6757, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Insilico Medicine nominates novel CDK12/13 small ...", "url": "https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/1004993", "domain": "eurekalert.org", "snippet": "Insilico Medicine (“Insilico”), a generative artificial intelligence (AI)-driven clinical-stage drug discovery company, today announced the nomination of ISM9274, a highly selective covalent dual-inhibitor of CDK12/13 as a preclinical candidate (PCC) for cancer treatment.\n...\nAfter determining the target, Insilico researchers designed a series of compounds for CDK12 using  the company’s generative AI small molecule engine Chemistry42, and nominated ISM9274 as the preclinical candidate at the end of August 2023.\n...\nISM9274 showed robust in vivo efficacy in multiple xenograft models for both monotherapy and combo-therapy.\n...\nISM9274 is now being progressed to IND-enabling studies and we plan to submit an IND application in the second half of 2024.”", "date": "2023-10-17", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.88, "evidence": 0.7367}, "jev_score": 0.6483, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "インシリコ・メディシン、生成AI技術を活用し腫瘍治療のための新規CDK12/13デュアル阻害剤を発表", "url": "https://sciencesources.eurekalert.org/news-releases/1074881?language=japanese", "domain": "sciencesources.eurekalert.org", "snippet": "臨床段階の生成AI駆動バイオテクノロジー企業であるインシリコ・メディシン（以下「インシリコ」）は本日、経口投与可能な新規共有結合型CDK12/13デュアル阻害剤のシリーズを発表しました。このシリーズは、難治性および治療抵抗性癌に対する潜在的な治療オプションとなる可能性があります。この研究は、\n...\n前臨床試験における_in vitro_および_in vivo_のテストでは、化合物12bが癌細胞株においてナノモルレベルの活性を示し、望ましいADME（吸収、分布、代謝、排泄）特性を有することが確認されました。\nまた、乳癌および急性骨髄性白血病（AML）モデルにおいて有意な有効性を示し、耐え難い副作用を誘発することなく、さらなる治療開発への道を開きました。\n...\nこのプログラムの化合物を近い将来、臨床試験段階へと進めることを目指しています。」", "date": "2025-02-26", "last_updated": "2025-05-22", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.7067}, "jev_score": 0.636, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "CDK12/13", "url": "https://insilico.com/pipeline_target_cdk12/13", "domain": "insilico.com", "snippet": "CDK12\nCyclin-dependent kinase 12/13 (CDK12/13) Inhibitor:\nTreating Tumors through Induction of BRCAness\n(IND-Enabling)\nAssays Completed\nWholly-owned and Available for Licensing\n- *In vivo* efficacy studies – Combo study with Olaparib and Cisplatin\n- *In vivo* efficacy studies – Single agent\n- *In vivo* PK-PD\n- *In vivo* PK studies\n- *In vitro* ADMET studies\n- Developability/CMC\n- *In vitro* cell-based\n- Toxicology studies\n- Enzymatic\n...\nInhibition or loss of CDK12/CDK13 provokes a ''BRCAness'' phenotype that results in deficiencies in DNA damage repair, promoting synergy with DNA-damaging chemotherapy and PARP inhibitors.\n...\nInsilico Medicine CDK12 Inhibitor Summary – IND-Enabling\n...\nEffective in both mono and combo therapies\n...\n- Potential broader indications such as AML, HCC, SCLC, Colorectal cancer, and Ovarian cancer\n...\nInsilico Medicine developed an AI-designed covalent inhibitor targeting CDK12.\n*In vivo* efficacy studies in SUM149PT CDX mice models showed strong dose-dependent anti-tumor activity with robust and durable tumor regression.\nThe data also indicated that the inhibitor could induce BRCAness in the tumor cells.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-22", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.9, "evidence": 0.6567}, "jev_score": 0.591, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Novel CDK12/13 dual inhibitors for tumour treatment - ecancer", "url": "https://ecancer.org/en/news/26083-novel-cdk12-13-dual-inhibitors-for-tumour-treatment", "domain": "ecancer.org", "snippet": "27 Feb 2025\nInsilico Medicine (\"Insilico\"), a clinical stage generative artificial intelligence (AI)-driven biotechnology company, announced the publication of a novel series of orally available covalent CDK12/13 dual inhibitors, as a potential option against refractory and treatment-resistant cancers.\nPublished in the *Journal of Medicinal Chemistry* (IF=7.2), the study showcases the discovery of compound 12b, a potent, selective, and safe therapy targeting CDK12/13, empowered by Insilico’s proprietary generative AI platforms including PandaOmics and Chemistry42.\n...\nFurthermore, 12b exhibited significant efficacy in breast cancer and acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) models, all without inducing intolerable side effects, paving the way for further therapeutic development.\n...\n\"The research and development process showcases how AI-guided drug design can achieve both precision and safety in cancer therapeutics, and we are striving to advance the compound in this programme into clinical trials in the near future.\"", "date": "2025-02-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.84, "evidence": 0.7}, "jev_score": 0.588, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Press Releases", "url": "https://insilico.com/news", "domain": "insilico.com", "snippet": "20 October 2023\nInsilico Medicine nominates novel CDK12/13 small molecule inhibitor as preclinical candidate for multiple tumors", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.86, "evidence": 0.6233}, "jev_score": 0.536, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Design, Synthesis, and Biological Evaluation of Novel Orally Available Covalent CDK12/13 Dual Inhibitors for the Treatment of Tumors", "url": "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/acs.jmedchem.4c01616", "domain": "pubs.acs.org", "snippet": "Here, we report the discovery of compound\n**12b** as a novel, potent, orally available covalent CDK12/13 dual inhibitor with a promising safety profile and robust *in vivo* antitumor properties.", "date": "2025-02-14", "last_updated": "2025-04-10", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.75, "evidence": 0.5467}, "jev_score": 0.41, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pipeline | Insilico Medicine", "url": "https://insilico.com/pipeline", "domain": "insilico.com", "snippet": "CDK12\nCyclin-dependent kinase 12 (CDK12) Inhibitor: Treating Tumors by Induction of BRCAness\n...\nFGFR2/3\nFGFR2/3 Dual Inhibitor: Treatment of Solid Tumors", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.77, "evidence": 0.2867}, "jev_score": 0.2208, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Insilico Medicine: Main", "url": "https://insilico.com/", "domain": "insilico.com", "snippet": "CDK12\nCyclin-dependent kinase 12 (CDK12) Inhibitor: Treating Tumors by Induction of BRCAness\nFGFR2/3\nFGFR2/3 Dual Inhibitor: Treatment of Solid Tumors", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.7, "evidence": 0.25}, "jev_score": 0.175, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}], "total_ms": 5267.5, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:35+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q041-3", "row_id": "q041", "mode": "queries", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication DB-3Q, Direct Biologics LLC, Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease", "query": "DB-3Q MSC-derived EV medically refractory Crohn's", "anchors": ["DB-3Q"], "search_latency_ms": 539.1, "target": "drugs competing with DB-3Q: other agents in the same mechanistic class or for the same indication, as described in `objective`", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 387.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9574, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000402, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.2769, "results": [{"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 ...", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/news/columbia-enrolls-nations-first-patient-direct-biologics-phase-2-clinical-trial-fistulizing", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "snippet": "Columbia University Irving Medical Center has enrolled the first patient in a multicenter Phase 2 clinical trial investigating the effectiveness of Direct Biologics' proprietary extracellular vesicle technology (DB-3Q, extracellular vesicles (EVs) secreted from bone marrow-derived mesenchymal stem cells) for the treatment of perianal fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n\"Crohn's-related fistulas are very difficult to treat; patients often require multiple surgeries, and the recurrence rates are high with a substantial risk of incontinence\" says Marco Bertucci Zoccali, MD, colorectal surgeon and global PI for the study.\n\"This study aims to determine if this approach is a viable option to treat anal fistula in patients with Crohn's disease specifically.\"\n...\nAnal fistulas can be treated with surgical procedures – often complex and painful - but recurrence rates are high, especially in people with Crohn's disease.\nEVs may offer a game-changing avenue for treating autoimmune diseases, with their ability to repair damaged tissues thanks to their anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties.\n\"The advantages of EVs over traditional stem cell therapies are multiple”, notes Dr. Zoccali.\n\"While derived from stem cells, it is a much more stable product, hence easier to store and administer.\nIt's also less immunogenic, which can mitigate some of the problems associated with stem cell treatment due to the body's response to foreign DNA.\"\nThis phase 2 multicenter randomized study will have multiple cohorts with three different doses and administration routes.\nThe first uses an initial dose administered in the operating room via direct injection near the fistula.\nThe second phase will double that dose.\nThe third phase will include local injections and add an IV infusion of the medication.\n\"These patients are already optimized on their medical therapy for Crohn's\" says Dr. Zoccali.\n\"This treatment is for treating the associated fistulas, which has an enormous impact on a patient's quality of life and can cause serious complications.\nWe are very optimistic that this trial will determine the appropriate dosage to help patients with this novel modality.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.71, "evidence": 0.39}, "jev_score": 0.2769, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease", "url": "https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT06918808", "domain": "ichgcp.net", "snippet": "# DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\n...\n## A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n...\nRecruiting\n...\n#### Enrollment (Estimated)\n36\n#### Phase\n- Phase 2\n...\nNew York\nNew York, New York, United States, 10032\n...\n- Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NYPH\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n- Written informed consent from participant\n- Men and women 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn's Disease for at least six months duration prior to Day 1\n- Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively draining\n- Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year including, but not limited to, antibiotics, immunomodulators (6-MP, methotrexate, azathioprine), monoclonal antibodies (adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab, guselkumab, infliximab, risankizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab), or small molecule inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib)\n- Previous failed surgical intervention, including seton placement at least two weeks prior to screening, or are not candidates for surgical intervention or are not willing to undergo surgical intervention for the management of their fistula\n- Medical therapy for CD stable for at least 2 months prior to Day 1 (Changes in dosing or dosing intervals related to serum drug levels are not permitted)\n...\n- Active perianal abscess or infection at screening or Day 1\n...\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 ml IV and DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 ml IV and Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n...\n|## Outcome Measure|## Measure Description|## Time Frame|\n|--|--|--|\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 24 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|24 Weeks|\n...\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 12 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|12 Weeks|\n...\n#### Study Start (Actual)\nMay 19, 2025\n#### Primary Completion (Estimated)\nApril 30, 2026\n#### Study Completion (Estimated)\nOctober 31, 2026", "date": "2025-06-13", "last_updated": "2025-07-24", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.52, "evidence": 0.51}, "jev_score": 0.2652, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics | MTEC", "url": "https://mtec-sc.org/life-sciences/direct-biologics", "domain": "mtec-sc.org", "snippet": "Developer of investigational secretome-based biologics derived from adult bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (MSC).\n...\n### DB-3Q — Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate being evaluated in clinical trials for perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease (Phase II noted).\n### DB-3Q — Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate under clinical evaluation for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\n...\nAn investigational secretome-based product being evaluated in clinical trials for Perianal Fistulizing Crohn’s Disease and Medically Refractory Crohn’s Disease.", "date": "2025-02-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-26", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.6, "evidence": 0.35}, "jev_score": 0.21, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics, LLC - MedPath Trial", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/organization/direct-biologics-llc", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "### DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nPhase 2\nRecruiting\ninterventional\nJun 202636 participants\n### DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nPhase 2\nRecruiting\ninterventional\nMay 202536 participants", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.65, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.1971, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/76f5bfcac1da44b8ac9629daf1034a48", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)\n...\nDrug Type\nExosomes, Mesenchymal stem cell therapy\nSynonyms\nDB-3Q\n...\nActive Indication\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nInactive Indication\n...\nDirect Biologics LLC\n...\nNCT06918808\nRecruiting\nPhase 2\nA Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\nStart Date\n19 May 2025\nSponsor / Collaborator\nDirect Biologics LLC\n...\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nPhase 2\nUnited States\nDirect Biologics LLC\n19 May 2025\n...\nNo Data", "date": "2026-08-18", "last_updated": "2026-05-25", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.55, "evidence": 0.33}, "jev_score": 0.1815, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Patients | Direct Biologics", "url": "https://www.directbiologics.com/patients", "domain": "directbiologics.com", "snippet": "Our unique biological therapies are composed of growth factors and cytokines, which are proteins, and EVs secreted by bone marrow mesenchymal stem/stromal cells (bmMSCs).\n...\nWe're currently conducting clinical trials to evaluate the safety and efficacy of our therapies in different diseases.\n...\n### Perianal Fistulizing Crohn’s Disease\nA study of DB-3Q for patients with medically refractory perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease\n### Medically Refractory Crohn’s Disease\nA study of DB-3Q for patients with medically refractory Crohn’s disease", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-10-09", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.2967}, "jev_score": 0.1662, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07625293 | DB-3Q for the Treatment of ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07625293", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "## DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nClinicalTrials.gov ID ** NCT07625293\n...\n**Not yet recruiting** **\n...\nThis research study is studying DB-3Q as a possible treatment for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\nThe purpose of this study is to research and evaluate safety and effectiveness of the administration of bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular vesicles product, DB-3Q, the study drug for Crohn's disease.\nOfficial Title\nA Phase 2a Study of DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\n...\nIntervention/Treatment **\n- Biological: DB-3Q\n- Other: Placebo\n...\nStudy Start (Estimated) **\n2026-06\nPrimary Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-06\nStudy Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-12\nEnrollment (Estimated) **\n36\nStudy Type **\nInterventional\nPhase **\nPhase 2\n...\n**Name: ** Bill Arana\n**Phone Number: ** 15123547124\n**Email: ** barana@directbiologics.com\nThis study has 1 location\nUnited States\nMissouri Locations\n**St Louis, Missouri, United States, 63110**\nWashington University, St. Louis\nContact :\nStudy Coordinator\n314-273-0301 luluhuang@wustl.edu\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n1. Written informed consent from participant\n2. Males and females 18-75 years of age\n3. Diagnosed with Crohn's disease of at least 6 months duration with medically refractory symptoms that failed to respond or responded but recurred after one advanced immunologic therapy (must have been receiving at least one advanced immunological therapy for 14 weeks duration prior to screening, including, but not limited to, adalimumab, certolizumab, , infliximab, risankizumab, upadacitinib, ustekinumab and vedolizumab), or is intolerant, or has a contraindication to advanced immunological therapy with a next step of subtotal colectomy or escalation in medical management\n4. Active CD as defined by a CDAI score ≥ 220 and/or SES-CD score ≥ 4\n...\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Intervention/Treatment **|\n|--|--|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 15 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 30 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 45 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Placebo Comparator: Placebo IV|\n...\n|Change from baseline in SES-CD Score|Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn's Disease (SES-CD) is a standardized tool used by gastroenterologists to assess and quantify the severity of Crohn's disease during an endoscopy or colonoscopy.\nThe scale goes from 0 to greater than 15, with a lower score being better.|12 Weeks|", "date": "2026-06-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-30", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.55, "evidence": 0.2933}, "jev_score": 0.1613, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Stem cell- and extracellular vesicle-based therapies for ...", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/1007-9327/full/v31/i39/112229", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "Evidence from animal models and clinical trials indicates that MSC injection into PFCD lesions suppresses the infiltration of inflammatory cells and cytokines, resulting in complete fistula healing.\nMore recently, MSC-derived extracellular vesicles (EVs) have shown promising results in promoting fistula healing, particularly in cases of refractory or relapsing fistulas.\n...\nBoth animal models and clinical trials have shown that mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) and their extracellular vesicles (EVs) can reduce inflammation and promote healing in refractory PFCD effects that may be further enhanced by autophagy activation.\n...\nMSC treatment has emerged as a promising strategy to reinforce surgical closure of the internal opening in patients with complex Crohn’s perianal fistulas.\n...\nIn line with this, patients with medical therapy-refractory Crohn’s perianal fistulas, with a previous history of failed anti-TNF treatment and surgical closure, were treated with a single intralesional dose of a cell suspension containing 120 × 10^6^ cells (expanded adipose-derived stem cells).\n...\nResults showed more than 40% radiological remission and 70% clinical closure.\n...\nAfter a median follow-up of 92 weeks, 57.1% achieved successful fistula closure.\n...\nA systematic review and meta-analysis by Guillo *et al* [20], which included 25 studies involving 596 patients, demonstrated that MSC therapies are effective in treating refractory PFCD.\nThe remission rates were 36.2% after 3 months and 57.9% after 6 months, with MSC therapy being significantly more effective compared to placebo.\n...\nThe long-term application of MSC therapy in 24 patients with PFCD was investigated for five years.\nAfter five years of follow-up and treatment, 54.2% of patients showed clinical remission, and 25% showed sustained clinical response.\nRadiologically, 75% of patients experienced improvement within the first 12 months, with 37.5% demonstrating long-term improvement and 16.7% achieving complete healing.\nAdditionally, 58.3% of patients did not require additional surgeries, and no adverse events or long-term incontinence were reported.\nThese findings confirm MSC therapy as a promising treatment for PFCD, providing durable clinical benefits and safety over the long term[21].\n...\nThe use of MSC-derived EVs for the treatment of PFCD offers several advantages over MSCs, including lower immunogenicity, reduced risk of tumor formation, and more accessible storage and handling requirements[36,37].\nMechanistically, EVs exert their effects *via* pathways like MSCs in the treatment of PFCD, as described above.\n...\nA few clinical studies using EV therapy in PFCD human patients demonstrated healing success, with no reported recurrence.\nA phase I clinical trial demonstrated that local injection of cord MSC-derived exosomes (5 mL of a 50 μg/mL solution) into five patients with refractory PFCD resulted in high efficacy fistula healing within six months, and no reported complications based on MRI imaging[38].\nIn a phase II clinical trial, the efficacy of MSC-derived exosomes was tested in 23 patients with refractory perianal fistulas in CD.\nAfter three local injections of exosomes, 60% of patients showed complete fistula closure, and 69.7% of the treated fistula tracts closed completely.\nHistopathological analysis showed reduced inflammation and enhanced tissue regeneration[39].\n...\nMSCs and their EVs have shown promise in promoting perianal fistula healing in animal models and clinical trials.\nNotably, autophagy activation in these cells enhances their function and EV production, accelerating the healing process.", "date": "2025-10-21", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.32, "evidence": 0.4933}, "jev_score": 0.1579, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Stem Cells for the Treatment of Pouchitis", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT05578508", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "The purpose of this study is to determine the safety and feasibility of using allogeneic bone marrow derived mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) to treat people with medically refractory Pouchitis.\n...\n- **DRUG**\nMesenchymal Stem Cells (MSCs) Endoscopic injection of allogeneic bone marrow derived mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) to the ileal pouch.\n...\n- **Start**\n2022-04-30\n- **Primary Completion**\n2024-02-29\n- **Completion**\n2024-02-29\n- **FDA Drug**\nYes\n...\n- DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nNCT06918808 ·Status: RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.38, "evidence": 0.3533}, "jev_score": 0.1343, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Extracellular vesicle-based therapies in IBD - Frontiers", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2026.1943448", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "The evidence is dominated by chemically induced rodent colitis, particularly acute mucosal injury models; controlled efficacy data in patients with luminal ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease are not available, and the small uncontrolled perianal-fistula study provides only preliminary local safety and feasibility observations.\n...\nIn experimental colitis, EV-based interventions have been associated with reductions in disease activity, histological injury, myeloperoxidase activity, and pro-inflammatory mediators, together with increases in anti-inflammatory and tissue-protective responses ().\nHowever, these findings mainly provide preclinical proof-of-concept.\nThey do not establish a shared mechanism across EV sources, and clinical evidence for EV-based treatment of luminal UC or CD remains extremely limited.\n...\nUnmodified MSC-EVs are the most extensively studied mammalian EV platform in experimental IBD.\nHere, the term refers to preparations produced without deliberate donor conditioning.\n...\nMost studies use acute DSS or TNBS colitis, which models epithelial injury, innate inflammation, and early repair but not chronic, relapsing, transmural, fibrotic, fistulizing, or postoperative IBD.\nMammalian EV efficacy in these models therefore supports biological plausibility, not clinical readiness.\nClinical evidence is limited to a small uncontrolled phase I study in five patients with refractory perianal Crohn’s disease fistulas: four were responders at six months, three achieved complete healing, and no local or systemic adverse events were reported ().\nThis is a local feasibility and safety signal, not evidence for luminal UC or CD or for oral or systemic administration.\n...\nIn the small uncontrolled phase I study of locally injected MSC-derived exosomes for refractory perianal Crohn’s disease fistulas, five patients were treated; four were reported as responders at six months and three achieved complete healing, with no reported local or systemic adverse events ().\nThese observations provide a preliminary local safety and feasibility signal but do not establish efficacy and cannot be generalized to oral or systemic treatment of luminal IBD.\n...\nCurrent evidence supports preparation-specific anti-inflammatory, epithelial-protective, and barrier effects mainly in acute preclinical mucosal-colitis models.\nIt does not establish class-wide efficacy in chronic, fibrostenotic, postoperative, or human luminal IBD; the five-patient fistula study remains a local feasibility signal.", "date": "2026-09-07", "last_updated": "2026-09-10", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.21, "evidence": 0.49}, "jev_score": 0.1029, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 926.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:36+00:00"}
