{"case_id": "q056-2", "row_id": "q056", "mode": "queries", "objective": "MNPS Asset planned or expected data readouts, regulatory submissions, regulatory decisions, and expected timing in Head and Neck Cancer", "query": "MNPS Asset head and neck cancer regulatory submission", "anchors": ["MNPS"], "search_latency_ms": 1234.8, "target": "MNPS", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 358.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7253, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000305, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 2, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.5478, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "PDF | Head And Neck Cancer | Radiation Therapy - Scribd", "url": "https://www.scribd.com/document/1004838520/Disease-Analysis-Head-and-Neck-Cancer-214483", "domain": "scribd.com", "snippet": "Junshi and US partner Coherus BioSciences, Inc.\nhave resubmitted a Biologics License Application for toripalimab after receiving a\nComplete Response Letter (CRL) in May 2022 for use of the PD-1-targeting drug in combination with gemcitabine and cisplatin in first-", "date": "2026-03-09", "last_updated": "2026-03-09", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.66, "evidence": 0.83}, "jev_score": 0.5478, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Head & Neck Cancer | Clinical - CancerNetwork", "url": "https://www.cancernetwork.com/clinical/head-neck-cancer", "domain": "cancernetwork.com", "snippet": "The regulatory decision for subcutaneous amivantamab in recurrent/metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma was based on phase 1b/2 OrigAMI-4 data.", "date": "2017-09-15", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.61, "evidence": 0.8033}, "jev_score": 0.49, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Mononuclear phagocytes in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2811252/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In a deficient immunosurveillance situation, a switch may occur where cells of the MNP (mainly TAMs) change their phenotype from pro-inflammatory to pro-angiogenic, contributing to tumour progression via secretion of proteases, cytokines and angiogenic factors [34].\n...\nAfter maturation and subsequent activation MNPs achieve varied morphological forms.\nMonocytes (MOs) may, after 2–3 days in circulation at baseline conditions, move into different tissues and further differentiate into macrophages (Mφs).\nMφs associated with tumours are called tumour-associated macrophages (TAMs).\nMNPs, in general, may act as defence cells and phagocytes even in the absence of activation.\nMNPs are effective as Antigen presenting cells (APCs) and are involved in the regulation of immune responses via interaction with lymphocytes and as effector cells in adaptive immune response [51].\nThe functional capacity of MNPs depends on the activation level of the cells.\n...\nCells of the MNP system secrete many different mediators of the immune response, such as cytokines and chemokines.\n...\nStudies have been performed where MNPs have been used as an adjuvant tool in treatment of HNSCC [104, 105].", "date": "2009-12-05", "last_updated": "2025-07-31", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.26}, "jev_score": 0.0208, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in nanomaterials for the diagnosis and treatment of head ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2452199X22003474", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "Multiple studies have shown that NMs such as polymeric nanoparticles (NPs), dendrimers, metallic nanoparticles (MNPs), nanodiamonds (NDs), and core–shell NPs can be outstanding carriers for different imaging techniques, and could therefore be used to solve some of these problems.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-29", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.2567}, "jev_score": 0.0154, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "NCCN Guidelines® Insights: Head and Neck Cancers ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39938471/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The NCCN Guidelines for Head and Neck Cancers address tumors arising in the oral cavity (including mucosal lip), pharynx, larynx, and paranasal sinuses, as well as occult primary cancer, salivary gland cancer, and mucosal melanoma (MM).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.22}, "jev_score": 0.0066, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "9196json-file-1.pdf", "url": "https://www.gov.br/anvisa/pt-br/assuntos/snvs/financiamento/arquivos/9196json-file-1", "domain": "gov.br", "snippet": "Criado em 21/09/2020 00:00 9196json-file-1.pdf — 4.87 MB", "date": "2026-09-25", "last_updated": "2026-09-25", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.17, "evidence": 0.0133}, "jev_score": 0.0023, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "MPSA", "url": "https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/55388048/mpsa", "domain": "yumpu.com", "snippet": "Hooray!\nYour file is uploaded and ready to be published.", "date": "2016-04-06", "last_updated": "2025-04-06", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.2, "evidence": 0.0067}, "jev_score": 0.0013, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "NCCN - head and neck cancers v4.2024 - ARUP Consult", "url": "https://arupconsult.com/reference/nccn-head-and-neck-cancers-v42024", "domain": "arupconsult.com", "snippet": "Please do not include any patient-specific or personal health information (PHI) in your message.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-30", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.0267}, "jev_score": 0.0011, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] 2022 Update edited by the Japan Society for Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0385814623001402", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "try accessing the content again or\ncontact our support team\n...\nPlease supply the following details:", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.0067}, "jev_score": 0.0004, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Server Error", "url": "https://cdas.cancer.gov/approved-projects/5003/", "domain": "cdas.cancer.gov", "snippet": "Please contact the server administrator at webcontacts@imsweb.com to inform them of the time this error occurred, and the actions you performed just before this error.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-20", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.22, "evidence": 0.0}, "jev_score": 0.0, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1592.9, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:41+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q056-1", "row_id": "q056", "mode": "queries", "objective": "MNPS Asset planned or expected data readouts, regulatory submissions, regulatory decisions, and expected timing in Head and Neck Cancer", "query": "MNPS Asset first-line recurrent metastatic head and neck cancer data readout", "anchors": ["MNPS"], "search_latency_ms": 1757.5, "target": "MNPS", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 369.2, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9886, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000415, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 0, "abstain": true, "top_score": 0.1624, "results": [{"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "First-line therapy for recurrent metastatic head and neck ...", "url": "https://tcr.amegroups.org/article/view/103355/html", "domain": "tcr.amegroups.org", "snippet": "Current first line treatment of locoregionally advanced HNSCC includes primary surgery followed by risk-adapted radiation with or without chemotherapy or with concurrent definitive chemoradiotherapy (CRT) (2).\n...\nCompared to E, the PC arm showed significantly increased OS in the all biomarker subgroups, with a safety profile comparable to E.\nThese results led to the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approval of PC as a first line treatment in the platinum sensitive R/M HNSCC population, regardless of PD-L1 status, and P in the PD-L1 positive [combined positive score (CPS) ≥1] population.\n...\n*discusses the results of a phase IV clinical trial exploring carboplatin, paclitaxel, and pembrolizumab (CPP) as first line therapy for R/M HNSCC (6).\n...\nThe confirmed ORR was 50%, with 8% of patients achieving a complete response (CR).\nThe median PFS was 5.6 months, with an estimated 12-month PFS rate of 12%.\nThe median OS was 13.1 months, with a 12-month OS rate of 52%.\n...\nResults of KN-B10 appear promising and suggest that combination carboplatin/paclitaxel/pembrolizumab therapy provides antitumor activity similar to platinum/5-FU/pembrolizumab.", "date": "2025-07-30", "last_updated": "2025-08-30", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.29, "evidence": 0.56}, "jev_score": 0.1624, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Driving Progress in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13101959/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The KEYNOTE-048 study^8^ established the use of pembrolizumab in the first-line setting, either as monotherapy (limited to patients with PD-L1 ≥ 1 expression) or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU).\nAs a result, immunotherapy was established as the core element of standard-of-care in the first-line treatment of platinum-sensitive R/M HNSCC.\nThe updated results from this study with a median follow-up of 45 months have shown an improved median OS in the pembrolizumab group compared to chemotherapy in the CPS ≥ 20 (14.9 vs 10.8 months; HR 0.61; 95% CI 0.46–0.81) and CPS ≥ 1 populations (12.3 vs 10.4 months; HR 0.74; 95% CI 0.61–0.89)^14^.\nAlso the combination of pembrolizumab-chemotherapy group compared to chemotherapy alone demonstrated a benefit in median OS in the PD-L1 CPS ≥ 20 (14.7 vs 11.1 months; HR 0.62; 95% CI 0.46–0.84), CPS ≥ 1 (13.6 vs 10.6 months;\n...\nThe phase 4 study KEYNOTE-B10^15^ was a non-randomized, single arm study with ORR reaching 49% in the overall population.\nDespite the inherent differences between real-world populations and clinical trial cohorts, pembrolizumab-based regimens have shown comparable OS results in the real-world population to previously demonstrated in the KEYNOTE-048 trial^16,17^.\n...\nDespite biological^10^ and technical limitations, PD-L1 remains a dominant factor to guiding first-line treatment decision.\n...\nPreliminary results demonstrated an ORR of around 32% in the first-line setting for patients with PD-L1 CPS ≥1^58^.\n...\nImmune checkpoint blockade with different agents such as nivolumab, pembrolizumab or toripalimab paired with EGFR inhibitors have demonstrated overall response rates (ORR) ranging from 30% to 55% in immune-naïve populations^86,87,88,89^.\n...\nPetosentamab (MCLA-158), a bispecific antibody that targets the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) and the leucine-rich repeat containing G-protein-coupled receptor 5 (LGR5), currently holds two breakthrough therapy designations by the FDA for patients with R/M HNSCC who have experienced disease progression following treatment with platinum-based chemotherapy and a PD-L1/PD-1 inhibitor^91^ and also in the first-line setting in combination with pembrolizumab for patients with PD-L1 (CPS≥1)^92^.\nThe combination of petosentamab with pembrolizumab continues to be investigated in the front-line setting (NCT03526835).\nPreliminary results have shown ORR of 60% (50% for p16+ and 66% for p16-HNSCC).\n...\nPrevious data suggest that second-line treatment should be considered after IO progression in patients with HNSCC, even in cases with poor PS^98^.", "date": "2026-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.32, "evidence": 0.3333}, "jev_score": 0.1067, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma: A Retrospective, Multicenter, Real-World Study", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hed.28211", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "In Europe, pembrolizumab with or without chemotherapy is the recommended first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nIn France, the TPExtreme (TPEx) regimen is preferred for CPS < 1.\n...\nWe reviewed all R/M HNSCC cases treated with first-line regimens between January 2021 and December 2023 in Nancy and Lille.\nData on patient history, treatments, survival, response, and safety were collected.\n...\nThe analysis included 290 patients; 19% received pembrolizumab alone, 28% received pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, 19% received TPEx, and 20% received adapted regimens.\nPFS and OS differed significantly between groups.\nMedian PFS and OS were respectively 2.9 and 9.4 months for pembrolizumab, 6 and 15.5 months for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 and 13.7 months for TPEx, and 4.3 and 10 months for adapted regimens.\nPembrolizumab alone is the safest regimen.\n...\nThis retrospective study reflects real-world first-line therapy for R/M HNSCC in two French cancer institutes.\nPembrolizumab combined with chemotherapy is the preferred treatment option.\n...\nHistorically, the first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) has been chemotherapy alone.\n...\nThe European Society for Medical Oncology (ESMO) recommends [9] the use of pembrolizumab for patients with a CPS ≥ 1, potentially associated with chemotherapy, and the EXTREME or TPEx regimens for patients with a CPS < 1.\n...\nThis retrospective, multicentric study included patients treated for R/M HNSCC from January 1st 2021 to December 31th 2023, in Nancy (ICL) and Lille (COL).\n...\nPFS differed significantly between groups, with a median PFS of 2.9 months [95% CI 2.1–3.7] for pembrolizumab, 6 [4.7–7.4] for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 [4.3–7.4] for TPEx, and 4.3 [3.2–5.4] for adapted regimens (Figure 1).", "date": "2025-11-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.21, "evidence": 0.32}, "jev_score": 0.0672, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma...", "url": "https://datalookout.com/head-neck-cancer-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "snippet": "The checkpoint immunotherapy era transformed recurrent/metastatic HNSCC: pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is FDA-approved as first-line monotherapy for high CPS scores and in combination with chemotherapy for all-comers; nivolumab (Opdivo) is approved in platinum-refractory disease.\n...\n|NCT06589804|Cetuximab + pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab in 2L+ relapsed/metastatic HNSCC|National Cancer Institute (NCI)|Recruiting|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-22", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.15, "evidence": 0.42}, "jev_score": 0.063, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40491285/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** In Europe, pembrolizumab with or without chemotherapy is the recommended first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nIn France, the TPExtreme (TPEx) regimen is preferred for CPS < 1.\n...\n** We reviewed all R/M HNSCC cases treated with first-line regimens between January 2021 and December 2023 in Nancy and Lille.\nData on patient history, treatments, survival, response, and safety were collected.\n...\n** The analysis included 290 patients; 19% received pembrolizumab alone, 28% received pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, 19% received TPEx, and 20% received adapted regimens.\nPFS and OS differed significantly between groups.\nMedian PFS and OS were respectively 2.9 and 9.4 months for pembrolizumab, 6 and 15.5 months for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 and 13.7 months for TPEx, and 4.3 and 10 months for adapted regimens.\nPembrolizumab alone is the safest regimen.\n...\n** This retrospective study reflects real-world first-line therapy for R/M HNSCC in two French cancer institutes.\nPembrolizumab combined with chemotherapy is the preferred treatment option.", "date": "2025-11-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.23, "evidence": 0.26}, "jev_score": 0.0598, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Real-World Treatment Patterns and Outcomes", "url": "https://www.ispor.org/docs/default-source/intl2021/ispor-2021-ion-hnscc-us-posterfinal7may2021-submitted-pdf.pdf?sfvrsn=4f13fcb8_0", "domain": "ispor.org", "snippet": "In June 2019, pembrolizumab was also approved by the FDA for 1L treatment of patients with R/M HNSCC as either monotherapy in patients with tumors that express programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1; combined positive score [CPS] ≥1) or as combination therapy with platinum and fluorouracil in all-comers.[9]\n- Despite the improved overall survival (OS) outcomes achieved with checkpoint inhibitors in R/M HNSCC,", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-10", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.16, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.0485, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Real-world treatment patterns and outcomes among ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10241070/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Pembrolizumab, a PD-1 immune checkpoint inhibitor, is approved as first-line (1L) treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) as monotherapy or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil chemotherapy.\nLimited data exist on the use of these regimens in real-world settings.\n...\nA 4-year follow-up study found continued survival benefits with 1L pembrolizumab-containing therapies compared to the EXTREME regimen (13).\nConsistent with the FDA approval, the National Comprehensive Cancer Network’s 2022 Clinical Practice Guidelines for Head and Neck Cancers recommend pembrolizumab monotherapy for tumors that express PD-L1 with CPS ≥1 (category 1) and in combination with platinum and 5-FU chemotherapy (category 1) as the preferred 1L treatment option for R/M HNSCC (14).\n...\nA total of 1,952 adults ≥18 years of age had a diagnosis of R/M HNSCC and initiated 1L therapy between July 1, 2019 and December 31, 2021, of whom 1,779 met all study criteria ( **Figure 1** ).\nAmong this group, 749 individuals received pembrolizumab-containing therapies, of whom 431 (57.5%) received pembrolizumab monotherapy, 215 (28.7%) received pembrolizumab in combination with platinum and 5-FU (‘pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy’; 84.2% of this group received carboplatin and 15.8% received cisplatin), and 103 (13.8%) received a different pembrolizumab combination therapy.\n...\nDistant metastatic disease (HNSCC with distant recurrence OR Stage IVc at initial diagnosis) was reported for 56.8% of the study population, HNSCC with locoregional recurrence for 23.1%, HNSCC not cured at initial diagnosis for 16.4%, and HPV-positive Stage IV oropharyngeal tumor at initial diagnosis for 3.7%.\nThe ECOG PS on the index date was 0–1 for 66.1% of the study population, 2–4 for 20.9%, and unknown or not documented for 13.0%.\nEvidence of PD-L1 testing of any kind was identified for 71.7% of the study population, and a CPS test result was available for 352 individuals (54.5%).\n...\nThe median (range) follow-up periods were 7.9 (0.0–35.1) months for individuals who received 1L pembrolizumab monotherapy and 8.5 (0.1–35.1) months for those who received 1L pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy.\n...\nThe median (95% CI) rwToT for the monotherapy group was 4.2 (3.5–4.6) months, while the median (95% CI) rwTTNT was 6.5 (5.4–7.4) months **Figures 2A, C, E** );\n...\nIn conclusion, this study provides information on the use of pembrolizumab-containing therapies for HNSCC in real-world US settings.\nOS outcomes were comparable between the pembrolizumab monotherapy and pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy groups.\nThe survival outcomes of both groups were also similar to the corresponding data observed in a registration clinical trial, despite the real-world study population being more heterogeneous.", "date": "2023-05-22", "last_updated": "2026-07-13", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.15, "evidence": 0.29}, "jev_score": 0.0435, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Real world treatment patterns for recurrent and metastatic ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2025.1577509/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "While KEYNOTE-048 established anti-PD1 with or without chemotherapy as first-line treatment for recurrent and metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) with combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1, treatment choice remains ambiguous given additional toxicity of combination treatment.\n...\nPatients treated first-line with anti-PD1 monotherapy, anti-PD1+chemotherapy, or cetuximab+chemotherapy in the Flatiron Health database were included.\n...\nOf 2577 patients included, Anti-PD1 monotherapy (n=1410) improved survival over cetuximab+chemotherapy (n=577, median survival 14.6 vs. 12.6 months, p=0.015), while anti-PD1+chemotherapy (n=590) showed a nonsignificant trend towards improvement (median survival 14.3 vs.\n12.6 months, p=0.053).\nIn HPV-associated disease, survival was equal between regimens.\nAddition of chemotherapy improved survival over anti-PD1 monotherapy in non-HPV associated tumors with CPS 1-9 (median survival 18.0 vs. 10.3 months, p=0.029) and in oral cavity primaries (median survival 10.3 vs. 7.6 months, p=0.003).\n...\nIn this cohort, 60.4% of patients had non-HPV associated disease, 32.6% had HPV-associated disease, and 7.0% had unknown HPV status or primary tumor site.\n1410 patients (54.7%) were treated with anti-PD1 monotherapy, 577 (22.4%) with combination anti-PD1 plus chemotherapy, and 590 (22.9%) with cetuximab plus chemotherapy in the front-line setting for recurrent or metastatic disease.\n...\nOut of 2577 patients, 1128 patients (43.8%) were reported to have received second line treatment (**Supplementary Tables 3, 4**).\nOf the 365 patients who received second line treatment after front line treatment with Cetuximab + chemotherapy, 191 (52.3%) of patients received an immunotherapy containing regimen.\n...\nThe median follow-up interval from initiation of front-line treatment was 9.7 months (range 0.03 – 131 months).\nIn the overall cohort, patients treated with anti-PD1 monotherapy had significantly improved survival compared to patients treated with cetuximab plus chemotherapy (median survival 14.6 months versus 12.6 months, p = 0.015 on log rank test, **Figure 1B**).", "date": "2025-05-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.16, "evidence": 0.22}, "jev_score": 0.0352, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Current Therapy for Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer: Evidence, Opportunities, and Challenges", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/EDBK_350442", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "1,2However, as systemic therapy has been more widely studied, the reproducible impact of multidrug chemotherapy on response and survival for the majority of fit patients has become clear.\n...\nNew drug development in metastatic HNSCC now takes place in the context of a clear role for first-line PD-1 inhibitor therapy when the cancer expresses PD-L1.\n...\n5The novel combination of chemotherapy with pembrolizumab improved overall survival (OS) compared with chemotherapy plus cetuximab in the total population (13 months vs. 10.7 months; HR, 0.77; p = .0034).\n...\n11Among patients with PD-L1 CPS greater than 20, survival was superior at 14.9 months for pembrolizumab monotherapy compared with 12.3 months for chemotherapy plus cetuximab.\nSimilarly, pembrolizumab was superior when PD-L1 CPS was 1 or greater, with OS of 12.3 compared with 10.3 months.\nAmong all patients treated with pembrolizumab, the inclusion of chemotherapy led to a higher objective response, and among HPV+ cancers, the relative benefit of pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy was strikingly greater than the benefit of pembrolizumab monotherapy when compared with chemotherapy and cetuximab, perhaps explained by the intrinsic chemosensitivity of HPV+ HNSCC.", "date": "2022-04-30", "last_updated": "2025-02-14", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.2867}, "jev_score": 0.0229, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Recurrent/metastatic relapse after definitive treatment for HNSCC", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2026.1738860/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Patients treated with high-risk treatment types (Surg + CRT, CRT) had higher disease stage at presentation, shorter TTRM (median 6–7 vs 17–19 months), and shorter OS from R/M treatment to death (median 8 vs 12 months) compared to patients in low-risk treatment groups (RT only, Surg + RT, Surg only).\n...\nPatients with locoregionally advanced HNSCC treated with CRT and surgery plus CRT represent a high-risk cohort with rapid R/M disease and poor survival, who need improved therapeutic options.\n...\nDespite definitive treatment with curative intent, approximately 15-50% of patients will develop recurrent or metastatic (R/M) disease (, ), which occurs within 6 months (rapid recurrences) after definitive therapy in about half of patients ().\n...\nWe included adult patients who received systemic treatment for mucosal R/M HNSCC (oropharynx, larynx, oral cavity, hypopharynx, or unknown primary site) from the Flatiron Health electronic medical record (EHR)-derived de-identified electronic medical records from 01/13/2011-09/28/2023.\n...\nAs previously described (), frontline R/M systemic therapy (1L), intended to capture initial systemic treatment for recurrent/metastatic disease, was defined as systemic therapy starting ≥ 60 days after any recorded radiation to the primary site, on order to avoid capturing chemoradiotherapy in the definitive setting.\n...\nIn this US nationwide study of over 7,500 patients who received systemic therapy for R/M HNSCC, most recurrences occurred within 2 years of definitive therapy (median TTRM 14 months).\n...\nIn conclusion, this nationwide retrospective observational study confirms that the majority of recurrences of both HPV+ and HPV- HNSCC occur within 2 years of definitive therapy.\nRelapse occurs most rapidly in those with high-risk disease treated with primary chemoradiation and trimodality therapy, who also have significantly shorter OS after initiating R/M therapy compared to those treated with lower-risk modalities.", "date": "2026-03-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-09", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.2133}, "jev_score": 0.0064, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 2126.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:41+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q055-2", "row_id": "q055", "mode": "queries", "objective": "DB-1305/Anti-PD-L1/VEGF Bispecific Antibody patients studied in Recurrent or Disseminated HNSCC (Oral Cavity/Oropharynx/Hypopharynx/Larynx), Incurable by Local Therapies trials: biomarker selection, disease severity, line of therapy, and required prior treatment", "query": "DB-1305 HNSCC PD-L1 biomarker eligibility", "anchors": ["DB-1305"], "search_latency_ms": 5007.9, "target": "DB-1305", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 398.8, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10231, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00043, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.6335, "results": [{"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 ...", "url": "https://www.uclahealth.org/clinical-trials/db-1311-combination-with-bnt327-or-db-1305", "domain": "uclahealth.org", "snippet": "Patients must have platinum-resistant disease.\n- For HNSCC patients: Histologically or cytologically confirmed recurrent (recurrent disease that is not amendable to curative treatment with local/ or systemic therapies)/ (disseminated) HNSCC of the oral cavity, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx that is considered incurable by local therapies.\n- For NSCLC patients: Pathologically documented Stage IIIB or IIIC NSCLC not amenable for radical surgery or definitive chemoradiation or Stage IV NSQ NSCLC.\nNot harboring an EGFR-sensitizing mutation or ALK gene rearrangements or other onco-driver gene mutations\n...\n- Has an uncontrolled concomitant or intercurrent illness, that in the opinion of the investigator, contra-indicates trial participation, limits compliance with trial procedures or substantially increases the risk of incurring AEs.\n...\n- Has a history of (non-infectious) ILD/pneumonitis.\n- Any autoimmune, connective tissue or inflammatory disorders.\n- Has spinal cord compression or clinically active central nervous system (CNS) metastases.\n- Has unresolved toxicities from previous anticancer therapy, defined as toxicities (other than alopecia) not yet resolved to Grade ≤1 or baseline.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.83, "evidence": 0.7633}, "jev_score": 0.6335, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy in recurrent/metastatic head and neck ...", "url": "https://iris.uniroma1.it/retrieve/16ede4e5-db57-47be-99f8-357552f12751/Ascione_Immunotherapy_2025.pdf", "domain": "iris.uniroma1.it", "snippet": "The immuno- histochemical assessment of PD-L1 expression was initially identified as a powerful and easily accessible predictive tool, and gained its place as the current standard for patient selection, but it has clear limitations.\n...\nwhose tumors express a PD-L1 combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nThe expression of PD-L1 on tumor cells and infiltrating immune cells is currently as- sessed by immunohistochemistry on formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue to identify patients eligible for ICI therapy.\nThe CPS is calculated as the number of PD-L1 positive invasive cancer cells, lym- phocytes, and macrophages, divided by the number of viable tumor cells and multiplied by 100.\nUnfortunate- ly, this predictor is not infallible and may not indicate a long-term response.\n...\nIn general, tumor PD-L1 expression is associated with improved efficacy with anti-PD-1/PD-L1 therapy in\nR/M HNSCC, with its predictive value being enriched by the consideration of PD-L1 expression on both tu- mor cells and tumor-infiltrating immune cells (CPS) 21.\n...\nThere- fore, CPS emerged as the best scoring method and is currently recommended, with evidence pointing at the thresholds of CPS ≥20 and ≥ 1 as clinically significant, with improved overall response rate, overall survival and progression-free survival in this population when treated with ICIs 18,19.\nThe predictive value of PD-L1 expression scored as \n...\nPD-L1 should be preferably tested on the primary tumor, lymph node metastasis, or distant metasta- sis when these options are simultaneously available.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-10-12", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.13, "evidence": 0.3433}, "jev_score": 0.0446, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Assessing PD-L1 Expression in Head and Neck Squamous ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10739626/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "PD-L1 expression assessed by immunohistochemistry is the single most clinically relevant biomarker predicting response to PD-1-blocking antibodies.\n...\nDespite extensive ongoing efforts to identify novel predictive biomarkers, only PD-L1 expression assessed by immunohistochemistry (IHC) on formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue is used to influence treatment decisions in the palliative treatment setting of HNSCC patients.\n...\nUnfortunately, because these exploratory translational efforts are not often followed up by prospective validation trials, the single best predictive biomarker in HNSCC remains PD-L1 expression as assessed by IHC.\nPD-L1-positive cells are defined by having (even weak) partial or complete membranous staining.\n...\nHowever, only PD-L1 CPS and TPS are clinically relevant in HNSCC.\n...\nTo date in HNSCC, therapies involving anti-PD-1 blockade are U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved only in the palliative setting, i.e., R/M HNSCC: Nivolumab or pembrolizumab monotherapy—compared to monotherapy with standard chemotherapeutic agents—was shown to improve overall survival (OS) in HNSCC patients that suffered early disease recurrence after platinum-containing chemotherapy that was used in the adjuvant setting or as treatment of primary or metastatic disease [3, 4].\n...\nIn all three trials, higher PD-L1 expression correlated with improved OS with anti-PD-1 therapy compared to PD-L1 low or negative tumors, suggesting PD-L1 expression is a predictive biomarker for response to anti-PD-1 antibodies in R/M HNSCC.\n...\nPD-L1 CPS ≥ 1 was observed between 78 and 85% of the study populations in large clinical trials [2, 4].\nFurthermore, high PD-L1 expression as defined by TPS ≥ 50% was observed in 23–26% of head and neck cancers [2, 4].\n...\n|1^st^-line palliative|Pembrolizumab monotherapy|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|Not approved|[2, 19, 20, 22]|\n|1^st^-line palliative|Pembrolizumab + platinum/5-FU|–|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|[2, 19, 20, 22]|\n|Early recurrence after platinum|Pembrolizumab monotherapy|–|PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50|PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50|[3, 19, 21, 22]|\n...\nIn summary, PD-L1 testing in HNSCC is only clinically relevant in the palliative setting with the use of pembrolizumab.\n...\nThus, obtaining a biopsy before the initiation of an ICI is not only important to confirm the clinical suspicion of HNSCC relapse but also to test for PD-L1 CPS of R/M HNSCCs.\n...\nThus, in HNSCC employing the companion diagnostic to pembrolizumab, PD-L1 IHC 22C3 pharmDx (PD-L1 antibody clone 22C3 together with the EnVision FLEX visualization system on Autostainer Link 48) is formally required and currently sufficient as the only other approved therapeutic in HNSCC, the anti-PD-1 antibody nivolumab, does not require PD-L1 testing.\n...\n- PD-L1 testing should be performed before the initiation of treatment with pembrolizumab.\n...\n- In the case of suspected R/M disease, a fresh biopsy should be obtained to (i) confirm recurrent and/or metastatic disease and (ii) to perform PD-L1 IHC on the tumor burden being treated with immunotherapy.\nThis recommendation has to be weighed based on a favorable benefit:risk ratio.\n- FNA specimens can be used for PD-L1 IHC.\nHowever, assessing TPS rather than CPS may be more accurate on FNA samples.\nIf the FNA is PD-L1 negative, obtaining a new tissue biopsy (e.g., core biopsy) may be considered to confirm a true-negative result, given the tumor heterogeneity.\n- Alcohol-based fixation methods should be avoided for FNA specimens.", "date": "2023-11-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.11, "evidence": 0.3333}, "jev_score": 0.0367, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune biomarkers for head and neck cancer - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12715102/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "These therapies have shown better outcomes in terms of overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival (PFS) relative to standard chemotherapy, with the greatest benefits observed in HNSCC cases with elevated levels of PD-L1 [7, 12].\n...\nPD-L1 level, TMB, and microsatellites are the only approved biomarkers for ICI responses [41].\nIn 2016, FDA approval was granted to two PD-1 inhibitors in patients with relapsed or metastatic HNSCC.\nThese two monoclonal antibodies were pembrolizumab and nivolumab.\n...\nPD-L1 expression levels could be used as biomarkers for anti-PD-L1 drugs.\nTherefore, PD-L1 is a static predictive biomarker for relapsed or metastatic HNSCC exhibiting a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1 [41].\n...\nIn KEYNOTE-012, a phase 1b clinical trial of 188 HNSCC patients, a CPS of 1 or higher was considered a significant predictor of HNSCC’s response to pembrolizumab.\nCPS-positive patients accounted for 81% of the cohort and had better ORRs, PFS, and OS than CPS-negative patients.\n...\nConsequently, following the findings of the randomized phase 3 KEYNOTE-040 trial demonstrating that the CPS demonstrated greater sensitivity than TPS at lower threshold values in predicting pembrolizumab efficacy, CPS developed as the preferred scoring method for evaluating PD-L1 expression HNSCC [119].\n...\nAccordingly, the anti-PD-1 antibodies pembrolizumab and nivolumab received FDA approval to treat HNSCC recurring after the use of platinum-based regimens.\nPembrolizumab also was approved alongside chemotherapy (platinum and fluorouracil) for all HNSCC patients and received approval as a single-agent therapy for patients PD-L1-positive patients with CPS ≥ 1 [123].\n...\nFor HNSCC that is recurring or metastatic, a CPS ≥ 1 is the FDA-approved threshold that justifies the using pembrolizumab in the first-line setting either as single agent, or alongside chemotherapy.\n...\nPD-L1 remains a key predictive biomarker, but inconsistencies in testing methods and thresholds hinder its clinical utility [42, 43, 175].\n...\nCurrently, HPV status and PD-L1 CPS remain the most widely established biomarkers with direct implications for clinical decision-making in head and neck cancer.", "date": "2025-12-18", "last_updated": "2026-08-06", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.1, "evidence": 0.3367}, "jev_score": 0.0337, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "PD-L1 Testing in Cancer - MyPathologyReport", "url": "https://www.mypathologyreport.ca/biomarkers/pd-l1-testing-in-cancer/", "domain": "mypathologyreport.ca", "snippet": "PD-L1 testing is mandatory for all patients with advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) because the result directly determines first-line treatment.\n...\n- **TPS ≥ 50% (PD-L1 high).** Pembrolizumab monotherapy is approved as a first-line treatment for advanced NSCLC without actionable driver mutations (EGFR, ALK, and others) when TPS is ≥ 50%.\n...\nPD-L1 testing is standard for all patients with recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC).\nThe 22C3 assay with CPS scoring is used, and two cut-offs are clinically meaningful:\n- **CPS ≥ 1.** Pembrolizumab combined with platinum-based chemotherapy is approved for first-line treatment of recurrent/metastatic HNSCC with CPS ≥ 1, based on the KEYNOTE-048 trial.\n- **CPS ≥ 20.** Pembrolizumab monotherapy (without chemotherapy) is approved as first-line treatment for CPS ≥ 20, based on demonstrated non-inferiority to chemotherapy with a more favorable tolerability profile.\nThis higher threshold identifies patients most likely to achieve durable responses from immunotherapy alone.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.0261, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy and Biomarker Testing in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancers: ASCO Guideline", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/JCO.22.02328", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "2-4With the advent of ICIs in cancer treatment, biomarkers such as programmed death ligand-1 (PD-L1) and tumor mutational burden (TMB) are gaining importance in selecting treatment options.\n...\nIn post hoc receiver operating characteristic curve analysis of KEYNOTE-040 data, CPS and TPS appeared to show equivalent performance at a cutoff of ≥ 50; however, CPS was more sensitive than TPS at a lower cutoff of ≥ 1.\n14As such, CPS scoring is preferred for maximum sensitivity, although a TPS score indicating ≥ 1% positivity is correlated with benefit to ICI and may be interpreted as positive if available.\nA lower threshold for defining CPS positivity of ≥ 1 is also preferred.\n...\n39Therefore, HNSCC patients with TMB of ≥ 10 MB should be considered high, and testing may be performed in patients for whom CPS is not available, as those with high TMB may benefit from PD-1 inhibitors.\n...\n43However, current data do not support changing recommendations for the addition or exclusion of anti–PD-1 therapies in the recurrent and/or metastatic setting with regard to the HPV status.\n...\n21These findings supported the US Food and Drug Administration approval of both pembrolizumab and nivolumab in the advanced-line setting after progression on or following platinum-containing therapies, regardless of PD-L1 expression.\nTherefore, we recommend single-agent pembrolizumab or nivolumab for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with disease progression within 6 months of platinum-based chemotherapy.", "date": "2023-02-10", "last_updated": "2025-02-21", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.3267}, "jev_score": 0.0261, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "The Society for Immunotherapy of Cancer consensus statement on immunotherapy for the treatment of squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck (HNSCC)", "url": "https://jitc.bmj.com/content/7/1/184", "domain": "jitc.bmj.com", "snippet": "The European Commission followed in 2017 with approval of nivolumab for treatment of the same patient population, and shortly thereafter with approval of pembrolizumab monotherapy for the treatment of recurrent or metastatic HNSCC in adults whose tumors express PD-L1 with a ≥ 50% tumor proportion score and have progressed on or after platinum-containing chemotherapy.\nThen in 2019, the FDA granted approval for PD-1 inhibition as first-line treatment for patients with metastatic or unresectable, recurrent HNSCC, approving pembrolizumab in combination with platinum and fluorouracil for all patients with HNSCC and pembrolizumab as a single agent for patients with HNSCC whose tumors express a PD-L1 combined positive score ≥ 1.\n...\nFor frontline therapy, pembrolizumab was approved for use in combination with platinum and fluorouracil for all patients with R/M HNSCC and as a single agent for patients whose tumors express PD-L1 with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥1 as determined by an FDA-approved test.\nThe FDA also expanded the intended use for the PD-L1 IHC 22C3 pharmDx kit to include use as a companion diagnostic for selecting patients with HNSCC for treatment with pembrolizumab as a single agent.\n...\nImportantly, while both checkpoint inhibitors are FDA approved for patients with advanced R/M HNSCC who have progressed on or after platinum-based chemotherapy, neither require PD-L1 expression analysis prior to treatment except within the EU, where TPS > 50% is required.\n...\nThe majority of the subcommittee recommended treatment with anti-PD-1 monotherapy (82%) for R/M HNSCC patients who are platinum-refractory including those who progressed within six months of platinum-based chemotherapy.\n...\nBoth nivolumab and pembrolizumab are approved for the treatment of patients with R/M HNSCC with disease progression on or after platinum-based therapy, without the requirement for biomarker testing.\nHowever, the majority of HNSCC patients will progress on these agents, highlighting the importance of developing predictive biomarkers to better determine who will benefit from treatment with anti-PD-1/PD-L1 ICIs.\nSpecific prognostic biomarkers that have been evaluated in HNSCC include programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1) expression, tumor mutational burden (TMB), and immune gene signatures within both the tumor and the surrounding tissue [11–18].\n...\nPD-L1 expression has been extensively analyzed as a biomarker and has shown moderate predictive value across multiple solid tumors [53].\n...\nTumor PD-L1 expression generally correlates with improved efficacy with anti-PD-1/PD-L1 ICIs in R/M HNSCC, with increased predictive value when including PD-L1 expression on tumor infiltrating immune cells.\nThe predictive value of PD-L1 expression is not absolute, however, and some patients who are PD-L1 negative still benefit from treatment with these agents.\n...\nKEYNOTE-048 provided category 1 evidence for PD-L1 expression analysis by CPS ≥20 and ≥ 1, showing improved overall survival for such patients upon treatment with pembrolizumab monotherapy.\nThe subsequent approval of pembrolizumab for use as a single agent for patients whose tumors express PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1 marks the first mandated biomarker testing of patients with R/M HNSCC tumors.\n...\nThe majority of the subcommittee (81%) also agreed that the best use of biomarker testing when treating patients with HNSCC with immunotherapy is by combined positive score (CPS).\n...\nCurrently, FDA-approved immunotherapies for head and neck cancer patients include pembrolizumab with platinum and fluorouracil (FU) for all patients and as a single agent for patients whose tumors express PD-L1 CPS ≥1.", "date": "2019-12-01", "last_updated": "2024-10-16", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.08, "evidence": 0.32}, "jev_score": 0.0256, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "PD-L1: Test Selection Guide", "url": "https://testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com/test/test-guides/TG_PD-L1/pd-l1-test-selection-guide", "domain": "testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com", "snippet": "|HNSCC|Opdivo® (nivolumab),8 see test code 93793 in **Table 2** for test information|*PD-L1 testing optional (complementary diagnostic)*- Treatment for recurrent or metastatic disease with progression on or after platinum-based therapy|", "date": "2026-07-12", "last_updated": "2026-07-13", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.28}, "jev_score": 0.0252, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Multicenter real-world data on immunotherapy for R/M HNSCC from ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913446/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "PD-L1 expression alone has limited predictive value.\n...\nHigher combined positive score (CPS) predicted better outcomes.\n...\nIn patients with PD-L1 (combined positive score, CPS) ≥ 1%, both Pembrolizumab alone and combined with chemotherapy improved OS over the EXTREME regimen.\n...\nPD-L1 expression remains the sole guideline-recommended biomarker for selecting immunotherapy candidates, but inconsistent study results question its reliability (11).\nSome patients with negative PD-L1 expression still benefit from ICI, highlighting the need for additional predictive markers (12, 13).\n...\nAfter applying predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria, 105 eligible patients were included.\n...\n1. Patients aged ≥ 18 years with a pathological diagnosis of R/M HNSCC.\n2. Initiation of first-line systemic therapy with PD-1 inhibitors (imported or domestic), administered as monotherapy or in combination with other agents.\n3. Imaging assessments were performed after completion of at least two treatment cycles.\n4. Presence of at least one measurable lesion according to the immune response evaluation criteria in solid tumors (iRECIST), version 1.1 (21).\n5. Not eligible for local curative-intent therapy.\n6. PBMs examination data must be available at baseline (prior to treatment), 6 weeks post-treatment (6w pt), and 12 weeks post-treatment (12w pt).\n...\n1. Diagnosis of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\n2. History of a second primary malignancy within the past five years.\n3. Significant missing clinical or radiological data that would compromise the integrity and validity of the analysis.\n4. Prior exposure to anti-PD-1 or anti-PD-L1 therapies.\n...\nNo single biomarker reliably predicts immunotherapy efficacy (35).", "date": "2026-02-04", "last_updated": "2026-04-10", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.3033}, "jev_score": 0.0212, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "UCLA Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma Clinical Trials", "url": "https://ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org/head-and-neck-squamous-cell-carcinoma", "domain": "ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org", "snippet": "open to eligible people ages 18 years and up\n...\n- ## FORTIFI-HN01: A Study of Ficerafusp Alfa (BCA101) or Placebo in Combination With Pembrolizumab in First-Line PD-L1-pos, R or M HNSCC\nopen to eligible people ages 18 years and up\nFicerafusp alfa is directed against two targets, Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR) and Transforming Growth Factor beta (TGF-β).\nThis study intends to evaluate the safety and efficacy of ficerafusp alfa in combination with pembrolizumab versus placebo with pembrolizumab in 1L PD-L1-positive, recurrent or metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma (HNSCC).\n...\nAn open-label, controlled, multi-site, interventional, 2-arm, Phase II/III trial of BNT113 in combination with pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab monotherapy as first line treatment in patients with unresectable recurrent or metastatic HPV16+ HNSCC expressing programmed cell death ligand-1 (PD-L1) with combined positive score (CPS) ≥1.", "date": "2026-09-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.1}, "jev_score": 0.004, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 5406.7, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:42+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q057-1", "row_id": "q057", "mode": "queries", "objective": "CNP200137 first regulatory approval or market launch year for acute myeloid leukemia", "query": "CNP200137 regulatory approval AML", "anchors": ["CNP200137"], "search_latency_ms": 2431.2, "target": "CNP200137", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 373.6, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 16849, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000708, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.8019, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Menin Inhibitor Drug Development Pipeline: AML, Diabetes ...", "url": "https://www.inflectionlabs.bio/reports/menin-inhibitor-pipeline", "domain": "inflectionlabs.bio", "snippet": "Revumenib and ziftomenib became the first approved menin inhibitors, treating KMT2A-rearranged and NPM1-mutant acute myeloid leukemias.\n...\n**3.\nCNP200137 (CoBX Bio, Pharos iBio)**\nThis is a pre-clinical stage, next-generation menin inhibitor that is designed to address menin resistance challenges.^[58]^ CNP200137 may well be the same molecule as CXB-001 with a different name, or a completely separate molecule being developed by the same South Korean biotech partnership.^[59]^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8267}, "jev_score": 0.8019, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Regulatory timeline - OnCo", "url": "https://onco.cc/regulatory/", "domain": "onco.cc", "snippet": "|20 Jul 2023|Quizartinib Small-molecule kinase inhibitor (FLT3-ITD, type II)|Approval|US|Frontline FLT3-ITD AML (QuANTUM-First)|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.56, "evidence": 0.9767}, "jev_score": 0.547, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Important announcement - Home | AML Hub", "url": "https://aml-hub.com/dr