{"case_id": "q060-2", "row_id": "q060", "mode": "queries", "objective": "EOM613 sponsor development stage indication public source", "query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease Phase 2 initiation 2026", "anchors": ["EOM613 Crohn's disease", "EOM613"], "search_latency_ms": 4444.8, "target": "EOM613 Crohn's disease / EOM613", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 343.7, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8367, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000351, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9604, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports Promising Preclinical", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/01/07/3214524/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-promising-preclinical-results-and-provides-an-update-on-eom613-and-eom147.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "Based on these data, combined with previously reported promising results of our Phase I/IIa 2023 human clinical trial in hospitalized COVID-19 patients with cytokine-driven respiratory inflammation in Brazil, we are planning an initial Phase II exploratory clinical trial in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is designed to include 15-20 patients and will be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial will enroll subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the first quarter 2026 to test for signs of clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.", "date": "2026-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.98}, "jev_score": 0.9604, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports FDA Clearance of ...", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/05/27/3301943/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-fda-clearance-of-an-ind-for-a-clinical-trial-in-cachectic-cancer-patients.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "The trial is anticipated to be initiated early in the third quarter of 2026.\nThe Company is also planning an additional exploratory, open-label EOM613 clinical trial in Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is intended to be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial is planned to enroll 15 to 20 subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the fourth quarter 2026 to test for signs of both clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 monotherapy treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.", "date": "2026-05-27", "last_updated": "2026-05-28", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9467}, "jev_score": 0.9183, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceuticals", "url": "http://biobase.whitefordresearch.com/companies/eom-pharmaceuticals", "domain": "biobase.whitefordresearch.com", "snippet": "The named pipeline consists of EOM613 and EOM147, both of which the company says have prior Phase 2 clinical experience and are intended to use a 505(b)(2) development pathway where possible.[5] EOM613 has been studied historically in cancer cachexia and in a Phase 1/2a COVID-19 respiratory-inflammation trial, and the company announced in 2023 that it was preparing a Crohn's disease clinical program based on cytokine findings from the COVID-19 study.[\n...\nEOM remains a development-stage therapeutics company, and its public filing states that neither EOM613 nor EOM147 is approved in any jurisdiction.[5] The company says it intends to pursue FDA 505(b)(2) filings for both assets, relying partly on prior clinical and public-domain data, but it also acknowledges that the FDA may require additional preclinical or clinical work.[5] Current value creation therefore depends on financing, execution of planned studies, and regulatory acceptance of the proposed development pathway.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9467}, "jev_score": 0.9183, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Provides an Update on its Lead Drug Candidate EOM613", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-provides-an-update-on-its-lead-drug-candidate-eom613-301978052.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "In light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\nEOM is also assembling a team of key opinion leader gastroenterologists, who are experts in clinical research relating to novel treatment approaches to IBD, to advise on the design and execution of the planned clinical trial.\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B. Taraporewala, PhD, Chief Executive Officer of EOM.", "date": "2023-11-07", "last_updated": "2025-07-05", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.88}, "jev_score": 0.8624, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "reticulose (EOM613) / EOM Pharma", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=8bd00719-c964-4a7b-85e5-3bcf4092483b", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "In light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\"", "date": "2023-11-07", "last_updated": "2025-10-28", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.8367}, "jev_score": 0.8032, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Methods of treating inflammatory bowel diseases", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/WO2025049974A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "Of those patients, only approximately 20% to 30% go into remission, and of those in remission, at least half remain in remission over time based on clinical trial data (AC Moss, Gastroenterol. Hepatol. (NY), 2022: 18 (6) 360-363).\n...\n- the therapy was safe and well-tolerated using daily injections\n...\n- EOM613 was not previously investigated for use in treating IBD, such as Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.\n...\nDisclosed herein are methods of treating inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) in a subject, including administering a therapeutically effective amount of EOM613 to the subject.\nIn some aspects, \nthe methods include a first step of selecting a subject having inflammatory bowel disease for treatment with EOM613.\nIn some aspects, the IBD is Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, or indeterminate colitis.\n...\nIn some aspects, EOM613 treatment decreases the frequency or severity of one or more symptoms of IBD in the subject.", "date": "2024-08-30", "last_updated": "2025-07-20", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.6667}, "jev_score": 0.6467, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] EOM PHARMACEUTICAL HOLDINGS, INC. - OTC Markets", "url": "https://www.otcmarkets.com/filing/conv_pdf?id=16268613&guid=Bhj-k6XpGGX4V3h", "domain": "otcmarkets.com", "snippet": "We are in the process of initiating all the necessary IND-enabling preclinical safety pharmacology studies and toxicology studies that would enable us to proceed into new Phase II clinical trials in cancer cachexia and/or rheumatoid arthritis.\n...\nThis drug product is currently being evaluated in a multicenter, open-label Phase\n1/2a clinical trial in Brazil to treat hospitalized COVID-19 patients who have the deleterious “cytokine storm”- related hyperimmune response to viral\ninfection.\n...\nA Phase II open label study of EOM613 (then known as AVR/OHR118) in cancer-related anorexia-cachexia was conducted by Ohr Pharmaceuticals, Inc.\nunder a Health Canada IND at the McGill University Cancer Center and the University of Ottawa Cancer Center, under the stewardship of Dr. Martin Chasen, who served as Principal Investigator.\nA total of 29 patients were enrolled in this trial (NCT01206335), of which 18 completed the trial.\n...\nUpon successful completion of these preclinical toxicity studies, projected to be completed by 4Q 2022, and if the animal model studies warrant, we intend to commence a placebo-controlled larger multi-center Phase 2 clinical trial in cancer cachexia patients in the USA under an IND.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.81, "evidence": 0.41}, "jev_score": 0.3321, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_evidence"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Crohn's disease | pharmaphorum", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/tag/crohns-disease", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "snippet": "### Earendil raises $787m as lead TL1A antibody starts phase 2", "date": "2026-09-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.39, "evidence": 0.71}, "jev_score": 0.2769, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Highlights of the 2026 European Crohn's and Colitis Conference", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/crohnscolitis360/article/8/3/otag075/8726059", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "This phase 2, multicenter, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial evaluated the efficacy and safety of picankibart in patients with moderate-to-severe active UC.", "date": "2026-07-08", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.2, "evidence": 0.31}, "jev_score": 0.062, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials | EOM Pharmaceuticals, Inc.", "url": "https://eompharma.com/clinical-trials/", "domain": "eompharma.com", "snippet": "|**Macular edema due to retinal vein Occlusion^1^** (Wroblewski and Hu, 2016)|- Phase 1, proof-of-concept study - Single-center, open-label randomized trial - Patients treated with topical squalamine (OHR102) and intravetral ranibizumab for 10 weeks, followed by randomization to continue or discontinue squalamine|20 treatment-naïve patients|At week 38, best-corrected visual acuity improved mean best-corrected visual acuity significantly more in patients who continued squalamine vs stopped|**Squalamine and ranbizumab were well tolerated**|", "date": "2020-12-02", "last_updated": "2026-09-05", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.03, "evidence": 0.0167}, "jev_score": 0.0005, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 4788.5, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:44+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q059-4", "row_id": "q059", "mode": "queries", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "query": "HEC234055 orthotopic pancreatic model", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "search_latency_ms": 4715.9, "target": "HEC234055", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 428.1, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 9283, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.00039, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 1, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.699, "results": [{"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "HEC234055 — Orally Available High-Activity Pan-RAS Molecular Glue Inhibitor\nTargeting the ‘‘Undruggable’’ (Abstract #2634)\n...\nThe drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nBroad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (‘‘GTP’’)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models\n(including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce\ndose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials\nin\nhumans.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-23", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.7133}, "jev_score": 0.699, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic and heterotopic murine models of pancreatic ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29903803/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Syngeneic, immunocompetent allograft tumor models recapitulate important aspects of the tumor microenvironment and have short tumor latency with predictable growth kinetics, making them useful for trialing novel therapeutics. Here, we describe surgical techniques for orthotopic and heterotopic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) tumor implantation and characterize phenotypes based on implantation site.Mice (*n*=8 per group) were implanted with 10^4^ cells in the pancreas or flank.\nHy15549 and Han4.13 cell lines were derived from primary murine PDAC (Ptf1-Cre; LSL-KRAS-G12D; Trp53 Lox/+) on C57BL/6 and FVB strains, respectively. Single-cell suspension and solid tumor implants were compared. Tumors were treated with two intravenous doses of FOLFIRINOX and responses evaluated.All mice developed pancreatic tumors within 7 days.\nOrthotopic tumors grew faster and larger than heterotopic tumors. By 3 weeks, orthotopic mice began losing weight, and showed declines in body condition requiring euthanasia starting at 4 weeks. Single-cell injection into the pancreas had near 100% engraftment, but solid tumor implant engraftment was ∼50% and was associated with growth restriction.\nOrthotopic tumors were significantly more responsive to intravenous FOLFIRINOX compared with heterotopic tumors, with greater reductions in size and increased apoptosis. Heterotopic tumors were more desmoplastic and hypovascular. However, drug uptake into tumor tissue was equivalent regardless of tumor location or degree of fibrosis, indicating that microenvironment differences between heterotopic and orthotopic tumors influenced response to therapy.Our results show that orthotopic and heterotopic allograft locations confer unique microenvironments that influence growth kinetics, desmoplastic response and angiogenesis.\nTumor location influences chemosensitivity to FOLFIRINOX and should inform future preclinical trials.This article has an associated First Person interview with the first author of the paper.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-08", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.1, "evidence": 0.4367}, "jev_score": 0.0437, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic and Heterotopic Murine Models of Pancreatic ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2022.863346/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Herein, we describe surgical techniques for a novel orthotopic implantation of syngeneic pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) tissue slices.\nThis method has a high success modeling rate and stable growth kinetics, which makes it useful for testing novel therapeutics.\n...\nIn summary, this study describes construction of a novel orthotopic PDAC model through implantation of tissue slices and discusses resistance to immunotherapy from the perspective of a PDAC microenvironment.\n...\nTo obtain the tumor graft, Panc02 cells were subcutaneously inoculated in 6-week-old nude mice (2 × 10^7^ cells in each mouse).\nTumors were allowed to grow for 2-3 weeks until they were approximately 8~10 mm in diameter.\nNext, tumor samples were obtained and immediately transferred into ice-cold UW (University of Wisconsin) preservation solution.\nEach tumor was then wrapped in 5% low-melt agar and quickly sectioned using a tissue slicer at a thickness of 500μm.\n...\nAfter disinfection and sterilization, ophthalmic scissors were used to cut a 1 cm port above the spleen.\nAtraumatic forceps were then used to gently squeeze out the pancreas tail with spleen.\nThe capsule of the pancreas was opened with blunt dissection.\nNext, the open edge of the capsule was gently lifted and a tumor slice was carefully inserted into the subcapsular space.\nThe graft was fitted to the pancreas with 2μL of Vetbond (3M, 1469SB), followed by reinforcement using 5-0 absorbable sutures.\nFinally, the spleen and pancreas were incorporated into the abdominal cavity and the abdomen was closed.\n...\nThe pancreas was exposed following the same steps described above.\nBefore injection, the injection areas were warmed using 37°C saline.\nNext, 3×10^6^ Panc02 cells resuspended in 30μL Matrigel (Yeasen, 40134ES08) were immediately administered into the pancreas capsule.\nBefore needle drawing, a heating lamp was used to heat the injection site to promote solidification of the Matrigel.\nFinally, the spleen and pancreas were incorporated into the abdominal cavity and the abdomen was closed.\n...\nBriefly, the pre-formed tumor was uniformly sliced and used as a “seed”, which was then sutured with an absorbable thread to form a tumor in the tail of the mice pancreas (Figures 1A, B).\nHerein, we established and evaluated PDAC models using the TST and OI methods, respectively.\n...\nResults showed that the TST method formed larger orthotopic tumor sizes than the OI method but had a smaller coefficient of variation (9.9% vs. 53.57%), with the TST method exhibiting more stable growth kinetics (Figure 1C).\n...\nIn conclusion, the TST method is a reliable method for developing pancreatic cancer orthotopic mice models.", "date": "2022-07-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-18", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.09, "evidence": 0.4433}, "jev_score": 0.0399, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "An Orthotopic Resectional Mouse Model of Pancreatic Cancer - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33044459/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To address this deficiency, we describe a mouse model involving orthotopic implantation of PC followed by distal pancreatectomy and splenectomy.\nThe model has been demonstrated to be safe and suitably flexible for the study of various therapeutic approaches in adjuvant and neo adjuvant settings.\nIn this model, a pancreatic tumor is first generated by implanting a mixture of human pancreatic cancer cells (luciferase-tagged AsPC-1) and human cancer associated pancreatic stellate cells into the distal pancreas of Balb/c athymic nude mice.\nAfter three weeks, the cancer is resected by re-laparotomy, distal pancreatectomy and splenectomy.\nIn this model, bioluminescence imaging can be used to follow the progress of cancer development and effects of resection/treatments.\nFollowing resection, adjuvant therapy can be given.\nAlternatively, neoadjuvant treatment can be given prior to resection.\n...\nAll mice underwent successful distal pancreatectomy/splenectomy with no issues of hemostasis.\nA macroscopic proximal pancreatic margin greater than 5 mm was achieved in 43 (96%) mice.\nThe technical success rate of pancreatic resection was 100%, with 0% early mortality and morbidity.\nNone of the animals died during the week after resection.\nIn summary, we describe a robust and reproducible technique for a surgical resection model of pancreatic cancer in mice which mimics the clinical scenario.\nThe model may be useful for the testing of both adjuvant and neoadjuvant treatments.", "date": "2020-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-05-05", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.4533}, "jev_score": 0.0317, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinically relevant orthotopic pancreatic cancer models for ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38191243/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Using flow cytometry and immunohistochemistry, we characterized the immunological features and trafficking patterns of adoptively transferred T cells in orthotopic PDAC (C57BL/6) models using two mouse cell lines, KPC-Luc and MT-5, isolated from C57BL/6 KPC-GEMM (Kras^LSL-G12D/+^p53^-/-^ and Kras^LSL-G12D/+^p53^LSL-R172H/+^, respectively).\n...\n** The MT-5 orthotopic model best recapitulates the cellular and stromal features of the TME in the PDAC GEMM.\nIn contrast, far more host immune cells infiltrate the KPC-Luc tumors, which have less stroma, although CD4^+^ and CD8^+^ T cells were similarly detected in the MT-5 tumors compared with KPC-GEMM in mice.\n...\n** Our data establish for the first time a reproducible and realistic clinical system useful for modeling stroma-rich and stroma-devoid PDAC tumors.\nThese models shall serve an indepth study of how to overcome barriers that limit antitumor activity of adoptively transferred T cells.", "date": "2024-01-08", "last_updated": "2026-07-19", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.07, "evidence": 0.3867}, "jev_score": 0.0271, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic models of human pancreatic cancer - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10415855/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Orthotopic transplantation of solid tumor fragments of human tumors in nude mice reproduces their pattern of local growth and distal dissemination.\nWhile lymphatic, hepatic or peritoneal dissemination can be reproduced, perineural invasion is absent.\nEarly passages (less than 3) of xenografts show a high degree of stability regarding K-ras, p53 and p16 gene status.\nOn the other hand, advanced passages of tumors acquire additional alterations in the p15 and Smad4 genes.\nMutations in K-ras, p53, p15 and Smad4 genes can be acquired, in this model system, in the more advanced stages of pancreatic tumor dissemination.\nFinally, it is also possible to standardize local growth of these tumors as well as its dissemination pattern giving us a preclinical tool to evaluate the anticancer activity of new drugs.", "date": "1999-06-30", "last_updated": "2025-07-02", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.4233}, "jev_score": 0.0254, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Inoculation of Pan02 cells produces tumor nodules in mouse ...", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0300723", "domain": "journals.plos.org", "snippet": "Collectively, these data indicate that a 5x10^6^ Pan02-luc IP inoculation in syngeneic mice produces orthotopic pancreatic tumor consistently by day 7.\n...\nIn summary, we have developed and characterized a novel and high efficiency orthotopic pancreatic tumor model based on a one-step Pan02 pancreatic ductal carcinoma inoculation into the peritoneum.", "date": "2024-03-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-23", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.37}, "jev_score": 0.0222, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinically relevant orthotopic pancreatic cancer models ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10806555/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The MT-5 orthotopic model best recapitulates the cellular and stromal features of the TME in the PDAC GEMM.\n...\n## WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC\n...\n- To combat this barrier, we present a reproducible alternative: orthotopic implantation of genetically defined syngeneic murine cell lines into the pancreas of C57BL/6 mice.\n...\nOver time, these GEMMs have given rise to useful syngeneic cell lines that can be orthotopically implanted into the pancreas of C57BL/6 mice to generate organotropic tumors.\n...\nFor orthotopic experiments, 2×10^5^ murine PDAC cells were orthotopically implanted into the tail of the pancreas of 6-week to 8-week-old female 16 C57BL/6 mice (n=8 per cell line; Strain 000664, The Jackson Laboratory).\n...\nOrthotopic implantation of tumor cells into the pancreas of immune-competent mice represents a practical approach for testing immunotherapy in vivo.\n...\nOrthotopic KPC-Luc was a more aggressive model, with a median survival of mice at 23 days, while MT-5-bearing mice survived a median time of 36 days (figure 1B).", "date": "2024-01-08", "last_updated": "2026-03-22", "orig_rank": 9, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.34}, "jev_score": 0.0204, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "LP – Orthotopic Tumor Models", "url": "https://td2inc.com/orthotopic-tumor-models", "domain": "td2inc.com", "snippet": "TD2 has developed a comprehensive library of orthotopic models in mouse and human cell lines (Figure 1) at various implantation sites.\n...\n|Intrapancreatic|Pano2|\n...\n**Pan02 Syngeneic Orthotopic Pancreatic Tumor Model**", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.2367}, "jev_score": 0.0142, "jev_rank": 9, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Orthotopic Models", "url": "https://dda.creative-bioarray.com/orthotopic-models.html", "domain": "dda.creative-bioarray.com", "snippet": "Orthotopic tumor models (also known as* in situ* models) are created by injecting tumor cells/tissue fragments into the organ of origin.\nUnlike subcutaneous xenografts, orthotopically implanted tumors grow within the organ microenvironment, reconstituting more realistic stromal, vascular and immune interactions.\n...\n|Spleen/Intra-splenic|Pancreatic/Colorectal|PANC-1, MIA PaCa-2|\n...\n|Pancreas|Pancreatic Cancer|BxPC-3, PANC-1|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.04, "evidence": 0.2567}, "jev_score": 0.0103, "jev_rank": 10, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 5144.0, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:44+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q053-1", "row_id": "q053", "mode": "queries", "objective": "ADT-1004 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "query": "ADT-1004 quantitative tumour growth inhibition percentage PDAC monotherapy", "anchors": ["ADT-1004"], "search_latency_ms": 780.6, "target": "ADT-1004", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 326.9, "calls": 8, "attempts": 8, "usage": {"input_tokens": 7220, "output_tokens": 288}, "cost_usd": 0.000303, "n_candidates": 8, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9042, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004: a first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with robust ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-025-02288-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d921b7d0-2285-448c-af48-bb748e5ce2b0", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "ADT-1004 potently blocked tumor growth and RAS activation in mouse PDAC models without discernable toxicity with target engagement and reduced activated RAS and ERK phosphorylation.\nIn addition, ADT-1004 suppressed tumor growth in PDX PDAC models with KRAS^G12D^, KRAS^G12V^, KRAS^G12C^, or KRAS^G13Q^ mutations and increased CD4^+^ and CD8^+^ T cells in the TME consistent with exhaustion and increased MHCII^+^ M1 macrophage and dendritic cells.\n...\nDoses of ADT-1004 from 20 to 50 mg/kg demonstrated comparable statistically significant reductions in tumor weights compared to vehicle-treated mice or mice treated with 10 mg/kg ADT-1004 (Fig. 1G).\n...\nADT-1004 treatment significantly inhibited the growth of PDAC tumors with KRAS^G12D^ (Fig. 5A and C, Supplementary Fig. 5A), KRAS^G12C^ (Fig. 5E and G, Supplementary Fig. 5B), KRAS^G12V^ (Fig.\n...\nThese results lead us to conclude that ADT-1004 selectively inhibits RAS-dependent tumor growth in mouse tumor PDAC models and are consistent with previous reports characterizing the in vitro growth inhibitory activity of ADT-1004 [14].\n...\nIn conclusion, this study establishes ADT-1004 as a 1st -in-class orally bioavailable pan-RAS inhibitor specific for the apoenzyme state of mutant RAS.\nADT-1004 exhibits promising antitumor activity against RAS-mutant PDAC, including resistant phenotypes.", "date": "2025-03-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-09", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.9133}, "jev_score": 0.9042, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract LB322: Antitumor activity of 1st-in-class pan-RAS inhibitor ADT-1004 in mouse models of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article/83/8_Supplement/LB322/725683/Abstract-LB322-Antitumor-activity-of-1st-in-class", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "As a prodrug, ADT-1004, generates an active metabolite, ADT-007, that potently inhibits the growth of human and murine PDA cell lines\n*in vitro* with single-digit nM IC 50 irrespective of specific RAS mutational codon or isozyme by inhibiting activated RAS and signaling downstream of KRAS (ERK1/2 and AKT phosphorylation).\n...\nOnce daily oral administration of ADT-1004 significantly inhibited the growth of orthotopically implanted PDA tumors with a corresponding reduction in RAS/MAPK signaling.\n...\nThese data support future clinical trials of ADT-1004 as a monotherapy for the treatment of patients with PDA regardless of the underlying mutation.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-04", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.8133}, "jev_score": 0.8052, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Broad-Spectrum RAS Inhibition in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma: Mechanistic Advances and Therapeutic Promise", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8247/18/12/1788", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Its prodrug, ADT-1004, exhibited improved pharmacokinetic stability and potent tumor regression in PDAC xenografts and syngeneic models.\n...\nIn patients with PDAC, the Disease Control Rate (DCR) and Objective Response Rate (ORR) were 87% and 20%, respectively [47].\n...\nOral administration of ADT-1004 demonstrated significant antitumor activity across multiple PDAC models, comprising orthotopically implanted murine cell lines and patient-derived xenografts (PDXs) possessing various KRAS mutations.\nThe therapy successfully suppressed RAS activation and subsequent MAPK signaling, as seen by reduced RAS-GTP and pERK levels.\n...\nADT-007 entirely suppressed colony formation in KRAS^G12C^ PDAC cells, surpassing the efficacy of sotorasib, adagrasib, the KRAS-specific inhibitor BI-2865, and the pan-RAS inhibitor RMC-6236.\nIn various PDAC models containing KRAS^G12D^ and KRAS^G12C^ mutations, ADT-007 demonstrated greater growth inhibitory efficacy than RMC-6236, indicating that targeting the nucleotide-free RAS state may yield improved anticancer effectiveness compared to inhibitors that interact with RAS in either its active or inactive forms [41,48].", "date": "2025-11-24", "last_updated": "2026-08-12", "orig_rank": 5, "answers": {"on_target": 0.95, "evidence": 0.83}, "jev_score": 0.7885, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Adt-1004: A First-in-Class, Orally Bioavailable Selective ...", "url": "https://augusta.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/adt-1004-a-first-in-class-orally-bioavailable-selective-pan-ras-i/fingerprints/", "domain": "augusta.elsevierpure.com", "snippet": "- PDAC Cells 75%\n- Tumor Growth 50%\n- Antitumor Activity 50%\n...\n- Tumor Growth 66%", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.8}, "jev_score": 0.776, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "A potent and selective pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004, targeting complex KRAS mutations for pancreatic cancer.", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/JCO.2024.42.23_suppl.90", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "When administered orally at 40 mg/kg body weight, ADT-1004 displayed robust antitumor activity in mouse tumor models using patient or mouse derived PDAC cell lines with G12D, G12V, G12C, or G13Q KRAS mutations, causing significant inhibitory effects on ERK phosphorylation.\n**Conclusions:**ADT-1004 inhibited tumor growth of KRAS mutant PDA tumors by targeting activated RAS to disrupt downstream MAPK/AKT signaling.", "date": "2024-08-10", "last_updated": "2025-03-03", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.99, "evidence": 0.78}, "jev_score": 0.7722, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Treatment of KRAS-Mutated Pancreatic Cancer: New Hope for the Patients?", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/17/15/2453", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "ADT-1004 (an oral prodrug of ADT-007) also showed strong antitumor activity in the PDAC model.\nMoreover, it had greater efficacy than sotorasib, adagrasib, and MRTX1133; however, it did not impact the growth of wild-type RAS PDAC cells [87].\n...\n|ADT-007|Pan-RAS|Preclinical|—|Better than sotorasib, adagrasib, MRTX1133 (preclinical)|Targets nucleotide-free RAS; preclinical efficacy superior to other agents|No data from clinical studies Does not impact the growth of wild-type RAS PDAC|", "date": "2025-07-24", "last_updated": "2026-08-13", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.96, "evidence": 0.7233}, "jev_score": 0.6944, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS Inhibition in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12842425/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Novel agents continue to diversify available options: AZD0022 (G12D-selective, oral) and RMC-5127 (G12V-selective, RAS(ON) tri-complex) extend allele coverage; pan-RAS small molecules (ADT-1004/ADT-007) inhibit nucleotide binding across mutant and WT RAS, priming immune responses in PDAC models;\n...\n181.Bandi D.S.R., Nagaraju G.P., Sarvesh S., Carstens J.L., Foote J.B., Graff E.C., Fang Y.-H.D., Keeton A.B., Chen X., Valiyaveettil J., et al.\nADT-1004: A first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with robust antitumor activity in preclinical models of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.", "date": "2026-01-21", "last_updated": "2026-09-27", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.6733}, "jev_score": 0.6598, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "link.springer.com · content · pdfCurrent advances in immunotherapy for KRAS-Mutant pancreatic...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10238-026-02201-8.pdf?error=cookies_not_supported&code=ce0f09ef-fe92-4325-aec6-159721a555d1", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "G12C inhibitors as monotherapy in PDAC remains modest.\nFor example, treatment with sotorasib alone achieved an \nORR of only 21%, with a median PFS of 4.0 months and a median OS of 6.9 months [83].\nA subsequent meta-analysis further reported an overall ORR of 25.1% for KRAS G12C inhibitor monotherapy, with median PFS and OS of 5.4 months and 8.2 months, respectively [84].\n...\nal. demonstrated that the orally available pan-RAS inhibitor ", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.06, "evidence": 0.2633}, "jev_score": 0.0158, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}], "total_ms": 1107.5, "ts": "2026-10-04T12:16:44+00:00"}
{"case_id": "q060-1", "row_id": "q060", "mode": "queries", "objective": "EOM613 sponsor development stage indication public source", "query": "EOM613 Crohn's disease trial current status", "anchors": ["EOM613 Crohn's disease", "EOM613"], "search_latency_ms": 11968.8, "target": "EOM613 Crohn's disease / EOM613", "ok": true, "error": null, "jev_latency_ms": 359.9, "calls": 10, "attempts": 10, "usage": {"input_tokens": 8793, "output_tokens": 360}, "cost_usd": 0.000369, "n_candidates": 10, "n_kept": 5, "abstain": false, "top_score": 0.9636, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports Promising Preclinical", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/01/07/3214524/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-promising-preclinical-results-and-provides-an-update-on-eom613-and-eom147.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "In 2025, the company completed IND-enabling toxicological and dosing preclinical animal studies for EOM613 with favorable results.\nNo adverse drug-related toxic effects were seen with daily subcutaneous injections of EOM613 for 28 consecutive days, including at 13.5 times the highest anticipated dosing levels in humans, indicating a wide margin of tolerability.\n...\nBased on these data, combined with previously reported promising results of our Phase I/IIa 2023 human clinical trial in hospitalized COVID-19 patients with cytokine-driven respiratory inflammation in Brazil, we are planning an initial Phase II exploratory clinical trial in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is designed to include 15-20 patients and will be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial will enroll subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the first quarter 2026 to test for signs of clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.\n...\nIn light of the Brazilian clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn’s disease.", "date": "2026-01-07", "last_updated": "2026-05-19", "orig_rank": 1, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9833}, "jev_score": 0.9636, "jev_rank": 1, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical plans 15-20-patient Phase II Crohn's disease ...", "url": "https://www.sahmcapital.com/news/content/eom-pharmaceutical-plans-15-20-patient-phase-ii-crohns-disease-trial-of-eom613-in-q1-2026-2026-03-24", "domain": "sahmcapital.com", "snippet": "EOM Pharmaceutical plans 15-20-patient Phase II Crohn’s disease trial of EOM613 in Q1 2026\n...\n- EOM reported that it completed IND-enabling toxicology and dosing animal studies for EOM613 with no adverse drug-related toxic effects observed after 28 days of daily subcutaneous dosing.\n- Prior Phase I/IIa human trial results in hospitalized COVID-19 patients were previously presented, and the company said it plans an initial Phase II exploratory trial of EOM613 in Crohn’s disease.\n- The planned Crohn’s study is expected to enroll 15–20 patients and is intended to start in Q1 2026 at sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, New Jersey.", "date": "2026-03-24", "last_updated": "2026-04-05", "orig_rank": 4, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.9833}, "jev_score": 0.9636, "jev_rank": 2, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceuticals", "url": "http://biobase.whitefordresearch.com/companies/eom-pharmaceuticals", "domain": "biobase.whitefordresearch.com", "snippet": "The named pipeline consists of EOM613 and EOM147, both of which the company says have prior Phase 2 clinical experience and are intended to use a 505(b)(2) development pathway where possible.[5] EOM613 has been studied historically in cancer cachexia and in a Phase 1/2a COVID-19 respiratory-inflammation trial, and the company announced in 2023 that it was preparing a Crohn's disease clinical program based on cytokine findings from the COVID-19 study.[\n...\nEOM remains a development-stage therapeutics company, and its public filing states that neither EOM613 nor EOM147 is approved in any jurisdiction.[5] The company says it intends to pursue FDA 505(b)(2) filings for both assets, relying partly on prior clinical and public-domain data, but it also acknowledges that the FDA may require additional preclinical or clinical work.[5] Current value creation therefore depends on financing, execution of planned studies, and regulatory acceptance of the proposed development pathway.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-15", "orig_rank": 6, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.94}, "jev_score": 0.9118, "jev_rank": 3, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Reports FDA Clearance of ...", "url": "https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/05/27/3301943/0/en/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-reports-fda-clearance-of-an-ind-for-a-clinical-trial-in-cachectic-cancer-patients.html", "domain": "globenewswire.com", "snippet": "The trial is anticipated to be initiated early in the third quarter of 2026.\nThe Company is also planning an additional exploratory, open-label EOM613 clinical trial in Crohn’s disease.\nThis trial is intended to be conducted at clinical sites in New Brunswick and Lakewood, NJ, under Principal Investigator Arkady Broder, MD, Director of Gastroenterology at the St. Peter’s Healthcare System.\nThe trial is planned to enroll 15 to 20 subjects with moderate to severe Crohn’s disease who have failed or could not tolerate at least one conventional therapy.\nEOM anticipates initiating the trial in the fourth quarter 2026 to test for signs of both clinical remission and endoscopic remission with EOM613 monotherapy treatment and to measure biomarkers such as C-reactive protein, calprotectin and pro-inflammatory serum cytokines.", "date": "2026-05-27", "last_updated": "2026-05-28", "orig_rank": 3, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.9333}, "jev_score": 0.9053, "jev_rank": 4, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceutical Holdings Provides an Update on its Lead Drug Candidate EOM613", "url": "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/eom-pharmaceutical-holdings-provides-an-update-on-its-lead-drug-candidate-eom613-301978052.html", "domain": "prnewswire.com", "snippet": "In light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\nEOM is also assembling a team of key opinion leader gastroenterologists, who are experts in clinical research relating to novel treatment approaches to IBD, to advise on the design and execution of the planned clinical trial.\nOn September 1, 2023, EOM filed a provisional patent application for EOM613 for use in inflammatory bowel disease with the United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO).\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B. Taraporewala, PhD, Chief Executive Officer of EOM.", "date": "2023-11-07", "last_updated": "2025-07-05", "orig_rank": 7, "answers": {"on_target": 0.98, "evidence": 0.8933}, "jev_score": 0.8754, "jev_rank": 5, "kept": true, "reason": "kept"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "ImmunoCellular Therapeutics Ltd. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/5ce8654ab76cb12db589696a5cb380f9", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "NCT05212532\n/ Unknown statusPhase 1\nA Proof-of-Concept Study Evaluating Safety, Tolerability, and Preliminary Efficacy of EOM613 in COVID-19 Infected Patients With Severe Symptoms\n...\nIn light of these clinical trial results, the company is currently preparing to initiate an EOM613 clinical program in Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM is now planning and designing a future clinical program for Crohn's disease patients who have failed currently available therapies or are unable to tolerate them,\" stated Shalom Z. Hirschman, MD, Chief Medical Officer of EOM.\n...\nEOM is planning to undertake IND-enabling animal toxicology and preclinical studies relevant to Crohn's disease.\n...\n\"EOM's priority is to initiate a clinical program in inflammatory bowel disease as soon as possible,\" added Irach B.\n...\nEOM613, administered by subcutaneous (SC) injection was well-tolerated by hospitalized COVID-19 patients.\n...\nPost-baseline clinically significant beneficial changes were seen with EOM613 treatment for two of the blood serum cytokines involved in the cytokine storm in hospitalized COVID-19 patients, namely, soluble interleukin-2 receptor (sIL-2R) and interleukin-10 (IL-10).\n...\n|AVR-118|Crohn Disease More|Preclinical|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30", "orig_rank": 8, "answers": {"on_target": 0.97, "evidence": 0.85}, "jev_score": 0.8245, "jev_rank": 6, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "EOM Pharmaceuticals Appoints Business Veteran Wayne I. Danson ...", "url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20201217006039/en/EOM-Pharmaceuticals-Appoints-Business-Veteran-Wayne-I.-Danson-as-Chief-Financial-Officer-and-Treasurer", "domain": "businesswire.com", "snippet": "“Wayne brings a wealth of financial experience and executive leadership to EOM as we prepare to secure additional funding to further invest in clinical trial development for our lead compounds, EOM613 and EOM147, both of which have already been in Phase 2 clinical trials and have demonstrated improved relevant clinical measures and tolerability,” said Dr. Taraporewala, EOM Chief Executive Officer and Director.\n...\nEOM613 is an investigational, first-in-class, dual-acting, broad-spectrum immunomodulator designed to provide both an anti-inflammatory effect at the site of cytokine and chemokine overactivity, and a pro-inflammatory effect, when needed.\n...\nEOM613 has already demonstrated clinical improvements in various disease states, and immune-related biomarkers and general tolerability across five Phase 2 clinical trials in patients with cachexia associated with AIDS and cancer, and in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.\n...\nClinical trials are currently being planned to study EOM613 to treat patients with the most severe effects of COVID-19 and for patients with cancer cachexia, a chemokine-related body-wasting syndrome.\n...\nMultiple Phase 2 trials suggest EOM613 and EOM147 have improved relevant clinical measures and are well tolerated.", "date": "2020-12-17", "last_updated": "2026-07-07", "orig_rank": 10, "answers": {"on_target": 0.85, "evidence": 0.84}, "jev_score": 0.714, "jev_rank": 7, "kept": false, "reason": "max_keep"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "DataLookout — Clinical Trial Intelligence", "url": "https://dashboard.datalookout.com/disease/crohn-s-disease", "domain": "dashboard.datalookout.com", "snippet": "Trial completed\nFinal enrollment: 243 participants (planned 200)", "date": "2026-08-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03", "orig_rank": 2, "answers": {"on_target": 0.14, "evidence": 0.5833}, "jev_score": 0.0817, "jev_rank": 8, "kept": false, "reason": "low_on_target"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Clinical Trials for Crohn's Disease